</div>

<script>
const DATA = {"namas":[{"n":1,"gloss":"THE holy Mother","body":"The mother is usually called upon in times of sorrow; but our natural mothers are not able to remove the three kinds of pains (Tāpatraya). Great men have said: “Since I have had many thousands of births, I have had many mothers; many also have been my fathers; I know not how many I am yet to have in the future; and their number is beyond calculation. O Treasurehouse of compassion! save me, who am overpowered with fear and have no other refuge, from the vast and disastrous ocean of Saṃsāra.\" The greatest worldmother is the only one who is capable of removing the endless misery (of existence). We should praise her as the mother so that she may be induced to show mercy to us.\nOr, Srīmāta: Sri Lakṣmī or Sarasvatī, and mātā, the mother, i.e., the mother of Lakṣmī or Sarasvatī ; the Vyadikosa says: Sri means Lakṣmī, Sarasvatī, nurse or earth (Dhātrī), Varga (group or family) prosperity, success, beauty, means of subsistence, the decoration of ornaments, wisdom and the first day of the fortnights.' Hence, Śrīmata does not mean here the wife of Rudra, the equal of Sarasvatī and Lakṣmī, but, the creator of the three, the spouse of Paramaśiva, the Great Queen (Parā Bhattarika). Hence in order to attain mokṣa she should be praised.\nOr, ma to measure, that which measures S'ri, i.e., Lakṣmī, that which limits being necessarily superior to\nthe thing limited; thus the meaning is that She is the limitless Sri, i.e., Salvation.\nOr, Sri, as said in the Śrutis, \"which is the ambrosia to great men means Vedas; She who taught Brahmā the three Vedas little by little (ma); the Śruti (Sve. Up., 18) says: 'He who made Brahmā in the beginning and tanght the Vedas to him.\"1\n6-\nOr, She is the first manifestation in the form of Vyāsa (ma, the classifier of the Vedas).\nOr, Sri, poison; ma, to fix—(referring to the poison fixed in Śiva's throat). In these two last cases the name S'rīmātā is masculine, Śiva and Śakti being one and the same; or it (Srīmātā) is feminine qualifying (Viseshana) one of Devī's aspects Prakāśa or Vimarśa (Primary or Secondary) which are understood in that name.\n2):\nOr, the names of great men and things should have the honorific word Sri prefixed to them. Hence we find the words, Śrīcakra, Srīšaila, Śrīvidyā, Sriphala, etc. In this case Mața means progenitor of all. This meaning is supported by the Śruti (Tai.-Up., III, That from which these beings are born.\" As we do not find the word Sri prefixed to the names Īśvara, Īśāna, etc., meaning Paraśiva, so also the word Mata means Tripurasundarī.\nOr, when the six letters, Ha, Sa, Ra, Ka, La, Da, of the mantra called Bālā' are combined with three vowels at the end, the result is the mantra forming the three groups (Kūṭas) called Mātā. This explanation is supported by great men who in their commentaries on the Śloka (Laghu Stuti Slo. 18) beginning with \"Māyā, Kundalini, etc. in which the word Mata occurs, explained it in this 'way.'\n1 If the other reading Tasmāt, adopted then the meaning would be \"promulgated the Vedas through him.\"\nBhaṭṭa Nārāyaṇa.—Srī, Universe, and Mătă, creator; or Sri, aisvarya, and Mātā, knower or witness.","dev":"श्रीमाता","iast":"śrīmātā","pada":[{"dev":"श्री","iast":"śrī"},{"dev":"माता","iast":"mātā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":59,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":0},{"n":2,"gloss":"The great Queen","body":"The controller of the created Universe. The Śruti (Tai:-Up., III, 2) says \"That by which, when created they live.\"\nThe three letters of Śrīvidyā are hidden here; one of them is called Shodasikala (the sixteenth and last part). For it is said, \"That Kāla (sixteenth) should be imparted to a faithful student, devoted to his Guru.\" This has not been learned by many modern students from the Guru; but it has been revealed by Lolla in his commentary on the Saundaryalaharī (32nd Slo.) where he has made it plain that it is the first among the Mantras of Chaturlakshmi. The other two (letters) are called Prakāva and Vimarśa (Primary and Secondary). For the Sanketapaddhati says,\nA is the first of all letters, Prakāśa, and the supreme Śiva; Ha is the last and stands for Kāla (Devī) and it is said to be Vimarśa.\" These two are very secret, and their nature is described in the Yoginīhṛdaya (II, 21) rather in a veiled manner. “To Her the Viyat and Kāla (i.e., a and ha) are the other (Śabda) forms, which are in the second bindu and at the end of Visarga, respectively, which are supreme, crossed (ie., tended towards manifestation).'' We have ourselves dealt with the subject in full in the Varivasyārahasya.\nAgain the part of the word Rājñī (Queen) contains the Mantra called “Māyārājni ”.","dev":"श्रीमहाराज्ञी","iast":"śrīmahārājñī","pada":[{"dev":"श्री","iast":"śrī"},{"dev":"महाराज्ञी","iast":"mahārājñī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":61,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":0},{"n":3,"gloss":"The ruler on the lionthrone'","body":"The throne occupied by kings is called Siṃhāsana, i.e., the best seat.\n1 Bhāskarārāya wrote, after this present work, an elaborate commentary on Yoginīhṛdaya and named it Setubandha. He says, when he comments on the above verse, that there are four bindus named Turīya, or Kāmakalā (the unity of Śiva and Devī), (2) Kamabindu or Madhyahindu (a, Śiva himself), (3) Visarga (Ha, Devī), and (4) Hārdakalā.\n*Thrones are generally supported by golden figures of lions placed at the corners.\nThe word Siṃha may mean the lion that is the vehicle of Devī. The Devī Pr. in the chapter on the meaning of her names says, \"The Devī, in her girlhood, seated on a lion, slew Mahiṣa (an Asura); hence she is known as the slayer of Mahiṣa and as Simhasanesvari (the Queen seated on a lion).\nAgain, Siṃha, pain. The grammarians say: word Siṃha came out of the root himsa, pain, Śiva from the root Vas desire; these two words are formed by reversing their respective letters like the word Pasyaka (seer) to Kasyapa.\" Āsana expelling, and Īśvarī powerful; i.e., she is the destroyer of the Universe. Thus the Śruti (Tai.- Up.. III, 2) says, 'That into which they enter at their\ndeath.\"\nOr, ma five. There are eight Mantras called Siṃhāsana, from Chaitanyabhairavi to Sampatpradabhairavi. Six of these taken together form three pairs, which with the remaining two make up five, situated in the four quarters and in the centre. The Jñānārṇava says: O great ruler, tell me how Devī Tripurā, ascended the five thrones and how the thrones came into existence.\" Thus questioned Śiva replied, \"O ruler of the Devas, Brahmā, when he began to create, was devoid of intellect; he worshipped Tripurasundarī and became the creator. Having with great Tapas worshipped Brahmā, Indra became the king of the Devas and the protector of the Eastern quarter. Then Tripurā became pleased and established herself in the eastern part of the throne, etc.\" Thus the name which contains the Mantras is the Siṃhāsana mantra.1\nHaving thus by the above three names indicated Brahman in his three aspects of creator, preserver, and destroyer, the author proceeds to state from names 4 to 999, the birth, etc., of the Mother, according to the Purāṇas,\n1 Bh. Nārāyaṇa says, Sri, Lakṣmīs, i.e., Devī's Siṃhāsana is surrounded by many Lakṣmīs.\nindicating the other two aspects, disappearance, and conferring benefits, otherwise called the cause of bondage, and the removal from it.","dev":"श्रीमत्सिंहासनेश्वरी","iast":"śrīmatsiṃhāsaneśvarī","pada":[{"dev":"श्रीमत्","iast":"śrīmat"},{"dev":"सिंह","iast":"siṃha"},{"dev":"आसन","iast":"āsana"},{"dev":"ईश्वरी","iast":"īśvarī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":61,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":0},{"n":4,"gloss":"Who was born from the altar of the fire of consciousness","body":"Cit, pure Brahman, and he is the altar of fire; for he dispels the darkness of ignorance (Avidyā). In the stanza, In the fire of consciousness that burns within uninterruptedly, without fuel, dispelling the darkness of illusion \" we see that consciousness is compared with fire. commentary in the Śakti Sūtras on the Sūtra beginning chitvahni ..\" (the fire of consciousness, etc.) says consciousness is fire because by its nature it consumes the Universe.\" Born (Sambhūta): By the quality called consciousness she abides in that fire, but is not born from it. Because consciousness and the thing possessed of that is one and the same. Sankshepasārīrakāchārya says: 66 The pure energy of consciousness of the Highest Lord is said to be consciousness itself.\"\nOr, agnikunda: the ordinary altar of the fire of consciousness. The simile is used in the Bh.-Gītā also (IV, 37): \"0 Arjuna, so does the fire of wisdom reduce all the actions to ashes.\" Devī took her birth from the sacrificial fire as it is said in the Reṇukā Pr. 'There was in the family of Ikshvāku, a prince named Renu, etc.,\" after describing his penance and the boon got from Devī it goes on, meanwhile, O twiceborn one, out of the sacrifi cial fire slowly arose a woman divinely beautiful, adorned with divine ornaments and bright as the moon, and stepped out quickly. She is the only one, the supporter of the Universe, there is none beside her.' The Brahmāṇḍa Pr., after describing how Indra was persecuted by Bhaṇḍāsura, says: Then he constructed a very beautiful sacrificial altar many Yojanas in area\nthe flesh of their body as oblations\nand the Devas offered\nWhen the Devas\nwere about to offer up the whole of their bodies there\nappeared a great blaze of light, bright as millions of suns and cool as millions of moons put together; in the midst of this light was seen\nan indescribable wheelform (Śrīcakra); from its centre arose the great Devī shining like the morning sun\nOn beholding the great Devī, the soul of all, Indra and the Devas were filled with ecstasy and they bowed down to her again and again.\"\nTo the doubter of the birth of the eternal Devī, the reply is given by the following name.","dev":"चिदग्निकुण्डसंभूता","iast":"cidagnikuṇḍasaṃbhūtā","pada":[{"dev":"चित्","iast":"cit"},{"dev":"अग्नि","iast":"agni"},{"dev":"कुण्ड","iast":"kuṇḍa"},{"dev":"संभूता","iast":"saṃbhūtā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":63,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":0},{"n":5,"gloss":"Manifested herself for fulfilling the objects of the Devas. (Devakārya samudyatā. ̄ ̄","body":"Manifested herself for fulfilling the objects of the Devas. (Devakārya samudyatā. ̄ ̄ ̄\nShe became manifest in order to slay the Asuras, Bhaṇḍāsura and Mahishasura and for other reasons. The Mārkaṇḍeya Pr. says: Though eternal, she is said to be born whenever she manifests herself for the accomplishment of the object of the Devas.\" In the Karma Pr. the Devī says to Himavat, Called upon by the Devas and remembering the greatness of the task, disregarding my father Dakṣa, who insulted Maheśvara (my husband), and for the sake of establishing righteousness, and by reason of your devotion, I was born from the body of Menā, having chosen you as my father\n(1)\nThus having described the real (Prakāśa) form of Devī, he now describes her secondary (Vimarśa) form.","dev":"देवकार्यसमुद्यता","iast":"devakāryasamudyatā","pada":[{"dev":"देव","iast":"deva"},{"dev":"कार्य","iast":"kārya"},{"dev":"समुद्यता","iast":"samudyatā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.25,"cpage":64,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":0},{"n":6,"gloss":"Bright as thousands of rising suns","body":"This indicates that She is very red. The Svachchandatantra says: Lalitā the universal form, is said to be Her own self (i.e., Prakāśa), the red colour is her Vimarśa one. To worship Her is to meditate on Her as such.\" The Vāmakeś varatantra also says, The Devī Tripurā herself is her real form, and her redcomplexion is the manifested one.\"\nThus there are three forms of Devī, which equally partake of both the Prakāśa and Vimarśa aspects, viz., the physical (Sthūla), the subtle (Sūkshma), and the supreme\n(Parā); the physical form has hands, feet, etc. ; the subtle consists of Mantra and the supreme is the Vāsanā (though it means ideal or mental; according to Mantra Śāstra it is a technical word meaning real or own). The subtle form again is threefold (vide the com. on 11th name).' Of these three forms, the physical one is now described.","dev":"उद्यद्भानुसहस्राभा","iast":"udyadbhānusahasrābhā","pada":[{"dev":"उद्यत्","iast":"udyat"},{"dev":"भानु","iast":"bhānu"},{"dev":"सहस्र","iast":"sahasra"},{"dev":"आभा","iast":"ābhā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.833,"cpage":64,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":1},{"n":7,"gloss":"Endowed with four arms","body":"","dev":"चतुर्बाहुसमन्विता","iast":"caturbāhusamanvitā","pada":[{"dev":"चतुर्","iast":"catur"},{"dev":"बाहु","iast":"bāhu"},{"dev":"समन्विता","iast":"samanvitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":65,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":1},{"n":8,"gloss":"Holding the noose of desire","body":"After mentioning her arms, he describes her weapons which are of three kinds, by this and the three next names. Desire (Rāga) or a peculiar mental modification or wish (Icchā). Desire is the Supreme (the 3rd) vāsanā form, the noose being the gross form. This weapon she has in her lower left hand.","dev":"रागस्वरूपपाशाढ्या","iast":"rāgasvarūpapāśāḍhyā","pada":[{"dev":"राग","iast":"rāga"},{"dev":"स्वरूप","iast":"svarūpa"},{"dev":"पाश","iast":"pāśa"},{"dev":"आढ्या","iast":"āḍhyā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":65,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":1},{"n":9,"gloss":"Shining with the elephant hook of both wrath and worldly knowledge","body":"Wrath (Krodha) is the mental modification known as hatred. Ākāra worldly knowledge. Some say krodha means knowledge; but it is opposed to the Śruti which says, wrath is elephanthook.\" Hence she shines holding in her lower right hand the elephant hook which is both hatred and worldly knowledge. The Parvachatussatisāstra The noose and the elephanthook of Her are spoken of as desire and anger.\" The Tantra Raja in the Vāsanā chapter says: '66 Mind is the bow of Sugarcane, desire the noose, anger the goad, and the five subtle elements (Tanmātras) the five arrows of flowers.” But\nsays:\nThe MS. has the following lines: in the Yogavasishta the Lord \"O Sinless one know that I have two forms, ordinary and supreme. The ordinary is possessed with hands, etc., which the ignorant men worship. My supreme form, which has no beginuing nor end the only one, without affliction, is named by the words Brahman, Ātman, Paramatma, etc.\" \"The ordinary is divided into two as physical and subtle. The In another place also it is said water, etc., of the Gaṅgā are the fourth form more dense.\"\n2 It is said to be another name for the first part of the Vāmakesvara tantra, and the second one being called Uttarachatussati or Yoginīhṛdaya '.\nYoginīhṛdaya (I, 53) says: The noose is Icchāśakti, the goad, Jñāna, and the bow and arrows Kriyāśakti.” (2)","dev":"क्रोधाकाराङ्कुशोज्ज्वला","iast":"krodhākārāṅkuśojjvalā","pada":[{"dev":"क्रोधा","iast":"krodhā"},{"dev":"कारा","iast":"kārā"},{"dev":"अङ्कुश","iast":"aṅkuśa"},{"dev":"उज्ज्वला","iast":"ujjvalā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":65,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":1},{"n":10,"gloss":"Armed with the sugarcane bow of mind","body":"Mind characterised with both Saṃkalpa and Vikalpa. This is in her left upper hand.1","dev":"मनोरूपेक्षुकोदण्डा","iast":"manorūpekṣukodaṇḍā","pada":[{"dev":"मनस्","iast":"manas"},{"dev":"रूप","iast":"rūpa"},{"dev":"इक्षु","iast":"ikṣu"},{"dev":"कोदण्डा","iast":"kodaṇḍā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":66,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":2},{"n":11,"gloss":"Having the arrows of the five subtle elements","body":"By the qualities of\netc.\"\nThe Svacchandasaṃgraha says : sound, etc., the elements are to be decided The five subtle elements, viz., sound, touch, etc., are the arrows; these are in her upper right hand. The Vāmakeśvaratantra says, sound, touch, and other subtle elements are her arrows and mind her bow.\" The Kādimata also says: The arrows are of three kindsgross, subtle and supreme; the gross are flowers, the subtle are the mantras, and the supreme are the vāsanās. The gross arrows are the five flowers, the lotus, the Rakta Kairava, the Kalhāra, the Indivara and the mangoflower.\" The Kālikā Pr. describes them thus causing joy,' 'pleasing,' 'causing confusion,' causing to fade,' and 'causing death,' are the five arrows which produce confusion even in ascetics.\" The Jñānārṇava says: confusing,'putting to flight,' \"attracting,\" controlling' and 'causing madness,' are the five names of the arrows.' The Trantrarāja says, ing,' 'maddening,' 'confusing,' 'stimulating,' and 'causing to fade' are the five arrows.\nexcit-\nAgain these names (8 to 11) contain the weapon Mantras. The word 'Ragasva (of 8th) can be divided into Ra+aga+Sva. Aga means Sthānu, i.e., Śiva whose letter is Ha. The lexicon says : Ha means Śiva, ether, and Sthanu (a post and one of the aspects of Śiva). Sva, i with the Bindu (m). Thus the subtle form of the noose is ihe Mantra formed by combining Ra, Ha, and i, with the\n1Bh. Närä: mind represents the whole universe; so the Gopavana grati says \"Mind has five qualities.\"\nBindu; of course ra follows ha as usual. Krodhākāra, etc., (9th) Kro+dha+a=Krodha. The word kāra joins at the end of each of the three; Ankusa, i.e., the Anusvara\n(m)..\nManorupa, etc. (10th) Manas means tha. The lexicon says, tha means the ruler of the right nose and mind. Kodaṇḍa Anusvāra (m). The Kośa says, 66 Kodaṇḍa means Anusvāra.\" The names 9, 10 and 11 contains Da, Ra, Ka, La, Ya, Sa, and Va, and a, i, u, with Bindu. When combined according to the rules, these give the Bīja (seed letter) of the Mantras of weapons. The division of the Bījas for the weapons can only be learned from the Guru.’","dev":"पञ्चतन्मात्रसायका","iast":"pañcatanmātrasāyakā","pada":[{"dev":"पञ्चतन्मात्र","iast":"pañcatanmātra"},{"dev":"सायका","iast":"sāyakā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":66,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":2},{"n":12,"gloss":"Bathing the whole universe with her own rosy effulgence","body":"Rosyeffulgenceas precribed for the morning contemplation in the Nyāsas, viz., Saubhāgya, etc. (vide Yoginīhṛdaya, III, 48 to 74).","dev":"निजारुणप्रभापूरमज्जद्ब्रह्माण्डमण्डला","iast":"nijāruṇaprabhāpūramajjadbrahmāṇḍamaṇḍalā","pada":[{"dev":"निज","iast":"nija"},{"dev":"अरुण","iast":"aruṇa"},{"dev":"प्रभा","iast":"prabhā"},{"dev":"पूर","iast":"pūra"},{"dev":"मज्जत्","iast":"majjat"},{"dev":"ब्रह्माण्ड","iast":"brahmāṇḍa"},{"dev":"मण्डला","iast":"maṇḍalā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.957,"cpage":67,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":2},{"n":13,"gloss":"Her hair is adorned with the (flowers of) Champaka, Asoka, Punnaga and Saugandhika","body":"The physical form of Devī as born from the altar of fire (4th name) is described from head to foot, the head being taken up first as it also represents the first Kata named Vāgbhava (group) of the Pañcadaśī Mantra.\nHer hair gives scent to the flowers. knowest, O. honey bee, the smell of the flowers. Give'me It is said, Thou the truth. Can I compare the odours of the hair of Devī with anything?\ngems.","dev":"चम्पकाशोकपुन्नागसौगन्धिकलसत्कचा","iast":"campakāśokapunnāgasaugandhikalasatkacā","pada":[{"dev":"चम्पक","iast":"campaka"},{"dev":"अशोक","iast":"aśoka"},{"dev":"पुन्नाग","iast":"punnāga"},{"dev":"सौगन्धिक","iast":"saugandhika"},{"dev":"लसत्","iast":"lasat"},{"dev":"कचा","iast":"kacā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":67,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":3},{"n":14,"gloss":"Her crown is resplendent with rows of Kuruvinda","body":"Kuruvinda gems are called Padmarāga, etc. Garuḍa Pr. in the Ratna chapter describes different kinds of Here the commentator has not given the Mantra, the result of the division.\n2 Bh. Nārā, like the idea of serpent in a rope the whole universe is false.\nrubies which can be obtained from different mines and rivers. The rubies obtained from Kuruvinda are to be preferred to those from Sphatika; they confer love, prosperity, and devotion to Hari.\" The drift is, if one who meditates on Her as wearing such gems, his devotion will be increased.\n(4)","dev":"कुरुविन्दमणिश्रेणीकनत्कोटीरमण्डिता","iast":"kuruvindamaṇiśreṇīkanatkoṭīramaṇḍitā","pada":[{"dev":"कुरुविन्द","iast":"kuruvinda"},{"dev":"मणि","iast":"maṇi"},{"dev":"श्रेणी","iast":"śreṇī"},{"dev":"कनत्","iast":"kanat"},{"dev":"कोटीर","iast":"koṭīra"},{"dev":"मण्डिता","iast":"maṇḍitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":67,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":3},{"n":15,"gloss":"Her forehead is bright as the moon on the eighth day","body":"","dev":"अष्टमीचन्द्रविभ्राजदलिकस्थलशोभिता","iast":"aṣṭamīcandravibhrājadalikasthalaśobhitā","pada":[{"dev":"अष्टमी","iast":"aṣṭamī"},{"dev":"चन्द्र","iast":"candra"},{"dev":"विभ्राजत्","iast":"vibhrājat"},{"dev":"अलिक","iast":"alika"},{"dev":"स्थल","iast":"sthala"},{"dev":"शोभिता","iast":"śobhitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":68,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":4},{"n":16,"gloss":"The tiny mark of Kastūrī on it is like the spot in the moon","body":"(5)","dev":"मुखचन्द्रकलङ्काभमृगनाभिविशेषका","iast":"mukhacandrakalaṅkābhamṛganābhiviśeṣakā","pada":[{"dev":"मुख","iast":"mukha"},{"dev":"चन्द्र","iast":"candra"},{"dev":"कलङ्क","iast":"kalaṅka"},{"dev":"आभ","iast":"ābha"},{"dev":"मृगनाभि","iast":"mṛganābhi"},{"dev":"विशेषका","iast":"viśeṣakā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":68,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":4},{"n":17,"gloss":"Her eyebrows are the entrance arches of the palace of Kamarāja","body":"","dev":"वदनस्मरमाङ्गल्यगृहतोरणचिल्लिका","iast":"vadanasmaramāṅgalyagṛhatoraṇacillikā","pada":[{"dev":"वदन","iast":"vadana"},{"dev":"स्मर","iast":"smara"},{"dev":"माङ्गल्य","iast":"māṅgalya"},{"dev":"गृह","iast":"gṛha"},{"dev":"तोरण","iast":"toraṇa"},{"dev":"चिल्लिका","iast":"cillikā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":68,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":5},{"n":18,"gloss":"Her eyes are like fishes playing in the tank of the beauty of her face","body":"(6)","dev":"वक्त्रलक्ष्मीपरीवाहचलन्मीनाभलोचना","iast":"vaktralakṣmīparīvāhacalanmīnābhalocanā","pada":[{"dev":"वक्त्रलक्ष्मी","iast":"vaktralakṣmī"},{"dev":"परीवाह","iast":"parīvāha"},{"dev":"चलन्","iast":"calan"},{"dev":"मीन","iast":"mīna"},{"dev":"आभ","iast":"ābha"},{"dev":"लोचना","iast":"locanā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":68,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":5},{"n":19,"gloss":"Her nose is beautiful like the newly blown champaka tower","body":"","dev":"नवचम्पकपुष्पाभनासादण्डविराजिता","iast":"navacampakapuṣpābhanāsādaṇḍavirājitā","pada":[{"dev":"नव","iast":"nava"},{"dev":"चम्पक","iast":"campaka"},{"dev":"पुष्प","iast":"puṣpa"},{"dev":"आभ","iast":"ābha"},{"dev":"नासा","iast":"nāsā"},{"dev":"दण्ड","iast":"daṇḍa"},{"dev":"विराजिता","iast":"virājitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.947,"cpage":68,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":6},{"n":20,"gloss":"Shining with the jewels on her nose, which excel the stars in splendour","body":"Tārā may also mean, two goddesses, Maṅgala and Suklā; or one of the goddesses, Tāraka. The jewels are Māṇikya and Mauktika.\n(7)","dev":"ताराकान्तितिरस्कारिनासाभरणभासुरा","iast":"tārākāntitiraskārināsābharaṇabhāsurā","pada":[{"dev":"तारा","iast":"tārā"},{"dev":"कान्ति","iast":"kānti"},{"dev":"तिरस्कारि","iast":"tiraskāri"},{"dev":"नासा","iast":"nāsā"},{"dev":"आभरण","iast":"ābharaṇa"},{"dev":"भासुरा","iast":"bhāsurā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":68,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":6},{"n":21,"gloss":"Decked with clusters of the Kadamba flowers worn above her ears","body":"","dev":"कदम्बमञ्जरीक्ऌप्तकर्णपूरमनोहरा","iast":"kadambamañjarīkḷptakarṇapūramanoharā","pada":[{"dev":"कदम्ब","iast":"kadamba"},{"dev":"मञ्जरी","iast":"mañjarī"},{"dev":"क्लृप्त","iast":"klṛpta"},{"dev":"कर्णपूर","iast":"karṇapūra"},{"dev":"मनोहरा","iast":"manoharā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":68,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":7},{"n":22,"gloss":"The two jewels in them are the sun and the moon","body":"It is said, The sun and the moon are the breasts, eyes, and earrings of Devī; so says the Śruti.”\nWa","dev":"ताटङ्कयुगलीभूततपनोडुपमण्डला","iast":"tāṭaṅkayugalībhūtatapanoḍupamaṇḍalā","pada":[{"dev":"ताटङ्क","iast":"tāṭaṅka"},{"dev":"युगली","iast":"yugalī"},{"dev":"भूत","iast":"bhūta"},{"dev":"तपन","iast":"tapana"},{"dev":"उडुप","iast":"uḍupa"},{"dev":"मण्डला","iast":"maṇḍalā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":68,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":7},{"n":23,"gloss":"Her cheeks eclipse the brightness of the ruby, (8) Padmarāga","body":"","dev":"पद्मरागशिलादर्शपरिभाविकपोलभूः","iast":"padmarāgaśilādarśaparibhāvikapolabhūḥ","pada":[{"dev":"पद्मराग","iast":"padmarāga"},{"dev":"शिला","iast":"śilā"},{"dev":"दर्श","iast":"darśa"},{"dev":"परिभावि","iast":"paribhāvi"},{"dev":"कपोल","iast":"kapola"},{"dev":"भूः","iast":"bhūḥ"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":68,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":8},{"n":24,"gloss":"Her lips put to shame the colour of fresh corals and Bimba fruit","body":"(9)\n7.","dev":"नवविद्रुमबिम्बश्रीन्यक्कारिरदनच्छदा","iast":"navavidrumabimbaśrīnyakkāriradanacchadā","pada":[{"dev":"नव","iast":"nava"},{"dev":"विद्रुम","iast":"vidruma"},{"dev":"बिम्ब","iast":"bimba"},{"dev":"श्री","iast":"śrī"},{"dev":"न्यक्कारि","iast":"nyakkāri"},{"dev":"रदन","iast":"radana"},{"dev":"च्छदा","iast":"cchadā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.973,"cpage":68,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":8},{"n":25,"gloss":"She shines with her two rows of teeth in the form of the bud of pure knowledge","body":"In the Sabhapati śuddhavidyā parinaya, the identity of the idea I' and 'this' is set forth. In the Dattātreya Saṃhitā and other works also Śrīvidyā is explained from the standpoint of that identity. Hence Śrīvidyā is meant by Śuddhavidyā. S'uddha, pure, that which is the negation of the stain of ignorance. Vidyā, Shodasīvidya. Her teeth resemble the buds of the sixteen syllabled Mantra.\nThis (Ṣoḍaśī) sprung from the Mūlādhāra of the great Mother, and proceeding through the stages of Pară, Paśyantī, etc., emerged from her mouth in the Vaikhārī form; and it was transmitted from teacher to pupil.\nThe Pară form is mere sound (Śabdabrahman), the potentiality of growth in the seed; Paśyantī is the seed beginning to sprout; the Madhyamā is when the first two small leaves appear, but are Vaikharī is when these two small leaves are separated but not yet separated; the joined at the root. This is what is here called the bud (aṅkura); in that state there is a resemblance to the teeth. As they are thirtytwo in number so are the sixteen twofold buds of the sixteensyllabled mantra. indicates another meaning also; the Vidyā beginning with Or the word Dvija the Vedas chiefly belong to the Brāhmaṇas. The Śruti'says, “Vidyā approached a Brahman.\" Taught by him it (vidyā). spread abroad. Hence the Brāhmaṇas are the buds of vidyā. Moreover as the Brāhmaṇas came forth from the mouth of Devī, they may bẹ conìpared to her teeth.\nOr, there are thirtytwo initiations (Dīkṣās) as laid down in the Tantras (Vide the Amnayastava) viz. S'uddhavidya, Bālā, Dvädasardhamatangini, etc., up to Anuttara; these are called the two rows of the twiceborn';\n1 Vide chapter 1, verse 15, com. supra..\nTwiceborn refers also to teeth as they grow twice.\nfor initiation is a birth, and, regarding the thread ceremony (Upanayana as the first birth), it is a second birth. This is not contradicted by the saying, \"Initiation is the third birth\" because there the birth from the mother's womb is counted (as the first birth).\nOr Śuddhavidyā, the threesyllabled Mantra, aṅkura, beginning, i.e., a certain initiation is called by that name and is to be begun before going on to the thirtytwo initiations referred to above. Hence Devī can only be attained by those great men whose souls are purified by the thirtytwo Dīkṣās (from Śuddhavidyā to Anuttara) handed down according to tradition, and not by others.","dev":"शुद्धविद्याङ्कुराकारद्विजपङ्क्तिद्वयोज्ज्वला","iast":"śuddhavidyāṅkurākāradvijapaṅktidvayojjvalā","pada":[{"dev":"शुद्ध","iast":"śuddha"},{"dev":"विद्या","iast":"vidyā"},{"dev":"अङ्कुर","iast":"aṅkura"},{"dev":"आकार","iast":"ākāra"},{"dev":"द्विज","iast":"dvija"},{"dev":"पङ्क्ति","iast":"paṅkti"},{"dev":"द्वय","iast":"dvaya"},{"dev":"उज्ज्वला","iast":"ujjvalā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":69,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":9},{"n":26,"gloss":"The fragrance from the betel leaves she chews attracts the quarters","body":"(10)","dev":"कर्पूरवीटिकामोदसमाकर्षद्दिगन्तरा","iast":"karpūravīṭikāmodasamākarṣaddigantarā","pada":[{"dev":"कर्पूर","iast":"karpūra"},{"dev":"वीटिका","iast":"vīṭikā"},{"dev":"मोद","iast":"moda"},{"dev":"समाकर्षत्","iast":"samākarṣat"},{"dev":"दिगन्तरा","iast":"digantarā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.974,"cpage":70,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":9},{"n":27,"gloss":"The sweet melody of her words put to shame the Kacchapī (vīṇā of Sarasvatī)","body":"The Amaraśeṣa says, \"The vin of Viśvāvasu is named Bṛhatī, that of Tumburu, Kalāvatī, that of Nārada Mahāṭī, and of Sarasvatī Kacchapī.\"\nThe desire for hearing the Vīṇā, in the world, is due to the beautiful melody coming out indistinctly from Shadja, etc., of the Gamut, though there is no clear hearing of the letters. The Kacchapī of Sarasvatī, speaks like the parrot speech, a little distinct, along with melody. But it is indisputable that with distinct utterance the sweeter melody of her words excels all the musical instruments. So it is said in the Saundaryalaharī (66 Slo.), \"When Sarasvatī with her Vīṇā sings the various triumphs, (i.e., the destruction of Tripurā and Dakṣa, etc.) of Paśupati, O thou, of beautiful speech, she has immediately to close her instrument with her finger tips, directly you start your vocal music, shaking your head, and with sweet melody which has the power of influencing the Vină\nsound.\"","dev":"निजसल्लापमाधुर्यविनिर्भर्त्सितकच्छपी","iast":"nijasallāpamādhuryavinirbhartsitakacchapī","pada":[{"dev":"निज","iast":"nija"},{"dev":"सल्लाप","iast":"sallāpa"},{"dev":"माधुर्य","iast":"mādhurya"},{"dev":"विनिर्भर्त्सित","iast":"vinirbhartsita"},{"dev":"कच्छपी","iast":"kacchapī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.977,"cpage":70,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":10},{"n":28,"gloss":"The mind of Kāmeśa is drowned in the fulness of the glory of her sweet smile","body":"Kāma means Bindu which forms the part of the body of the Kāmakalā and which is the sun termed fire, and moon (Agni-Soma). The Kamakalāvilasa says, Bindu is the form of Ahaṃkāra; the sun is the form of the pair (Kāma and Kāla) of equal essence. Kāma is that which is desired; so also the Kāla. The two Bindus (Kāma and Kāla) are manifested as fire and moon.' \"\" Īśvara lord, that is Rajarājesvara. According to the doctrine of Mīmāṃsā the mind is omnipresent; when it is said that mind is 'drowned,' it shows that Devī's glory is unlimited.\n(11)","dev":"मन्दस्मितप्रभापूरमज्जत्कामेशमानसा","iast":"mandasmitaprabhāpūramajjatkāmeśamānasā","pada":[{"dev":"मन्द","iast":"manda"},{"dev":"स्मित","iast":"smita"},{"dev":"प्रभा","iast":"prabhā"},{"dev":"पूर","iast":"pūra"},{"dev":"मज्जत्","iast":"majjat"},{"dev":"कामेश","iast":"kāmeśa"},{"dev":"मानसा","iast":"mānasā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":71,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":10},{"n":29,"gloss":"Illuminated by the beauty of (Her) chin the equal of which is not to be found","body":"","dev":"अनाकलितसादृश्यचुबुकश्रीविराजिता","iast":"anākalitasādṛśyacubukaśrīvirājitā","pada":[{"dev":"अनाकलित","iast":"anākalita"},{"dev":"सादृश्य","iast":"sādṛśya"},{"dev":"चुबुक","iast":"cubuka"},{"dev":"श्री","iast":"śrī"},{"dev":"विराजिता","iast":"virājitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":71,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":11},{"n":30,"gloss":"Adorned with the marriage thread tied by Kāmeśa around her neck","body":"2\n(12)","dev":"कामेशबद्धमाङ्गल्यसूत्रशोभितकन्धरा","iast":"kāmeśabaddhamāṅgalyasūtraśobhitakandharā","pada":[{"dev":"कामेश","iast":"kāmeśa"},{"dev":"बद्ध","iast":"baddha"},{"dev":"माङ्गल्य","iast":"māṅgalya"},{"dev":"सूत्र","iast":"sūtra"},{"dev":"शोभित","iast":"śobhita"},{"dev":"कन्धरा","iast":"kandharā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":71,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":11},{"n":31,"gloss":"Her arms are encircled with golden ornaments","body":"necklace of","dev":"कनकाङ्गदकेयूरकमनीयभुजान्विता","iast":"kanakāṅgadakeyūrakamanīyabhujānvitā","pada":[{"dev":"कनक","iast":"kanaka"},{"dev":"अङ्गद","iast":"aṅgada"},{"dev":"केयूर","iast":"keyūra"},{"dev":"कमनीय","iast":"kamanīya"},{"dev":"भुजा","iast":"bhujā"},{"dev":"अन्विता","iast":"anvitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":71,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":12},{"n":32,"gloss":"Having a pearl dangling from a gems and gold","body":"Or, Those who meditate (cintaka) on the throat (Grīvā) alone are called Graiveya chintākas, i.e., they who cannot fix the deity in the cavity of the heart, but worship her externally are the middle class of worshippers.\nThe pearl dangling (lola) means, those who are sound by earthly desires. The Agni Pr. says,\nLola\nThis belongs to one of the Devī Tantras written by Punyānandanatha and a commentary upon it by Natananandanātha.\nThe marriage emblem tied round the neck was not the Aryan custom and was not found referred to in any Vedic literature. Earring was the emblem.\nmeans change and desire.\" These are the lowest class of worshippers.\nMuktas are the highest class of worshippers. She is in each case the fruit (Phala) to be obtained by these three classes according to their deserts. (13)","dev":"रत्नग्रैवेयचिन्ताकलोलमुक्ताफलान्विता","iast":"ratnagraiveyacintākalolamuktāphalānvitā","pada":[{"dev":"रत्न","iast":"ratna"},{"dev":"ग्रैवेय","iast":"graiveya"},{"dev":"चिन्ताक","iast":"cintāka"},{"dev":"लोल","iast":"lola"},{"dev":"मुक्ता","iast":"muktā"},{"dev":"फल","iast":"phala"},{"dev":"अन्विता","iast":"anvitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":71,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":12},{"n":33,"gloss":"Her two breasts are the price offered in exchange for that priceless gemthe love of Kāmeśvara","body":"","dev":"कामेश्वरप्रेमरत्नमणिप्रतिपणस्तनी","iast":"kāmeśvarapremaratnamaṇipratipaṇastanī","pada":[{"dev":"कामेश्वर","iast":"kāmeśvara"},{"dev":"प्रेम","iast":"prema"},{"dev":"रत्न","iast":"ratna"},{"dev":"मणि","iast":"maṇi"},{"dev":"प्रतिपण","iast":"pratipaṇa"},{"dev":"स्तनी","iast":"stanī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.976,"cpage":72,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":13},{"n":34,"gloss":"These two breasts are the two fruits growing on the creeperlike hair which springs from her deep navel","body":"(14)","dev":"नाभ्यालवालरोमालिलताफलकुचद्वयी","iast":"nābhyālavālaromālilatāphalakucadvayī","pada":[{"dev":"नाभि","iast":"nābhi"},{"dev":"आलवाल","iast":"ālavāla"},{"dev":"रोम","iast":"roma"},{"dev":"आलि","iast":"āli"},{"dev":"लता","iast":"latā"},{"dev":"फल","iast":"phala"},{"dev":"कुच","iast