ess of her worshippers","body":"Darkness, The worshippers, Brahimi, Viṣṇu, etc. ignorance. The meaning is the meditation on her feet dispels ignorance.\nIn the Matsya and Padma Purāṇas, there is a speech of Nārada, after interpreting the bodily marks (Simudrika) of Parvathi to her mother, Menā. A husband is not yet born for her, O Lady, She will be devoid of good bodily marks (because she is an unconditioned one) and she will always have her hand raised. (Because, a great giver herself, hers must necessarily be above that of the one who receives.) And her feet will err by their own shadow. What more should be said\"! Himavan hearing about these bad marks, addressed him with sorrow when Nārada replied: \"When you have an occasion for great joy why should you manifest sorrow! O Great Mountain; you are bewildered because you did not understand my speech.” After explaining the meaning of No husband is born,\" etc., (Śiva does not belong to the group of created beings) he says, \"I will explain what I meant by saying her feet will err by their own shadow.\" O best of Mountains, Her feet are like lotuses, shining with bright nails. When the Devas and Asuras prostrate before her, the various colours of their jewelled crowns reflected, will drive away the rays of her nails which entering their hearts dispel their Tamas quality.\"","dev":"नखदीधितिसंछन्ननमज्जनतमोगुणा","iast":"nakhadīdhitisaṃchannanamajjanatamoguṇā","pada":[{"dev":"नख","iast":"nakha"},{"dev":"दीधिति","iast":"dīdhiti"},{"dev":"संछन्न","iast":"saṃchanna"},{"dev":"नमज्जन","iast":"namajjana"},{"dev":"तमोगुणा","iast":"tamoguṇā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.914,"cpage":72,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":18},{"n":45,"gloss":"The soles of her feet by their beauty shame the lotus","body":"(19)","dev":"पदद्वयप्रभाजालपराकृतसरोरुहा","iast":"padadvayaprabhājālaparākṛtasaroruhā","pada":[{"dev":"पद","iast":"pada"},{"dev":"द्वय","iast":"dvaya"},{"dev":"प्रभा","iast":"prabhā"},{"dev":"जाल","iast":"jāla"},{"dev":"पराकृत","iast":"parākṛta"},{"dev":"सरोरुहा","iast":"saroruhā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":73,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":18},{"n":46,"gloss":"Her lotus feet are adorned with jewelled anklets that tinkle","body":"","dev":"शिञ्जानमणिमञ्जीरमण्डितश्रीपदाम्बुजा","iast":"śiñjānamaṇimañjīramaṇḍitaśrīpadāmbujā","pada":[{"dev":"शिञ्जान","iast":"śiñjāna"},{"dev":"मणि","iast":"maṇi"},{"dev":"मञ्जीर","iast":"mañjīra"},{"dev":"मण्डित","iast":"maṇḍita"},{"dev":"श्री","iast":"śrī"},{"dev":"पद","iast":"pada"},{"dev":"अम्बुजा","iast":"ambujā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":73,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":19},{"n":47,"gloss":"Her gait is that of the swan","body":"","dev":"मरालीमन्दगमना","iast":"marālīmandagamanā","pada":[{"dev":"मराली","iast":"marālī"},{"dev":"मन्द","iast":"manda"},{"dev":"गमना","iast":"gamanā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":73,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":19},{"n":48,"gloss":"The treasurehouse of beauty. sevadhih.)","body":"","dev":"महालावण्यशेवधिः","iast":"mahālāvaṇyaśevadhiḥ","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"लावण्य","iast":"lāvaṇya"},{"dev":"शेवधिः","iast":"śevadhiḥ"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.571,"cpage":73,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":19},{"n":49,"gloss":"All rosy hued","body":"(Mahalavanya-\n(20)\nHer garments, ornaments, flowers, colour, etc., are\nall rosy tinted.","dev":"सर्वारुणा","iast":"sarvāruṇā","pada":[{"dev":"सर्व","iast":"sarva"},{"dev":"अरुणा","iast":"aruṇā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":73,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":20},{"n":50,"gloss":"With faultless, limbs","body":"51.\nbhāsura.)\nAdorned with every ornament.\n(Sarvabharana-\nThe Kālikā Pr. describes forty jewels from crest jewel to the ring of the feet. The Kalpa Sūtras (of Parasurāma Khaṇḍa IV, 5) give more ornaments.","dev":"ऽनवद्याङ्गी","iast":"'navadyāṅgī","pada":[{"dev":"अनवद्य","iast":"anavadya"},{"dev":"अङ्गी","iast":"aṅgī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":74,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":20,"wbw":{"split":"अनवद्य-अङ्गी","splitIast":"anavadya-aṅgī","words":[{"dev":"अनवद्य","gloss":"faultless, flawless, beyond criticism"},{"dev":"अङ्गी","gloss":"one possessing limbs, one having a body"}],"meaning":"She whose limbs are flawless."}},{"n":51,"gloss":"With faultless, limbs, (Anavadyāngi.)","body":"51.\nbhāsura.)\nAdorned with every ornament.\n(Sarvabharana-\nThe Kālikā Pr. describes forty jewels from crest jewel to the ring of the feet. The Kalpa Sūtras (of Parasurāma Khaṇḍa IV, 5) give more ornaments.","dev":"सर्वाभरणभूषिता","iast":"sarvābharaṇabhūṣitā","pada":[{"dev":"सर्व","iast":"sarva"},{"dev":"आभरण","iast":"ābharaṇa"},{"dev":"भूषिता","iast":"bhūṣitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":74,"sure":0,"host":50,"v":20,"wbw":{"split":"सर्व-आभरण-भूषिता","splitIast":"sarva-ābharaṇa-bhūṣitā","words":[{"dev":"सर्व","gloss":"all"},{"dev":"आभरण","gloss":"ornaments, adornments"},{"dev":"भूषिता","gloss":"adorned, decorated"}],"meaning":"She who is adorned with every kind of ornament.","vigraha":["सर्वैः आभरणैः भूषिता।"]}},{"n":52,"gloss":"Sitting on the lap of Sivakamesvara","body":"The description of her physical form being ended that of the seat of Devī is commenced from this name.\nKāma means that which is desired; or he who assumes any form at will; this epithet (Kāma) is also applied to Manmatha (god of love) who can also assume any form at will. The Kālikā Pr. says, \"In the world there is none who like you is capable of assuming any form; hence, O mindborn one (Manmatha), you shall be known by the name Kāma.\"\nOr, Kāma means knowledge (Prajñāna). The Śruti says (Ait.-Up., 5-2): \"What is called the heart and the mind, are perception (Samjnana), command, understanding, knowledge, wisdom, seeing, holding, thinking, considering, readiness, (or suffering) remembering, conceiving, willing, breathing, loving, desiring; no, all these are only various names of knowledge.\" In the said Śruti the word Prajñāna means Śiva only. The Sūtasaṃhitā (Br.-Gītā, IV, 3-19 to 24) also supports this explanation. \"Vijñāna, which is Śaṃkara, is known by the learned under many names. Some say it is the heart Some other theists call it control (Vasya). But all these simply denote Śiva.\"\nOr, Kāma denotes the Lord who desires (Kāma) to create the universe. The Br. Up., (I, 4, 17) says: was Ātman only in the beginning.\nHe desired\nThere\nThus for the desire (Kāma).\" Hence the word Kāma\ndoes not mean here Rudra or Manmatha.","dev":"शिवकामेश्वराङ्कस्था","iast":"śivakāmeśvarāṅkasthā","pada":[{"dev":"शिव","iast":"śiva"},{"dev":"कामेश्वर","iast":"kāmeśvara"},{"dev":"अङ्क","iast":"aṅka"},{"dev":"स्था","iast":"sthā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":74,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":20,"wbw":{"split":"शिव-कामेश्वर-अङ्क-स्था","splitIast":"śiva-kāmeśvara-aṅka-sthā","words":[{"dev":"शिव","gloss":"Śiva"},{"dev":"कामेश्वर","gloss":"Kāmeśvara, Śiva as the Divine Consort"},{"dev":"अङ्क","gloss":"lap, bosom"},{"dev":"स्था","gloss":"seated, abiding, situated"}],"meaning":"She who is seated on the lap of Śiva-Kāmeśvara.","vigraha":["शिवकामेश्वरस्य अङ्के स्थिता।"]}},{"n":53,"gloss":"The beneficial (Śiva)","body":"From the root Vas desire, Śiva is derived.\"\nis she is the desire itself (Icchā) of the supreme Śiva. For this energy is worshipped by Śiva. The meaning of Śiva is given in the Śaivāgamas. Who is as a witness to the modifications (of the mind), who is before the arising of such modifications, who is in the modifications about to rise, who is the cause of sensation, who is the support of all false and inert matter, who is consciousness itself, who is beloved of all, who is bliss itself, who is the means of obtaining all, who is connected with all, the Omnipresent is called Śiva. The Selfshining, pure being who is devoid of the distinction of Jīva, Īśa, etc., is Śiva.\" Or, as she does good (Śiva) she is called S'iva; or that in which everything rests (S'ete); or, who possesses excellent (Śiva) qualities; or that which makes calm (S'amyati); as the Bhārata says, Because he fulfils all the actions (of men) intending their good (S'iva) he is known as Śiva. O gods, the Danavas, and the Devas are the same to me; I do good (S'iva) to all beings. Hence I am known as Śiva.\" The Śruti (Sve.-Up., 4-11)\n-6\nsays, The one who is the origin (of all) dwells in every womb, in whom all this is involved.\" (7) says, The Kaivalya Up.- threeeyed, bluethroated, and peaceful.\" All this has been brought together, by us in the commentary on the Sivashtottarasata (a work enumerating one hundred and eight names of Śiva). \"By natural purity, by possessing stainless qualities, by superiority, by sup porting the Universe, by conferring immortality (ou worshippers), by the strength of Icchā śakti, O Paramaśiva, thou art known by the special name as S'iva in the scriptures.\" Or, she is identical with Śiva. The Liṅga Pr. says, 'As is Śiva, so is Devī, as is Devī, so is S'iva, hence, as the notions are the same, Devī is called S'iva.\" In another place in the same book, \"In reality there is no difference between Umā and Śaṃkara; the one has assumed the two forms. There is no doubt in this. The Paramatman\nis called S'iva as well as Śiva.\" The Sutasambita (IV, 13-2 to 41) also says, O best of twiceborn ones, she who has assumed the energising aspect of that Māyā which is connected with one consciousness (chinmatra), who is intellect, without attributes, selfshining, unchangeable, supreme bliss, and the cause of the destruction of Saṃsāra. She is Sira, she is the supreme Devī, one with S'iva, and doer of good... he who worships this S'ankari, the ocean of mercy, what does he not obtain by her grace?\". Or S'iva may mean the wife of Vāyu. The Liṅga Pr. says, 66 The great god lsana, who pervades the whole Universe, the supporter of all beings is called Vāyu, in his aspect of wind God. His wife is called Śiva and his son Manojava.” Again, He who has the cresent moon as a crest is Vāyu and his wife is Śiva.\" In the Vāyu Pr. also we read, 66 Vāyu is the fourth body of Īśāna and his wife is S'iva and his son is Manojava.\"\nOr, she who bestows Salvation is Śiva. It is said in the Devī Pr.: \"Śiva is salvation and she bestows salvation to Yogins ; she works for good (Śiva); hence she is known by men as Śiva;\" or men worship Devī to reach Śiva, hence she is S'iva. In the Āgamas we read, 44 As heat is to fire, as light to the Sun, and moonlight to the Moon, so is Śiva to Śiva.”","dev":"शिवा","iast":"śivā","pada":[{"dev":"शिवा","iast":"śivā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":74,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":20,"wbw":{"split":"शिवा","splitIast":"śivā","words":[{"dev":"शिवा","gloss":"the Auspicious One; feminine form of Śiva"}],"meaning":"The Auspicious Goddess, the Divine Power of Śiva.","vigraha":["शिवं कल्याणं ददातीति शिवा।","शिवस्य शक्तिरूपा शिवा।"]}},{"n":54,"gloss":"She has won over her Lord","body":"This name indicates though Devī is Icchā and other qualities, yet she never becomes subject to him who possesses the qualities. Lord, Kāmeśvara. The Kālikā Pr. says, There he resides for ever playing with Pārvatī; the house of Devī is in the centre; and Śiva is subject to it.\" The Agama also says, \"To Her, through whom Śiva is enabled to bestow happiness and salvation on the mankind, who is consciousness itself, the first, with my whole being I prostrate myself.\" The St. Sam. (IV, 13-24) says, 0 sages, Śiva became the cause of the Universe, Devī is his Śakti; without Her he is powerless.\" The Ācārya also\n8*\n(Saun. La. Slo. I) says, When Śiva is united with Śakti he is able to create, otherwise he is unable even to move.\"\nOr, she through whom husbands are subjected. story runs thus in the seventh book of the De. Bhag. Pr. : The Aśvins made the husband of Sukanya, the daughter of Saryati, similar to themselves in form and asked her to point him out. She then resorted to Devī. 'O worldmother, I am deeply afflicted and flee to you for protection. Protect now, my wifehood, I worship thy feet.\" Thus praised, \"Devī, Tripurasundarī, gave her wisdom by which her husband became subject to her.\" We also read in the sixth book of the same how Śacī recovered Indra. (21)","dev":"स्वाधीनवल्लभा","iast":"svādhīnavallabhā","pada":[{"dev":"स्व","iast":"sva"},{"dev":"अधीन","iast":"adhīna"},{"dev":"वल्लभा","iast":"vallabhā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":76,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":20,"wbw":{"split":"स्व-अधीन-वल्लभा","splitIast":"sva-adhīna-vallabhā","words":[{"dev":"स्व","gloss":"her own"},{"dev":"अधीन","gloss":"devoted to, under the influence of"},{"dev":"वल्लभ","gloss":"beloved, husband, dear one"}],"meaning":"She whose beloved Lord is completely devoted to her.","vigraha":["स्वाधीनः वल्लभः यस्याः सा।"]}},{"n":55,"gloss":"Dwelling on the middle peak of Mount Sumeru","body":"After describing the important seat of Devī as on the lap of Kāmeśvara's left thigh (52 No.), the author now proceeds to describe some other seats also of Devī. middle peak is the fourth one. The great ascetic Durvāsa says in his Lalitāstavaratna (Sl. 2 to 4): 'Let the gold mountain be victorious, whose body is made up of the whole universe, filled by the music of the divine ladies living in the golden creeperbowers of mountain peak; we salute the three peaks of it, which are the seats of Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Śiva, expanding to the four quarters (of the globe). In the middle of them there is another peak, four hundred Yojanas in height, beautifying the place by the golden rays of flowers and I worship it.\"\n56. nayika.)\nRuler of the beautiful city. (Sriman nagara\nThe cities are of two kinds; one of them is on the middle peak of Mount Meru. The Lalitāstavaratna (Sl. 5) says, “I salute the city of ancient Vidyā four hundred Yojanas in circumference constructed by the celestial architect, beautified with many walls.” It is said in the Vidyaratnabashya, \"There is also another city in the midst\nof the Ocean of milk.\" With many walls means twentyfive in number. The second city is outside and beyond all the worlds, in the island of gems (Ratnadvīpa), in the middle of the Ocean of Nectar. The Rudrayāmala says, Outside and beyond the countless myriads of world systems, in the centre of the Ocean of Nectar, more than a thousand crores in extent, in the Gemisland (Ratnadvīpa), a hundred crores in area, the lamp of the world, there is the supreme city of Śrīvidyā, three lacs of Yojanas in height and adorned with twentyfive walls representing the twentyfive Tattvas.\" But in the Vidyāratna Bhāṣya the word Srimannagara (beautiful city) is explained as Śrīcakra. The Viśva, also says, Cakra (wheel) means city, house, hamlet, town and abode ; \" and the commentator on the Sūtra (13) of Gauḍapada interpreted the word Śrīpura to mean Śrīcakra. One may enter the city (nagara) without knowing the Ṛṣi; the city (pur) of the Devas is impregnable; the city is surrouned by nectar, etc.,\" in these and other Śrutis, city (Purornagara) means Cakra; (vide Tai.-Är., I) and Nagara and Cakra are one\nand the same.","dev":"सुमेरुमध्यशृङ्गस्था","iast":"sumerumadhyaśṛṅgasthā","pada":[{"dev":"सुमेरु","iast":"sumeru"},{"dev":"मध्य","iast":"madhya"},{"dev":"शृङ्ग","iast":"śṛṅga"},{"dev":"स्था","iast":"sthā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":77,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":21,"wbw":{"split":"सुमेरु-मध्य-शृङ्ग-स्था","splitIast":"sumeru-madhya-śṛṅga-sthā","words":[{"dev":"सुमेरु","gloss":"Mount Meru, the golden axial mountain (सु = excellent, golden + मेरु = Meru)","iast":"sumeru"},{"dev":"मध्य","gloss":"middle, central","iast":"madhya"},{"dev":"शृङ्ग","gloss":"peak, summit","iast":"śṛṅga"},{"dev":"स्था","gloss":"abiding, dwelling, seated","iast":"sthā"}],"meaning":"She who dwells on the central peak of Mount Meru.","vigraha":["सुमेरोः मध्यशृङ्गे स्थिता या सा।"],"note":"Of Meru's three summits, the middle one bears Her city."}},{"n":56,"gloss":"Dwelling on the middle peak of Mount Sumeru. (Sumeru madhya sringasthā.)","body":"After describing the important seat of Devī as on the lap of Kāmeśvara's left thigh (52 No.), the author now proceeds to describe some other seats also of Devī. middle peak is the fourth one. The great ascetic Durvāsa says in his Lalitāstavaratna (Sl. 2 to 4): 'Let the gold mountain be victorious, whose body is made up of the whole universe, filled by the music of the divine ladies living in the golden creeperbowers of mountain peak; we salute the three peaks of it, which are the seats of Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Śiva, expanding to the four quarters (of the globe). In the middle of them there is another peak, four hundred Yojanas in height, beautifying the place by the golden rays of flowers and I worship it.\"\n56. nayika.)\nRuler of the beautiful city. (Sriman nagara\nThe cities are of two kinds; one of them is on the middle peak of Mount Meru. The Lalitāstavaratna (Sl. 5) says, “I salute the city of ancient Vidyā four hundred Yojanas in circumference constructed by the celestial architect, beautified with many walls.” It is said in the Vidyaratnabashya, \"There is also another city in the midst\nof the Ocean of milk.\" With many walls means twentyfive in number. The second city is outside and beyond all the worlds, in the island of gems (Ratnadvīpa), in the middle of the Ocean of Nectar. The Rudrayāmala says, Outside and beyond the countless myriads of world systems, in the centre of the Ocean of Nectar, more than a thousand crores in extent, in the Gemisland (Ratnadvīpa), a hundred crores in area, the lamp of the world, there is the supreme city of Śrīvidyā, three lacs of Yojanas in height and adorned with twentyfive walls representing the twentyfive Tattvas.\" But in the Vidyāratna Bhāṣya the word Srimannagara (beautiful city) is explained as Śrīcakra. The Viśva, also says, Cakra (wheel) means city, house, hamlet, town and abode ; \" and the commentator on the Sūtra (13) of Gauḍapada interpreted the word Śrīpura to mean Śrīcakra. One may enter the city (nagara) without knowing the Ṛṣi; the city (pur) of the Devas is impregnable; the city is surrouned by nectar, etc.,\" in these and other Śrutis, city (Purornagara) means Cakra; (vide Tai.-Är., I) and Nagara and Cakra are one\nand the same.","dev":"श्रीमन्नगरनायिका","iast":"śrīmannagaranāyikā","pada":[{"dev":"श्रीमत्","iast":"śrīmat"},{"dev":"नगर","iast":"nagara"},{"dev":"नायिका","iast":"nāyikā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":77,"sure":0,"host":55,"v":21,"wbw":{"split":"श्रीमत्-नगर-नायिका","splitIast":"śrīmat-nagara-nāyikā","words":[{"dev":"श्रीमत्","gloss":"possessing श्री — splendour, auspiciousness, prosperity (श्री + मतुप्; nominative श्रीमान्, stem श्रीमत् in composition)","iast":"śrīmat"},{"dev":"नगर","gloss":"city — here Śrīnagara / Śrīpura, the city on Meru's middle peak, where the previous nama has just placed us","iast":"nagara"},{"dev":"नायिका","gloss":"mistress, sovereign lady, leader (feminine of नायक, from नी \"to lead\": नी + ण्वुल् → नायक)","iast":"nāyikā"}],"meaning":"The Sovereign Lady of the resplendent City.","vigraha":["श्रीमतः नगरस्य नायिका। — ṣaṣṭhī tatpuruṣa"],"note":"Sandhi: श्रीमत् + नगर → श्रीमन्नगर। The final त् before the nasal न् assimilates in nasality (Pāṇini 8.4.45, यरोऽनुनासिकेऽनुनासिको वा), giving न् + न → न्न। The visible न्न is thus a joint, not a doubled root consonant.\n\nTwo readings are current. Descriptive: श्रीमत् qualifies नगर — the city possesses श्री, and She rules it. Proper name: श्रीमन्नगर taken as the city's own name (Śrīmannagara = Śrīnagara / Śrīpura), hence \"the Lady of Śrīnagara\"; Bhāskararāya reads the compound this way.\n\nNamas 55–58 form a nested descent inward — Meru's middle peak (55) → the city upon it (56) → the cintāmaṇi palace within the city (57) → the pañcabrahma seat within the palace (58). This nama is the second ring. The root नी here also anticipates Her sovereign and commander namas in the Bhaṇḍāsura war section."}},{"n":57,"gloss":"Residing in a house (built of) Cintāmaṇi","body":"Cintāmaṇi is that jewel which yields all the objects desired; of this the city is built. This house is described in detail in the Prahmanda Pr. : \"In that house of Cintāmaṇi all is Cintāmaṇi.\" This house having an extent of a thousand Yojanas is above the world system. Rudrayāmala says, “In that Cintāmaṇi house of a thousand Yojanas in extent.\"\nThe house which is on Meru is smaller in size. The Lalitāstavaratna (Slo. 105) says, \"May that house, built of Cintāmaṇi stone which is on the northern side of Sringāravana (on Meru), whither all the Devas go to worship, remove all my mental trouble (cintā).” In the commentary on the Gauḍapāda Sūtra (No. 7) the Cintāmaṇi honse is explained as the place of origin of all those\nMantras which bestow all desired objects (chintita), and its construction is elaborately described.","dev":"चिन्तामणिगृहान्तस्था","iast":"cintāmaṇigṛhāntasthā","pada":[{"dev":"चिन्तामणि","iast":"cintāmaṇi"},{"dev":"गृह","iast":"gṛha"},{"dev":"अन्तः","iast":"antaḥ"},{"dev":"स्था","iast":"sthā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":78,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":21,"wbw":{"split":"चिन्तामणि-गृह-अन्तः-स्था","splitIast":"cintāmaṇi-gṛha-antaḥ-sthā","words":[{"dev":"चिन्ता","gloss":"thought, wish","iast":"cintā"},{"dev":"मणि","gloss":"gem → चिन्तामणि = the wish-fulfilling gem","iast":"maṇi"},{"dev":"गृह","gloss":"house, palace, mansion","iast":"gṛha"},{"dev":"अन्तः","gloss":"within, inside","iast":"antaḥ"},{"dev":"स्था","gloss":"abiding, seated","iast":"sthā"}],"meaning":"She who abides within the palace built of wish-fulfilling gems.","vigraha":["चिन्तामणिगृहस्य अन्तः स्थिता।"],"note":"The Cintāmaṇi-gṛha stands at the heart of Śrīnagara."}},{"n":58,"gloss":"Sitting upon a seat formed by five Brahmas","body":"The Bahurūpāṣṭaka and the Bhairavayāmala Tantras say, There is the supreme house of Devī, built of chintamani stone; the great bed itself is Śiva, the pillow the great Īśāna; on that beautiful couch, the mat is Sadāśiva; the four supports are Brahmā, Hari, Rudra and Īśāna; and the great Indra is the spittoon; on that bed reclines the great Īśānī, the supreme Tripurasundarī.” The proper places of Brahmā, etc., should be known from the Purāṇas.\n(22)","dev":"पञ्चब्रह्मासनस्थिता","iast":"pañcabrahmāsanasthitā","pada":[{"dev":"पञ्च","iast":"pañca"},{"dev":"ब्रह्म","iast":"brahma"},{"dev":"आसन","iast":"āsana"},{"dev":"स्थिता","iast":"sthitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":79,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":21,"wbw":{"split":"पञ्च-ब्रह्म-आसन-स्थिता","splitIast":"pañca-brahma-āsana-sthitā","words":[{"dev":"पञ्च","gloss":"five","iast":"pañca"},{"dev":"ब्रह्म","gloss":"the Brahmas — Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Rudra, Īśvara, Sadāśiva","iast":"brahma"},{"dev":"आसन","gloss":"seat, couch","iast":"āsana"},{"dev":"स्थिता","gloss":"seated, established, firmly abiding","iast":"sthitā"}],"meaning":"She who is seated on the couch formed of the five Brahmās.","vigraha":["पञ्चब्रह्ममयम् आसनम्, तस्मिन् स्थिता।"],"note":"Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Rudra and Īśvara form its four legs; Sadāśiva is the plank (फलक) on which She rests."}},{"n":59,"gloss":"Residing in the great forest of lotuses","body":"The Rudrayāmala speaking of the real form of the lotus forest, beyond (the world system), says, “Surrounded by a forest of lotuses, three lacs of Yojanas in extent.\" Another one is (on the Meru). The Lalitāstavaratna says (Sl. 106 to 108), Let us worship the forest of lotuses\nwhich is ..\"\nAgain the thousand petalled lotus, that is in the Brahmarandhra, is also called Padmatavi. The Svachchandatantra says, Above that there is the Kula lotus, thousand petalled, facing downward . . . This is known as the great lotus garden, above that is Samană.\" For there is one form both for the body and for the Universe, (Piṇḍāṇḍa and Brahmāṇḍa). This has been dealt with in detail in the commentary on the verse Åndibhava, etc., Aruṇa.-Up. (vide Tai.-Ar., I.).","dev":"महापद्माटवीसंस्था","iast":"mahāpadmāṭavīsaṃsthā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"पद्म","iast":"padma"},{"dev":"अटवी","iast":"aṭavī"},{"dev":"संस्था","iast":"saṃsthā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":79,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":22,"wbw":{"split":"महा-पद्म-अटवी-संस्था","splitIast":"mahā-padma-aṭavī-saṃsthā","words":[{"dev":"महा","gloss":"great, vast"},{"dev":"पद्म","gloss":"lotus"},{"dev":"अटवी","gloss":"forest, wilderness"},{"dev":"संस्था","gloss":"fully abiding (सम् intensifier + स्था)"}],"meaning":"She who abides in the vast forest of great lotuses.","note":"The compound splits at पद्म + अटवी (a → ā), so the surface महापद्माटवी hides a four-member sequence rather than three. And संस्था here carries the सम् prefix, a slight intensification over the plain स्था used in the previous verse's namas."}},{"n":60,"gloss":"Living in a grove of Kadamba trees. vanavasini)","body":"of\n(Kadamba-\nThe palace of Cintāmaṇi is surrounded by a gallery formed of gems (Manimantapa), around this is the grove of Kadamba trees. The Bhairavayāmala says, 66 The abode of bindu is the ocean of nectar, the five Yonis, the five\nS'akti angles in the (Śrīcakra) are the divine trees, there is the grove of Nipa trees, within that is the gallery of gems, within that again is the palace of Cintāmaṇi, and so on.\"\nIn the Purāṇas there is also a description which says, There are Kadamba trees seven Yojanas in height in the spaces between the walls of gold and silver.\"","dev":"कदम्बवनवासिनी","iast":"kadambavanavāsinī","pada":[{"dev":"कदम्ब","iast":"kadamba"},{"dev":"वन","iast":"vana"},{"dev":"वासिनी","iast":"vāsinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":79,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":22,"wbw":{"split":"कदम्ब-वन-वासिनी","splitIast":"kadamba-vana-vāsinī","words":[{"dev":"कदम्ब","gloss":"the kadamba tree"},{"dev":"वन","gloss":"grove, forest"},{"dev":"वासिनी","gloss":"she who dwells (from वस्)"}],"meaning":"She who dwells in the grove of kadamba trees."}},{"n":61,"gloss":"Residing in the centre of the ocean of nectar","body":"That ocean is the one above (in heaven). The Śruti (Tai.-Bra., 1-6-2) declares, The city is surrounded by nectar.\" Another one is in the place of bindu in the centre of the moon in the pericarp of the thousandpetalled lotus. The third one is, In the city called Aparăjita (unconquerable) to be attained with devotion by the Saguṇa Brahman ; there are two oceanlike lakes of nectar named respectively ara and nva\" (vide Chchan.-Up., VIII, 5, 4). These two are dealt with in the commentary on the Vedānta S'ūtra of Vyāsa. (IV, 4-22) 'No return according to Scriptures.\" All these oceans are here meant.","dev":"सुधासागरमध्यस्था","iast":"sudhāsāgaramadhyasthā","pada":[{"dev":"सुधा","iast":"sudhā"},{"dev":"सागर","iast":"sāgara"},{"dev":"मध्य","iast":"madhya"},{"dev":"स्था","iast":"sthā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":80,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":22,"wbw":{"split":"सुधा-सागर-मध्य-स्था","splitIast":"sudhā-sāgara-madhya-sthā","words":[{"dev":"सुधा","gloss":"nectar, ambrosia"},{"dev":"सागर","gloss":"ocean"},{"dev":"मध्य","gloss":"midst"},{"dev":"स्था","gloss":"abiding"}],"meaning":"She who abides in the midst of the ocean of nectar."}},{"n":62,"gloss":"Lovely eyed","body":"'\nOr, She whose eyes are Kāma (Śiva). This is the special name of the presiding deity of Kanchipura.","dev":"कामाक्षी","iast":"kāmākṣī","pada":[{"dev":"काम","iast":"kāma"},{"dev":"अक्षी","iast":"akṣī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":80,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":22,"wbw":{"split":"काम-अक्षी","splitIast":"kāma-akṣī","words":[{"dev":"काम","gloss":"desire, love; also Kāma"},{"dev":"अक्षी","gloss":"eye, eyes"}],"meaning":"She whose eyes are filled with love.","note":"Or, taking काम as the object given: \"whose glance grants what is desired.\""}},{"n":63,"gloss":"The fulfiller of desires","body":"Omniscient, she fulfils\nThe Brahmāṇḍa Pr. says, all desires through her aspect as the witness. Brahmā the grandsire of the world, seeing these doings of Devī, gave her the name Kamākshi and Kāmetvarı.”\nOr, she who bestows (Da) Kāmeśvara on her worshipper, in the sense of bestowing identity with Śiva. Or she who destroys (Dya, to kill), Kāma, manmatha (the god of love). Or, Kamada Śiva and ayini, endowed with good fortune; hence Śiva endows her with good fortune. Or, Kāma, Śiva and Daya, inheritance; she whose inheritance\n1 Bh. Nārā, Kā (Sarasvatī) and Mā (Lakṣmī) are her eyes\nis Sivathat ownership is hers, inseparably fixed from time immemorial.\nThe 10th verse on Paribhabha gives out as usual the division of the names from 64 to 127. (From 10th to 32 are not translated.)\nNow he begins a clear description, of the doings of the physical form of Devī, indicating her most secret supreme form by secret sayings.","dev":"कामदायिनी","iast":"kāmadāyinī","pada":[{"dev":"काम","iast":"kāma"},{"dev":"दायिनी","iast":"dāyinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":80,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":22,"wbw":{"split":"काम-दायिनी","splitIast":"kāma-dāyinī","words":[{"dev":"काम","gloss":"desire, the thing desired"},{"dev":"दायिनी","gloss":"bestower (from दा)"}],"meaning":"She who bestows all that is desired."}},{"n":64,"gloss":"Her power is praised by the assemblies of multitudes of Devas and Ṛṣis","body":"In\nVaibhava (power); this may mean the allpervading quality of Devī. Sanghāta (assemblies) separate assem blies in different ways; or Sanghata (lit. much destruction) is one of the names of hell—to be saved from this hell. Or, Sam, entirely: ghāta, slaying, i.e., Bhaṇḍāsura. the Brahmāṇḍa Pr. it is said by the Devas who were troubled by Bhandăsura, Ó Devī, mother of the Universe, victory to thee.\" being asked by her to choose a boon, the Devas say, \"If, After praising Devī in a hymn, and O beneficial one, thou art pleased, let us, who are troubled by the great Daityas, and whose life is (therefore) difficult (to live) take refuge in thee.\" In another place, the divine sage, Nārada approached the supreme Śakti, and bowing down with deep reverence said sura, O Devī, afflicts the three worlds; by you alone, and by no other deity, can he be conquered.\"\nOnce\nThis Bhaṇḍa-\nThe Gaṇa\nThe real meaning of the text is, as follows: Devas, Brahmā, etc., Ṛṣis, Vasiṣṭha, etc., and Devarṣis, Nārada, etc., Gaṇa, Āditya, etc. The Agni Pr. says, Devatās are, the Ādityas, Visvavasus, Tushitas, Bhisvaras, Anilas, Mahārājikasādhyas, and Rudras.\" According to the Rudrayāmala, her greatness is praised \"by millions of Dikpalas, suns, moons, and Vasus, etc.\"\nAgain, if we take to explaining the deeper meaning (Parā Rūpa) of this name, the result is described in Śaiva\nscriptures that Ātman alone is praised by Devas, etc. (Ṛṣis and Devarṣis). The Chaitanyawho enters into everything (Akhilānugata), wno is all experience (akhila parichita), and that who is that feeling which is the seat of all love (corresponding to sat, cit, ānanda). The Agni Pr. says, The first Sūtra (Śiva Sūtra, I, 1) says 'Ātman is his consciousness (caitanya) '; the second Sūtra (1, 2) says 'the (worldly) knowledge is bondage.' 77 Hence what is praised or experienced by the Devas and others is the Ātman ; for the Tantrarāja says, 66 The universal form Lalitā is declared to be the very Self \" ; as she is inseparable from the Self, her Vaibava is allpervading, possessed with infinite powers, etc.","dev":"देवर्षिगणसंघातस्तूयमानात्मवैभवा","iast":"devarṣigaṇasaṃghātastūyamānātmavaibhavā","pada":[{"dev":"देव","iast":"deva"},{"dev":"ऋषि","iast":"ṛṣi"},{"dev":"गण","iast":"gaṇa"},{"dev":"संघात","iast":"saṃghāta"},{"dev":"स्तूयमान","iast":"stūyamāna"},{"dev":"आत्म","iast":"ātma"},{"dev":"वैभवा","iast":"vaibhavā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":81,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":23,"wbw":{"split":"देव-ऋषि-गण-संघात-स्तूयमान-आत्म-वैभवा","splitIast":"deva-ṛṣi-gaṇa-saṃghāta-stūyamāna-ātma-vaibhavā","words":[{"dev":"देव","gloss":"gods, celestials"},{"dev":"ऋषि","gloss":"seers, sages (देव + ऋषि → देवर्षि, अ + ऋ → अर्)"},{"dev":"गण","gloss":"host, troop, band"},{"dev":"संघात","gloss":"assemblage, massed multitude (सम् + घात)"},{"dev":"स्तूयमान","gloss":"being praised, being hymned (passive participle of स्तु \"to praise\")"},{"dev":"आत्म","gloss":"own, her own self"},{"dev":"वैभव","gloss":"glory, majesty, greatness"}],"meaning":"She whose own glory is hymned by the massed hosts of gods and sages.","vigraha":["देवर्षिगणसंघातैः स्तूयमानम् आत्मवैभवं यस्याः सा।"]}},{"n":65,"gloss":"She is endowed with an army of Sakties for the sake of slaying Bhaṇḍāsura","body":"By name (No. 5) manifested for the sake of fulfilling the actions of the Devas \" the activity of her Sthala form was described; he now continues that description.\nThe fighting, etc., between Devī and Bhandăsura is fully described in the Lalitopākhyāna. Concerning the burning of Manmatha, it is said in the same book, “Gaṇeśa, the skilful worker, seeing the ashes of the god of Love, made from them a beautifully formed man Brahmā on seeing this action, said, “ well done, well done\" (Bhaṇḍa, Bhaṇḍa), hence, in the world he is called Bhaṇḍa.” fierce quality (asuratva) is explained in the same Pr., the powerful Bhaṇḍa sprang from the fire of Rudra's anger, his nature is terrible (Raudra), hence he is the terrible one (dânava).\n66 As\nOr, endowed with (Samavṛita), entirely identified with; Gauḍapāda Sūtra (No. 8) says: \"For the sake of destroying Bhaṇḍāsura she, though one became many.'\nOr, Bhaṇḍa, lit. shameless. Here it means the embodied soul endowed with life. For it is said, \"Though he is existence, consciousness and bliss, this embodied\nsoul (Bhandimā), is afflicted by ignorance, impermanancy,\npain, etc.\"\nAsura: Asu, life, ra to take away; he who takes away life. Life (asu) means the real nature, i.e., the imprisonment (Bandha) as expressed in a Sūtra (Si.-Sa., I, 2) which says, \"Knowledge is imprisonment.\" 'Knowledge' means, according to the second Satra (of the same) not recognising the reality in the Self, and recognising the reality in what is notself. This word Bhaṇḍa is also well known by the word Ånavamala. (Vide the Com. No. 354.)\nThe Śiva Sūtras (I, 5, 6 and 21) say, \"The effort (Udyama) is called Bhairava; when united with S'akti cakra, the involation of the universe comes. When Śakti is united, the creation of the body comes to exist. When the Bhūta is united, the separation of the Bhūtas, the composition, etc., of the universe come.\" The Vārtikas on the above Sūtras are as follow: 66 From the Self- Knowledge of vimarza aspect (of Devī), who is tended towards creation, comes out an effort quickly (Udyama) with complete egoism (Pūrnāhambhāva) agitated within, merging in the reflective self (Pratibhā). That Udyama is called by the derivative name Bhairava, because he is completely equipoised with all other S'aktis, he is allpervading, he is the consumer of all the armies of the different modifications (of the mind). Bhairava, as said above of Svasamvit, has a great Śakti That Udyoga, by name which is above the order or otherwise, standing entirely in its own citśakti, powerful to overcome the armies (dualities), in the void as well as shaped forms, United with Śakticakra means realisation as said before. When that process is performed, involution of the Universe from Kālāgni begins and Kalā remains in the state of the fire of Evasamvit. S'akti: His Śakti as described (in the Si.- Sa., I, 13) is Icchā Śakti, Umā; is united, is meditated upon the effect of the meditation gives power to create\nany body as he wishes, etc.\nBhūtas: Bhūtas are the body,\nBuddhi and Prāṇa, which are the graspers of the object, and immovable things which are to be grasped. United, nourished; separation, parting with them owing to disease, etc. All these and other things come to a Yogi when he realises Śakti.\"\nJust as virility is latent in boyhood and manifests itself in youth, the various powers (Śaktis) that are in the Jīva, remain latent by reason of ignorance. When an effort is made all these shine forth. This is the purport of the above quotation.\n(24)","dev":"भण्डासुरवधोद्युक्तशक्तिसेनासमन्विता","iast":"bhaṇḍāsuravadhodyuktaśaktisenāsamanvitā","pada":[{"dev":"भण्डासुर","iast":"bhaṇḍāsura"},{"dev":"वध","iast":"vadha"},{"dev":"उद्युक्त","iast":"udyukta"},{"dev":"शक्ति","iast":"śakti"},{"dev":"सेना","iast":"senā"},{"dev":"समन्विता","iast":"samanvitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":82,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":23,"wbw":{"split":"भण्डासुर-वध-उद्युक्त-शक्ति-सेना-समन्विता","splitIast":"bhaṇḍāsura-vadha-udyukta-śakti-senā-samanvitā","words":[{"dev":"भण्ड","gloss":"Bhaṇḍa, the demon's name"},{"dev":"असुर","gloss":"demon → भण्डासुर = the demon Bhaṇḍāsura"},{"dev":"वध","gloss":"slaying, killing"},{"dev":"उद्युक्त","gloss":"girded up, ready, intent upon (उत् + युक्त; वध + उद्युक्त → वधोद्युक्त)"},{"dev":"शक्ति","gloss":"the Śaktis, Her divine powers"},{"dev":"सेना","gloss":"army, host"},{"dev":"समन्विता","gloss":"accompanied by, attended by (सम् + अनु + इता)"}],"meaning":"She who is attended by the army of Śaktis girded for the slaying of Bhaṇḍāsura.","vigraha":["भण्डासुरस्य वधाय उद्युक्ताभिः शक्तिसेनाभिः समन्विता।"]}},{"n":66,"gloss":"Attended by herds of elephants conducted by Sampatkārī","body":"Sampatkart is the name of a certain deity about whom the Svatantratantra says, 'There is a certain Vidyā called Sampatkārī of unimaginable power .. thus it is threesyllabled and I will explain to you the method of using it.\" This goddess is described in the Lalitopākhyāna as the Mistress of the elephants belonging to Devī. The goddess named Sampatkārī sprang from the elephantgoad of the Supreme Lalitā... she rode upon the elephant named 'Ranakolahala' (delighting in war) ... Millions of the finest elephants followed her.\"\nSindhura, elephants. There are many kinds of elephants, i.e., Bhadra, Mandra, Mṛga, etc.\nOr, Sampatkārī means, the fulness of a joyous state of mind. In that emotional state she is attended by herds of elephants, i.e., aggregates of objects such as sound, etc. In the Kādimata we read, The objects of the senses are (called) elephants, so say the sages of old times. . .'\nThe mental modification, having the ability of judging distinctly the relations of the objects or the triad' appeared in one knowledge is known as Sukha Sampatkarī. The Pratyabhijna (II. Com.) says, \"To the ordinary person the connection between the receiver and the thing received The triad is the knower, the knowing and the thing known.\n9*\nseems natural, but the peculiarity of Yogins is that they carefully discriminate between the two.\" This is made clear by Varadarāja in his commentary on the Śiva Sūtras (I, 19), \" Worldly joy and ecstatic bliss \" (Lokanandah and Samādhi sukham). It is this: \"When a man realises that the objects of senses are his own mental ideas, his last (mental) state (Turīya) reappears ;\" and further adds, that this state (turīya) may again disappear and reappear. This state of Union called Sampatkarī is permanent only with great yogins.\"","dev":"संपत्करीसमारूढसिंधुरव्रजसेविता","iast":"saṃpatkarīsamārūḍhasiṃdhuravrajasevitā","pada":[{"dev":"सम्पत्करी","iast":"sampatkarī"},{"dev":"समारूढ","iast":"samārūḍha"},{"dev":"सिन्धुर","iast":"sindhura"},{"dev":"व्रज","iast":"vraja"},{"dev":"सेविता","iast":"sevitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":84,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":24,"wbw":{"split":"सम्पत्करी-समारूढ-सिन्धुर-व्रज-सेविता","splitIast":"sampatkarī-samārūḍha-sindhura-vraja-sevitā","words":[{"dev":"सम्पत्करी","gloss":"wealth, abundance (सम्पत्) + maker, fem. (करी) → Sampatkarī, commander of Her elephant division"},{"dev":"समारूढ","gloss":"mounted upon (सम् + आ + रूढ, from रुह्)"},{"dev":"सिन्धुर","gloss":"elephant"},{"dev":"व्रज","gloss":"herd, troop"},{"dev":"सेविता","gloss":"attended, served"}],"meaning":"She who is attended by the elephant host led by Sampatkarī, seated upon it."}},{"n":67,"gloss":"Surrounded by many crores of horses conducted by Aśvārūḍhā. (As'vārūdhādhishtitas'vak","body":"Surrounded by many crores of horses conducted by Aśvārūḍhā. (As'vārūdhādhishtitas'vakoti kotibhi rāvrită.) Asvärudha is a certain goddess wellknown in the Tantras; her Mantra is composed of thirteen syllables and she is the mistress of the horses of Devī. The Brahmāṇḍa Pr. says, As'värudha sprang from the noose of Lalitā, and rode in front with great speed a horse called aparājitā, and crores of swift horses followed her.\" In Tripurasiddhānta the explanation of the name is given. 'As she is the mistress of the horses she is called asvārudha.\"\nOr the senses are called horses. In a certain Sūtra of the Kādimata, we are told that the senses are meant by the word horses. Ārūḍha rider (of horses) the controller of the senses, i.e., mind. Because mental modifications arise through the senses. Thus one (yogi) enjoys pleasures governing countless senseimpressions by a single mind. If we take the word aridha as a plural form (riders) the meaning is that she (Devī) directs endless senseimpressions in many minds simultaneously. The meaning is that if a Yogi realises his divine self as said above and develops the power called Udyama, he becomes at his will master of everybody. So say the Śiva Sūtras (I, 12) Vismayo Yogabhumika\" (a wonder is the step of Yoga).\nThe commentary explains it thus: \"Just as amazement arises in excessive joy, so also to the Yogi, there is\nalways amazement in perceiving whatever is to be known. Vismaya is that wisdom, perfect contentment in the supreme Self, whose senses are merged in the enjoyment of unparalelled bliss. The wonder (Vismaya) of the Yoga which leads to the supreme reality, has degrees (Bhumika); this indicates that the supreme rest is to be attained gradually.\"\nA second Sūtra (I, 13) rans: 66 'Ichchâsaktih Umā Kumārī.\" (The energy of desire is Umā, the unmarried). The commentary : 'The eternal state is his whose mind has ascended the degrees of Yoga called Vismaya, and who realises by Yoga the supreme Bhairavata (an aspect of Śiva). To that Yogi the energy of desire comes by degrees, that energy is called Parābhattarika (Supreme Queen). She is known as the 'anmarried' (Kumārī). As play, she creates the Universe from Sadāśiva to the Earth (36 Tattvas); hence she is Kumārī. Or, she destroys (mārayate) the ground (Ku) of the great illusion, (hence she is Kumārī). Kumārī is enjoyer of everything. Kumārī is not to be enjoyed by the Yogi who is the Bhairava himself, who can enjoy everything (except Kumārī) as she is one with the enjoyer. Kumārī is Umā, she who has renounced all attachment and is chiefly devoted to the lord. Such is the Icchāśakti in Yogins; this is the easy path discovered by the great teachers (Anuttaradešikas).”\nA third Sūtra (I, 14) is \"Drisyam Sariram\". (The body is visible.) Com.: Thus to the Yogi who possesses the powerful energy of desire, whatever he sees becomes his body; seeing all as himself without separateness, through his likeness to Śiva; thus in the body externally and everywhere, whatever is visible is the body of the Yogi and his body is whatever is visible.\"\nThe fourth Sūtra (III, 14) says, \"Yatha tatra tathāanyatra (whatever is there is everywhere). Com. : \"Just as in the natural body selfdependence is manifest, so also the Yogin's selfdependence is manifested in the bodies\nof others. The selfdependence of the devoted Yogin is the fulness of individuality.\"\n(25)","dev":"अश्वारूढाधिष्ठिताश्वकोटिकोटिभिरावृता","iast":"aśvārūḍhādhiṣṭhitāśvakoṭikoṭibhirāvṛtā","pada":[{"dev":"अश्वारूढा","iast":"aśvārūḍhā"},{"dev":"अधिष्ठित","iast":"adhiṣṭhita"},{"dev":"अश्व","iast":"aśva"},{"dev":"कोटि","iast":"koṭi"},{"dev":"कोटिभिः","iast":"koṭibhiḥ"},{"dev":"आवृता","iast":"āvṛtā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":85,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":24,"wbw":{"split":"अश्वारूढा-अधिष्ठित-अश्व-कोटि-कोटिभिः-आवृता","splitIast":"aśvārūḍhā-adhiṣṭhita-aśva-koṭi-koṭibhiḥ-āvṛtā","words":[{"dev":"अश्वारूढा","gloss":"horse (अश्व) + mounted (आरूढा) → Aśvārūḍhā, commander of Her cavalry"},{"dev":"अधिष्ठित","gloss":"marshalled, presided over (अधि + स्थित)"},{"dev":"अश्व","gloss":"steed"},{"dev":"कोटि","gloss":"a crore → कोटिकोटिभिः = by crores upon crores (instrumental plural)"},{"dev":"आवृता","gloss":"surrounded, encompassed (आ + वृत्)"}],"meaning":"She who is encompassed by crores upon crores of steeds marshalled by Aśvārūḍhā.","note":"Nama 66 pairs with 65: the Śakti army named there is now itemised by division — elephants under Sampatkarī, cavalry under Aśvārūḍhā.\n\nText: the Devanagari reads कोटिकोटिभिरावृता (ट, not ठ). The सिंधुर / सिन्धुर difference is only anusvāra versus conjunct spelling; both are current, and the verse line keeps the anusvāra form."}},{"n":68,"gloss":"Seated in the chariot named Cakrarāja, she is armed with all the weapons","body":"Cakrarāja: several kinds of chariots are mentioned Kiriin the Lalitopäkhyāna, of which Cakrarāja is one. cakra and Geyacakra are examples of other sorts. following is a description of the Cakrarāja chariot :\nThe best of chariots, Cakrarāja, which belongs to the great queen, which consists of nine angles and which is ten Yojanas in height and four in circumference, moved on with its Ānanda flag. Gitichakra which is the next best, which has huge wheels and seven angies, was occupied by Mantrinitha; Kiricakra which is considered one of the best chariots, which too has seven angles, was also there. Listen, O wise one! to the names of its deities. Listening to their names would bring you victory. Where the chariot Chakrarija was, Geyacakra was also there, and where the Geya chariot stood, there the Kiricakra was also seen. These three chariots seemed like the three animated worlds.\"\nOr, Cakrarāja may also be taken to mean Śrīcakra. All the weapons: Devī possesses all the means of attaining the knowledge of Self. These means are explained in Śaiva Sūtras as Ānavopāya, Saktopāya, Sambhavopāya, etc. Yoga Śāstras also refer to these means of attaining the knowledge of Self. In all these by the Śrīcakra is meant that which is pointed to as the pathway to knowledge. The Yoga Sūtra has the following: If you concentrate on the inner apex of the nose what remains there for you in the right, the left and central paths?\" That is to sey, when the Siddhi is attained (when the Śrīcakra is reached) there is nothing more left for you to gain by Yoga practice. Śrīcakra is the highest point to which Yoga practices can lead.\n'All\nOr Chakrarājaratha may be taken as one word meaning the Siddhi called Chakresatva, or power of controlling the Cakras. Ārūḍha: capable of conferring; Sarvayudha all the rites. : The Bh.-Gītā says (IV, 33): rites, O Pārtha, are included in knowledge ; knowledge means Śuddhavidyā. The Śiva Sūtra (I, 22) says: “The dawn of Śuddhavidyā implies success in the mastery of the Cakras.” The commentary on this passage runs thus : 'When not desiring limited powers he is prompted by a broad view to embrace all things in himself, his knowledge develops and Śuddhavidyā dawns. It is then he attains mastery over all the Cakras. When the Yogi develops the power or capacity for embracing all things in himself and has touched the feet of Sadāśiva, he realises by the pure knowledge of Suddhavidyä which knows not the difference of Aham and Idam, 'I' and 'this,' that the Lord is both within and without him. Thus the eternal idea that I am the whole universe' is pure and stainless (Śuddha) knowledge (Vidyā). When this idea is developed and he is merged in it he, for ever, recovers his own Citśakti. When by complete absorption in Devī the Yogi does so, he attains supreme lordship over the Cakras.”","dev":"चक्रराजरथारूढसर्वायुधपरिष्कृता","iast":"cakrarājarathārūḍhasarvāyudhapariṣkṛtā","pada":[{"dev":"चक्रराज","iast":"cakrarāja"},{"dev":"रथ","iast":"ratha"},{"dev":"आरूढ","iast":"ārūḍha"},{"dev":"सर्व","iast":"sarva"},{"dev":"आयुध","iast":"āyudha"},{"dev":"परिष्कृता","iast":"pariṣkṛtā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":87,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":25,"wbw":{"split":"चक्रराज-रथ-आरूढ-सर्व-आयुध-परिष्कृता","splitIast":"cakrarāja-ratha-ārūḍha-sarva-āyudha-pariṣkṛtā","words":[{"dev":"चक्र","gloss":"wheel, disc; also the Śrīcakra"},{"dev":"राज","gloss":"king, chief → चक्रराज = Cakrarāja, the sovereign cakra, here the name of Her own nine-tiered chariot"},{"dev":"रथ","gloss":"chariot"},{"dev":"आरूढ","gloss":"mounted upon, having ascended (आ + रूढ, from रुह्)"},{"dev":"सर्व","gloss":"all, every"},{"dev":"आयुध","gloss":"weapon"},{"dev":"परिष्कृता","gloss":"fully equipped, furnished, decked out (परि + कृता, with ष् by the ruki rule)"}],"meaning":"She who, mounted upon the chariot Cakrarāja, is fully equipped with every weapon.","vigraha":["चक्रराजरथम् आरूढा सर्वायुधैः परिष्कृता।"]}},{"n":69,"gloss":"She is attended by mantriui who rides the chariot Geyacakra","body":"Mantriṇī is another name for Śyāmalā Devī, the duskycoloured deity.\nGeyacakra: Geya, to be praised; Cakra, wheel; Ratha, the disk of the sun. The sun's chariot is praised in the scriptures; (vide Tai.-Ar., Chap. I). Those who ride the Geyacakra are Yogins devoted to Śrīvidyā. They are called Mantrinis. Those who have reached Süryaloka, meditate on Devī to enable them to ascend to higher planes. Again; Geyacakra : Geya, important; Cakra, Śrīcakra; Ratha chariot; Àrūdha, contemplated upon.\nTripurasundarī whose chariot is that important Cakra, viz., Śrīcakra, is contemplated upon. The person that does so obtains the power of the Mantra, i.c., becomes a Mantriṇī. When he realises, he obtains the power of using the Mantra, which is described in Tantrarāja and which has to be learnt from a Guru. The Śiva Sūtra (I, 23) says: \"By concentrating upon Mahāhrada the power of the Mantra is obtained.\" (Com.) Mahāhrada, the supreme divine energy (Śakti); concentration (Anusandhāna), realisation of identity with Śakti; the power of the Mantra (Mantravīrya), the perception of the fulness of individuality; obtaining (Anubhava), full development of such perception.\" The above passage from Śiva Sūtra is explained at length by the holy Kṣemarāja in his commentary' wherein he states that the development of the Vidyabody is the secret of Mantras (II, 3).\n(26)","dev":"गेयचक्ररथारूढमन्त्रिणीपरिसेविता","iast":"geyacakrarathārūḍhamantriṇīparisevitā","pada":[{"dev":"गेयचक्र","iast":"geyacakra"},{"dev":"रथ","iast":"ratha"},{"dev":"आरूढ","iast":"ārūḍha"},{"dev":"मन्त्रिणी","iast":"mantriṇī"},{"dev":"परिसेविता","iast":"parisevitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":88,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":25,"wbw":{"split":"गेयचक्र-रथ-आरूढ-मन्त्रिणी-परिसेविता","splitIast":"geyacakra-ratha-ārūḍha-mantriṇī-parisevitā","words":[{"dev":"गेय","gloss":"that which is to be sung, song, music (from गै \"to sing\")"},{"dev":"चक्र","gloss":"wheel, cakra → गेयचक्र = Geyacakra, the \"chariot of song\""},{"dev":"रथ","gloss":"chariot"},{"dev":"आरूढ","gloss":"mounted upon"},{"dev":"मन्त्रिणी","gloss":"the Minister — Śyāmalā (Rāja-mātaṅgī), Her prime minister (feminine of मन्त्रिन्)"},{"dev":"परिसेविता","gloss":"closely attended upon, waited on all round (परि + सेविता)"}],"meaning":"She who is closely attended by the Minister Śyāmalā, mounted upon the Geyacakra chariot.","vigraha":["गेयचक्ररथम् आरूढया मन्त्रिण्या परिसेविता।"],"note":"These two open the chariot sequence — 68 is the Goddess's own Cakrarāja, 69 the Geyacakra of Her minister Śyāmalā, and 70 follows with the Kiricakra of Daṇḍanāthā (Vārāhī)."}},{"n":70,"gloss":"She is preceded by Daṇḍanāthā who rides her chariot Kiricakra","body":"Kiricakra, Kiri, a boar. cakra is of the form of a boar.\nThe chariot called Kiri-\nIt may also mean that it\nis drawn by boars. Daṇḍanāthā is the goddess Vārāhī. The Tripurasiddhānta says, She is called Dandanāthā because she always carries a rod (Danda) in her hand.\"\nKiricakra: Kiri, beams of the light (Kiranas) of Creation; Cakra, the cycle of creation, preservation and destruction. Even though the Yogi has mounted the chariot Kiricakra, i.e., even though he is entangled in the cycle of Creation, Preservation and Destruction, he is (Apuraskrita) beyond the scope, reach or touch of Daṇḍanāthā (Yama or Death); he is not subject to death or destruction. The Śiva Sūtra (III, 33) says: Though\nCatalogus Catalogorum mentions five commentaries on Śiva Sūtras :-One by an unknown author; another by Kṣemarāja, called Śiva Sätravimarasini; a third by Bhāskara, called Vārtika ; a fourth by Varadarāja which is frequently quoted in this commentary; and a fifth by one Rājānākarāma.\nsubject to the cyclic law, he is not excluded from the state of selfexperience.\" The holy Kṛṣṇadāsa comments upon this passage thus: Although the Yogi is involved in the incessantly revolving cycle of Creation, Preservation and Destruction, if, in virtue of his spiritual attainments, he steadfastly clings to the higher path (of Yoga), he does not forfeit the state of selfexperience, which leads to the highest or Turīya condition.\"","dev":"किरिचक्ररथारूढदण्डनाथापुरस्कृता","iast":"kiricakrarathārūḍhadaṇḍanāthāpuraskṛtā","pada":[{"dev":"किरिचक्र","iast":"kiricakra"},{"dev":"रथ","iast":"ratha"},{"dev":"आरूढ","iast":"ārūḍha"},{"dev":"दण्डनाथा","iast":"daṇḍanāthā"},{"dev":"पुरस्कृता","iast":"puraskṛtā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":89,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":26,"wbw":{"split":"किरिचक्र-रथ-आरूढ-दण्डनाथा-पुरस्कृता","splitIast":"kiricakra-ratha-ārūḍha-daṇḍanāthā-puraskṛtā","words":[{"dev":"किरि","gloss":"boar, hog → किरिचक्र = Kiricakra, the \"boar chariot\" of Vārāhī"},{"dev":"चक्र","gloss":"wheel, cakra"},{"dev":"रथ","gloss":"chariot"},{"dev":"आरूढ","gloss":"mounted upon (आ + रूढ, from रुह्)"},{"dev":"दण्ड","gloss":"staff, rod, chastisement"},{"dev":"नाथा","gloss":"mistress, lady → दण्डनाथा = Daṇḍanāthā (Vārāhī), commander-in-chief, wielder of the rod of punishment"},{"dev":"पुरस्कृता","gloss":"placed in front, led by, honoured (पुरः + कृता)"}],"meaning":"She who is preceded by Daṇḍanāthā, mounted upon the Kiricakra chariot.","vigraha":["किरिचक्ररथम् आरूढया दण्डनाथया पुरस्कृता।"]}},{"n":71,"gloss":"She resides in the centre of the fortress of fire constructed by Jvälämalini","body":"Jvālāmālinī is the Nityā deity of the fourteenth day of the lunar month. In the Brahmāṇḍa Pr. Devī addresses Jvālāmālinī thus: \"O Child! Fire is thy form, Fire is thy body. Do thou protect the great army. Do thou construct a fortress of Fire which shall cover the earth and extend over a hundred Yojanas in circumference, and thirty Yojanas in height.\"\nJvala, fire; Mālā, garland; Äkshipta, destroying ; Vahniprākāra, sparks of fire; Madhyaga, he who is seated in the midst.\nIt was said that though the Jnani or wise man, is subject to the cyclic law of creation, preservation and destruction, yet there is no break, in his experience of unity with self. This seeming inconsistency, is explained by this name.\nAs a seer, he is in the midst of a blaze of fire' which destroys the darkness of ignorance. And as a creator, he is in the midst of sparks (Vahniprākāra) which are thrown forth. He is like one who, standing between two combatants, is himself unaffected. Though by the actions of Creation, etc., modifications arise, yet he remains unchanged. The Spanda Śāstra (Kārikā 14) says : There are two states or conditions (Avasthas), action and energy. The one (ie., action) perishes, while the other is imperishable. 1 Jvālāmālā, lit., garland of fire.\nEffort directed towards action may be stopped; when that effort ceases, the ignorant man thinks, that he is himself asleep. But his inner self which is the seat of omniscience never sleeps, though others may, for want of perception in themselves, believe it to be asleep.\" This means that though action manifested as the universe in the form of sparks, flame, etc., perishes, the wise man who is in the fire of consciousness, who is the brightness of the fire and who is brightness itself, never perishes.\nBut supposing the energy of Śiva, does not cease to exist, although his actions perish, what becomes of the aspiring Yogi? This Śiva Sūtra (III, 26) says, He becomes one with Śiva.\" Hence there is no room for the above question. Another Sūtra (III, 31) says, \"The universe is an aggregate of his energies.\" The commentator explaining this Sūtra writes: \"His energies constitute the universe. He who possesses these energies is called Maheśvara. Āgamas state, everything is (due to) the aggregation of his energies.\" This applies to Śiva, to him who is the same as Śiva, and to him who is aspiring to become Śiva.1\nOr, Jvalamalinika, the five angles of Śakti; Kshipta, equally partaking of the essence; Valmiprākāra, the four angles of Śiva; Madhyagă, residing at the centre in the form of Bindu. Yoginīhṛdaya (I, 8) says: When (Devī) had created the five energies, and (Śiva) the four fires, the Cakra (Śrīcakra) came into existence by their union.\"(27)","dev":"ज्वालामालिनिकाक्षिप्तवह्निप्राकारमध्यगा","iast":"jvālāmālinikākṣiptavahniprākāramadhyagā","pada":[{"dev":"ज्वाला","iast":"jvālā"},{"dev":"मालिनिका","iast":"mālinikā"},{"dev":"आक्षिप्त","iast":"ākṣipta"},{"dev":"वह्नि","iast":"vahni"},{"dev":"प्राकार","iast":"prākāra"},{"dev":"मध्यगा","iast":"madhyagā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":90,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":26,"wbw":{"split":"ज्वाला-मालिनिका-आक्षिप्त-वह्नि-प्राकार-मध्यगा","splitIast":"jvālā-mālinikā-ākṣipta-vahni-prākāra-madhyagā","words":[{"dev":"ज्वाला","gloss":"flame, blaze"},{"dev":"मालिनिका","gloss":"the garlanded one (मालिनी + क) → ज्वालामालिनिका = Jvālāmālinī, the Śakti garlanded with flames"},{"dev":"आक्षिप्त","gloss":"flung up, cast out, projected (आ + क्षिप्त, from क्षिप् \"to throw\")"},{"dev":"वह्नि","gloss":"fire"},{"dev":"प्राकार","gloss":"rampart, enclosing wall, fortification"},{"dev":"मध्यगा","gloss":"middle, centre (मध्य) + abiding in, situated in (गा, from गम्) → she who dwells at the centre"}],"meaning":"She who abides at the centre of the rampart of fire flung up by Jvālāmālinī.","vigraha":["ज्वालामालिनिकया आक्षिप्तस्य वह्निप्राकारस्य मध्यगा।"],"note":"Nama 70 closes the chariot triad (Cakrarāja, Geyacakra, Kiricakra); 71 turns to the fiery rampart Jvālāmālinī threw up around the host in the war against Bhaṇḍāsura."}},{"n":72,"gloss":"She is delighted at the activity of the Śaktis that are prepared to destroy the army of Bhaṇḍa","body":"S'aktis, Nākulī and other goddesses.\nOr, Bhaṇḍa, the fettered soul; army, ideas of duality, etc., corresponding to the nature of a fettered soul; S'aktis are the energies or ideas of nonduality, that are prepared to destroy the former. Devī is delighted at the onset of these ideas of nonduality. For, when a man enjoys even 1 According to another reading, it applies also to the great Yogin'.\nbut a small portion of (true) bliss, the veil (of ignorance) is destroyed. The Śakti Sūtra' says: When a person does not recognise it, he gets bewildered by his own energies, and is involved in Saṃsāra.\" When a person does not recognise the existence of his own five powers of action, the energies, or more correctly, the three Yoga states called Khechari, Dikchari and Bhuchari take advantage of his weakness. These energies have two aspects, subjective (Pasubhumikā) and objective (Patibhūmikā) and accordingly manifest themselves, in man, both internally and externally. The commentary on the Sūtra, quoted above, explains this point well. These energies should be killed out, by other energies because they are hindrances to the progress of the aspirant. The next Sūtra says: When he recognises it and his thoughts converge inwardly to the seat of the mind, he becomes selfconscious.\" Thoughts here mean the modifications of the mental energy.","dev":"भण्डसैन्यवधोद्युक्तशक्तिविक्रमहर्षिता","iast":"bhaṇḍasainyavadhodyuktaśaktivikramaharṣitā","pada":[{"dev":"भण्ड","iast":"bhaṇḍa"},{"dev":"सैन्य","iast":"sainya"},{"dev":"वध","iast":"vadha"},{"dev":"उद्युक्त","iast":"udyukta"},{"dev":"शक्ति","iast":"śakti"},{"dev":"विक्रम","iast":"vikrama"},{"dev":"हर्षिता","iast":"harṣitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":91,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":27,"wbw":{"split":"भण्ड-सैन्य-वध-उद्युक्त-शक्ति-विक्रम-हर्षिता","splitIast":"bhaṇḍa-sainya-vadha-udyukta-śakti-vikrama-harṣitā","words":[{"dev":"भण्ड","gloss":"Bhaṇḍa, i.e. Bhaṇḍāsura"},{"dev":"सैन्य","gloss":"army, host, forces (from सेना)"},{"dev":"वध","gloss":"slaying, slaughter"},{"dev":"उद्युक्त","gloss":"girded up, ready, intent upon (उत् + युक्त; वध + उद्युक्त → वधोद्युक्त)"},{"dev":"शक्ति","gloss":"the Śaktis, Her divine powers"},{"dev":"विक्रम","gloss":"valour, prowess, heroic stride"},{"dev":"हर्षिता","gloss":"delighted, gladdened (from हृष् \"to rejoice\")"}],"meaning":"She who is delighted by the valour of the Śaktis girded for the slaughter of Bhaṇḍa's army.","vigraha":["भण्डसैन्यवधाय उद्युक्तानां शक्तीनां विक्रमेण हर्षिता।"]}},{"n":73,"gloss":"She rejoices at beholding the rising valour of the Nityās","body":"The Nityās are the fifteen Devatās, Kāmeśvarī to Chitra, who preside over the fifteen days of the lunar month. Their Mantras are given differently in the Jñānārṇava and in the Tantrarāja.\nThe rising valour of the Nityās: this is in reference to the prowess of the Nityās displayed in destroying fifteen of the leaders of armies, Damanaka, Citragupta, etc.\nOr, Nityās, the eternal energies of one's soul; Rising valour (Parakramatopa), continual progress. Once developed the germ of knowledge in the inner soul continues to increase. The Yogavāsiṣṭha says: \"All human arts perish by nonuse, but the germ of wisdom once risen (in the heart) is never destroyed, but continues to grow.\" (28)","dev":"नित्यापराक्रमाटोपनिरीक्षणसमुत्सुका","iast":"nityāparākramāṭopanirīkṣaṇasamutsukā","pada":[{"dev":"नित्या","iast":"nityā"},{"dev":"पराक्रम","iast":"parākrama"},{"dev":"आटोप","iast":"āṭopa"},{"dev":"निरीक्षण","iast":"nirīkṣaṇa"},{"dev":"समुत्सुका","iast":"samutsukā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":92,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":27,"wbw":{"split":"नित्या-पराक्रम-आटोप-निरीक्षण-समुत्सुका","splitIast":"nityā-parākrama-āṭopa-nirīkṣaṇa-samutsukā","words":[{"dev":"नित्या","gloss":"the Nityās, the fifteen eternal Śaktis of the lunar days"},{"dev":"पराक्रम","gloss":"might, valour, martial prowess (परा + क्रम)"},{"dev":"आटोप","gloss":"swelling, exuberance, imposing magnitude"},{"dev":"निरीक्षण","gloss":"beholding, looking on, witnessing (नि + ईक्षण)"},{"dev":"समुत्सुका","gloss":"keenly eager, full of longing (सम् + उत्सुका)"}],"meaning":"She who is eager to behold the swelling valour of the Nityā deities.","vigraha":["नित्यानां पराक्रमाटोपस्य निरीक्षणे समुत्सुका।"],"note":"Nama 72 answers 65 — there She is attended by the Śaktis girded to slay Bhaṇḍāsura himself, here gladdened by their prowess against his army. In 72–73 the Goddess is watcher rather than combatant: delighted, then eager to watch on."}},{"n":74,"gloss":"She rejoices in the valour of Bālā, (who was) ready to slay the sons of Bhaṇḍa","body":"These Sūtras are not yet printed.\n10*\nThe sons of Bhaṇḍa were thirty in number, viz., Chaturbahu, to Upamaya. Bālā a goddess nine years old. The Brahmāṇḍa Pr. says: On hearing them, i.e., the exploits of the arms of her daughter, Devī was delighted.\"","dev":"भण्डपुत्रवधोद्युक्तबालाविक्रमनन्दिता","iast":"bhaṇḍaputravadhodyuktabālāvikramananditā","pada":[{"dev":"भण्ड","iast":"bhaṇḍa"},{"dev":"पुत्र","iast":"putra"},{"dev":"वध","iast":"vadha"},{"dev":"उद्युक्त","iast":"udyukta"},{"dev":"बाला","iast":"bālā"},{"dev":"विक्रम","iast":"vikrama"},{"dev":"नन्दिता","iast":"nanditā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":92,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":28,"wbw":{"split":"भण्ड-पुत्र-वध-उद्युक्त-बाला-विक्रम-नन्दिता","splitIast":"bhaṇḍa-putra-vadha-udyukta-bālā-vikrama-nanditā","words":[{"dev":"भण्ड","gloss":"Bhaṇḍa, i.e. Bhaṇḍāsura"},{"dev":"पुत्र","gloss":"son → भण्डपुत्र = the sons of Bhaṇḍāsura (thirty, per the Lalitopākhyāna)"},{"dev":"वध","gloss":"slaying"},{"dev":"उद्युक्त","gloss":"girded up, intent upon (उत् + युक्त; वध + उद्युक्त → वधोद्युक्त)"},{"dev":"बाला","gloss":"Bālā, the young Goddess Bālā Tripurasundarī, Her nine-year-old daughter"},{"dev":"विक्रम","gloss":"valour, prowess"},{"dev":"नन्दिता","gloss":"gladdened, made joyful (from नन्द् \"to rejoice\")"}],"meaning":"She who is gladdened by the valour of Bālā, girded to slay the sons of Bhaṇḍāsura.","vigraha":["भण्डपुत्रवधाय उद्युक्तायाः बालायाः विक्रमेण नन्दिता।"]}},{"n":75,"gloss":"She is delighted at the destruction of Viṣaṅga effected by mother Mantriṇī","body":"Mantriṇī, Syamalimba,\nViṣaṅga and Viṣukra are\nthe two brothers of Bhaṇḍa. The Brahmāṇḍa Pr. says: 64 Once Bhaṇḍāsura, the head of the Asuras, desired to create different kinds of Asuras. From his right shoulder he created Viṣukra, who was able to protect the company of the Daityas and who was equal to Śukra (the Guru of Asuras in the matter of counselling), and from his left shoulder, he created Viṣaṅga.\"\n(29)","dev":"मन्त्रिण्यम्बाविरचितविषङ्गवधतोषिता","iast":"mantriṇyambāviracitaviṣaṅgavadhatoṣitā","pada":[{"dev":"मन्त्रिणी","iast":"mantriṇī"},{"dev":"अम्बा","iast":"ambā"},{"dev":"विरचित","iast":"viracita"},{"dev":"विषङ्ग","iast":"viṣaṅga"},{"dev":"वध","iast":"vadha"},{"dev":"तोषिता","iast":"toṣitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":93,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":28,"wbw":{"split":"मन्त्रिणी-अम्बा-विरचित-विषङ्ग-वध-तोषिता","splitIast":"mantriṇī-ambā-viracita-viṣaṅga-vadha-toṣitā","words":[{"dev":"मन्त्रिणी","gloss":"the Minister, Śyāmalā (Rāja-mātaṅgī)"},{"dev":"अम्बा","gloss":"mother, revered lady → मन्त्रिण्यम्बा = Mother Mantriṇī (मन्त्रिणी + अम्बा → मन्त्रिण्यम्बा, ई + अ → य् अ)"},{"dev":"विरचित","gloss":"wrought, accomplished, brought about (वि + रचित)"},{"dev":"विषङ्ग","gloss":"Viṣaṅga, Bhaṇḍāsura's brother and general"},{"dev":"वध","gloss":"slaying"},{"dev":"तोषिता","gloss":"pleased, gratified, satisfied (from तुष् \"to be content\")"}],"meaning":"She who is gratified by the slaying of Viṣaṅga wrought by Mother Mantriṇī.","vigraha":["मन्त्रिण्यम्बया विरचितेन विषङ्गवधेन तोषिता।"],"note":"Namas 74–75 continue the spectator sequence, now naming who kills whom — Bālā takes Bhaṇḍāsura's sons, Mantriṇī takes his brother Viṣaṅga, and 76 gives Vārāhī the other brother, Viśukra."}},{"n":76,"gloss":"Rejoicing in the strength of Vārāhī, the taker of the life of Vis'ukra","body":"Vārāhī, a goddess, by name Daṇḍinī. The Tripurā siddhānta explains the word Vārābi as follows: 66 Because Devī, the Supreme, appeared to Varāhānandanātha (one of the Gurus who was devoted to Devī), and because she has the face of a boar, she is called Vārāhī.”\nThe Sons of Bhaṇḍa (No. 74) are the stains called Ånava, and others mālā (impurities); Viṣaṅga (No. 75): Vi, opposed to, Sanga, attachments, or the craving for worldly objects. Or, Viṣa poison; ga, to go; i.c., perceiving sensuous objects with a poisoned view. Hence Kṣemarāja, commenting on the Śiva Sūtra (III, 30, Yovishasthojnahetuscha) beginning with, Yovishayastha, etc., explains that the word Avisha refers to Maheśvarī and other energies that are opposed to Viṣa, i.e., poison, evil. etc. Virukra: Vi opposed to; śukra, Tejas, or light, i.e., the embodied soul. Or, Vi more, Suk pain; ra receive ; i.e., Saṃsāra.\nBālā (No. 74), mantriṇī (No. 75), Vārāhī and (No. 76) represent certain internal modifications, when these destroy the opposed tendencies (indicated in the names of 74, 75 and 76) his own divine Self becomes pleased.\nIt is said in the Śakti Sūtra, \"When he ob.ains strength (Bālā) he makes the universe his own.\" The commentary on the above says, Bālā, (universal) consciousness; obtaining becoming merged in it. The Universe appears as identical with himself.\" The Śiva Sūtra (III, 42) says, When, by true knowledge of reaching that, by killing that, JIva is destroyed.' Com. That, the frequently entered abode which is the reality of knowledge (or according to another reading, the place of realisation); reaching, lead the true knowledge, i.e., the mind reaching forward to and devoted to the above abode. Killing that, the glazing the strong desire of the man who is stained by āṇavamala. Consequently the embodiment Jivatva also perishes.","dev":"विशुक्रप्राणहरणवाराहीवीर्यनन्दिता","iast":"viśukraprāṇaharaṇavārāhīvīryananditā","pada":[{"dev":"विशुक्र","iast":"viśukra"},{"dev":"प्राण","iast":"prāṇa"},{"dev":"हरण","iast":"haraṇa"},{"dev":"वाराही","iast":"vārāhī"},{"dev":"वीर्य","iast":"vīrya"},{"dev":"नन्दिता","iast":"nanditā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":93,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":29,"wbw":{"split":"विशुक्र-प्राण-हरण-वाराही-वीर्य-नन्दिता","splitIast":"viśukra-prāṇa-haraṇa-vārāhī-vīrya-nanditā","words":[{"dev":"विशुक्र","gloss":"Viśukra, Bhaṇḍāsura's brother"},{"dev":"प्राण","gloss":"life, vital breath"},{"dev":"हरण","gloss":"taking away, carrying off (from हृ \"to seize\")"},{"dev":"वाराही","gloss":"Vārāhī, i.e. Daṇḍanāthā, the boar-faced commander-in-chief"},{"dev":"वीर्य","gloss":"might, heroism, valour"},{"dev":"नन्दिता","gloss":"gladdened, made joyful (from नन्द् \"to rejoice\")"}],"meaning":"She who is gladdened by the might of Vārāhī in taking the life of Viśukra.","vigraha":["विशुक्रस्य प्राणहरणे वाराह्याः वीर्येण नन्दिता।"]}},{"n":77,"gloss":"Sri Gaṇeśvara was formed by her glances at Kāmeśvara","body":"Seeing the Devas fettered by magical figures set up by the Daityas, Devī, by merely looking at her husband, gave birth to the great Gaṇapati whose (mantra is) the twentyeight syllables, by which the fettering influence of the magical figures was destroyed and the Devas released. Thus says the Brah. Pr. : Then Devī Lalitā looking again at the face of her Lord, smiled, and from the rays of that smile a certain god arose, having the head of an elephant with ichor flowing from the temples.\"\nOr, it has just been said that the embodied soul was destroyed, but since it is eternal this is impossible. To remove this doubt this name, concerning the destruction of the soul (the vehicle of the soul, i.e., upadhi) is introduced. Kāmešvara, is the pure Śiva without attributes; Glances, personal knowledge (of him); Gaṇeśvara: Gaṇa the city formed of eight things (Puryaṣṭaka,) consisting\nof (1) Ave Karmendriyas, (2) five Inanendriyas, (3) four Manas, etc., (4) five Prānas, (5) five Bhūtas, (6) Kāma, (7) Karma, and (8) avidyā; and Īśvara the lord; Kalpita, attributed,\nThe word Jīva' means the man possessed of the idea of egotism and of the idea that he directs the Puryastaka. When these ideas are destroyed by his knowledge of the (true) lord of the Puryaṣṭaka, which knowledge is created by ascertaining the true nature of his own self, then as there are no qualities there is nothing to be qualified.\nThe Śiva sūtra (III, 42) says, 'When the wearer of the garment formed of the (five) elements is released, he again becomes supreme and equal to the Lord.\" Com. “By the extinction of attachment, as described above, the Jīva (his embodiment) is destroyed, by removing the idea of the direction of the Puryaṣṭaka and he is wholly released from the seat of egotism together with the elements which caused the origin of his body and no longer affect him, like one who wears a garment and attains Nirvāṇa (emancipation) hence he becomes equal to the supreme Lord. entered into the substance of reality, the essence of con- Having sciousness and bliss, and thus being entirely filled, he becomes that (reality) itself.\"\nThis is confirmed by a Śakti Sūtra which says: 'When a man attains consciousness and bliss (cit and ānanda), though body, life, etc., appear, he attains the firm union with the (Universal) consciousness and (hence) emancipation (Jivanmukti).\" 64 'This (Universal) consciousness and bliss are obtained by Another Śakti Sūtra says, developing the mean (Madhyavikāsa).” Madhyavikāsa means the great Ganera which is mentioned This (word) in (No. 78). The manner of developing the mean (Madhyavikāsa) is said in the succeeding Sūtra to be \"by the destruction of doubt\". These words, by the destruction\nThe meaning is that when the Jīva is absorbed in Brahman, there is no longer any Jíva remaining as such,\nof doubt, are indicated in the text by looking at the face of Kamervara. Delighted (harṣitā) which comes in the next name (No. 78) is indicated by the above Sūtra attainment of consciousness of bliss; for further explanation of the above, the reader is referred to the Partyabhijnähridaya.\n(30)","dev":"कामेश्वरमुखालोककल्पितश्रीगणेश्वरा","iast":"kāmeśvaramukhālokakalpitaśrīgaṇeśvarā","pada":[{"dev":"कामेश्वर","iast":"kāmeśvara"},{"dev":"मुख","iast":"mukha"},{"dev":"आलोक","iast":"āloka"},{"dev":"कल्पित","iast":"kalpita"},{"dev":"श्री","iast":"śrī"},{"dev":"गणेश्वरा","iast":"gaṇeśvarā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":94,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":29,"wbw":{"split":"कामेश्वर-मुख-आलोक-कल्पित-श्री-गणेश्वरा","splitIast":"kāmeśvara-mukha-āloka-kalpita-śrī-gaṇeśvarā","words":[{"dev":"कामेश्वर","gloss":"Kāmeśvara, Śiva as Her consort"},{"dev":"मुख","gloss":"face, countenance"},{"dev":"आलोक","gloss":"a look, glance, beholding (आ + लोक; मुख + आलोक → मुखालोक)"},{"dev":"कल्पित","gloss":"fashioned, brought into being, created"},{"dev":"श्री","gloss":"auspicious, glorious (honorific)"},{"dev":"गणेश्वर","gloss":"Lord of the gaṇas, Gaṇeśa → गणेश्वरा, feminine bahuvrīhi: \"she by whom he was made\""}],"meaning":"She who brought forth Śrī Gaṇeśvara by a glance at the face of Kāmeśvara.","vigraha":["कामेश्वरमुखालोकेन कल्पितः श्रीगणेश्वरः यया सा।"],"note":"Nama 76 closes the roll of slain commanders — Bhaṇḍāsura's sons to Bālā, Viṣaṅga to Mantriṇī, Viśukra to Vārāhī. 77 turns to Viśukra's Jayavighna yantra, which had struck the host with torpor: at Her glance upon Kāmeśvara's face Gaṇeśa arose and broke the obstruction."}},{"n":78,"gloss":"She is delighted at the great Gaṇeśa's breaking the obstacle formed of the magic figure","body":"The Magic Figure named Jayavighna (obstruction to victory) which had eight angles and eight tridents, was defended by eight devatās, viz., Alasa and others, was inscribed on a stone slab two Krosas (four miles) in extent and thrown by Viṣukra on the army of Devī. It was broken into pieces by Mahāgaṇeśa. This is described well in the Lalitopākhyāna.","dev":"महागणेशनिर्भिन्नविघ्नयन्त्रप्रहर्षिता","iast":"mahāgaṇeśanirbhinnavighnayantrapraharṣitā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"गणेश","iast":"gaṇeśa"},{"dev":"निर्भिन्न","iast":"nirbhinna"},{"dev":"विघ्न","iast":"vighna"},{"dev":"यन्त्र","iast":"yantra"},{"dev":"प्रहर्षिता","iast":"praharṣitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":96,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":30,"wbw":{"split":"महा-गणेश-निर्भिन्न-विघ्न-यन्त्र-प्रहर्षिता","splitIast":"mahā-gaṇeśa-nirbhinna-vighna-yantra-praharṣitā","words":[{"dev":"महा","gloss":"great"},{"dev":"गणेश","gloss":"Gaṇeśa → महागणेश = Mahāgaṇeśa, the great Gaṇeśa born of Her glance"},{"dev":"निर्भिन्न","gloss":"shattered, split apart, broken through (निः + भिन्न, from भिद् \"to cleave\")"},{"dev":"विघ्न","gloss":"obstacle, obstruction"},{"dev":"यन्त्र","gloss":"yantra, mystic device → विघ्नयन्त्र = the Jayavighna yantra of obstruction"},{"dev":"प्रहर्षिता","gloss":"greatly delighted, thrilled (प्र + हर्षिता, intensive of हृष्)"}],"meaning":"She who was thrilled when Mahāgaṇeśa shattered the yantra of obstruction.","vigraha":["महागणेशेन निर्भिन्नं विघ्नयन्त्रं, तेन प्रहर्षिता।"]}},{"n":79,"gloss":"She is showering missiles in response to the weapons thrown by Bhaṇḍāsura","body":"According to Dhanurveda, the difference between Śāstra and Astra is as follows : S'astra is that with which one strikes his enemies. It is always held in hand. Astra is that which is thrown out, or discharged by the hand, as the arrow of a bow.\nNames 78 and 79 are also explained as follows: Mahāgaṇeśa, is the mastery over Puryaṣṭaka or the city of eight things. It means the realisation of self. The magic figure of Viṣukra and the missiles of Bhaṇḍāsura, refer to the different modifications of nescience (Avidyā); and \"Devī showering missiles means the continuance of the (31)\nact of realising the supreme individuality.","dev":"भण्डासुरेन्द्रनिर्मुक्तशस्त्रप्रत्यस्त्रवर्षिणी","iast":"bhaṇḍāsurendranirmuktaśastrapratyastravarṣiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"भण्डासुर","iast":"bhaṇḍāsura"},{"dev":"इन्द्र","iast":"indra"},{"dev":"निर्मुक्त","iast":"nirmukta"},{"dev":"शस्त्र","iast":"śastra"},{"dev":"प्रत्यस्त्र","iast":"pratyastra"},{"dev":"वर्षिणी","iast":"varṣiṇī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":96,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":30,"wbw":{"split":"भण्डासुर-इन्द्र-निर्मुक्त-शस्त्र-प्रत्यस्त्र-वर्षिणी","splitIast":"bhaṇḍāsura-indra-nirmukta-śastra-pratyastra-varṣiṇī","words":[{"dev":"भण्डासुर","gloss":"the demon Bhaṇḍa"},{"dev":"इन्द्र","gloss":"chief, foremost, lord → भण्डासुरेन्द्र = Bhaṇḍāsura, lord of the asuras"},{"dev":"निर्मुक्त","gloss":"released, discharged, let fly (निः + मुक्त)"},{"dev":"शस्त्र","gloss":"weapon, hurled or hand-held arm"},{"dev":"प्रत्यस्त्र","gloss":"counter-missile, answering astra (प्रति + अस्त्र)"},{"dev":"वर्षिणी","gloss":"she who rains down, showers forth (from वृष् \"to rain\")"}],"meaning":"She who rains down counter-missiles against the weapons discharged by Bhaṇḍāsura, lord of demons.","vigraha":["भण्डासुरेन्द्रेण निर्मुक्तेषु शस्त्रेषु प्रत्यस्त्राणि वर्षतीति।"],"note":"शस्त्र and अस्त्र are not interchangeable — शस्त्र is the ordinary weapon, अस्त्र the missile impelled by mantra, so प्रत्यस्त्र is the mantra-answer to what he throws. 79 is also where the Goddess stops watching and enters the fight Herself."}},{"n":80,"gloss":"From the nails of her (ten) fingers sprang the ten forms of Nārāyaṇa","body":"Nails, joints of the nails, or finger tips. Ten forms (Dasa+Åkritih) of Nārāyaṇa are the ten incarnations of Viṣṇu, viz., Fish, Tortoise, Boar, Manlion, Dwarf,\nParaśurāma, Rāma, Balarama, Kṛṣṇa and Kalki. The Daityas sprung into existence from the missile called Sarvasurastra, which was aimed by Bhaṇḍāsura, were, Somaka, Ravana, Bali, Hiranyaksha and others who were known to have fought, several battles, had their origins thus. In order to destroy these Daityas, Devī created from her finger ends the ten incarnations of Viṣṇu (Matsya, Karma, etc.) in regular order from her right thumb to the left small finger. The following is from the Lalitop khyāna where it is well described: From the right thumb nail of the great Queen there sprang the divine, allpervading Nārāyaṇa in the form of a great fish. . . . The ten Avataras having performed their respective onerous tasks, stood with folded hands before Mother Lalitā saluting her.\"\nOr, Ten forms: the word in the text is Dasakritih, which may also be taken to mean the five states (Dasa) of Jīva and the five functions (Kritih) of Īśvara. The five states of Jīva are Waking, Dreaming, etc., and the five functions of Īśvara are Creation, Preservation,\netc.","dev":"कराङ्गुलिनखोत्पन्ननारायणदशाकृतिः","iast":"karāṅgulinakhotpannanārāyaṇadaśākṛtiḥ","pada":[{"dev":"कर","iast":"kara"},{"dev":"अङ्गुलि","iast":"aṅguli"},{"dev":"नख","iast":"nakha"},{"dev":"उत्पन्न","iast":"utpanna"},{"dev":"नारायण","iast":"nārāyaṇa"},{"dev":"दश","iast":"daśa"},{"dev":"आकृतिः","iast":"ākṛtiḥ"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":96,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":31,"wbw":{"split":"कर-अङ्गुलि-नख-उत्पन्न-नारायण-दश-आकृतिः","splitIast":"kara-aṅguli-nakha-utpanna-nārāyaṇa-daśa-ākṛtiḥ","words":[{"dev":"कर","gloss":"hand"},{"dev":"अङ्गुलि","gloss":"finger"},{"dev":"नख","gloss":"nail → कराङ्गुलिनख = Her fingernails"},{"dev":"उत्पन्न","gloss":"sprung forth, arisen (उत् + पन्न)"},{"dev":"नारायण","gloss":"Nārāyaṇa, Viṣṇu"},{"dev":"दश","gloss":"ten"},{"dev":"आकृतिः","gloss":"form → दशाकृतिः = the ten forms, the ten avatāras"}],"meaning":"She from whose fingernails sprang forth the ten forms of Nārāyaṇa.","note":"Text: the ङ्गु ligature in कराङ्गुलि had dropped its ङ् in the scanned Devanagari, a common loss.\n\nNama 80 ends in a visarga (दशाकृतिः, an -i stem nominative), unlike the -ā endings around it — worth keeping when reciting the pair."}},{"n":81,"gloss":"She burnt up the army of Daityas with the fire of the astra called Mahipasupata","body":"This Mahāpasupata Mantra is different from the sixsyllabled parupatastra mantra. The former belongs to Sadāśiva and the latter to Īśvara. The Liṅga Pr. says, From Rudra to Piśācas are called Paśus, as Śiva is the lord of these, he is called Paśupati (lord of Paśus).\" Hence this mantra belongs to Sadāśiva.\nOr, The fire of Mahaparupata weapons, the highest mental modifications connected with nonduality, which arise progressively according to the progressive practice (of the devotion).\nThe army of the Daityas, the mental modifications, connected with ignorance.\n(32)","dev":"महापाशुपतास्त्राग्निनिर्दग्धासुरसैनिका","iast":"mahāpāśupatāstrāgninirdagdhāsurasainikā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"पाशुपत","iast":"pāśupata"},{"dev":"अस्त्र","iast":"astra"},{"dev":"अग्नि","iast":"agni"},{"dev":"निर्दग्ध","iast":"nirdagdha"},{"dev":"असुर","iast":"asura"},{"dev":"सैनिका","iast":"sainikā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":97,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":31,"wbw":{"split":"महा-पाशुपत-अस्त्र-अग्नि-निर्दग्ध-असुर-सैनिका","splitIast":"mahā-pāśupata-astra-agni-nirdagdha-asura-sainikā","words":[{"dev":"महा","gloss":"great, supreme"},{"dev":"पाशुपत","gloss":"of Paśupati (Śiva) → महापाशुपतास्त्र, Śiva's supreme missile"},{"dev":"अस्त्र","gloss":"mantra-impelled missile"},{"dev":"अग्नि","gloss":"fire"},{"dev":"निर्दग्ध","gloss":"burnt utterly (निः + दग्ध, from दह्)"},{"dev":"असुर","gloss":"demon"},{"dev":"सैनिका","gloss":"soldier (सैनिक) → सैनिका, feminine to agree with Her"}],"meaning":"She by whom the demon soldiery was burnt to ash in the fire of the great Pāśupata missile.","note":"Nama 81 chains three a + a → ā joins in a row (पाशुपत + अस्त्र, अस्त्र + अग्नि, निर्दग्ध + असुर), which is why the surface form hides so many members."}},{"n":82,"gloss":"Bhaṇḍāsura with his army was burned up by the fire of the (weapon of) Kāmeśvara","body":"With his army.\nAccording to another reading, with\nhis town called S'ünyaka,\nThis weapon of Kāmeśvara is more powerful than the one mentioned in the preceding name.\nThe Brah. Pr. says, The supreme Mother, Lalitā blazing with anger, and throwing the whole universe into confusion, suddenly rendered lifeless, with the great weapon of Kāmeśa shining like the sun, the mighty Asura, the fierce and wicked Bhaṇḍa who was left alone, all his relations being slain. By flames discharged from that weapon his city called S'anyaka (emptiness) with the women, children, cattle, and wealth, was suddenly burnt up and its site alone remained; thus the city became empty and its name (S'ānyaka) was justified.”\nOr, having described up to this (name) the condition of equality with Śiva of him who has attained the last stage (Sayniya) of the emancipation of the divine Self, while in this present life, he now shows that Śiva alone remains (i.e., he becomes Śiva himself) after the destruction, by the knowledge of the Self, of the condition brought about by Prarabdha (the Karma which began its operation in the present body), with the subtle body, etc., which is then like a burned cloth, a mere appearance\nof duality.\nThe fire of Kāmeśvara, the fire of consciousness, because he is the universal object of desire. The Śruti also (Bra.-Up., II, 4-5) says, For the sake of the Self\neverything is dear.\"\nBhaṇḍāsura, the state of Jīva (individuality); the cause of the appearance of duality ; the army, the subtle body, etc.; or according to another reading Sūnyaka (emptiness) the appearance of duality as is seen in a burned cloth; or it applies to the emptiness, or unreality of the Atheistic School of duality.\nWhen that state of Saṃsāra (Jivabhäva) which appears to the devotee as void, is removed, himself remains, as consciousness alone.","dev":"कामेश्वरास्त्रनिर्दग्धसभण्डासुरशून्यका","iast":"kāmeśvarāstranirdagdhasabhaṇḍāsuraśūnyakā","pada":[{"dev":"कामेश्वर","iast":"kāmeśvara"},{"dev":"अस्त्र","iast":"astra"},{"dev":"निर्दग्ध","iast":"nirdagdha"},{"dev":"स","iast":"sa"},{"dev":"भण्डासुर","iast":"bhaṇḍāsura"},{"dev":"शून्यका","iast":"śūnyakā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":98,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":32,"wbw":{"split":"कामेश्वर-अस्त्र-निर्दग्ध-स-भण्डासुर-शून्यका","splitIast":"kāmeśvara-astra-nirdagdha-sa-bhaṇḍāsura-śūnyakā","words":[{"dev":"कामेश्वर","gloss":"Kāmeśvara, Her consort → कामेश्वरास्त्र = the Kāmeśvara missile"},{"dev":"अस्त्र","gloss":"missile, weapon impelled by mantra"},{"dev":"निर्दग्ध","gloss":"burnt down utterly, reduced to ash (निः + दग्ध, from दह्)"},{"dev":"स","gloss":"together with, along with (the prefix स in the sense of सह)"},{"dev":"भण्डासुर","gloss":"Bhaṇḍāsura → सभण्डासुर = together with Bhaṇḍāsura"},{"dev":"शून्यका","gloss":"Śūnyaka, Bhaṇḍāsura's capital city (शून्य = empty, void) → शून्यका, feminine bahuvrīhi agreeing with Her"}],"meaning":"She by whom Śūnyaka, along with Bhaṇḍāsura himself, was burnt to ash by the Kāmeśvara missile.","vigraha":["कामेश्वरास्त्रेण निर्दग्धं सभण्डासुरं शून्यकं यया सा।"]}},{"n":83,"gloss":"Her supreme power is praised by Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Mahendra and others (deities)","body":"The Brab. Pr. says, On that occasion all the devas headed by Brahmā and Viṣṇu, delighted at the destruction of Bhaṇḍa, came to wait upon her, etc.\"\nOr, Samstuta, (praised) realised in themselves; Vaibhava, infiniteness and Omnipresence of the self. (33)","dev":"ब्रह्मोपेन्द्रमहेन्द्रादिदेवसंस्तुतवैभवा","iast":"brahmopendramahendrādidevasaṃstutavaibhavā","pada":[{"dev":"ब्रह्म","iast":"brahma"},{"dev":"उपेन्द्र","iast":"upendra"},{"dev":"महेन्द्र","iast":"mahendra"},{"dev":"आदि","iast":"ādi"},{"dev":"देव","iast":"deva"},{"dev":"संस्तुत","iast":"saṃstuta"},{"dev":"वैभवा","iast":"vaibhavā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":99,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":32,"wbw":{"split":"ब्रह्म-उपेन्द्र-महेन्द्र-आदि-देव-संस्तुत-वैभवा","splitIast":"brahma-upendra-mahendra-ādi-deva-saṃstuta-vaibhavā","words":[{"dev":"ब्रह्म","gloss":"Brahmā (ब्रह्म in compound; ब्रह्म + उपेन्द्र → ब्रह्मोपेन्द्र)"},{"dev":"उपेन्द्र","gloss":"Upendra, Viṣṇu — \"the younger Indra\" (उप + इन्द्र)"},{"dev":"महेन्द्र","gloss":"Mahendra, the great Indra (महा + इन्द्र)"},{"dev":"आदि","gloss":"beginning with, and the rest"},{"dev":"देव","gloss":"gods, celestials"},{"dev":"संस्तुत","gloss":"fully extolled, praised in chorus (सम् + स्तुत, from स्तु)"},{"dev":"वैभवा","gloss":"glory, majesty, greatness (वैभव) → वैभवा, feminine bahuvrīhi"}],"meaning":"She whose glory is extolled by Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Indra and the other gods.","vigraha":["ब्रह्मोपेन्द्रमहेन्द्रादिभिः देवैः संस्तुतं वैभवं यस्याः सा।"],"note":"Nama 82 ends the war — the city Śūnyaka burns with its lord. 83 answers 64: there Her glory was hymned by the massed hosts of gods and sages, here by the chief gods named one by one, with the victory now behind it."}},{"n":84,"gloss":"She was the lifegiving medicine to Manmatha who was burnt up by the fire from the eye of Śiva","body":"The eye the third eye of Śiva.\nIn the Brahm. Pr. occurs this story of how Dev! who had enchained even her husband (Śiva) though he had renounced (the world) at the prayer of Brahmā, etc., restored Manmatha, to life. This agrees with the saying, Children rebuked by the father are consoled by the mother.\" Similarly the Brahmā Vaivarta Pr. says, If Hari becomes angry, the Guru is the protector, but if the Guru becomes angry there is no one to protect.\" But it may be said, since here Hari is the deity to be worshipped, Paramaśiva should be the saviour from the anger of Devī since he is the highest Guru, and without exception (because Śiva is called Ādinātha, the first Guru). This is wrong, because Paramaśiva also is one of the devotees of Devī and the devotee requires a Guru, hence Tripurasundarī must be his Guru; and in the Yoginīhṛdaya (III, 211) Śiva says to Pārvatī, 0 noble Lady, (this knowledge) should not be given indiscriminately to atheists. Thus by you, who art in the form of my Icchā, was I commanded.\" To every effort desire is cause, Devī is the wellknown cause of Śiva's activity and she is his Guru, hence one of her names is\n\"in the form of Guru\" (No. 603). The Chatussati Śāstra says, Without Śakti Śiva has neither name nor abode.\" According to the Tantras, Śiva became the first Guru (Ādinātha), because he possesses the Icchāśakti. Therefore, there is no need to ask whether Devī is included in the circle of the Gurus, or whether she is superior to Śiva, the first Guru. So the Mahāsvachandatantra says, \"Sadāśiva himself standing as both Guru and Pupil promulgated the Tantras in the form of question and answer.\" The word himself (Svayameva) means, by dividing his real and manifested forms (Prakāśa and Vimarśa) he became both Guru and Pupil. Suggesting and questioning were the part of Devī, and teaching and responding, that of Śiva. To express the above, Śiva speaks (in the Yoginīhṛdaya (III, 211) of Devī as in the form of his Icchā,\" and addresses her as 'Noble Lady \". Really speaking, (among the ranks of Gurus) next to Ādinātha (Śiva, the first Guru) is counted Ādiśakti, and Ādinātha is second to none. There is no authority for taking either of those two as the first, but Ådisakti united with Ādinātha, should be taken in the second rank, as she is the teacher of the third Guru, Sadāśiva by name (¿.e., among the ranks of Gurus, first is Ādinatha, second Ādiśakti, and third Sadāśiva).\nOr, Hara, taking the real nature of the Self; Neta, leader; fire, causing forms to change; that is to say the root of ignorance (malājnāna); burned by that, Kāma, the embodied soul whose real nature is Śiva; Sanjivana : Sam, entirely getting rid for ever of the veil (of māyā), and jivana, the attainment of his own real nature; medicine, i.e., Devī is the means to attain one's own real nature. The meaning is that Mokṣa is the attainment of one's own real nature, through the worship of that which is in the form of gidya and by getting rid of avidyā. Before burning, Manmatha was embodied, afterwards he was living without a body; this illustration shows that the\n11*\njīvas which were before Brahman, became embodied through the influence of nescience; when he has got rid of that influence, he has no longer any body and becomes one with Brahman. This (idea) is also contained in the last Śiva Sūtra (III, 46), “The union already described again occurs.\" Com. union (Milana), again realising one's own caitanya; this occurs to the Yogi who has reached the highest stage of Yoga. Again, the attainment of union with Śiva is not a new thing for the Yogi, because it is his own real nature; his own nature was hidden by the depravity, brought about by the many material images, raised by the power of illusion, and the nature of Śiva becomes manifest (in him) when the covering is dispersed by following the method taught by the Guru.\nsaid.\"\nThus Śiva\nThe last Śakti Sūtra also says, *The attainment of his own Divine Nature and the mastery over the cakras.\"","dev":"हरनेत्राग्निसंदग्धकामसंजीवनौषधिः","iast":"haranetrāgnisaṃdagdhakāmasaṃjīvanauṣadhiḥ","pada":[{"dev":"हर","iast":"hara"},{"dev":"नेत्र","iast":"netra"},{"dev":"अग्नि","iast":"agni"},{"dev":"संदग्ध","iast":"saṃdagdha"},{"dev":"काम","iast":"kāma"},{"dev":"संजीवन","iast":"saṃjīvana"},{"dev":"औषधिः","iast":"auṣadhiḥ"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":99,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":33,"wbw":{"split":"हर-नेत्र-अग्नि-संदग्ध-काम-संजीवन-औषधिः","splitIast":"hara-netra-agni-saṃdagdha-kāma-saṃjīvana-auṣadhiḥ","words":[{"dev":"हर","gloss":"Hara, Śiva, \"the remover\""},{"dev":"नेत्र","gloss":"eye — here the third eye"},{"dev":"अग्नि","gloss":"fire → नेत्राग्नि = the fire of that eye"},{"dev":"संदग्ध","gloss":"wholly burnt, consumed (सम् + दग्ध, from दह् \"to burn\")"},{"dev":"काम","gloss":"Kāma, Manmatha, the god of love"},{"dev":"संजीवन","gloss":"restoring to life, reviving (सम् + जीवन)"},{"dev":"औषधिः","gloss":"healing herb, medicament (संजीवन + औषधि → संजीवनौषधि, अ + औ → औ)"}],"meaning":"She who is the life-restoring herb for Kāma, burnt to ash by the fire of Hara's eye.","vigraha":["हरनेत्राग्निना संदग्धस्य कामस्य संजीवनौषधिः।"]}},{"n":85,"gloss":"Her lotus face represents the divine Vāgbhava group (a group of syllables in the pañcadaśī)","body":"Up to this the supreme form of Devī has been set forth by the actions of her physical form such as 'the slaying of Bhaṇḍāsura,' intended to convey a double meaning, because it is subtler than the subtle and therefore must be described in an esotric manner, and now her subtle form which is inseparable from her physical form is described.\nThis subtle form has three divisions, subtle, subtler and subtlest; the first representing the fifteen syllabled Mantra (Pañcadaś), the second Kāmakalā, and the third one Kundalini.\nThe first is described under three names :—\nŚrīmat (divine) having power of conferring wisdom and other exalted powers. Vāgbhava, that by which a person attains the power of speech. The Vagbhavakūta is the group of five syllables.\n(34)","dev":"श्रीमद्वाग्भवकूटैकस्वरूपमुखपङ्कजा","iast":"śrīmadvāgbhavakūṭaikasvarūpamukhapaṅkajā","pada":[{"dev":"श्रीमत्","iast":"śrīmat"},{"dev":"वाग्भव","iast":"vāgbhava"},{"dev":"कूट","iast":"kūṭa"},{"dev":"एक","iast":"eka"},{"dev":"स्वरूप","iast":"svarūpa"},{"dev":"मुख","iast":"mukha"},{"dev":"पङ्कजा","iast":"paṅkajā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":101,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":33,"wbw":{"split":"श्रीमत्-वाग्भव-कूट-एक-स्वरूप-मुख-पङ्कजा","splitIast":"śrīmat-vāgbhava-kūṭa-eka-svarūpa-mukha-paṅkajā","words":[{"dev":"श्रीमत्","gloss":"glorious, resplendent (श्रीमत् + वाग्भव → श्रीमद्वाग्भव, त् → द् before voiced व)"},{"dev":"वाग्भव","gloss":"the Vāgbhava kūṭa, first segment of the Pañcadaśī mantra (वाक् = speech + भव = origin, \"the source of speech\")"},{"dev":"कूट","gloss":"peak, segment, cluster — a kūṭa of the mantra"},{"dev":"एक","gloss":"one, sole, single (कूट + एक → कूटैक, अ + ए → ऐ)"},{"dev":"स्वरूप","gloss":"own form, essential nature"},{"dev":"मुख","gloss":"face"},{"dev":"पङ्कजा","gloss":"lotus (पङ्क = mud + ज = born) → मुखपङ्कजा = she whose face is a lotus"}],"meaning":"She whose lotus face is one in essence with the glorious Vāgbhava kūṭa.","vigraha":["श्रीमद्वाग्भवकूटस्य एकस्वरूपं मुखपङ्कजं यस्याः सा।"],"note":"Nama 84 pivots from war to grace — the Kāma burnt in the old myth is revived by Her. 85 opens the mantra-body sequence: the three kūṭas of the Pañcadaśī are mapped onto Her form, Vāgbhava as the face here, Kāmarāja from neck to waist in 86, and the Śakti kūṭa below the waist in 87."}},{"n":86,"gloss":"From the throat to the waist she represents the Madhyakūta","body":"Madhyakūta, Kamarāja kūta, a group of six syllables. It is said, As Kāma (desire to create the universe) resides in thy heart (thy mantra of the 2nd part) is called Kamarāja. O, Ambā, as thou art about to create (the universe), the next (3rd part), is called Saktikūta.”","dev":"कण्ठाधःकटिपर्यन्तमध्यकूटस्वरूपिणी","iast":"kaṇṭhādhaḥkaṭiparyantamadhyakūṭasvarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"कण्ठ","iast":"kaṇṭha"},{"dev":"अधः","iast":"adhaḥ"},{"dev":"कटि","iast":"kaṭi"},{"dev":"पर्यन्त","iast":"paryanta"},{"dev":"मध्य","iast":"madhya"},{"dev":"कूट","iast":"kūṭa"},{"dev":"स्वरूपिणी","iast":"svarūpiṇī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":102,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":34,"wbw":{"split":"कण्ठ-अधः-कटि-पर्यन्त-मध्य-कूट-स्वरूपिणी","splitIast":"kaṇṭha-adhaḥ-kaṭi-paryanta-madhya-kūṭa-svarūpiṇī","words":[{"dev":"कण्ठ","gloss":"throat, neck"},{"dev":"अधः","gloss":"below, beneath (कण्ठ + अधः → कण्ठाधः)"},{"dev":"कटि","gloss":"waist, hip"},{"dev":"पर्यन्त","gloss":"extending as far as, up to (परि + अन्त)"},{"dev":"मध्य","gloss":"middle"},{"dev":"कूट","gloss":"segment of the mantra → मध्यकूट = the middle kūṭa, i.e. the Kāmarāja kūṭa"},{"dev":"स्वरूपिणी","gloss":"she who has that as her very form (स्वरूप + इनी)"}],"meaning":"She whose form from below the throat down to the waist is the middle, Kāmarāja, kūṭa.","vigraha":["कण्ठात् अधः कटिपर्यन्तं मध्यकूटस्वरूपिणी।"]}},{"n":87,"gloss":"From the waist downwards she represents Saktikūta","body":"S'akti is so called because it is the Śakti of the wise. This is a group of four syllables.\n(35)","dev":"शक्तिकूटैकतापन्नकट्यधोभागधारिणी","iast":"śaktikūṭaikatāpannakaṭyadhobhāgadhāriṇī","pada":[{"dev":"शक्ति","iast":"śakti"},{"dev":"कूट","iast":"kūṭa"},{"dev":"एकता","iast":"ekatā"},{"dev":"आपन्न","iast":"āpanna"},{"dev":"कटि","iast":"kaṭi"},{"dev":"अधः","iast":"adhaḥ"},{"dev":"भाग","iast":"bhāga"},{"dev":"धारिणी","iast":"dhāriṇī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":102,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":34,"wbw":{"split":"शक्ति-कूट-एकता-आपन्न-कटि-अधः-भाग-धारिणी","splitIast":"śakti-kūṭa-ekatā-āpanna-kaṭi-adhaḥ-bhāga-dhāriṇī","words":[{"dev":"शक्ति","gloss":"Śakti, power → शक्तिकूट = the third segment of the Pañcadaśī"},{"dev":"कूट","gloss":"segment, peak"},{"dev":"एकता","gloss":"oneness, identity (एक + ता; कूट + एकता → कूटैकता, अ + ए → ऐ)"},{"dev":"आपन्न","gloss":"attained, come into, arrived at (आ + पन्न; एकता + आपन्न → एकतापन्न)"},{"dev":"कटि","gloss":"waist"},{"dev":"अधः","gloss":"below (कटि + अधः → कट्यधः, इ + अ → य्)"},{"dev":"भाग","gloss":"part, portion"},{"dev":"धारिणी","gloss":"she who bears, carries (from धृ \"to bear\")"}],"meaning":"She who bears the portion below the waist as having become one with the Śakti kūṭa.","vigraha":["शक्तिकूटेन एकताम् आपन्नं कट्यधोभागं धारयतीति।"],"note":"Namas 85–87 close the triad — Vāgbhava as the face, Kāmarāja from throat to waist, Śakti kūṭa below it. The three namas together say that the Pañcadaśī mantra and Her body are one thing, so that reciting the mantra is itself the worship of Her form."}},{"n":88,"gloss":"She is the root Mantra itself","body":"Mūla root, this is the fifteen syllabled mantra, Pañcadaśī, it is the root of the four objects of human desires (Puruṣārthas): Mantra, man repetition; tra protection -it protects those who repeat it. It is declared, Mantra is said to be that, with increasing repetition (Manana) of which, with full reflection of Iness,' destroys one's transmigratory life (Saṃsāra), and protects (tra) him.\"","dev":"मूलमन्त्रात्मिका","iast":"mūlamantrātmikā","pada":[{"dev":"मूल","iast":"mūla"},{"dev":"मन्त्र","iast":"mantra"},{"dev":"आत्मिका","iast":"ātmikā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":102,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":35,"wbw":{"split":"मूल-मन्त्र-आत्मिका","splitIast":"mūla-mantra-ātmikā","words":[{"dev":"मूल","gloss":"root, original, fundamental"},{"dev":"मन्त्र","gloss":"mantra → मूलमन्त्र = the root mantra, the Pañcadaśī (or Ṣoḍaśī)"},{"dev":"आत्मिका","gloss":"whose self or essence it is (from आत्मन्, feminine)"}],"meaning":"She whose very self is the root mantra.","vigraha":["मूलमन्त्रः आत्मा यस्याः सा।"]}},{"n":89,"gloss":"The three divisions of the root (Mantra) form her body","body":"The three divisions of the Mantra may be taken as forming either her physical or her subtle form.\nReally the word mūla (of the previous and this name) means the subtler body called Kāmakalā. 'The three divisions are the parts of Kāmakalā. This Kāmakalā with its three parts is to be learned from a Guru alone. The first part of the Kāmakalā is (called) Urdhva bindu (one bindu at the top), the next is two crossed bindus and the lowest is called hårdakalā, These three in order take the form of the three groups of the fifteensyllabled mantra and as well as the face and other parts (face, throat to waist and down the waist) of the physical form (of Devī). The Näthacharanagama explains this and the preceding name to\nindicate the subtler form of Devī, in her collective and individual aspects.","dev":"मूलकूटत्रयकलेबरा","iast":"mūlakūṭatrayakalebarā","pada":[{"dev":"मूल","iast":"mūla"},{"dev":"कूट","iast":"kūṭa"},{"dev":"त्रय","iast":"traya"},{"dev":"कलेबरा","iast":"kalebarā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":102,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":35,"wbw":{"split":"मूल-कूट-त्रय-कलेबरा","splitIast":"mūla-kūṭa-traya-kalebarā","words":[{"dev":"मूल","gloss":"root → मूलकूट = the kūṭas of the root mantra"},{"dev":"कूट","gloss":"segment, peak"},{"dev":"त्रय","gloss":"triad, the three"},{"dev":"कलेबरा","gloss":"body, bodily frame (कलेबर, variant of कलेवर) → कलेबरा, feminine"}],"meaning":"She whose body is the three kūṭas of the root mantra.","vigraha":["मूलमन्त्रस्य कूटत्रयं कलेबरं यस्याः सा।"]}},{"n":90,"gloss":"She has the special taste of the nectar of kula","body":"After describing the form of Devī, in Braḥmånda, he now proceeds to describe her subtlest form called Kundalini in the Piṇḍāṇḍa (in the body). That Kundalini in the Mūlādhāra, sleeping in three and half coils, roused by yogins, breaks through the six cakras as well as the three knots, called Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Rudragranthis, and proceeding to the Sahasrāra, from the moon's orb, which is the pericarp of the lotus, she causes the nectar to flow; even those who are not Yogins attain all these advantages by mere thinking (Bhāvanā).\nKula, genus. This has one common knowledge of the triad of the known, the knowing and the knowledge. When one says 'I know the pot,' there thought alone remains, and if that requires another knowledge it is as if one light requires another light (to illuminate it). So the triad is called Kula. The Chidghanachandrika says, measurer, the thing to be measured and the measuring.\" Kula is the There are thirtytwo lotuses (in the body) some facing upwards, and some downwards; these are described in the Svacchandasaṃgraha and other works. the lowest one is called akula because it has no connection Amongst these, with the triad (measurer, etc.); and the other lotuses which are above it are called Kulas. (In the Setubandha, the same commentator, enumerates twentytwo lotuses with their names and their proper places; but here thirtytwo lotuses. Vide Yoginīhṛdaya, I, 27.)\nOr, Ku, earth and la absorption, i.e., Mūlādhāra, because, in it the earth (Tattva) is absorbed. The road of Suṣumnā is called Kula, because it is connected with the Mūlādhāra.\nThe nectar flowing from the Sahasrāra is called Kulāmṛta; or according to the Svacchandasaṃgraha,\n66 'body is called Kula\". Kulāmṛta is so called because it is connected with the body.","dev":"कुलामृतैकरसिका","iast":"kulāmṛtaikarasikā","pada":[{"dev":"कुल","iast":"kula"},{"dev":"अमृत","iast":"amṛta"},{"dev":"एक","iast":"eka"},{"dev":"रसिका","iast":"rasikā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":103,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":35,"wbw":{"split":"कुल-अमृत-एक-रसिका","splitIast":"kula-amṛta-eka-rasikā","words":[{"dev":"कुल","gloss":"the Kula, the Kaula lineage and its inner meaning"},{"dev":"अमृत","gloss":"nectar, ambrosia (कुल + अमृत → कुलामृत)"},{"dev":"एक","gloss":"one, sole, alone (अमृत + एक → अमृतैक, अ + ए → ऐ)"},{"dev":"रसिका","gloss":"she who savours, relishes, has a taste for (from रस \"flavour, relish\")"}],"meaning":"She who delights in the nectar of Kula alone.","vigraha":["कुलामृतम् एकम् एव रसयतीति।"]}},{"n":91,"gloss":"She is the protector of the secrets of the family","body":"Kula, the scripture, because it explains and is equal to the thought of the worshipper and the object of worship. So the Kalpa Sūtra (Parasu, VI, 39) says, One should keep in secret the Kula scriptures.\" The Āgamas also say, 66 All the Śāstras enter the Kula\" (i.e., explain the object of the worship); or Kula the (right) conduct; for the Bhavi Utta. Pr. says, \"Kula means not family, but right conduct.'\nThe Protector: Devī never allows the revealing the secret of these two (scripture and right conduct) to the ignorant, and makes the thread' (instruction) unbroken b revealing it (the secret of the above) to those who follow the tradition. The secrets are threefold, viz., the secret of the cakra, of the mantra and of worship. (Yoginīhṛdaya, I, 5) says, O Great ruler, thus Devī Tripurā, has the threefold secrets.\" In the Chintamanistava also it is said, \"This family woman (Kundalini) entering the royal road (Suṣumnā) taking rest at intervals in the secret places (in the cakras), embraces the Supreme husband and makes the nectar to flow (in the Sahasrāra).\"","dev":"कुलसंकेतपालिनी","iast":"kulasaṃketapālinī","pada":[{"dev":"कुल","iast":"kula"},{"dev":"संकेत","iast":"saṃketa"},{"dev":"पालिनी","iast":"pālinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":104,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":35,"wbw":{"split":"कुल-संकेत-पालिनी","splitIast":"kula-saṃketa-pālinī","words":[{"dev":"कुल","gloss":"the Kaula tradition"},{"dev":"संकेत","gloss":"secret sign, esoteric convention, agreed code (सम् + केत)"},{"dev":"पालिनी","gloss":"protectress, guardian (from पाल् \"to protect\")"}],"meaning":"She who guards the secret conventions of the Kula tradition.","vigraha":["कुलसंकेतं पालयतीति कुलसंकेतपालिनी।"],"note":"Namas 88–89 carry the mantra-body identity of 85–87 to its conclusion — first the mantra as Her self, then its three kūṭas as Her frame. 90–91 then turn to the Kaula transmission that guards it.\n\nText: कलेबर and कलेवर are both attested; the ब / व interchange is normal, and LSN editions usually print कलेबरा."}},{"n":92,"gloss":"The family woman","body":"(36)\nKula the chaste family. She belongs to such a family. The meaning is that the respectable woman is well protected, so, she also is protected (kept in secret) by the veil of avidyā. The Kulārṇava says, Every other vidyā is as public as a courtesan but this Śāmbhavī vidyā is protected like a respectable woman.' The holy Paraśurāma also says (Kalpa Sūtras, I, 30) \"The other vidyās are like courtesans, quite public.\"\nThe word used is Sampradāya, i.e., one who belongs to a particular established doctrine, which has come by transmitting from teacher to student.\n2 This is attributed to Sri Sankarāchārya (Vide Burnell's cataloguc of Tanjore, No. 199 B.","dev":"कुलाङ्गना","iast":"kulāṅganā","pada":[{"dev":"कुल","iast":"kula"},{"dev":"अङ्गना","iast":"aṅganā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":104,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":36,"wbw":{"split":"कुल-अङ्गना","splitIast":"kula-aṅganā","words":[{"dev":"कुल","gloss":"family, noble lineage; in Śrīvidyā, the Kula — Śakti, the mūlādhāra"},{"dev":"अङ्गना","gloss":"woman, lady, one of graceful limbs (from अङ्ग \"limb\")"}],"meaning":"The Lady of the Kula — she who is the woman of that noble house."}},{"n":93,"gloss":"Residing in the Kula","body":"She is standing between the measurer and the thing measured; or Kula: Scripture, and she is in the midst of the scriptures, because she is the object of Kula knowledge.","dev":"कुलान्तस्था","iast":"kulāntasthā","pada":[{"dev":"कुल","iast":"kula"},{"dev":"अन्तः","iast":"antaḥ"},{"dev":"स्था","iast":"sthā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":105,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":36,"wbw":{"split":"कुल-अन्तः-स्था","splitIast":"kula-antaḥ-sthā","words":[{"dev":"कुल","gloss":"the Kula (also read as the mūlādhāra, or the assembly of tattvas)"},{"dev":"अन्तः","gloss":"within, inside"},{"dev":"स्था","gloss":"abiding, seated"}],"meaning":"She who abides within the Kula.","vigraha":["कुलस्य अन्तः स्थिता।"]}},{"n":94,"gloss":"Belonging to Kula","body":"Because she is worshipped in every house, in every place, in every body, and in every family; according to the Viśva, \"Kula means, country, house, a collection of things belonging to the same species, tribe, and body.\" The Bha. Utt. Pr. says, \"Devī is to be worshipped, in every place, town, house, village, and forest by men who are devoted to Śakti.\"\nAgain, the lower thousand petalled red lotus is also called Kula. The Svachchandatantra says, “In the pericarp of it, Kula Devī resides and in the petals Kulasaktis.\"\nOr, according to the Tantras Kula means Śakti, akula, Śiva, and union of Kula with Akula is called Kaula.\" Kaula means the essence common to both Śiva and Śakti, hence Devī is called Kaulinī,","dev":"कौलिनी","iast":"kaulinī","pada":[{"dev":"कौलिनी","iast":"kaulinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":105,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":36,"wbw":{"split":"कौलिनी","splitIast":"kaulinī","words":[{"dev":"कौल","gloss":"belonging to the Kula, of the Kaula path — vṛddhi derivative of कुल"},{"dev":"कौलिनी","gloss":"the presiding Goddess of Kaula worship; the Śakti in the mūlādhāra who joins kula (Śakti) to akula (Śiva)"}],"meaning":"She who is the Deity of the Kaula path."}},{"n":95,"gloss":"Kulayoginī. It is commonly said by some that Kaula means offering worship, etc., to a cakra (mentally) inscribed in ether. She is called Kulayoginī because she is connected","body":"with the cakra just mentioned.\nNos. 94 & 95 are named as one by the author of the Chchalakshara Sūtras. We will explain the reason of it in the 10th Hundred.","dev":"कुलयोगिनी","iast":"kulayoginī","pada":[{"dev":"कुल","iast":"kula"},{"dev":"योगिनी","iast":"yoginī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":105,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":36,"wbw":{"split":"कुल-योगिनी","splitIast":"kula-yoginī","words":[{"dev":"कुल","gloss":"the Kula"},{"dev":"योगिनी","gloss":"yoginī, female adept; she who is joined, who unites (from युज् \"to yoke\")"}],"meaning":"The Yoginī of the Kula."}},{"n":96,"gloss":"Having no Kula (Akulā)","body":"The thousand petalled lotus at the base of the Suṣumnā is called akula. The Svachchandasangrah says, Suṣumnā has a thousand petalled lotus, at the top and as well as at the bottom.. is a pair of shining lotuses, O ruler, the Kula and akula.” She is called akula because she is in the form of akula or her abode is in\nit.\nOr, because she has no family (Kula) body, etc., she is called akula.","dev":"अकुला","iast":"akulā","pada":[{"dev":"अ","iast":"a"},{"dev":"कुला","iast":"kulā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":105,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":36,"wbw":{"split":"अ-कुला","splitIast":"a-kulā","words":[{"dev":"अ","gloss":"not, beyond (negative prefix)"},{"dev":"कुला","gloss":"kula (कुल) → अकुल = that which is past kula; in Kaula usage Akula is Śiva, seated in the sahasrāra, as Kula is Śakti"}],"meaning":"She who is Akula — beyond the Kula, one with Śiva."}},{"n":97,"gloss":"Living in the Samaya","body":"Samaya is commonly (lit. traditional custom) explain- .ed as offering worship, etc., to a cakra in the ether of the heart. That (worship) is unanimously decided by all the yogins as the supreme, hence it is called Samaya. Samaya also means the five books of Vasiṣṭha, Śuka, Sanaka, Sanandana, and Sanatkumāra, because they describe this internal worship.\nOr, Samaya: Sama, equality ya, he who attains, i.e., both Śiva and Devī. The equality between Devī and Śiva is fivefold, viz., equality of place (i.e., cakra, rites, etc., are the same), functions (creation, etc.), actions (dancing, etc.), name (Śiva and Śiva, etc.), and form (red complexion, etc.). This is explained in detail by Lolla in the commentary on the Vāsanā Subhagodaya.'\nAnta (in) means according to the Viśva, corner, near, destruction, real nature, and beauty.\" She is the real nature of these two (heart and the five books).","dev":"समयान्तस्था","iast":"samayāntasthā","pada":[{"dev":"समय","iast":"samaya"},{"dev":"अन्तः","iast":"antaḥ"},{"dev":"स्था","iast":"sthā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":106,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":36,"wbw":{"split":"समय-अन्तः-स्था","splitIast":"samaya-antaḥ-sthā","words":[{"dev":"समय","gloss":"the Samaya doctrine and its worship; literally agreement, convention — also read as सम + अय, the state in which Śiva and Śakti are equal"},{"dev":"अन्तः","gloss":"within"},{"dev":"स्था","gloss":"abiding"}],"meaning":"She who abides within the Samaya path.","vigraha":["समयस्य अन्तः स्थिता।"]}},{"n":98,"gloss":"Devoted to the conduct of Samaya","body":"Samayācāra means the conduct described in ten chapters of the Rudrayāmala.\nOr, when the initiate, through the influence of the Guru's side glance, becomes steady by the practice of either the six or the four kinds of union, and has been purified by the mystical practice called 'Mahāvedha,' then on the ninth day (Mahānavamī of the lunar month) Devī, (Kundalini) awakens, leaving the Mūlādhāra, and appears to him in the Maṇipūra (cakra). In that cakra she is to be worshipped by the ceremonies beginning with radya (offering water for the feet) and ending with Bhashana (decking her with jewels); then having led her to Anāhata,\nI This, I suppose, is another name for Gauḍapāda's Subhagodaya (see also Anandalaharî Slo. 41).\nthe ceremonies should be continued to Tambula (giving betel); next having led her to Viśuddhi, she should be worshipped, surrounded by the rays of the Chandrakalas which are there; having led her to Åjnachakra, the Nirajana ceremony (waving lights before her, this indicates the end of the ceremony) should be performed. (Next and last) in the thousand petalled lotus in the centre of the Saragha, she should be united with Sadāśiva; then drawing a veil, he should remain in the next house waiting till she returns to and enters the Maladhārakunda. The method of effecting this must be learned from a Guru. This is what is meant here by Samayācāra.\nDevoted, She is devoted to these two (the conduct, as in the Rudrayāmala, and the Suṣumnā method).\n(37)","dev":"समयाचारतत्परा","iast":"samayācāratatparā","pada":[{"dev":"समय","iast":"samaya"},{"dev":"आचार","iast":"ācāra"},{"dev":"तत्परा","iast":"tatparā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":106,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":36,"wbw":{"split":"समय-आचार-तत्परा","splitIast":"samaya-ācāra-tatparā","words":[{"dev":"समय","gloss":"the Samaya doctrine (समय + आचार → समयाचार)"},{"dev":"आचार","gloss":"practice, observance, prescribed conduct"},{"dev":"तत्परा","gloss":"intent upon, holding that as highest (तत् + पर) → तत्परा, feminine"}],"meaning":"She who is devoted to the observances of the Samaya path.","note":"Namas 92–96 belong to the Kaula reckoning and 97–98 to the Samaya, the two being the outer and the inner worship — Samaya performed within the six cakras and held the higher by Bhāskararāya. The sequence honours both, and 96 अकुला is the hinge: having been named through Kula five times, She is then named as what lies past it.\n\nअन्तस्था in 93 and 97 is अन्तः + स्था with the visarga assimilated, as in 57 चिन्तामणिगृहान्तस्था."}},{"n":99,"gloss":"Chiefly residing in the Mūlādhāra","body":"There is a fourpetalled lotus called Maladhāra; in its pericarp there is a Madhyabindu, called Kulakuṇḍa ; in that Kundalini sleeps, always having her face covered. Mūla the root, adhāra, support, the Miladhāra is the root of Suṣumnā and the support of Kundalini.","dev":"मूलाधारैकनिलया","iast":"mūlādhāraikanilayā","pada":[{"dev":"मूल","iast":"mūla"},{"dev":"आधार","iast":"ādhāra"},{"dev":"एक","iast":"eka"},{"dev":"निलया","iast":"nilayā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":107,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":37,"wbw":{"split":"मूल-आधार-एक-निलया","splitIast":"mūla-ādhāra-eka-nilayā","words":[{"dev":"मूल","gloss":"root"},{"dev":"आधार","gloss":"support, base, foundation → मूलाधार = the root-support cakra at the base of the spine"},{"dev":"एक","gloss":"one, sole, alone (आधार + एक → आधारैक, अ + ए → ऐ)"},{"dev":"निलया","gloss":"abode, dwelling; she whose abode it is (नि + लय)"}],"meaning":"She whose sole abode is the mūlādhāra.","vigraha":["मूलाधारः एकः निलयः यस्याः सा।"]}},{"n":100,"gloss":"Severing the knot","body":"called Brahmagrandhi.\nOf the six cakras, each has two knots on both sides. The Svādhiṣṭhāna cakra has three including Brahmagranthi.\nThus ends the second Kală called Tāpinī, with the first hundred names in the work called Saubhāgyabhāskara of Bhäsurananda.","dev":"ब्रह्मग्रन्थिविभेदिनी","iast":"brahmagranthivibhedinī","pada":[{"dev":"ब्रह्म","iast":"brahma"},{"dev":"ग्रन्थि","iast":"granthi"},{"dev":"विभेदिनी","iast":"vibhedinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":107,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":37,"wbw":{"split":"ब्रह्म-ग्रन्थि-विभेदिनी","splitIast":"brahma-granthi-vibhedinī","words":[{"dev":"ब्रह्म","gloss":"Brahmā → ब्रह्मग्रन्थि = the Brahma-knot, first of the three obstructing knots, at the mūlādhāra"},{"dev":"ग्रन्थि","gloss":"knot, node, tie (from ग्रन्थ् \"to bind\")"},{"dev":"विभेदिनी","gloss":"she who splits open, pierces, sunders (वि + भेदिनी, from भिद् \"to cleave\")"}],"meaning":"She who pierces the Brahma-knot.","vigraha":["ब्रह्मग्रन्थिं विभिनत्तीति।"]}},{"n":101,"gloss":"Appearing in the maṇipūra. rudita.) (Manipuranta Maṇipūra cakra is in the navel and there is a tenpetalled lotus; in that while engaged in the act of Samaya worship (Vide under name 98)","body":"Devī is decked (pūra) with gems (maṇi). Hence this cakra is called Maṇipūra. Appearing, by removing the knot (Brahmagranthi) which is below this cakra.","dev":"मणिपूरान्तरुदिता","iast":"maṇipūrāntaruditā","pada":[{"dev":"मणिपूर","iast":"maṇipūra"},{"dev":"अन्तः","iast":"antaḥ"},{"dev":"उदिता","iast":"uditā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":107,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":37,"wbw":{"split":"मणिपूर-अन्तः-उदिता","splitIast":"maṇipūra-antaḥ-uditā","words":[{"dev":"मणि","gloss":"gem, jewel"},{"dev":"पूर","gloss":"filled, replete; also city → मणिपूर = the navel cakra, \"the one filled with gems\""},{"dev":"अन्तः","gloss":"within, inside (मणिपूर + अन्तः → मणिपूरान्तः; अन्तः + उदिता → अन्तरुदिता, visarga → र् before a vowel)"},{"dev":"उदिता","gloss":"risen, arisen, come forth (उत् + इता, from इ \"to go\")"}],"meaning":"She who rises up within the Maṇipūra cakra.","vigraha":["मणिपूरस्य अन्तः उदिता।"]}},{"n":102,"gloss":"Severing the knot called","body":"Viṣṇugranthi.\nAbove the Maṇipūra is the knot called Viṣṇugranthi ; Severing (Vibhedinī): Vicompletely and Bheda to break. As Viṣṇu resides in the Maṇipūra, this knot is called Viṣṇugranthi. Similarly, the Brahmā and Rudragranthis are so called, because Brahmā and Rudra reside in these centres respectively.\n(38)","dev":"विष्णुग्रन्थिविभेदिनी","iast":"viṣṇugranthivibhedinī","pada":[{"dev":"विष्णु","iast":"viṣṇu"},{"dev":"ग्रन्थि","iast":"granthi"},{"dev":"विभेदिनी","iast":"vibhedinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":108,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":37,"wbw":{"split":"विष्णु-ग्रन्थि-विभेदिनी","splitIast":"viṣṇu-granthi-vibhedinī","words":[{"dev":"विष्णु","gloss":"Viṣṇu → विष्णुग्रन्थि = the Viṣṇu-knot, second of the three, at the heart (anāhata)"},{"dev":"ग्रन्थि","gloss":"knot, node"},{"dev":"विभेदिनी","gloss":"she who pierces, splits open (वि + भिद्)"}],"meaning":"She who pierces the Viṣṇu-knot.","vigraha":["विष्णुग्रन्थिं विभिनत्तीति।"],"note":"From 99 the namas trace the kuṇḍalinī ascent, and they alternate — a cakra reached, then the knot above it broken. The three granthis are Brahma at the base, Viṣṇu at the heart, Rudra at the brow; 103–104 complete the pattern with आज्ञाचक्रान्तरालस्था and रुद्रग्रन्थिविभेदिनी before the sahasrāra is reached in 105."}},{"n":103,"gloss":"Residing in the centre of the Ajnāchakra","body":"Between the eyebrows there is a twopetalled lotus ; it is called ajnachakra, because in it the Guru, who instructs (ājnā) resides. Or, Å, a little; jnā, knowledge, i.e., knowledge arises first in him who mentally attains the conquest of that cakra. Therefore it is called Ājñā.","dev":"आज्ञाचक्रान्तरालस्था","iast":"ājñācakrāntarālasthā","pada":[{"dev":"आज्ञा","iast":"ājñā"},{"dev":"चक्र","iast":"cakra"},{"dev":"अन्तराल","iast":"antarāla"},{"dev":"स्था","iast":"sthā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":108,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":38,"wbw":{"split":"आज्ञा-चक्र-अन्तराल-स्था","splitIast":"ājñā-cakra-antarāla-sthā","words":[{"dev":"आज्ञा","gloss":"command → आज्ञाचक्र, the wheel of command at the brow"},{"dev":"चक्र","gloss":"wheel"},{"dev":"अन्तराल","gloss":"the interior, intervening space (अन्तर + आल)"},{"dev":"स्था","gloss":"abiding"}],"meaning":"She who abides in the interior of the Ājñā cakra."}},{"n":104,"gloss":"Severing the knot called Rudragranthi","body":"The two Rudragranthis are in the anabata centre of the heart. In practice the order is reverse.\nOr, there are four parts (Khandas) in the Srividyamantra called respectively, agni, sūrya, candra, and kāla, also called, Vāgbhava, kāmarā ja, Śakti and Turīya (Kütas) (Vide the names 85 to 90). Among these four there are three hrillekhas (hrims) these are called 'knots' and named respectively, Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Rudragrandhis. Severing means penetrating into.\nIn really speaking these six (cakras 94 to 104 names) signify the roots or origins (of the universe) as said in the fourth chapter of the Dattātreya Saṃhitā. Mūlādhāra and the other five cakras are together called Kula; there are three knots among them, these three are called Devī cakras. The earth and (water) cakras are indicated by\n12*\nthe Brahmagranthi. The next two powerful and shining cakras are fire and sun; these two are indicated by the Viṣṇugranthi; this shining one confers all the siddhis. The next two cakras in the form of air and ether are indicated by the Rudragranthi, the seat of mighty benefits.\"","dev":"रुद्रग्रन्थिविभेदिनी","iast":"rudragranthivibhedinī","pada":[{"dev":"रुद्र","iast":"rudra"},{"dev":"ग्रन्थि","iast":"granthi"},{"dev":"विभेदिनी","iast":"vibhedinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":108,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":38,"wbw":{"split":"रुद्र-ग्रन्थि-विभेदिनी","splitIast":"rudra-granthi-vibhedinī","words":[{"dev":"रुद्र","gloss":"Rudra → रुद्रग्रन्थि, the last knot, at the brow"},{"dev":"ग्रन्थि","gloss":"knot"},{"dev":"विभेदिनी","gloss":"she who pierces (वि + भिद्)"}],"meaning":"She who pierces the Rudra-knot."}},{"n":105,"gloss":"Having ascended the thousandpetalled lotus","body":"This is in the lower part of Brahmarandhra.","dev":"सहस्राराम्बुजारूढा","iast":"sahasrārāmbujārūḍhā","pada":[{"dev":"सहस्रार","iast":"sahasrāra"},{"dev":"अम्बुज","iast":"ambuja"},{"dev":"आरूढा","iast":"ārūḍhā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":109,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":38,"wbw":{"split":"सहस्रार-अम्बुज-आरूढा","splitIast":"sahasrāra-ambuja-ārūḍhā","words":[{"dev":"सहस्रार","gloss":"thousand (सहस्र) + spoke (अर) → सहस्रार, the thousand-petalled crown cakra"},{"dev":"अम्बुज","gloss":"lotus (अम्बु = water + ज = born)"},{"dev":"आरूढा","gloss":"having ascended (आ + रूढ, from रुह्)"}],"meaning":"She who has ascended the thousand-petalled lotus."}},{"n":106,"gloss":"Showering down torrents of ambrosia","body":"This ambrosia comes from the moon of the pericarp of the lotus. The Śruti (Tai. Bra., III, 12. 3) says, Let the feet (of Devī) which cause torrents of nectar to flow on all sides make us satisfied.\"","dev":"सुधासाराभिवर्षिणी","iast":"sudhāsārābhivarṣiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"सुधा","iast":"sudhā"},{"dev":"सार","iast":"sāra"},{"dev":"अभिवर्षिणी","iast":"abhivarṣiṇī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":109,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":38,"wbw":{"split":"सुधा-सार-अभिवर्षिणी","splitIast":"sudhā-sāra-abhivarṣiṇī","words":[{"dev":"सुधा","gloss":"nectar, ambrosia"},{"dev":"सार","gloss":"essence, choicest extract"},{"dev":"अभिवर्षिणी","gloss":"she who showers down abundantly (अभि + वृष्)"}],"meaning":"She who showers down the very essence of nectar.","note":"The ascent completes at 105 and then reverses direction: 106 pours the amṛta back down. The root वृष् recurs from nama 79 — counter-missiles rained on Bhaṇḍāsura there, nectar on the devotee here. Likewise आरूढा, last seen mounting war-chariots in 67–70, now mounts a lotus."}},{"n":107,"gloss":"Brilliant as the lightning flash. samaruchih.)","body":"(39) (Tatillekha-\nThe Śruti (Tai. Ar., X, 13-2) says, “Bright as the flash of Lightning.\"","dev":"तटिल्लतासमरुचिः","iast":"taṭillatāsamaruciḥ","pada":[{"dev":"तटित्","iast":"taṭit"},{"dev":"लता","iast":"latā"},{"dev":"सम","iast":"sama"},{"dev":"रुचिः","iast":"ruciḥ"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.769,"cpage":109,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":39,"wbw":{"split":"तटित्-लता-सम-रुचिः","splitIast":"taṭit-latā-sama-ruciḥ","words":[{"dev":"तटित्","gloss":"lightning, a flash of lightning"},{"dev":"लता","gloss":"creeper, vine — तटिल्लता, the \"lightning-creeper,\" the branching tendril of a flash"},{"dev":"सम","gloss":"equal to, like, resembling"},{"dev":"रुचिः","gloss":"lustre, radiance, brilliance (from रुच् \"to shine\")"}],"meaning":"She whose radiance is like a creeper of lightning.","vigraha":["तटिल्लतायाः समा रुचिः यस्याः सा। — bahuvrīhi"],"note":"Sandhi: तटित् + लता → तटिल्लता। The final त् becomes ल् before ल् (Pāṇini 8.4.60, तोर्लि), giving the doubled ल्ल.\n\nThe simile is exact rather than decorative — not lightning at large but a lightning-creeper, the slender branching filament of a flash, which is how the rising kuṇḍalinī is seen. लता carries the sense of a slender climbing plant, so the image is at once brilliance and thin ascending line; it pairs with 111 बिसतन्तुतनीयसी, where the same slenderness becomes a lotus fibre. Bhāskararāya cites Taittirīya Āraṇyaka X.13.2, \"bright as the flash of lightning\"; the variant reading तटिल्लेखा, \"a streak of lightning,\" is also recorded."}},{"n":108,"gloss":"Residing above the six cakras","body":"Samsthita (Residing): Sam, permanently, sthita, residing above the six cakras, viz., Mūlādhāra, Svadhishthāna, Maṇipūra, Anāhata, Viśuddhi and Ājnā.","dev":"षट्चक्रोपरि संस्थिता","iast":"ṣaṭcakropari saṃsthitā","pada":[{"dev":"षट्","iast":"ṣaṭ"},{"dev":"चक्र","iast":"cakra"},{"dev":"उपरि","iast":"upari"},{"dev":"संस्थिता","iast":"saṃsthitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":109,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":39,"wbw":{"split":"षट्-चक्र-उपरि-संस्थिता","splitIast":"ṣaṭ-cakra-upari-saṃsthitā","words":[{"dev":"षट्","gloss":"six"},{"dev":"चक्र","gloss":"wheel, disc — the six cakras of the subtle body"},{"dev":"उपरि","gloss":"above, over, beyond (an indeclinable, अव्यय)"},{"dev":"संस्थिता","gloss":"firmly and permanently established (सम् + स्थिता, from स्था; सम् giving the sense \"completely, abidingly\")"}],"meaning":"She who abides above the six cakras.","vigraha":["षट्चक्राणाम् उपरि संस्थिता।"],"note":"Sandhi: चक्र + उपरि → चक्रोपरि (अ + उ → ओ by guṇa, Pāṇini 6.1.87 आद्गुणः).\n\nThe six the commentary names are mūlādhāra, svādhiṣṭhāna, maṇipūra, anāhata, viśuddhi and ājñā; what lies above them is the sahasrāra, where 105 सहस्राराम्बुजारूढा has already seated Her. Where 99–104 climbed the cakras one at a time, this nama states the destination as a single fact.\n\nBecause उपरि is an indeclinable, editions commonly print षट्चक्रोपरि संस्थिता as two words even though it counts as one nama."}},{"n":109,"gloss":"Greatly attached to festivals","body":"Festival means the union of Śiva and Śakti (in the Sahasrāra),\nOr Maha, tejas; Åsakti, devoted to.\nThe Viśva says, “Maha means festival and tejas.” Or Mahat, greatness, Å, on all sides, Śakti,\nattachment.","dev":"महासक्तिः","iast":"mahāsaktiḥ","pada":[{"dev":"मह","iast":"maha"},{"dev":"आसक्तिः","iast":"āsaktiḥ"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":109,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":39,"wbw":{"split":"मह-आसक्तिः","splitIast":"maha-āsaktiḥ","words":[{"dev":"मह","gloss":"festival, celebration; also tejas, radiance — the Viśva lexicon gives both senses"},{"dev":"आसक्तिः","gloss":"attachment, devotion, being fixed upon (आ + सक्ति, from सञ्ज् \"to cling\")"}],"meaning":"She who is devoted to the festival — the festival being the union of Śiva and Śakti in the sahasrāra.","vigraha":["महे आसक्तिः यस्याः सा। — bahuvrīhi"],"note":"Sandhi: मह + आसक्तिः → महासक्तिः (अ + आ → आ, Pāṇini 6.1.101 अकः सवर्णे दीर्घः). The long आ is a joint, not a महा-.\n\nThree readings, all in Bhāskararāya. (1) मह = festival + आसक्ति = devoted to; the festival is Śiva's union with Śakti in the sahasrāra, so the nama names Her delight in the very consummation the preceding namas have been climbing towards. (2) मह = tejas + आसक्ति: devoted to radiance. (3) महत् + आ + सक्ति, read as greatness, on all sides, attachment.\n\nEditions also read महाशक्तिः, \"the Great Power,\" a straightforward महा + शक्तिः. The text followed here reads महासक्तिः, which requires the मह + आसक्ति split above — the two variants differ by a single sibilant and give quite different namas."}},{"n":110,"gloss":"Kundalini. Kundala means coiled hence Kundalini what is coiled. Its own form is like a coiled serpent. The Tantrarāja says, \"The shining","body":"vital energy (Jivasakti), which is the manifestation of life (Prāṇa), is called Kundalini, which resides in the centre of the flames of\nב.\nfire of Mūlādhāra. She is sleeping like a serpent, having three (and half) coils, radiant, she is ever hissing in the centre of suṣumnā where she resides, in the head of māyā. When a man after closing his ears does not hear this sound he is near to death.\" The Yogavāsiṣṭha in the story of Chudala says, Recognise Kundalini in your Self, which is the very life of the mind which is also called Puryaṣṭaka, like the scent in the flower.\" The Devī Pr. explains that, \"She is called Kundalini because she has the Sringataka form\" (triangle form, and the three angles are Icchā, jñāna and Kriyā, vide Yoginibridaya, I, 51 com.)\nOr, the Vagbhavabbīja is known as Kundalini.","dev":"कुण्डलिनी","iast":"kuṇḍalinī","pada":[{"dev":"कुण्डल","iast":"kuṇḍala"},{"dev":"इनी","iast":"inī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":109,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":39,"wbw":{"split":"कुण्डल-इनी","splitIast":"kuṇḍala-inī","words":[{"dev":"कुण्डल","gloss":"a coil, ring, circle (from कुण्ड, a bowl or hollow)"},{"dev":"इनी","gloss":"the possessive suffix इनि with the feminine ङीप् — \"she who has\" (Pāṇini 5.2.115, अत इनिठनौ)"}],"meaning":"The Coiled One — she who is possessed of coils.","vigraha":["कुण्डलम् अस्ति अस्याः इति कुण्डलिनी। — matvarthīya"],"note":"कुण्डल + इनि gives the stem कुण्डलिन्, feminine कुण्डलिनी. The split falls at कुण्डल, not कुण्ड — the ल belongs to the stem, not to the suffix.\n\nBhāskararāya's Tantrarāja citation gives the reason for the name: the vital energy (jīvaśakti) sleeps in the mūlādhāra \"like a serpent having three and a half coils,\" radiant, hissing in the centre of the suṣumnā — and a man who cannot hear that sound on stopping his ears is near death. The Devī Purāṇa offers a second derivation from the śṛṅgāṭaka or triangular form, its three angles being icchā, jñāna and kriyā. A third reading identifies कुण्डलिनी with the vāgbhava-bīja."}},{"n":111,"gloss":"Fine as the fibre of the lotus stock","body":"The Śruti (Tai. Ar., X, 13. 2) says, \"Fine as the point of an ear of rice, saffron coloured, radiant, and like an atom.\"\nThe names from 90th downwards to this name form one group.\nThe Śakti called beautiful, fine as Biting the pericarp pericarp of a lotus,\nThe Vāmakeśvara tantra says, Kundalini in the form of a serpent, lotus fibre, resides in the mülädhära. of the Mūlādhāra which is like the with its tail in its mouth and connected with the Brahmarandhra. Seated comfortably in the lotus posture (Padmasana) contracting the anus, his mind fixed upon Kumbhaka, the aspirant should force the breath upwards. By the compression of the breath, the fire in the Svadhisthana blazes up. By the force of this blaze and by the force of the breath, the serpent king (kundalini) awakes and breaks through the knots called Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Rudragranthis, as well as the six lotuses; this energy unites with Śiva in the thousandpetalled lotus. This should be known as the supreme state (parā) and it is the cause of final beatitude.\"\nThe Aruṇa, Up. (Tai. Ar., I, 27) also says, Arise; sleep not; ye who rejoice in the light, desire the fire; be satisfied (by the nectar) of the king who is with Umā, with the help of the hot sun.\" Thus the Upaniṣad exclusively refers to Devī. Hence we find in the Śyāmalā Sahasranāma among the thousand names of Syāmalā, one name is particularised as praised by the Arunopanishad.\" This is a dialogue between the devotees called Pris'nis. Who rejoice, etc., Bhārata: those who are devoted to Sarasvatīvidyā, i.e., the devotees of Śrīvidyā; arise: persevere in your devotion. Sleep not: do not be careless; or arise: raise (the Kundalini); sleep not, do not allow her to sleep. Fire, the fire in the Svādhiṣṭhāna, convert this in the form of Kundalini; desire, raise her by the force of your wish. With the help of the hot sun, the sun which is between the Anāhata, and Viśuddhi cakras accompanied by the fire. Of the king who is with Umā, the king of kings (Śiva) with his Queen, who is residing in the lunar orb belonging to Sahasrāra. The meaning is, having raised the Kundalini fire and united with the Kundalini sun and leading these two into the lunar orb and uniting them through the common essence of Śiva and Śakti; then by the streams of ambrosia flowing from this (Union) filling the seventytwo thousand channels he becomes satisfied thereby.\nThus (another Śruti says) (Tai. Ar., I), \"Whatever the hissing sound the girl makes, whatever the woman (does), and whatever good things, the woman after marriage does fire helps.\" Girl (Kumārī): Ku, earth, mri, absorption, i.e., Kulakuṇḍa (Mūlādhāra). Kundalini is so called because she belongs to Mūlādhāra. Kumārī is meant here as a newly born baby, because Kundalini, is described here with the sound of (hissing) crying when she has just risen from the Mūlādhāra. Woman marriageable, by the ripening influence of the sun above anāhata, she attains maturity. The\nwoman after marriage (Pativrată lit. the selection of a husband); Pati, husband, Kāmeśvara, who is in the Brahmarandhra. Whatever she does, in these three stages (of girlhood, etc.) Good things, the flowing of ambrosia, etc.; Fire in the Svādhiṣṭhāna: it helps because the lunar orb becomes liquified by that fire and thus causes the flow (of nectar). The Sanatkumāratantra supports the above assertion. The ascetics called Pris'nis devoted to Śrīvidyā having assembled instruct one another; arise, O Bhāratas, do not delay, desire the fire in the pericarp of the Svādhiṣṭhāna; with the help of the sun, having liquified (the moon's disc in Sahasrāra) be contented (by the flow of nectar caused by the union) of the king (Śiva) who is with Umā... in the marriage of the female serpent (kundalini), fire alone was the companion.\"\nThus another Śruti (Tai. Br., III, 12, 3) also says, \"May the pure feet which are blazing, illuminating, bright, glorious, and the means of entering the world (of Brahman) make us satisfied.\" Blazing, illuminating, etc., indicate respectively, fire, sun, moon, and Kalā. (feet) moving to and fro; hence Kundalini which moves Charana (between Mūlādhāra and Sahasrāra). The meaning is, let the Kundalini satisfy us by the flow of nectar entering the seats of fire, etc. This is further explained in the Sukasamhita: May Kundalini, whose movements are secret and who by the blaze of the fire, by the illumination of the sun, and by the brightness of the moon, causes the ambrosia to flow through the seventytwo thousand channels, make us contented.\" Thus also explained we find the same in the Vasiṣṭha and other Tantras. (40)","dev":"बिसतन्तुतनीयसी","iast":"bisatantutanīyasī","pada":[{"dev":"बिस","iast":"bisa"},{"dev":"तन्तु","iast":"tantu"},{"dev":"तनीयसी","iast":"tanīyasī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":110,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":39,"wbw":{"split":"बिस-तन्तु-तनीयसी","splitIast":"bisa-tantu-tanīyasī","words":[{"dev":"बिस","gloss":"the lotus stalk, the fibrous stem of the lotus"},{"dev":"तन्तु","gloss":"thread, fibre, filament — बिसतन्तु, the thread drawn from a lotus stalk"},{"dev":"तनीयसी","gloss":"the slenderer, the very finest (तनु \"thin\" + the comparative suffix ईयसुन् → तनीयस्, feminine तनीयसी)"}],"meaning":"She who is finer than the fibre of a lotus stalk.","vigraha":["बिसतन्तोः अपि तनीयसी। — the comparative governs the ablative"],"note":"तनीयसी is a degree-form, so the compound is not \"thin as a lotus fibre\" but \"thinner even than\" one, with the ablative बिसतन्तोः understood. The Śruti Bhāskararāya cites (Taittirīya Āraṇyaka X.13.2) gives the same order of smallness: \"fine as the point of an ear of rice, saffron-coloured, radiant, and like an atom.\"\n\nRead with 107, the verse brackets the kuṇḍalinī between two similes of the fine bright thread — the lightning-creeper and the lotus fibre. The Vāmakeśvara Tantra quoted here makes the figure literal: she lies in the mūlādhāra in the form of a serpent, lotus-fibre fine, tail in mouth, joined to the brahmarandhra.\n\nThe commentary marks a structural close at this nama: the names from 90 down to this one form a single group — the kula, cakra and kuṇḍalinī sequence. Nama 112 opens a new run."}},{"n":112,"gloss":"Bhavānī. Bhava means, Mahādeva, Saṃsāra, and Manmatha; and and to give life. The Devī","body":"Bhavānī.\nBhava means, Mahādeva, Saṃsāra, and Manmatha; and and to give life. The Devī Pr. explains the meaning thus: Bhava means Rudra, Kāma, the ocean of Saṃsāra ; Devī is called Bhavānī because she gives life to the above.\"\nWe\nOr, the water form of Śiva (one of his eight forms) is called Bhava, his wife is Usha, and his son Śukra. read the same in the Liṅga and Vāyu Prs. The Vāyu Pr. adds, 'Because all beings came from him and subsist in water, as he is the origin and supporter of all beings, he is called Bhava.\" She is Bhavānī because she is the giver of life (ana) to Bhava, water.\nOr, according to the Padma Pr. in the chapter enumerating the hundred and eight places of pilgrimage, she is said to be the presiding deity of the place called Sthanesvara.","dev":"भवानी","iast":"bhavānī","pada":[{"dev":"भव","iast":"bhava"},{"dev":"आनी","iast":"ānī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":112,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":40,"wbw":{"split":"भव-आनी","splitIast":"bhava-ānī","words":[{"dev":"भव","gloss":"Bhava — Rudra, Mahādeva; also saṃsāra, the round of becoming; also Manmatha; also, among Śiva's eight forms, the water-form"},{"dev":"आनी","gloss":"the feminine ङीष् with its आनुक् augment (Pāṇini 4.1.49), the suffix that forms consorts' names"}],"meaning":"Bhavānī — the consort of Bhava.","vigraha":["भवस्य पत्नी भवानी।"],"note":"Pāṇini 4.1.49 (इन्द्रवरुणभवशर्वरुद्रमृडहिमारण्ययवयवनमातुलाचार्याणामानुक्) lists भव among the words taking the आनुक् augment before the feminine ङीष् — which is why the name is भवानी and not *भवी. The same rule yields शर्वाणी at nama 121, and रुद्राणी and मृडानी elsewhere.\n\nBhāskararāya works all four senses of भव. As Rudra's consort she is Bhavānī simply. Reading भव as saṃsāra, Kāma and Rudra together, the Devī Purāṇa derives the name instead from अन् \"to breathe, to live\" — she who gives life to those three. Taking भव as Śiva's water-form (the Liṅga and Vāyu Purāṇas: all beings arise and subsist in water, hence he is Bhava), she is the one who animates the waters. The Padma Purāṇa names her the presiding deity of Sthāneśvara.\n\nNamas 112–117 all open on भ, and 118 onwards carry the alliteration into भक्ति-; such phonic clusters are structural in the Sahasranāma, not incidental."}},{"n":113,"gloss":"She is to be attained by meditation","body":"Having described the five forms, he describes by a few names the means of reaching them.\nBhāvanā. There are two kinds of meditation, one on the words themselves (S'abda) and the other on the meanings (Artha); words mean here sacred words which are to be meditated upon according to the will of the Guru. We have explained in the MImām sakavāda kautûhala,' as depending on the will of the Lord. Meanings: tending towards action (Pravrittirupa). This is also explained in the above work as the relations of cause and effect. The word gamya may be taken as agamya unattainable.\nOr, She is unattainable by the path of action (Karmamärga). Or, it means the purification of the mind as the result of that (right action).\nOr, there are three kinds of meditation. The Karma Pr. says, There are three forms of meditation, Brahmi, Maheś'vari, and Akṣara, these three, O twiceborn, always, reside in Rudra.\" In another place it says, 0 Brāhmaṇa, learn the three kinds of meditation set forth by me; one is concerning me (akṣara); the second is upon the unmanifested (Maheśvara); the last is on Brahman with the three attributes (guṇas); thus the three kinds 1 The MSS. are available in the Southern Dis.\n(of meditation) should be known.\"\nThis was told to\nIndradyumna by the lord in his tortoise incarnation. The characteristics of these (meditations) should be learned from the Ratnatrayaparīkṣā of (Appaya) Dīkṣita.'\nOr, there are three states of meditation, named, Sakala, Sakala-Niṣkala and Niṣkala. The Yoginīhṛdaya distinguishes the three kinds of meditations thus (I, 27): \"Meditation (from Mūlādhāra) to Ājna is Sakala; (from Indu) to Unmanī is Sakalanishkala; in supreme abode (Mahābindu) is Niṣkala.”","dev":"भावनागम्या","iast":"bhāvanāgamyā","pada":[{"dev":"भावना","iast":"bhāvanā"},{"dev":"गम्या","iast":"gamyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":113,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":40,"wbw":{"split":"भावना-गम्या","splitIast":"bhāvanā-gamyā","words":[{"dev":"भावना","gloss":"meditation, contemplative realization — literally the bringing into being in the mind (from भू, causative भावय्, with the action-suffix अन and feminine आ)"},{"dev":"गम्या","gloss":"to be reached, attainable (गम् \"to go\" + the gerundive यत्)"}],"meaning":"She who is to be attained by meditation.","vigraha":["भावनया गम्या। — tṛtīyā tatpuruṣa"],"note":"The commentary marks a turn here: the five forms having been described, these namas give the means of reaching them.\n\nBhāskararāya divides भावना into meditation on the words (śabda) and on the meanings (artha), and records the classifications in threes — Brāhmī, Māheśvarī and Akṣara (Kūrma Purāṇa); or Sakala, Sakala-niṣkala and Niṣkala, which the Yoginīhṛdaya (I.27) maps onto the ascent just described: mūlādhāra to ājñā is sakala, indu to unmanī sakala-niṣkala, and the mahābindu niṣkala.\n\nA second reading takes गम्या as अगम्या, unattainable — She is not to be reached by the path of action, karma-mārga, which serves only to purify the mind for the meditation that does reach Her."}},{"n":114,"gloss":"The woodcutter of the forest of earthly existence","body":"","dev":"भवारण्यकुठारिका","iast":"bhavāraṇyakuṭhārikā","pada":[{"dev":"भव","iast":"bhava"},{"dev":"अरण्य","iast":"araṇya"},{"dev":"कुठारिका","iast":"kuṭhārikā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":114,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":40,"wbw":{"split":"भव-अरण्य-कुठारिका","splitIast":"bhava-araṇya-kuṭhārikā","words":[{"dev":"भव","gloss":"becoming, worldly existence, saṃsāra"},{"dev":"अरण्य","gloss":"forest, wilderness — भवारण्य, the forest of existence"},{"dev":"कुठारिका","gloss":"a small axe, a hatchet (कुठार \"axe\" + the diminutive क with feminine आ)"}],"meaning":"She who is the axe to the forest of worldly existence.","vigraha":["भवारण्यस्य कुठारिका। — ṣaṣṭhī tatpuruṣa"],"note":"Sandhi: भव + अरण्य → भवारण्य (अ + अ → आ, Pāṇini 6.1.101 अकः सवर्णे दीर्घः).\n\nThe diminutive कुठारिका rather than कुठार is not merely feminine agreement — the image is of a small hand-axe, the thing that actually clears a forest, tree by tree rather than at a stroke.\n\nभव carries straight over from 112, where it meant Bhava-Rudra; here it means the saṃsāra that is His other name. Two consecutive namas turn the same word to opposite ends — consort of Bhava, destroyer of bhava."}},{"n":115,"gloss":"Delighting in benevolence","body":"Or, Bradra may mean a kind of elephant.","dev":"भद्रप्रिया","iast":"bhadrapriyā","pada":[{"dev":"भद्र","iast":"bhadra"},{"dev":"प्रिया","iast":"priyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":114,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":40,"wbw":{"split":"भद्र-प्रिया","splitIast":"bhadra-priyā","words":[{"dev":"भद्र","gloss":"auspicious, good, blessed; welfare, benevolence; also, lexically, a species of elephant"},{"dev":"प्रिया","gloss":"dear to, fond of, delighting in (from प्री \"to please\")"}],"meaning":"She who delights in what is auspicious.","vigraha":["भद्रं प्रियं यस्याः सा। — bahuvrīhi"],"note":"Bhāskararāya notes that भद्र may also be taken in its lexical sense of a kind of elephant, giving \"she who is fond of the bhadra elephant\" — a reading that ties the nama to Airāvata and royal iconography rather than to abstract benevolence."}},{"n":116,"gloss":"Of benevolent appearance","body":"117. Conferring prosperity on the devotees. (Bhakta saubhāgya dayinî.)\nAccording to the Agni Pr., Bhaga means fortune, desire, magnanimity, strength, effort, sun, and fame.\" subhaga, Lalitā herself.\nSaughabyas are her qualities; she unites (the above mentioned ones) with herself. But in the Padma Pr. it is said, Sugarcane, Parijata (Taruraya), nishpa and vājīra seeds, cow's milk with its changes (i.e., butter milk, curd, ghee, etc.), the saffron coloured things, flower, and salt, thus the eight things are called Saubhagyashtaka.\" Because these are used on auspicious occasions.\nOr, Su much, Bhāgya luck, i.e., She makes her devotees fortunate.\nFrom 109 to","dev":"भद्रमूर्ति","iast":"bhadramūrti","pada":[{"dev":"भद्र","iast":"bhadra"},{"dev":"मूर्ति","iast":"mūrti"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.933,"cpage":114,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":40,"wbw":{"split":"भद्र-मूर्ति","splitIast":"bhadra-mūrti","words":[{"dev":"भद्र","gloss":"auspicious, benign, blessed"},{"dev":"मूर्ति","gloss":"form, embodiment, visible shape"}],"meaning":"She whose very form is auspiciousness.","vigraha":["भद्रा मूर्तिः यस्याः सा। — bahuvrīhi"],"note":"The text here prints भद्रमूर्ति without the final visarga; the nama is standardly भद्रमूर्तिः.\n\nWhere 115 said She delights in the auspicious, 116 says She is it — the pair moves from preference to identity, and 117 completes the movement by having Her confer it."}},{"n":117,"gloss":"Nine names are divided according to the Chchalākshara Sütras","body":"Nine names are divided according to the Chchalākshara Sütras.","dev":"भक्तसौभाग्यदायिनी","iast":"bhaktasaubhāgyadāyinī","pada":[{"dev":"भक्त","iast":"bhakta"},{"dev":"सौभाग्य","iast":"saubhāgya"},{"dev":"दायिनी","iast":"dāyinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0,"cpage":114,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":40,"wbw":{"split":"भक्त-सौभाग्य-दायिनी","splitIast":"bhakta-saubhāgya-dāyinī","words":[{"dev":"भक्त","gloss":"devotee, one who is devoted (from भज् \"to share, to worship\")"},{"dev":"सौभाग्य","gloss":"good fortune, prosperity, marital felicity (vṛddhi derivative of सुभग \"well-portioned,\" by the suffix ष्यञ्)"},{"dev":"दायिनी","gloss":"she who gives, bestower (दा \"to give\" + णिनि, feminine ङीप्)"}],"meaning":"She who bestows good fortune on her devotees.","vigraha":["भक्तानां सौभाग्यं ददातीति भक्तसौभाग्यदायिनी।"],"note":"सौभाग्य is built on सुभग, and Bhāskararāya identifies सुभगा with Lalitā herself — so the saubhāgyas She bestows are Her own qualities extended to the devotee.\n\nThe Agni Purāṇa gives भग seven senses: fortune, desire, magnanimity, strength, effort, sun, fame. The Padma Purāṇa instead lists a concrete saubhāgyāṣṭaka, the eight substances used on auspicious occasions — sugarcane, pārijāta, niṣpāva and vājīra seeds, cow's milk and its products, saffron-coloured things, flowers, salt. A simpler reading takes सु + भाग्य directly: She makes Her devotees fortunate.\n\nThe commentary closes a group at this nama: 109 to 117, nine names in all, are divided according to the Cchalākṣara Sūtras."}},{"n":118,"gloss":"Delighting in devotion","body":"(41)\nBhakti is of two kinds, primary (Mukhya) and secondary (gauna); primary devotion is a particular mental modification called longing' directed towards Īśvara. The\nThe author of 104 works on different subjects.\n\"It\nBhaktimimamsa Sūtra (Śāṇḍilya Sūtra, I, 2) says, (devotion) is supreme longing for the Lord.\" Supreme (Parā) indicates according to the commentator that there is another kind (secondary) of devotion. The secondary devotion is in the form of service (to Lord) as described in a Sūtra (I, 20), \"The attainment of concentration is by gauni.\" The Garuḍa Pr. says, The root Bhaj is repeated in the service; so service is declared by the wise to be an important means (of reaching the Lord).”\nThere are many kinds of devotion, such as, remembering, pronouncing the name of God, etc. The Āditya and Garuḍa Prs. say, \"The devotion is of eight kinds, even if found in a barbarian (mleccha) he is best of Brāhmaṇas, he is a Sanyasin, he is prosperous, he is an ascetic and he is a learned man,” \"\" etc.\nThe Vi. Bhag. Pr. says, 66 'There are nine kinds of devotion.\" The Bra. Nāradīya Pr. says, 66 Ten kinds of devotion should be known, they are like a conflagration to the forest of sin.\" So there are many kinds of Bhakti like the above which should be known. All these are here included in the word Bhakti. The Śiva Pr. says, 66 What can mortals do for me who is (ever) satisfied and who has accomplished his end; but (the devotees) whatever they do (by devotion) externally or internally, I receive.\"","dev":"भक्तिप्रिया","iast":"bhaktipriyā","pada":[{"dev":"भक्ति","iast":"bhakti"},{"dev":"प्रिया","iast":"priyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":114,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":41},{"n":119,"gloss":"To be approached by devotion","body":"The Śruti (Katha-Up., IV, 1) says, \"The selfexistent constrains the senses which turn to external objects; therefore man sees the external objects, not the internal self; but the wise, with eye averted, and desirous of immortality, behold the inner self.” The Smṛti also says, 66 The Yogins see the eternal Lord.\" The Yoga Sūtra (I, 23) says, \"By meditation also upon Īśvara.\" The commentary, Rajamärtända, explains meditation as devotion. The Brahmā Sūtra (III, 2-24) says, \"And in the state of perfect conciliation also (the Yogins apprehend the highest Brahman) according to Śruti and Smṛti.\" The meaning\nis although Brahman is unmanifest, it becomes perceptible through devotion; thus Śrutis and Smṛtis say. Bh. Gītā also (XI, 54), By undeviated devotion, I may thus be perceived, Arjuna, and known and seen and entered in essence, O Parantapa.\" Here entered means Salvation, the attainment of Brahman itself, that is to be attained by Devotion alone.\nTo be approached (Gamya) also means to be obtained ; because in the Śruti (Chānd. Up., II, 23-2): \"One who resides in the Brahman obtains immortality,\" the word reside means devotion. Also the Bhakti and Brahmā Mimamsas (Śāṇḍilya, I, 4 and Vedānta, I, 1-4) say, 66 Of him who is engaged in that (devotion) immortality is declared.\" In the Nityatantra, also after describing the characteristics of devotion, explained as that which confers all objects of human desire; it is said, One who places reliance on the Guru whose characteristics have been already described, and in his teachings, and in the practice taught by him, which destroys the doubts of his mind; thus the devotion, which gives everything, is the cause of crossing (the ocean of) Saṃsāra and making one firm. Devoid of which (devotion) men surely suffer here and hereafter.\"\nOr, Bhakti, characteristics (Lakṣaṇa), Gamya, to be known; because the words ' Satya,' 'Jñāna' (Tai. Up., II, 1), etc., each with its own meaning cannot describe the unqualified Brahman (directly). Hence the Triśatī (name 73) says, She is to be known by characteristics.\" In a certain Sūtra of Jaimini, the word 'Bhakti' is explained as characteristics. In our Śivastava we said, “O Lord, I have no power to praise even an atom of your greatness, yet resting on imperfect devotion to you I composed (this hymn). The couch never cries' (a proverb) so in the world, O Paramaśiva (men succeed) by devotion, and not by their own power.'","dev":"भक्तिगम्या","iast":"bhaktigamyā","pada":[{"dev":"भक्ति","iast":"bhakti"},{"dev":"गम्या","iast":"gamyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":115,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":41},{"n":120,"gloss":"Controlled by devotion","body":"13*\nIt is said, O Śiva, though independent, thou art become subservient by devotion.'","dev":"भक्तिवश्या","iast":"bhaktivaśyā","pada":[{"dev":"भक्ति","iast":"bhakti"},{"dev":"वश्या","iast":"vaśyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":116,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":41},{"n":121,"gloss":"Remover of fear","body":"The Śruti (Tai. Up., II, 9-1) says, By knowing the bliss of Brahman none fears anything.\" The Vāyu Pr. also says, In the forest, and other places, in water, on earth, in the presence of a tiger, and before wild beasts, and robbers, and specially in all difficulties and discases, the names of Devī should be repeated.\"","dev":"भयापहा","iast":"bhayāpahā","pada":[{"dev":"भय","iast":"bhaya"},{"dev":"अपहा","iast":"apahā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":117,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":41},{"n":122,"gloss":"Śāmbhavī. (The wife of Śiva.) Or, she is the mother of the devotees of Śambhu. According to the Yoga Śāstra","body":"it means a Mudrā which is there explained; The object is within, the sight without closing or opening the eyes is outside, this is called Śāmbhavī mudrā, concealed in all the Tantras.\" But the Kalpasūtra (of Parasurāma, I, 32) says, There are three initiations, Sāktī, Śāmbhavī, and Mantri,\" and the characteristics of each are described in that (Sūtra) in detail; according to the Devī. Bhag. Pr. A girl of eight years is called Śāmbhavī.”","dev":"शांभवी","iast":"śāṃbhavī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":117,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":41},{"n":123,"gloss":"Worshipped by Śāradā","body":"Śāradā, Sarasvatī, and the deities of speech. Or, she is to be worshipped in the spring, or in the rainy season (Sārada). The Mark. Pr. says, 64 She should be worshipped in the spring and in the rainy season. The Rudrayāmala says, The Raktadandika (an aspect of Devī) should be worshipped during the nine days in spring.\"\nOr, Saruda, the clear minded men ; or according to the Medinikosa it means, one of the states of life (âsrama) called Salina.\nOr, according to the Anekārthadhvanimanjari, means Vāsudeva and Ã Brahmā,\" she is to be worshipped by the great (S'arada) Viṣṇu and Brahmā. The Kālikā Pr. explains the word Śāradā thus: \"Once upon a time in spring, in the ninth day, you were awakened by the devas ;\nhence you are known, in the world as well as in the seat, by the name Saruda.'","dev":"शारदाराध्या","iast":"śāradārādhyā","pada":[{"dev":"शारदा","iast":"śāradā"},{"dev":"राध्या","iast":"rādhyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":117,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":41},{"n":124,"gloss":"S'arvani. She is the wife of Śiva, whose body is the Earth. The Liṅga and Vāyu Prs. s","body":"S'arvani.\nShe is the wife of Śiva, whose body is the Earth. The Liṅga and Vāyu Prs. say, 'Śiva is called Sarva in his earth form, his wife, Sukeśī and his son Angāraka.\"","dev":"शर्वाणी","iast":"śarvāṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":118,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":41},{"n":125,"gloss":"Conferring happiness","body":"The Devī Bhag. Pr. says, 'She bestows happiness on devotees, hence she is called the bestower of happiness.”","dev":"शर्मदायिनी","iast":"śarmadāyinī","pada":[{"dev":"शर्मद","iast":"śarmada"},{"dev":"आयिनी","iast":"āyinī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":118,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":41},{"n":126,"gloss":"Śaṃkarī","body":"S'am, happiness and Kara doer. The Kālikā Pr. says, In the beginning of each cycle (lit. creation, preservation, and destruction), I always accompany the stainless Śambhu, in the form of a woman, being born of Dakṣa. Then the Devas will praise me, the allpervading illusion (Viṣṇu Māyā), the Yogasleep, the universe itself, by the name of Śaṃkarī and Rudrâni.\"","dev":"शांकरी","iast":"śāṃkarī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.75,"cpage":118,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":42},{"n":127,"gloss":"Śrīkarī","body":"According to the Vishnusahasranama, Srikara is Viṣṇu and his wife is Śrīkarī.\n11th 46 names are divided.","dev":"श्रीकरी","iast":"śrīkarī","pada":[{"dev":"श्री","iast":"śrī"},{"dev":"करी","iast":"karī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":118,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":42},{"n":128,"gloss":"Chaste","body":"Because in the three times (past, present and future) she is attached, to none but to her own husband; hence she bas unequalled virtue. The Ācārya (Saundaryalaharī 96) says, How many poets share the wife of Brahmā ? Cannot every one by means of wealth, become the lord of Sri (the wife of Viṣṇu)? But, O virtuous one, first among faithful women, your breasts are untouched save by Mahādeva, not even by the paste of Kuravaka.' The Devī Bhag. Pr. says, Thou art praised as sådhvi (the virtuous one) an account of unequalled fidelity (to thy Lord).\"\nA peculiar state in which the Yogi realises supernatural consoiousness.\n2 The Kuravaka is a kind of paste made of the leaves of the red amaranth and is used to redden the cheeks, breasts, palms, and soles of Indian females.","dev":"साध्वी","iast":"sādhvī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":118,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":42},{"n":129,"gloss":"Having a face like the spring moon","body":"","dev":"शरच्चन्द्रनिभानना","iast":"śaraccandranibhānanā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":0.9,"cpage":119,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":42},{"n":130,"gloss":"Slender waisted","body":"Or, satodara, having hundreds of caves, i.e., Himavat, whose daughter she is.","dev":"शातोदरी","iast":"śātodarī","pada":[{"dev":"शात","iast":"śāta"},{"dev":"उदरी","iast":"udarī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":119,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":42},{"n":131,"gloss":"Peaceful","body":"As she does not appear rough to the devotees.","dev":"शान्तिमती","iast":"śāntimatī","pada":[{"dev":"शान्ति","iast":"śānti"},{"dev":"मती","iast":"matī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":119,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":42},{"n":132,"gloss":"Without dependence","body":"As she is the true support of the universe, she herself has no dependence.\nThe Sūta\nOr, it is the worship called Nirādhāra. Saṃhitā (I, 5, 3) says, There are two kinds of worshipping the supreme Śakti.\" It then describes the two kinds, as external and internal, and again the external has two divisions one Vedic, and the other Tāntric, with their characteristics; it then goes on to say (Sl. 11 to 19), “* The internal worship is also of two kinds, one with support (Sadhāra), and the other without support, (Nirādhāra) and the latter is higher; the Sādhāra worship is by means of mental images, etc.; the nirādhāra worship is by pure intellect1 (i.e., without form). In the Sadhara worship Devī should be joyfully worshipped in a figure made up of the sacred syllables, according to the instructions of the Guru. The worship described as niradhāra, is the absorption of the understanding (manolaya) in that kind of meditation (Saṃvit). In reality, pure intellect means nothing else, but the supreme Śakti. Hence for the destruction of Saṃsāra, one should worship the supreme Śakti, the very self, the witness, free from the glamour of the manifold universe. By one's own direct experience of Maheś vari as the Self, She should be worshipped with much reverence; this is the worship which leads to salvation.\"","dev":"निराधारा","iast":"nirādhārā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":119,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":42},{"n":133,"gloss":"Stainless","body":"'The words in the text are adhara and Saṃvit. So they may meau, mūlädhāra, anāhata cakras as the places of worship\nNext by certain names, the text sets forth, first, the determination of the nature of the pure intellect, which is the object of Niradhara worship (No. 132), by assertion, by negation, by positive definition, and by elimination of attributes; then the means of obtaining it, and the real nature of the results thereby produced.\nThere are three kinds of ignorant souls (Paśus), the Vijñānakevala, he who sees the self in the nonself and has therefore that impurity alone; the pralayākala, he who has the impurity of Karma, which is imperceivable and which gives him (new) bodies; and the Sakala, he who has the impurity of the Māyā, which creates the idea of duality. In these three, the second has also the impurity of the first and the third that of the second. These are explained in our work Setubandha.1\nShe is called stainless, because she is free from the impurity of the Sakula.\nAnjana, black, i.c., attached to ignorance because ignor. ance has the quality of veiling (nature) owing to its quality of darkness. The Yogavāsiṣṭha says, Existence and nonexistence of (desired) objects produce joy and sorrow. O Rāma; impure inclination is termed attachment' (sanga).\" The Śruti (S've. Up., 6-19) says, 66 Blameless and stainless.\" That is, she is free from contact with ignorance; because of the nonexistence of ignorance, which is pure illusion. The illusion means, that which corresponds to negation in the three times. Or, ni, much ranjana, redness, or joy.","dev":"निरञ्जना","iast":"nirañjanā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":119,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":42},{"n":134,"gloss":"Without impurity","body":"(43)\nShe is called so because she is free from the impurity of the Pralayākala. · Lepa, the impurity arising from action\nIn the Setubandha, he says, Sakala is he who worships Devī from Mūlādhāra to Ajua; Pralayākala, who worships Devī from Ājñā to Uumani; and Niṣkala or Vijnänakevala worships Devī in the supreme abode, Mahābindu. For more details, the commentator refers the readers to Lalitā Sahasranāma, i.e., this work, name 354. The MS. copy has no reference to the Setubandha.\n(Karma). The Bh. Gītā (IV, 14), says,\npolute me.\"\nActions never\nOr, by the knowledge of Devī the devotee becomes free from impurity. The Su. Sam. (IV, 25, 25) says, The best amongst the knowers of Brahman, is never stained by any action, as the lotus leaf is not stained by water. Such is the glory of him, who is the best of the knowers of Brahman.' Bh. Gītā (V, 10) also says, He is not poluted by sin, as a lotus leaf is not poluted by water.\"","dev":"निर्लेपा","iast":"nirlepā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":120,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":43},{"n":135,"gloss":"Without spot","body":"She is said to be so, because she is free from the impurity of the Vijñānakevala.\nMālā, āṇavamala, or any spot. Or, avidyā is called spot, which causes continual impurity, darkness and\nconfusion.","dev":"निर्मला","iast":"nirmalā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":121,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":43},{"n":136,"gloss":"Eternal","body":"Though impurities are not permanent, their ruler is not so, because she is irresistible in the three times.\nThis means the rejection of the theory of those, who say all is ideal and momentary (Kshanikavijnānavāda). The Śruti (Br. Up., IV, 5-14), \"O, this Self is indestructible.\"\nOr, (nityā), the deities of fornights, or of time, or the mantra called by this name.","dev":"नित्या","iast":"nityā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":121,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":43},{"n":137,"gloss":"Without form","body":"This rejects the theory of those who hold, that everything has form and it belongs to the Vijnānavāda School (idealism) and is called Madhyamika.\nÅkāra, the form to which the qualities are attributed. The Viṣṇu Bhg. Pr. says, That (Brahman) is neither Devas, nor Daityas, nor human, nor animal, nor woman, nor eunuch, nor man, nor insect, nor quality, nor action, nor existence, nor nonexistence. May victory attend Him, the infinite who remains after all negation.”","dev":"निराकारा","iast":"nirākārā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":121,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":43},{"n":138,"gloss":"Without agitation","body":"Though in contact with avidyā, she is not agitated.\nOr, she is far from (ni), those whose minds are agitated akula). Or, akula, void; this destroys the theory of the Gambhīra School, which holds that all is void (nihilism).","dev":"निराकुला","iast":"nirākulā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":121,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":43},{"n":139,"gloss":"Without quality","body":"The opinion of the Tarka School is rejected here. The Śruti (Sve. Up., 6-11) says, \"The witness, the thinker, pure, and without qualities.\" As the qualities are connected with the body (matter) they are absent from what belongs to spirit (cit). This is explained in the Matsya and Padma Prs. where Nārada says to Himavat : \"As I have said, Devī is devoid of attributes, listen well, to the explanation of the meaning of this saying: attributes (Lakṣaṇa) are the marks of Devas, and qualities (guna) depend upon body; this Devī is without qualities and cannot be distinguished by attributes.\"","dev":"निर्गुणा","iast":"nirguṇā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":122,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":43},{"n":140,"gloss":"Without parts","body":"This rejects the theory of those who say that Brahman has parts. The (Ved.) Sūtra (II-3-13) says, (the soul) is a part of the Lord, on account of the declarations of differ- .\" The Bh. Gītā (XV, 7), \"In the world my part is Jīva.\" These two, Śruti and Smṛti are not opposed to one another, because the parts are merely attributed.\nence\nOr, Niṣkala, contemplation on (Brahman) without qualities. Vijanabhairavabhattaraka says, \"Meditation on (Brahman) is without parts, since he is without dependence and with no fixed restingplace. It is not meditation upon form as attributing face, hands, etc.\"","dev":"निष्कला","iast":"niṣkalā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":122,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":43},{"n":141,"gloss":"Tranquillity","body":"Tripurā Up. (Sve. Up., 6, 19) says, without motion, tranquil.\"\n\"Without parts,\nOr, the syllable S'a is anta at the end of the Amritabīja (mantra), i.e., Devī is the amritabīja mantra itself. Or, Asanta: Asa, quarters, anta, reaching to; i.e., allpervading. By this teaching Digambara's theory that the Self is limited, is refuted.","dev":"शान्ता","iast":"śāntā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":122,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":43},{"n":142,"gloss":"Without desire","body":"This refutes the theory of those who hold, according to the Upaniṣads that the Lord's will is eternal and thought also. The Devī Bhag. Pr. says, \"When her wishes are all fulfilled nothing remains for her desire.\" The Śruti (Br. Up., 2-3-6) says, \"The Ātman is (defined) by not this, not this \"; (5-1-1) \"That is filled, this is filled.\" The other Śrutis which treat of qualities (guna) relate to upādhis (attributes).","dev":"निष्कामा","iast":"niṣkāmā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":122,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":43},{"n":143,"gloss":"Indestructible","body":"Or, nir much, upa, near, i.e., in the body, plava, flowing (of the nectar), i.e., by whom the abundant flowing of nectar in the body occurs (vide No. 111). Thus the Ar. Up. (Tai. Ar., I) says, 'Wet the whole (body in the streams of ambrosia), and further wet (the seventytwo thousand channels).\" Know me always as born from the universe, i.e., the individual soul is a part and parcel of the universal one.","dev":"निरुपप्लवा","iast":"nirupaplavā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":123,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":43},{"n":144,"gloss":"Ever free","body":"(44)\nOr, whose devotees are ever free ; or as she is eternally free, i.e., Salvation personified.","dev":"नित्यमुक्ता","iast":"nityamuktā","pada":[{"dev":"नित्यम्","iast":"nityam"},{"dev":"उक्ता","iast":"uktā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":123,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":44},{"n":145,"gloss":"Unchanging","body":"Vikāras, the twentythree catagories of the Sāṃkhyas, beginning with Mahat. The Sankhyatatva Kaumudī (Karikā, III), says, The Mālaprakṛti, (the root of all) is no production; the seven principles, the Mahat, etc., are productions and productive; sixteen are productions (but unproductive); the soul is neither a production nor productive.”","dev":"निर्विकारा","iast":"nirvikārā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":123,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":44},{"n":146,"gloss":"Without extension","body":"The Viśva says,\nPrapancha means accumulation, extension, expansion.” The Śruti (Man. Up., 7) says, They think the fourth state (the state of ecstasy) is the negation of illusion, peace, bliss, and nonduality.”","dev":"निष्प्रपञ्चा","iast":"niṣprapañcā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":123,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":44},{"n":147,"gloss":"Bodiless","body":"This refutes the theory of the Charvākas who hold that the Brahman is inseparable from the body. As she\nis that on which all depends, she herself depends on nothing. It is said, Where is the foundation of Her on whom the universe is founded.\"","dev":"निराश्रया","iast":"nirāśrayā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":123,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":44},{"n":148,"gloss":"Ever pure","body":"Because in the three times she is without blemish, she is called ever pure. The Śruti says, She is free from contact, great and purity itself \" ; and it is said, in the Smṛti also, The body is very impure, and the dweller in it is very pure \".","dev":"नित्यशुद्धा","iast":"nityaśuddhā","pada":[{"dev":"नित्य","iast":"nitya"},{"dev":"शुद्धा","iast":"śuddhā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":124,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":44},{"n":149,"gloss":"Ever wise","body":"66 For know- As she is in the\nAccording to the Śruti (Br. Up., IV, 3, 30), ledge is inseparable from the knower.\" form of cit itself she is ever wise.\nOr, Śuddha (No. 148) and Buddha may apply to the two individuals (Tirthankaras) of Jaina, who have become immortal (nityās) by the grace of Devī. Because we see that one of the worships of Devī is by following the six systems of philosophies, in which the Jaina scriptures are included. The meaning is that Devī is the Tārā (a deity of the Jaina) who is worshipped by these two.","dev":"नित्यबुद्धा","iast":"nityabuddhā","pada":[{"dev":"नित्य","iast":"nitya"},{"dev":"बुद्धा","iast":"buddhā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":124,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":44},{"n":150,"gloss":"Blameless","body":"Avadya, blame, i.c., the modifications of Avidyā. The Śruti (Sve. Up., 6, 19) says, Blameless and spotless.\" Or, avadya, is the name of a hell; by her grace the devotees escape that hell. The Kūrma Pr. says, 66 Hence if any one remembers Devī day and night he never goes to the hell called avadya, as he is cleansed from all sins.\" The Liṅga Pr. says, There are twentyeight crores of hells from Ghora to Māyā; in these cursed hells the sinners are scorched, who here failed to take refuge in the Lord of Devī, Śaṃkara, and Nilalohita.'","dev":"निरवद्या","iast":"niravadyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":124,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":44},{"n":151,"gloss":"Compact","body":"The Amara (Slo. 468) says,\nantara means opening, period, clothing, disappearance, difference, behalf, a hole, self, absence, outside, opportunity, middle, and individual soul.\" The Śruti (Tai. Up., II, 7-1) says, 66 He who makes\n14*\nbut the smallest distinction in separateness, fear arises in him.\" By this is refuted the theory of those who hold that the Brahman is subject to three kinds of distinction, i.e., sajatiya, etc. (difference in the same species, difference between different species, and individual differences.) (45)","dev":"निरन्तरा","iast":"nirantarā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":124,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":44},{"n":152,"gloss":"Without cause","body":"Since she is the cause of everything she is without cause. The Śruti (Sve. Up., 6, 9) says, He is the cause, he is the lord of the rulers of causes; none created him. none is his superior.'\nOr, Nis, much, Kārana, the first liquid, ¿.e., in her is fullness of the first liquid and she is (ever) joyful by that means. It is said, Residing in the big lotus garden (in Sahasrāra) and sharing the joy (cansed) by Kāraṇa (the ambrosia).\"\nsin.\"","dev":"निष्कारणा","iast":"niṣkāraṇā","pada":[{"dev":"निष्का","iast":"niṣkā"},{"dev":"रणा","iast":"raṇā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":125,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":45},{"n":153,"gloss":"Faultless","body":"The Śruti (Īśa. Up., 8) says, \"Pure, untouched by\nwhich","dev":"निष्कलङ्का","iast":"niṣkalaṅkā","pada":[{"dev":"निष्क","iast":"niṣka"},{"dev":"लङ्का","iast":"laṅkā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":125,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":45},{"n":154,"gloss":"Without Limitation","body":"Upa, near, adhi, gives. The red flower transfers its colour to the crystal near it, is the upādhi. Similarly, ignorance is the upɑdhi which causes the appearance of plurality in consciousness (cit).\nOr, Nirupūdhi, is the real cause (sathetu) to identify Devī with the special quality of \"without parts \".\nwhole the other\nOr, there are two Upādhis one ‘fragmentary '; and she is free from both.","dev":"निरुपाधि","iast":"nirupādhi","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":125,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":45},{"n":155,"gloss":"Without a superior","body":"superior. Or, there are\nBecause she is the ruler of all, she herself has no Sāṃkhya, the former is monism and the latter atheism, and two systems, Mīmāṃsā and she belongs to both.","dev":"र्निरीश्वरा","iast":"rnirīśvarā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.909,"cpage":125,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":45},{"n":156,"gloss":"Without passion","body":"The text now proceeds to reject the theory that, the differences in the mind belong to the Self, and to teach that renunciation is the means of overcoming the six\nLALITA SAHASRANAMÁ\nimpediments (ie., desire, wrath, covetousness, bewilderment, pride, and envy).\nRūga, desire, because she has obtained all her desires. Or, according to the Śāṇḍilya Sūtra (1, 6) “ (Devotion) is longing (Rīga) as it is opposed to hatred and as it is expressed (by the word) Rasa' (essence).\" Devotion means passion (or longing for); she is free from it (as she herself is supreme and she has no object to long for).\nOr Nira, water, and aya, mountain, both of them being her forms.","dev":"नीरागा","iast":"nīrāgā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":125,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":45},{"n":157,"gloss":"Destroying desire","body":"By not endowing her devotees with passion. By Rāga is meant here, 'Desire, aversion and ardent attachment to life as described in the Yoga Sūtra (II, 3).","dev":"रागमथना","iast":"rāgamathanā","pada":[{"dev":"राग","iast":"rāga"},{"dev":"मथना","iast":"mathanā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":126,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":45},{"n":158,"gloss":"Without pride","body":"","dev":"निर्मदा","iast":"nirmadā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":126,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":45},{"n":159,"gloss":"Destroying pride","body":"Or, madana, excessive anxiety, and āsana,\nconsuming.","dev":"मदनाशिनी","iast":"madanāśinī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":126,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":45},{"n":160,"gloss":"Without care","body":"(46)\nCintā, means recollection generally, here it means recollection of painful topics. It is said, Anxiety should be known as (resembling) a funeral pile, the only difference is the additional \" sound in cintā, bút as the pile (chita) burns the corpse, so anxiety burns the living '; or, according to the Vis'va, chintă means delusion also she is without delusion.\ninan","dev":"निश्चिन्ता","iast":"niścintā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":126,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":46},{"n":161,"gloss":"Without egoism","body":"Ahaṃkāra is of three kinds, Vaikārika,\nIt is said, Taijasa, and Bhatadi. Tamas, respectively.)\n(According to the Sattva, Rajas and","dev":"निरहंकारा","iast":"nirahaṃkārā","pada":[{"dev":"निरहम्","iast":"niraham"},{"dev":"कारा","iast":"kārā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.833,"cpage":126,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":46},{"n":162,"gloss":"Without bewilderment","body":"Moha, is confusion of thought, distraction of the mind.","dev":"निर्मोहा","iast":"nirmohā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":126,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":46},{"n":163,"gloss":"Destroying bewilderment","body":"By imparting the idea of unity (to her devotees). The Śruti (Īśa. Up., 7), \"To him who perceives unity what bewilderment and what sorrow can there be?\n\"9","dev":"मोहनाशिनी","iast":"mohanāśinī","pada":[{"dev":"मोहन","iast":"mohana"},{"dev":"आशिनी","iast":"āśinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":126,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":46},{"n":164,"gloss":"Without selfinterest","body":"Selfinterest necessarily implies separateness and as she is without separateness, she is said to be without selfinterest.","dev":"निर्ममा","iast":"nirmamā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":127,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":46},{"n":165,"gloss":"The destroyer of selfinterest","body":"The destroyer of selfinterest.","dev":"ममताहन्त्री","iast":"mamatāhantrī","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":0,"cpage":127,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":46},{"n":166,"gloss":"Without sin","body":"","dev":"निष्पापा","iast":"niṣpāpā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":127,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":46},{"n":167,"gloss":"Destroying sin","body":"(Mamatāhantri.)\nBy the repetition of (her) mantra, etc., she destroys the sins of devotees. The Śruti (Cheha. Up., V, 24, 3) says, \"Like the point of a reed in the fire, so all his sins are burned up.\" The Vasish/ha Smṛ. says, The Brāhmaṇa who is devoted to learning and penance and who continually repeats (mantras), even if he is always committing sinful actions, he is not afflicted thereby. Sin never resides in those who repeat (mantras), or offer oblations, or meditate, or make pilgrimages, or who perform Śirovrata (the rite of carrying fire on the head).” ' The Padma Pr. in its Puṣkarakhaṇḍa says, \"The mass of sins though as great as Mount Meru is instantly destroyed by approaching Kātyāyanī. He who is devoted to Durgā, is not stained even by committing heinous crimes, in the same manner as the lotus leaf which is not affected by water.\" The De. Bhag. Pr. also speaks to the same effect. The Brahmāṇḍa Pr. says, 66 The sinful actions of those who are devoid of varna and asrama, and the wretched, by mere meditation on Devī, become virtuous.\"","dev":"पापनाशिनी","iast":"pāpanāśinī","pada":[{"dev":"पाप","iast":"pāpa"},{"dev":"नाशिनी","iast":"nāśinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":127,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":46},{"n":168,"gloss":"Without anger","body":"(47)\nBecause she has no object of hatred. The Bh. Gītā says (9-29), “There is none hateful to me nor dear.”","dev":"निष्क्रोधा","iast":"niṣkrodhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":127,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":47},{"n":169,"gloss":"Destroyer of anger","body":"Anger, is one of the six enemies (ride No. 156); so Āpastamba says, He who sacrifices, offers oblation, or worships while angry, is deprived of all (benefit therefrom) like water in a vessel of unbaked clay.\"","dev":"क्रोधशमनी","iast":"krodhaśamanī","pada":[{"dev":"क्रोध","iast":"krodha"},{"dev":"शमनी","iast":"śamanī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":127,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":47},{"n":170,"gloss":"Without greed","body":"Śirovrata, according to the Sūta Saṃhitā is the Śaiva initiation.\nBecause she is exceedingly liberal.","dev":"निर्लोभा","iast":"nirlobhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":127,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":47},{"n":171,"gloss":"Destroying greed","body":"It is said, Greed destroys all good qualities.' Tantrarāja on the characteristics of the guru says, 46 He is the guru who is without doubt, the remover of doubt and expects nothing.\" After this the characteristics of these\n(latter) two qualities are explained.","dev":"लोभनाशिनी","iast":"lobhanāśinī","pada":[{"dev":"लोभन","iast":"lobhana"},{"dev":"आशिनी","iast":"āśinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":128,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":47},{"n":172,"gloss":"Without doubt","body":"Without doubt.","dev":"निःसंशया","iast":"niḥsaṃśayā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":128,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":47},{"n":173,"gloss":"Destroying doubt","body":"(Samsayaghni.)\nThe Śruti (Mun. Up., II, 2, 8), All doubts are cut away\"; and the Smṛti also echoes the same. 12th verse divides 49 names.","dev":"संशयनी","iast":"saṃśayanī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.8,"cpage":128,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":47},{"n":174,"gloss":"Without origin","body":"The Bh. Gītā (13-12) says, “The Supreme Brahman is without origin.\"","dev":"निर्भवा","iast":"nirbhavā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":128,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":47},{"n":175,"gloss":"Destroying samsāra","body":"The Śaktirahasya says, In the ninth day of the lunar month, according to the rule, as to him who anoints Caṇḍikā with ghee, hear the result of that action; he is raised out of the ocean of Saṃsāra and shines in the Durgiloka, together with the preceding ten and also succeeding ten generations.\" The Kūrma, and De. Bhag. Prs. speak to the same effect.\nOr according to the Brihatjābāla Up. (No. 26), Bhavanāzinī is the name of a river.","dev":"भवनाशिनी","iast":"bhavanāśinī","pada":[{"dev":"भवन","iast":"bhavana"},{"dev":"आशिनी","iast":"āśinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":128,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":47},{"n":176,"gloss":"(48) Without false imaginings","body":"Vikalpu, is a notion conveyed by (mere) words without corresponding objects. The Yoga Sūtra (I, 9) says, ·Vikalpa (fancy) is a notion conveyed by mere words, but of which there is no object corresponding to reality.\" The Khandana khandakhadya (a work on Logic by Harṣa) says, \"The word gives knowledge of an object which cannot possibly exist.\" The Gautama Sūtra also in the examination of the result (4-50) says, That is not real which is created in the mind.\" she is in the form of unconditioned eternal knowledge.\nHence\nLALITA SAHASRANAMÁ\nNirvikalpa, in the last mental modification, i.. (last stage of meditation), there are no (nr) specific distinctions (Vikalpa).\nOr Vi, opposed; kulpa, theory. There is no theory opposed to her existence because everything is inseparable from her, i.c., Devī is devoid of all separateness. The Vedin. Sūtra (III, 2, 14) says, For Brahman is merely devoid of form on account of this being the main purport of Scripture.\" By this it is established that the special characteristic of Brahman is that it has no such distinction as that of subject and object.","dev":"निर्विकल्पा","iast":"nirvikalpā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":128,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":48},{"n":177,"gloss":"Undisturbed","body":"When it is seen that the appearance of silver in the shell, is deceptive the illusion vanishes; there is no such liability to error in her nature.","dev":"निराबाधा","iast":"nirābādhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":129,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":48},{"n":178,"gloss":"Without difference","body":"As she is neither connected with Anuyogitva, (for) nor with Pratiyogitva (against) to the mutual nonexistence, She is said to be without difference. The Karma Pr. says, 'Thou art the Supreme Śakti, infinite, supreme ruler, devoid of all differences and the destroyer of all difference.\" In another place, Some ignorant persons say, there is a difference between Śakti and the possessor of it (Śiva), but those Yogins who meditate on reality recognise nonseparateness.'","dev":"निर्भेदा","iast":"nirbhedā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":129,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":48},{"n":179,"gloss":"Destroyer of difference","body":"Bheda the dual knowledge; she causes its destruction\nby the knowledge of reality.","dev":"भेदनाशिनी","iast":"bhedanāśinī","pada":[{"dev":"भेदन","iast":"bhedana"},{"dev":"आशिनी","iast":"āśinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":129,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":48},{"n":180,"gloss":"Imperishable","body":"Nasa, end. The Śruti, (Tai. Up., III) “Brahman is being, knowledge, and infinity.\"","dev":"निर्नाशा","iast":"nirnāśā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":129,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":48},{"n":181,"gloss":"Destroying death","body":"The death of devotees. The Tripurā Up. (No. 82) says, \"Why it is said, because from mortality he obtains immortality, attains imperishable state; attains the eternal existence; and he himself becomes Rudra.”","dev":"मृत्युमधनी","iast":"mṛtyumadhanī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.875,"cpage":129,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":48},{"n":182,"gloss":"Without action","body":"Because she is without action, whether ordained or prohibited. The Śruti, (Chānd. Up., 8, 12, 1)\nOnly one\nwho has no body is unaffected by likes or dislikes.\"\nOr, the expression may mean, one who has become the actor and the cause, etc., without the two means of action (material and instrumental causes). For the Viṣṇu Pr. says, Just as the fragrant object is enough by its very presence to distract the mind, so does the Supreme Lord without help (the creation of the Universe).”","dev":"निष्क्रिया","iast":"niṣkriyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":130,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":48},{"n":183,"gloss":"Receiving nothing","body":"According to the Medini, “ Parigraha means, attendants,\nwife, appropriation, root.\" She is none of these.","dev":"निष्परिग्रहा","iast":"niṣparigrahā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":130,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":48},{"n":184,"gloss":"Incomparable","body":"The Tripurā. Up. (No. 82) says,\ncomparison.\"\n185.\n186.","dev":"निस्तुला","iast":"nistulā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":130,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":49},{"n":185,"gloss":"Incomparable. (Nistula.)","body":"The Tripurā. Up. (No. 82) says,\ncomparison.\"\n185.\n186.","dev":"नीलचिकुरा","iast":"nīlacikurā","pada":[{"dev":"नील","iast":"nīla"},{"dev":"चिकुरा","iast":"cikurā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0,"cpage":130,"sure":0,"host":184,"v":49},{"n":186,"gloss":"Incomparable. (Nistula.)","body":"The Tripurā. Up. (No. 82) says,\ncomparison.\"\n185.\n186.","dev":"निरपाया","iast":"nirapāyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":130,"sure":0,"host":184,"v":49},{"n":187,"gloss":"Blue haired. (Nīlachikura.)","body":"Imperishable. (Nirapaya.) Without transgression.\n(49)\n46 Without cause or\n(Niratyayä.)\nAtyaya, means, transgression,\npunishment, obstruction, fault, and emaciation.”","dev":"निरत्यया","iast":"niratyayā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.444,"cpage":130,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":49},{"n":188,"gloss":"Difficult of attainment","body":"Even to yogins.","dev":"दुर्लभा","iast":"durlabhā","pada":[],"pconf":0.7,"fit":1,"cpage":130,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":49},{"n":189,"gloss":"Difficult of approach","body":"Or, Aduryamā, accessible; or, durgama is the name of a Daitya whom Devī caused to be slain.","dev":"दुर्गमा","iast":"durgamā","pada":[{"dev":"दुर्","iast":"dur"},{"dev":"गमा","iast":"gamā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":130,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":49},{"n":190,"gloss":"Durgā. The Mark. Pr. and the Lakshmitantra in the Pañcarātra say, In that place I am","body":"Durgā.\nThe Mark. Pr. and the Lakshmitantra in the Pañcarātra say, In that place I am going to kill a great Daitya named Durgama, hence my name shall be Durgā.” This is the goddess who resides in the place called Sannati on the bank of the river, Bhimarathi. Durgā is explained in the Devī Pr. : The word Devas were delivered from mental and physical fear, in Indra, and other difficulty and in battle, hence (Devī) is called Durgā (i.e., (leliverer).\" This Devī, solicited by king Subāhu, who\nbegged a boon of her, established herself under this name at Benares. This story also occurs in the De. Bhag. Pr. According to this a nine year old girl also is called Durgā.","dev":"दुर्गा","iast":"durgā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":130,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":49},{"n":191,"gloss":"Destroyer of pain","body":"Dukha, pain, caused by Saṃsāra. The Gautama Sūtra (I, 22) says, Salvation is the complete release from that (pain).' The Śruti also, He lives completely freed from pain.\"\n\"?","dev":"दु खहन्त्री","iast":"du khahantrī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":131,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":49},{"n":192,"gloss":"Conferring happiness","body":"Sukha, happiness, ¿.e., in this world, in the next world and beatitude. The Śruti (Tai. Up., II, 7) says, Only after obtaining the essence (Rasa, i.e., Brahman) does one become blissful.\" Refer for further details to the Padma Pr. Puṣkarakhaṇḍa last six chapters.\n(50)","dev":"सुखप्रदा","iast":"sukhapradā","pada":[{"dev":"सुख","iast":"sukha"},{"dev":"प्रदा","iast":"pradā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":131,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":49},{"n":193,"gloss":"Unattainable by Sinners","body":"The De. Bhag. Pr. says, \"Those who argue sophistically never worship Devī, the ruler of the Universe, the benevolent.\"","dev":"दुष्टदूरा","iast":"duṣṭadūrā","pada":[{"dev":"दुष्ट","iast":"duṣṭa"},{"dev":"दूरा","iast":"dūrā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.889,"cpage":131,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":50},{"n":194,"gloss":"Putting an end to evil customs","body":"Evil customs are contrary to the Scriptures; concerning this (more) will be said below as, She quickly destroys all the sins of men committed by neglecting the daily rites and observing the prohibited ones.\"","dev":"दुराचारशमनी","iast":"durācāraśamanī","pada":[{"dev":"दुराचार","iast":"durācāra"},{"dev":"शमनी","iast":"śamanī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":131,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":50},{"n":195,"gloss":"Devoid of faults","body":"Dosha, passion, hatred, etc.","dev":"दोषवर्जिता","iast":"doṣavarjitā","pada":[{"dev":"दोष","iast":"doṣa"},{"dev":"वर्जिता","iast":"varjitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":131,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":50},{"n":196,"gloss":"Omniscient","body":"The Śruti (Mund. Up., I, I, !) says,\nOne who\nperceives all and knows all.” The Devī Pr. also, “As she knows everything she is called Omniscient.\"","dev":"सर्वज्ञा","iast":"sarvajñā","pada":[{"dev":"सर्व","iast":"sarva"},{"dev":"ज्ञा","iast":"jñā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":131,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":50},{"n":197,"gloss":"Intense compassion","body":"","dev":"सान्द्रकरुणा","iast":"sāndrakaruṇā","pada":[{"dev":"सान्द्र","iast":"sāndra"},{"dev":"करुणा","iast":"karuṇā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":131,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":50},{"n":198,"gloss":"Having neither equal, nor Superior","body":"The Śruti (Sve. Up., 6, 8), “No one is seen equal or superior to him,”\n(51)","dev":"समानाधिकवर्जिता","iast":"samānādhikavarjitā","pada":[{"dev":"समान","iast":"samāna"},{"dev":"अधिक","iast":"adhika"},{"dev":"वर्जिता","iast":"varjitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":131,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":50},{"n":199,"gloss":"Possessing all Śaktis","body":"The text now sets forth her qualified form (Saguṇa),\nShe is not to be separated from any of the Śaktis such as, Bālā, Bagalā, etc. Or, she is the aggregate of all the divine powers. In the Lakṣmī Tr. Pañcarātra Devī says to Indra, \"O Indra, I am Mahālakṣmī, in the Svayambhava Kalpa, I was born as the slayer of Mahiṣa (daitya) for the wellbeing of all Devas. Those portions of my powers which entered the bodies of the Devas together became my supremely beautiful form. My powers, O Indra, became manifest in the form of the weapons of the Devas.\" The Märk. Pr. explains the same in detail.\nSarva may mean infinite, because the Liṅga Pr. says, The wise recognise whatever energies belong to objects, as Gaurī.\"","dev":"सर्वशक्तिमयी","iast":"sarvaśaktimayī","pada":[{"dev":"सर्वशक्ति","iast":"sarvaśakti"},{"dev":"मयी","iast":"mayī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.941,"cpage":132,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":51},{"n":200,"gloss":"(The source of) all good fortune","body":"The Devī Pr. says, She gives all the good fortune (longed for) in the heart, all desired good objects, hence she is called Sarvamangalā. And she removes the pain of the devotees and gives to Hara all the best and choicest things, hence, Sarvamangalā.”\nThus ends the third Kāla, called Dhamrika, with the second hundred, in the Saubhāgyabhāskara, composed by Sri Bhasurānanda.\nTHE THIRD HUNDRED","dev":"सर्वमङ्गला","iast":"sarvamaṅgalā","pada":[{"dev":"सर्व","iast":"sarva"},{"dev":"मङ्गला","iast":"maṅgalā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":132,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":51},{"n":201,"gloss":"Leading into the right path","body":"Satgati includes all stages from Svarga to Mokṣa ; or, sat, Brahman, gati, knowledge; or, sul, of wise, gati, goal. For it is said by great men, Thou art the way, thou alone art the way, O Bhavānī.”\nThe Padma Pr. says, “O King, he who worships (Devī) at the three times on the fourteenth day, goes to the supreme abode where Devī is . . Having\n15*\nperformed on a large or small scale, the rites according to his means, he shines in the world of Rudra.\" Thus in four chapters, the above book treats of the rites and ceremonies of worship which lead to all the worlds.\npath.","dev":"सद्गतिप्रदा","iast":"sadgatipradā","pada":[{"dev":"सद्गति","iast":"sadgati"},{"dev":"प्रदा","iast":"pradā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.857,"cpage":132,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":51},{"n":202,"gloss":"Ruler of all","body":"Because she is the ruler of all, she leads into the right","dev":"सर्वेश्वरी","iast":"sarveśvarī","pada":[{"dev":"सर्व","iast":"sarva"},{"dev":"ईश्वरी","iast":"īśvarī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":133,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":51},{"n":203,"gloss":"All","body":"Without nonseparation there cannot be unlimited dominion, hence this name.\nSarva includes all the Tattvas from Earth to Śiva.\nThe Kamika says, There are two hundred and twentyfour worlds; these should be meditated upon as if they were the hairs on the body of the Lord. This is the Bhuvana method. One who praises by means of the fifty letters, should meditate on them as on the skin of the Lord of Devas, the holder of the trident. This is the letter method. One should meditate on the blood of Śiva by means of the seven crores of mantras which arise from the root Vidyās (Vedas). This the mantra method. The words of the mantras which are various, according to different arrangements: stand for the flesh and the bloodvessels of the Lord. This is the word method. The thirtysix Tattvas, beginning with the earth are the forms (ie., Śiva's) sinews, bones and marrow. This process the comprehensers of the Vedas call the Tattva method.\"","dev":"सर्वमयी","iast":"sarvamayī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":133,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":51},{"n":204,"gloss":"She is the very essence of all mantras","body":"This and the next two following names together express the previous name.\nMantras, viz., seven crores of mantras. The Sundari Tāpaniya Up. says, \"Many mantras are arranged on either side of the Vidyā (the fifteen syllabled mantra).” This is more clearly explained by Gauḍapāda in seven Sūtras (3 to 9) and the commentator of them also explains that she is the essence of all Mantras.\n(52)","dev":"सर्वमन्त्रस्वरूपिणी","iast":"sarvamantrasvarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"सर्व","iast":"sarva"},{"dev":"मन्त्र","iast":"mantra"},{"dev":"स्वरूपिणी","iast":"svarūpiṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.88,"cpage":133,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":51},{"n":205,"gloss":"She is the soul of all Yantras","body":"Yantras are ghata, argala, etc.","dev":"सर्बयन्त्रात्मिका","iast":"sarbayantrātmikā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.9,"cpage":134,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":52},{"n":206,"gloss":"She is the spirit of all Tantras. rūpā.)","body":"(Sarvatantra-\nTantras are the Vāmakeśvara and others.\nOr, she\nis to be sought after by means of all the Tantras. It is said, “ Many paths, different according to the scriptures, and all leading to success, merge in thee alone as the rivers do into the ocean.\" These (āgamas) as her body, the Kamika says, (1) Kamika the lotus feet, (2) Yogaja -- ankles; (3) and (4) Kāraṇa and Prasrita —the toes ; (5) Ajita—the knees; (6) Diptathe thighs; (7) Amsumānback; (8) Suprabheda--navel; (9) Vijaya—stomach; (10) Nisvasaheart; (11) Svāyambhuva--the bosom; (12) Analathe three eyes; (13) Viragama -throat; (14) Rurutantraears; (15) Makuta crown; (16) Vipula arms; (17) Chandrajnanachest; (18) Bimba---lotus face; (19) Prodgīta -tongue; (20) Lalitā -cheeks; (21) Siddha -- forehead; (22) Santāna -earrings ; (23) Kiranared coloured gems; (24) Vātula--garments; all other auxiliary and supplementary Tantras--the hair on the body. Thus the body of the Great Queen should be contemplated on under\nthe form of the Tantras.'","dev":"सर्वतन्त्ररूपा","iast":"sarvatantrarūpā","pada":[{"dev":"सर्वतन्त्र","iast":"sarvatantra"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0,"cpage":134,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":52},{"n":207,"gloss":"Manonmanī. Manonmanī is the eighth place from the centre between the eyebrows, just b","body":"Manonmanī.\nManonmanī is the eighth place from the centre between the eyebrows, just below the Brahmarandhra ; being of that form, she is so called.” sangraha says, \"There is a Śakti the cause (of all causes), The Svachchandaabove that comes Unmanī. In that centre there is no\n1 Four numbers are left here as there are twentyeight Śaiva āgamas ; viz., (1) Sarvokta, (2) Paramesvara, (3) Chintya, (4) Sūkshma; and for Sahasra, Pragati is substituted.\n2 Yoginīhṛdaya, I, 26, says, from the Bhrumadhya there are oight places, viz., (1) Indu, (2) Rodhinī, (3) Näda, (4) Nädänta, (5) Śakti, (6) Vyapini, (7) Samanā, (8) Unmanī, (or Manoumani), and each succeeding one is more subtle than those preceding it. The ninth is called Mahābindu.\ntime or space, no Tattva, no deity, complete freedom; purity, supremacy; what is called Rudravaktra (mouth of Rudra). This is called the Śakti of Śiva; in it there is neither subject nor object, spotless.\" So in the Śruti (Rudradhyāya), “Salutation to Vāmadeva, supreme excellence,\" (it is explained), that the Śakti of Śiva is named Manonmanī. The Tripurā Up. also mentions, That when the mind, free from attachment to object. fixed on the heart, attains the state of Unmanī, then the supreme abode (one reaches).\" Again Manonmanī is a kind of Mudră; according to the yoga Śāstra, its characteristics are described thus: \"By this process the eyes neither close nor open, by which breath is neither inhaled nor exhaled and the mind is a blank neither speculating nor doubting, direct that Manoninant towards me.\" The Br. Nāradīya Pr. says, “When the process of meditation, meditator and the object of meditation are entirely destroyed, then Unmanī stage arises ; consequently he enjoys the ambrosia of wisdom.\"\nOr, the minds (of the devotees) are raised to (Unmanyante) the higher knowledge (Unma a lifting up).","dev":"मनोन्मनी","iast":"manonmanī","pada":[{"dev":"मन","iast":"mana"},{"dev":"उन्मनी","iast":"unmanī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":134,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":52},{"n":208,"gloss":"Maheś'varī (the wife of Mabesvara). The Śruti","body":"referring to Maheśvara says, \"It is the Svara (Oṃkāra) which is proclaimed in the beginning of the Vedas and also established at the end and which is involved in its own nature (on syllables) indicates him who is supreme Mahezvara,” Here the Śruti declares, Maheśvara as transcending the three guṇas. The Liṅga Pr. says, In his dark quality (Tamas) he is called Rudra, the destroyer; in his passionate quality (Rajas) the one born from the golden egg (Brahmā) ; in his pure quality (Sattva) he is the allpervading Viṣṇu ; devoid of the three qualities (Nirguza) he is Maheśvara. He is Maheś'vara whose emblem (the Liṅga) is worshipped by persons possessing purity of mind and practising celebacy.\" The Bhārata also says, \"He is the lord of all\n✓\ncreation and the lord of (human) greatness.\" Vatulas'udha (Agama) speaks of Maheśvara as the aggregate of twentyfive Tattvas.","dev":"माहेश्वरी","iast":"māheśvarī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":135,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":52},{"n":209,"gloss":"Mahādevī","body":"Great,\nPr. says,\nwhose body is immeasurable. The Devī Her body is immense and cannot be computed by any measurements, the root Maha, means worship; hence she is termed Mahādevī.\"\nOr, Śiva in his eighth form, viz., that of the moon is known as Mahādeva; his wife is Rohiṇī, his son is mercury (Budha). The Liṅga and Vāyu Prs. corroborate the\nsame.\nAccording to the Padma Pr. Mahādevī is the presiding deity at the Chakratirtha on the Gandaki river.","dev":"महादेवी","iast":"mahādevī","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"देवी","iast":"devī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":136,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":52},{"n":210,"gloss":"Mahālakṣmī. She is the presiding deity at the Karavīra","body":"according to the (Padma Pr.).\nOr, Maha, great, Lakṣmī, the wife of Viṣṇu.\nAccording to the Mailāra Tr. Mahala is the name of a daitya whom she destroyed (sya and kshapa to kill), hence she is called Mahalasa and Mahālakṣmī, residing on the shore of the western sea at the foot of the Sahya Mountain.\" The Śiva Pr. concerning Śiva says, \"The beautiful, azure (Śyāmā), allfascinating, Supreme Śakti seated on the lap of Śiva is called Mahālakṣmī.\" The Ayushyasukta says, \"Sri Lakṣmī .. Lakṣmī is called Pārvatī. The Mark. Pr. also says, 66 Mahālakṣmī is the origin of everything manifested as the three guṇas.\" Again according to Dhaumya (Smṛti) a girl of thirteen years of age is called Mahālakṣmī.","dev":"महालक्ष्मी","iast":"mahālakṣmī","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"लक्ष्मी","iast":"lakṣmī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":136,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":52},{"n":211,"gloss":"Beloved of Mṛḍa","body":"Mṛḍa, happiness; Śiva in his Sāttvic qualities called Mṛḍa. The Mahimmastava says, \"I bow down to Mridu who predominates in Sāttvic nature for the sake of confering happiness on men.\"","dev":"र्मृडप्रिया","iast":"rmṛḍapriyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.923,"cpage":136,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":52},{"n":212,"gloss":"Great shape","body":"(53)\nGreat, supreme form, i.e., supreme over the other four forms. The Viṣṇu Pr. (I-II) says, Purusha, O twice born, is the first form of the supreme Brahman, then the manifested (five gross elements) and unmanifested (Pradhana) and lastly time. The enlightened see the supreme abode of Viṣṇu which transcends these (four) Pradhina, Purusha, Vyakta, and time. These four forms, Pradhina, etc., in their due proportions are the causes of the production of the phenomena of creation, preservation and destruction.\"","dev":"महारूपा","iast":"mahārūpā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":136,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":53},{"n":213,"gloss":"Mighty object of worship","body":"That is, worshipped even by Śiva and others. Padma and De. Bhag. Prs. say, (1) Śiva, (2) Brahmā, (3) Viṣṇu, (4) Kubera, (5) Viśvedevas, (6) Vāyu, (7) Vasu, (8) Varuṇa, (9) Agni, (10) Sakra, (11) Sūrya, (12) Soma, (13) Graha (planets), (14) the Rākṣasas, (15) Piśācas, (16) Matriganas, etc., worship respectively by means of (1) Mantra, (2) stone, (3) blue stone, (4) gold, (5) silver, (6) copper, (7) brass, (8) crystal, (9) gems, (10) pearls, (11) coral, (12) lapis, (13) lazuli, (14) tin, (15) adamant, and (16) iron, etc. (According to some) Agni, Śukra, and Sūrya worship only idols made of\ngems.","dev":"महापूज्या","iast":"mahāpūjyā","pada":[{"dev":"मह","iast":"maha"},{"dev":"अपूज्या","iast":"apūjyā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":137,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":53},{"n":214,"gloss":"Destroying great sins","body":"The Brahmāṇḍa Pr. says,\nall sin whether committed knowingly or unknowingly is The highest expiation of brought about by the remembrance of the feet of the supreme Śakti.\" The Brahmottara Khaṇḍa also says, \"Briefly, I will tell you in half a verse that the worship of Śiva destroys the crime of even a hundred murders (lit. of Brahmins)\"; according to the Artharatnavali, great sins, the killing of heroes. In the Saubhagyaratnakara and other works, in the chapter on expiation the rules for repeating the Panchadasinantra according to the nature of the crime are explained.","dev":"महापातकनाशिनी","iast":"mahāpātakanāśinī","pada":[{"dev":"महापातक","iast":"mahāpātaka"},{"dev":"नाशिनी","iast":"nāśinī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":137,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":53},{"n":215,"gloss":"The great illusion","body":"Because she causes confusion to Brahmā, etc. The Mark. Pr. says, \"That divine Devī, Mahāmāyā, forcibly draws away the minds of even the sages and leads (them) into confusion.\" The Kālikā Pr. also says, She who always makes him devoid of knowledge, the being who possessed knowledge (of his real, nature) whilst in the womb, compelled him to take birth by means of the winds of delivery and leads him by reason of the Samskara of previous births to desire of food, etc., thence into confusion egotism, doubt, subsequently compelling him again and again to undergo (the stages of) anger, distress, and greediness and then leads him into (sensual) desire and causing anxiety day and night, producing sometimes pleasure and sometimes pain, is called the Mahāmāyā (great illusion); hence she is the ruler of the Universe\".\nOr according to one lexicọn Māya means pride and compassion; then Mahimaya means full of compassion.","dev":"महामाया","iast":"mahāmāyā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"माया","iast":"māyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":137,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":53},{"n":216,"gloss":"The great reality","body":"Sattva, existence, strength, quality and being. The Viśva says, Sattva means, quality of friends, etc., strength, things and reality.\"","dev":"महासत्वा","iast":"mahāsatvā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"सत्वा","iast":"satvā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":138,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":53},{"n":217,"gloss":"The great energy","body":"Because she is the energy of the whole universe.\nYadava says, Śakti, power, strength, strife, weapon.\" The Viṣṇu Pr. says, Just as the brightness of a fire is spread abroad, so the energy of the supreme Brahman (spreads throughout) the whole world; just as the brightness is greater or less according to the distance (of the fire) so it is with this energy, O Maitreya.\"","dev":"महाशक्ति","iast":"mahāśakti","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.909,"cpage":138,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":53},{"n":218,"gloss":"The great delight","body":"things.\n(54)\nBecause sages find more delight in her than in worldly Or Mahārati, the wife of Mahakama.","dev":"महारतिः","iast":"mahāratiḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":138,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":53},{"n":219,"gloss":"The great extension","body":"Extension, i.e., of the earth, etc. Or, Bhoga joy, wealth, etc.\n220, The great ruler, (Mahesvarya.)","dev":"महाभोगा","iast":"mahābhogā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"भोगा","iast":"bhogā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":138,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":54},{"n":220,"gloss":"The great extension. (Mahābhogā.)","body":"Extension, i.e., of the earth, etc. Or, Bhoga joy, wealth, etc.\n220, The great ruler, (Mahesvarya.)","dev":"महेश्वर्या","iast":"maheśvaryā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":138,"sure":0,"host":219,"v":54},{"n":221,"gloss":"The great strength","body":"Strength: according to the Viśva, riya means semen, might, glory, and strength.\"","dev":"महावीर्या","iast":"mahāvīryā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"वीर्या","iast":"vīryā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":139,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":54},{"n":222,"gloss":"The great might","body":"The Viśva says, Bālā, small, taste, colour, soul, corpulence, army, Balarama, one of the daityas, Mahabali, and a crow.\" If Bālā means a crow, Bhuśuṇḍa, etc., (crows) became great by the grace of Devī; there is in the Yogavāsiṣṭha an address from Bhuśuṇḍa. We are twenty. one crows, brothers, sons of Caṇḍa; by us, together with our sisters, the great Devī was worshipped for a long time; after finishing her contemplation Devī herself wishing to bless us favoured us; thus we were liberated.\"\n13th verse divides 40 names.","dev":"महाबला","iast":"mahābalā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"बला","iast":"balā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":139,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":54},{"n":223,"gloss":"The great intelligence","body":"When the intelligence is directed towards her nothing remains to be known. The Śruti (Chānd. Up., 6-1) says, “Which, when known, the whole universe is known.\" Or, from whom one obtains the highest (Mahat) intelligence.","dev":"महाबुद्धि","iast":"mahābuddhi","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"बुद्धि","iast":"buddhi"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":139,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":54},{"n":224,"gloss":"The great attainment","body":"This is to be explained in two ways, as in the preceding instance. The Siddhis are the wellknown superhuman powers, viz., animā, etc. Other Siddhis are also explained in the Skanda Pr. : The first Siddhi is the manifestation of taste (when no object is tasted); the second is the overcoming of the pairs of opposites; the third is the being both superior and inferior (i.c., he perceives no difference of degree); the fourth is the indifference towards the conditions of pleasure, pain, and life; the fifth called without sorrow (Visoka) is illuminator of body; the sixth is steadfastness in penance and contemplation of the Supreme Self; the seventh is the power of unrestrained motion (through space); and the eighth is the power of suspension (¡.e., uniform equilibrium).","dev":"र्महासिद्धि","iast":"rmahāsiddhi","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.909,"cpage":139,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":54},{"n":225,"gloss":"The ruler of the great rulers, vari.)","body":"","dev":"र्महायोगीश्वरेश्वरी","iast":"rmahāyogīśvareśvarī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.842,"cpage":139,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":54},{"n":226,"gloss":"The great Tantra","body":"Great because they confer many results. The Tantras are Kulārṇava, Jninarnava and others.","dev":"महातन्त्रा","iast":"mahātantrā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"तन्त्रा","iast":"tantrā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":140,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":55},{"n":227,"gloss":"The great Mantra","body":"The Mantras are Bālā, Bagalā and others.","dev":"महामन्त्रा","iast":"mahāmantrā","pada":[{"dev":"मह","iast":"maha"},{"dev":"आमन्त्रा","iast":"āmantrā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":140,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":55},{"n":228,"gloss":"The great Yantra","body":"The Yantras (magical figures) are Půjachakra, Padmachakra, Amritaghala, Merulinga, etc.\nOr, Tantra (No. 226), the Svatantra Tr. Mantra (No. 227) Śrīvidyā; and Yantra Siddhavajra; these (Tantra, etc.) are the best (Mahat) belonging to Devī. The Svatantra Tr. is so called because it is dependent on no other. It is said in that, “O great one, all Tantras formerly described by you, only create mental confusion since they depend on one another; therefore, O Lord, relate (to me) my independent Tantra.\" Thus asked, \"Listen, I will tell you the Kādimata (Svatantra) Tantra, which is complete and depends upon no other, to be concealed with all care; the concealment is enjoined in the Tantras.\" The Sau. La. (of Ācārya) also says (Slo. 31): “The Lord of beings stood after promulgating sixty four Tantras, each of which confer some Siddhi; then again by your intentness your Tantra which is independently capable of conferring all objects of desire, was brought to this world.”\nConcerning mantra (227) : Śrīvidyā is the best of mantras, etc.,\" and supported by a thousand other sayings which can be found in the Kulārṇava and the Śaktirahasya. The Nityā Tr. says regarding the Yantra, Siddhavara, \"He who regards as equal to the other (Vidyās) with the Vidyā of Lalitā, also this mantra with other mantras, and this yantra with other yantras--that man is only bewildered in mind.”","dev":"महायन्त्रा","iast":"mahāyantrā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"यन्त्रा","iast":"yantrā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":140,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":55},{"n":229,"gloss":"The great seat","body":"The seat, the thirtysix tattvas beginning with the earth which are her seat. The De. Bhag. Pr. 66 says, The divine one remains dwelling in all Tattvas,\"\n16*","dev":"महासना","iast":"mahāsanā","pada":[{"dev":"मह","iast":"maha"},{"dev":"आसना","iast":"āsanā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":140,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":55},{"n":230,"gloss":"Worshipped by the method of Mahāyāga","body":"Mahiyaga is the worship of sixtyfour yoginis from Akṣobhya, who are the part (amsa) of Brahmi, etc.; other rites give some results (this gives a quicker result). The Śāśvata (a lexicon) says, Krama, energy, arrangement, moving and shaking.”\nOr, Mahāyāga, explained in the Bhavanopanishad (NG 84) is most secret and is to be accomplished by Sivayogins alone. This method is given in detail in the commentary and in the Prayogavidhi compiled by us.","dev":"महायागक्रमाराध्या","iast":"mahāyāgakramārādhyā","pada":[{"dev":"मह","iast":"maha"},{"dev":"आयाग","iast":"āyāga"},{"dev":"क्रम","iast":"krama"},{"dev":"आराध्या","iast":"ārādhyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":141,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":55},{"n":231,"gloss":"Worshipped by Mahabhairava. vapujiti.) (Mahabhaira- Bhairava is Paramaśiva, the accomplisher of creation, preservation and destruction (of the universe). Each letter in the word Bhairava has a meaning; Bha, creation (Bharana)","body":"Ra, preservation (Ramaṇa) and va, destruction (vāmana) lit. to vomit. The Padma Pr. says, Śambhu, the origin of the Universe, worships the beneficent Devī who is the Śakti of the Mantras, holding the rosary in his hand, by means of Nyāsa.\" The Lalitopākhyāna says, \"The Lord Mahasambhu, by a great sacrifice caused Lalitā to become manifest from the alter of the fire of consciousness.\"","dev":"महाभैरवपूजिता","iast":"mahābhairavapūjitā","pada":[{"dev":"महाभैरव","iast":"mahābhairava"},{"dev":"पूजिता","iast":"pūjitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.462,"cpage":141,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":55},{"n":232,"gloss":"The witness to the great dance of Maheś'vara in (56) the great cycle","body":"Mahūkalpa, the great dissolution (Pralaya); Great dance is caused by the fact that the Self alone remaining and having become bliss after the universe has been drawn into it; as, at that time, there is no other beside herself, she is the witness. The Pañcadaś'Istava says, form alone is excellent, having the noose, elephant hook, bow of sugarcane, and the arrow of flowers (vide names 8-11 for the meaning of these), and witnessing the dance of the axebearing Parabhairava (Śiva in his aspect of\nYour\nB\ndestruction) started at the time of his drawing the universe (into himself).\" The De. Bhag. Pr. also says, This (Devī) at the dissolution (of the Universe), having drawn the universe into herself, sports, having absorbed the souls (liṅga) of all beings in her own body.\" The Vasiṣṭha Book VI, 2nd part, 81 chap. by more than hundred verses after describing the wonderful and terrible dance of these two (Śiva and Devī) concludes : 'Having a garland on his breast, having the wings of an eagle as his headornament, filled with red liquor, holding in his hand the great horn of the buffalo of Yama (Death), dancing to the sounds of his music, such as, Dhimba, Dimba, Sudimba, Pacha, Pacha, Jhamya, Jhamya, Prajhamya, may Bhairava with his (spouse) called Kālarātrī, who delights in dissolution, worshipped, protect you.”","dev":"महेश्वरमहाकल्पमहाताण्डवसाक्षिणी","iast":"maheśvaramahākalpamahātāṇḍavasākṣiṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0.34,"fit":1,"cpage":141,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":56},{"n":233,"gloss":"The wife of Mahākāmes'vara. mahishi.)","body":"(Mahākāmesa-","dev":"महाकामेशमहिषी","iast":"mahākāmeśamahiṣī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.545,"cpage":142,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":56},{"n":234,"gloss":"The great Tripurasundarī","body":"Tripurā, city of the three, i.e., the measurer, the measuring, and the thing measured.","dev":"महात्रिपुरसुन्दरी","iast":"mahātripurasundarī","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"त्रिपुरसुन्दरी","iast":"tripurasundarī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":142,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":56},{"n":235,"gloss":"To be adored by sixtyfour ceremonies. (Chatush- (57)","body":"shashtyupacharadhya.)\nThese are enumerated by the holy Parusurăma in his Kalpasūtras (IV, 5). In other Tantras eight more (ceremonies) are given. Wearing the flowers (after dedication), (2) making religious endowments, (3) and (4) worshipping the guru and the devotees, (5) worshipping the Śaiva books, (6) sacrificing in the fire of Śiva, (7) receiving water which has washed the feet of Śiva, and (8) performing the full ceremony called Pranignihotra.\" All these seventytwo are explained in our work (Vari. Rahasya) in the Paja chapter.","dev":"चतुःषष्ट्युपचाराढ्या","iast":"catuḥṣaṣṭyupacārāḍhyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.4,"cpage":142,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":57},{"n":236,"gloss":"Sixtyfour sciences","body":"These are differently enumerated in the Sarngadhariya, Sridhara's Katha Kośa and in the Lakshmipithika and these are set forth by us: (1) The knowledge of the eighteen\nletters (Lipi), (2) and (3) the power of writing and reading these quickly, (4) knowledge of different languages, (5) composing verses in them, (6) gambling, (7) to (14) the four Vedas and the four auxiliary Vedas, (15) to (26) the twice six auxiliary sciences, (27) Tantra, (28) Purāṇa, (29) Smṛti, (30) poetry, (31) rhetoric, (32) drama, (33) to (38) the six, pacifying, controlling, attracting, enmity, ruining by magical practices, and killing, (39) to (45) the art of opposing the effects of motion, water, sight, fire, weapons, speech, and semen, (46) to (49) training elephants, horses, chariots, and men, (50) to (53) the knowledge of divination by bodily marks (Samudrika), gymnastics, cooking, and power over snakes (Garudavidya), (54) Suṣira, (55) Anadha, (56) Ghana, (57) creating illusion (Indrajāla), (58) dancing, (59) singing, (60) alchemy, (61) knowledge of gems, (62) thiev ing, (63) knowledge of the pulse, and (64) disappearance, thus laid down by the poet Bhāskara the sixtyfour sciences after examining (them).\"\nOr, Kāla, works, the sixtyfour of them are enumerated in the Vāmakeś'vara Tr.","dev":"चतुःषष्टिकलामयी","iast":"catuḥṣaṣṭikalāmayī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.941,"cpage":142,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":57},{"n":237,"gloss":"Attended by the great sixtyfour crores of bands of yoginis","body":"The Brahmi and other seven divinities, each has eight Śaktis, Akṣobhya, etc., who are the parts of them; hence there are sixtyfour. Each has a crore of bands. Really Speaking these sixtyfour crores mean according to the Tantrarāja different Śaktis; \"In Lalitā's nine cakras, O beloved one, there are many Śaktis, numbering to 64\ncrores.\"\n(58)\nThe Great means nine times multiplied. Amongst the nine cakras, from Trailokya Mohana, in each cakra separately there are 64 crores of yogins dwelling. The total number comes to 5 abja, 7 arbuda and 6 crores of Śaktis.","dev":"महाचतुःषष्टिकोटियोगिनीगणसेविता","iast":"mahācatuḥṣaṣṭikoṭiyoginīgaṇasevitā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"चतुःषष्टि","iast":"catuḥṣaṣṭi"},{"dev":"कोटि","iast":"koṭi"},{"dev":"योगिनी","iast":"yoginī"},{"dev":"गण","iast":"gaṇa"},{"dev":"सेविता","iast":"sevitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.97,"cpage":143,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":57},{"n":238,"gloss":"Manuvidya. The MS. has a different enumeration of the f4 Kalās. The Śrīvidyā is of twelve kinds differentiated according to the twelve devotees, Manu, Candra, Kubera, Lopāmudra, Manmatha, Agastya, Agni, Sarya, Indra, Skanda, Śiva and Krodhabhatṭāraka","body":"These are the devotees of Devī.\" The description of each of these (mantras) is given in the Jñānārṇava.","dev":"मनुविद्या","iast":"manuvidyā","pada":[{"dev":"मनु","iast":"manu"},{"dev":"विद्या","iast":"vidyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":143,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":239,"gloss":"Chandravidyā","body":"Chandravidyā.","dev":"चन्द्रविद्या","iast":"candravidyā","pada":[{"dev":"चन्द्र","iast":"candra"},{"dev":"विद्या","iast":"vidyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":144,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":240,"gloss":"Residing in the centre of the moon's disc","body":"Because Kundalini breaks through the moon in the pericarp of the thousandpetalled lotus.\nOr, in the evening prayer, the ceremony to obtain long life, etc., Devī should be contemplated in the moon's disc. In the Śiva Pr. Śiva says to Devī, \"I am residing in the head of fire, you are residing in the head of the moon ; thus the world consisting of fire and moon is upheld by The secret meaning is the moon's disc is the Śrīcakra itself.\nus.","dev":"चन्द्रमण्डलमध्यगा","iast":"candramaṇḍalamadhyagā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":144,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":241,"gloss":"Exquisite beauty","body":"","dev":"चारुरूपा","iast":"cārurūpā","pada":[{"dev":"चारु","iast":"cāru"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":144,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":242,"gloss":"With beautiful smile","body":"The moon is thy sweet smile.\" This saying means there is a certain stage of consciousness (Prabodha) which gives the highest bliss, and which should be known from a Guru alone; thus a (commentator) explains (this name).","dev":"चारुहासा","iast":"cāruhāsā","pada":[{"dev":"चारु","iast":"cāru"},{"dev":"हासा","iast":"hāsā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":144,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":243,"gloss":"Wearing a beautiful crescent moon","body":"Caru, neither waxing nor waning, Candrakalā, is the everlasting Kāla (Sadakhyā), in the form of consciousness. Or, Candrakalā is the daughter of a king. To her in a dream Devī appeared and told her to marry a prince by name Sudarśana who was worshipping Devī (Kāmarājabija). According to that story the meaning is, Chāru, rightly, dharā supports. (Vide De. Bhåg Pr., Book III.)","dev":"चारुचन्द्रकलाधरा ।। ।।","iast":"cārucandrakalādharā  ","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":144,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":244,"gloss":"Ruler of animate and inanimate","body":"(59) worlds.","dev":"चराचरजगन्नाथा","iast":"carācarajagannāthā","pada":[{"dev":"चराचर","iast":"carācara"},{"dev":"जगन्नाथा","iast":"jagannāthā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":144,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":245,"gloss":"Abiding in the Śrīcakra","body":"The nine cakras beginning with Trailokyamohana are the only dwelling place of Devt.","dev":"चक्रराजनिकेतना","iast":"cakrarājaniketanā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":145,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":246,"gloss":"Pārvatī. The daughter of Mount Himavat","body":"Pārvatī.\nThe daughter of Mount Himavat.","dev":"पार्वती","iast":"pārvatī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":145,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":247,"gloss":"Lotus eyed","body":"","dev":"पद्मनयना","iast":"padmanayanā","pada":[{"dev":"पद्म","iast":"padma"},{"dev":"नयना","iast":"nayanā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":145,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":248,"gloss":"Shining like the ruby","body":"For\nOr, Padma, lotus, rāga, red, and prabhā, co!our. Kalyanacharana says, Protect, O Kundalini, whose colour is like the paste of saffron.\" Amṛta Kundalini also.\nHence this name refers to\n(60)","dev":"पद्मरागसमप्रभा","iast":"padmarāgasamaprabhā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":145,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":58},{"n":249,"gloss":"Seated on the seat (formed) of five corpses","body":"The five Brahmā, etc., when deprived of their respective Śaktis such as Vamā, etc., are incapable of action; hence deprived of their power, they become corpses. Seated, couch formed by these five. The Jnānarnava says, \"O Great Īśāna, tell me of the five corpses and their cause, how are they imperishable and eternal and yet lifeless ? Thus questioned by Devī, the Lord said, You have fitly questioned me, O Beneficent one, as to the seat formed of five corpses. Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Rudra, İsvara and Sadāśiva, these, O noble one, are the five corpses, they are always motionless. The activity of Brahmā manifested as creation should be known as Vāmā Śakti (devoid of it), Brahmā is certainly a corpse. (in himself) has no activity. Wherefore activity belongs to Śakti Pure Sadāśiva becomes a motionless corpse, O Dear one, without Śakti, O Devī, he is incapable of doing anything.\"\nŚiva","dev":"पञ्चप्रेतासनासीना","iast":"pañcapretāsanāsīnā","pada":[{"dev":"पञ्च","iast":"pañca"},{"dev":"प्रेत","iast":"preta"},{"dev":"आसन्","iast":"āsan"},{"dev":"आसीना","iast":"āsīnā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":145,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":59},{"n":250,"gloss":"The five Brahmans are her form","body":"śiva.\nThe five begin with Brahmā and end with Sadā- These are included under Brahman; because the\nTripurasiddhānta says, The unconditioned Brahman by the play of the Māyā in itself (becomes), Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Rudra, Īśvara, and Sadāśiva. Thus by name only Brahman is in five forms.\"\nOr, the five Brahmis are Īśāna, Tatpurusha, Aghora, Vāmadeva and Sadyojāta. The Liṅga Pr. says, kriti, Buddhi, Ahaṃkāra, and manas; the senses, viz., ear, Jīva, Praskin, eyes, tongue, and nose; and the subtle elements are the forms of the five Brahman.\" It is described in the same book that the above (five subtle elements) are the causes creating the five gross elements. These are her forms. 66 The one Śiva,\nThe St. Sam. (IV, 14, 2) says, characterised by existence, knowledge, etc., without change, pure, becomes five by his own Śakti.” Becomes five, i.e., by the Śaktis of creation, preservation, etc., he becomes Sadyojāta, etc. The Garuḍa Pr. also says, 46 Viṣṇu who confers blessings on the world and destroys all the wicked, although one, becomes five, viz., Vāsudeva, Sankarshana, Pradyumna, Aniruddha, and Nārāyaṇa.\" Ācārya also says, \"When you sport as a man (you become) the five persons; your casual talk (becomes) the three Vedas; O mother, a glance of your eyes, (becomes) the sun; and your sweet smile, the moon.\n251.","dev":"पञ्चब्रह्मस्वरूपिणी","iast":"pañcabrahmasvarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"पञ्च","iast":"pañca"},{"dev":"ब्रह्म","iast":"brahma"},{"dev":"स्वरूपिणी","iast":"svarūpiṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":145,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":59},{"n":251,"gloss":"The five Brahmans are her form. (Panchabrahmasvarūpini.)","body":"śiva.\nThe five begin with Brahmā and end with Sadā- These are included under Brahman; because the\nTripurasiddhānta says, The unconditioned Brahman by the play of the Māyā in itself (becomes), Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Rudra, Īśvara, and Sadāśiva. Thus by name only Brahman is in five forms.\"\nOr, the five Brahmis are Īśāna, Tatpurusha, Aghora, Vāmadeva and Sadyojāta. The Liṅga Pr. says, kriti, Buddhi, Ahaṃkāra, and manas; the senses, viz., ear, Jīva, Praskin, eyes, tongue, and nose; and the subtle elements are the forms of the five Brahman.\" It is described in the same book that the above (five subtle elements) are the causes creating the five gross elements. These are her forms. 66 The one Śiva,\nThe St. Sam. (IV, 14, 2) says, characterised by existence, knowledge, etc., without change, pure, becomes five by his own Śakti.” Becomes five, i.e., by the Śaktis of creation, preservation, etc., he becomes Sadyojāta, etc. The Garuḍa Pr. also says, 46 Viṣṇu who confers blessings on the world and destroys all the wicked, although one, becomes five, viz., Vāsudeva, Sankarshana, Pradyumna, Aniruddha, and Nārāyaṇa.\" Ācārya also says, \"When you sport as a man (you become) the five persons; your casual talk (becomes) the three Vedas; O mother, a glance of your eyes, (becomes) the sun; and your sweet smile, the moon.\n251.","dev":"चिन्मयी","iast":"cinmayī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":145,"sure":0,"host":250,"v":59},{"n":252,"gloss":"Consciousness itself. (Chinmayi.)","body":"Supreme bliss. (Paramanandı.) The Śruti (Chānd. Up., VII, 23), that is happiness.\"\nWhat is infinite,","dev":"परमानन्दा","iast":"paramānandā","pada":[{"dev":"परम्","iast":"param"},{"dev":"आनन्दा","iast":"ānandā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.222,"cpage":146,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":59},{"n":253,"gloss":"Consisting of permanent intelligence","body":"Vijñāna here means Caitanya, ghana one essence, i.e., one essence of consciousness. Thus in the Śruti (Bri. Up., II, 4, 12), the word vijnānaghana is explained. Or, vijñāna, jīva, because in the Śruti (Bri. Up. III, 4, 2)\nRemaining in vijñāna is explained as jīva. Ghana, mass, i.., aggregate of jīvas. Hiraṇyagarbha is the synthesis of the aggregate of the Jīvas. Where in the Śruti\n(Prs. Up., 5, 5), the word \"Jivaghana occurs, it is (61) explained thus.","dev":"विज्ञानघनरूपिणी","iast":"vijñānaghanarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"विज्ञानघन","iast":"vijñānaghana"},{"dev":"रूपिणी","iast":"rūpiṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":146,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":59},{"n":254,"gloss":"She is meditation, meditator, and the object of meditation","body":"The root Dhyai to think, also called measurement (mina) as explained in the Yoga Sūtra (III, 2), Contemplation is unison there of the understanding,\" i.e., she is the triad, of knowledge, of the knower and of the object of knowledge.","dev":"ध्यानध्यातृध्येय रूपा","iast":"dhyānadhyātṛdhyeya rūpā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":147,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":60},{"n":255,"gloss":"Devoid of virtue and vice. vivarjitā). (Dharmadharma Virtue and vice. Actions leading to desirable and undesirable results. It is said in the Matsya Pr., Dharma comes from the root Dharana (Dhri)","body":"and ma, in greatness, so, as it bears and as it is a great one, it is called Dharm :.\" Thus the word Dharma is explained, \"That which leads to a desirable result, and taught by teachers as Dharma, and Adharma is the opposite of this.\" The Saṃvarta Smṛti says, That conduct which prevails in each country in accordance with tradition and not contrary to the Scriptures, is called Dharma.\" Yājnavalka (I . . .) also says, (Among the following actions) sacrifice, good conduct, selfcontrol, noninjury, gift, and sacred study, the supreme dharma is that in which one perceives the Self by Yoga.\" Jaimini also (Sūtra, I, I, 2) Dharma is a purpose as characterised by the scriptural injunctions.\" Adharma is to be understood as the opposite. Devī is free from these two because the Scriptures are meant only for the ignorant and the Devas are not subject to them according to the rules applicable to the animal creation,' (Tiryagadhikarananyaya.)\nOr Dharma, bondage, and adharma, liberation, because these words are explained thus in a verse of the Nityā Tr.; She is free from these. The Tripurā Up. says, \"No end, no origin, no bondage, no aspirant, no desire of liberation, no liberation, that is the truth.\"\nOr, Dharma, the thing which possesses quality; and adharma is the quality itself; She neither possesses the quality nor is She the quality itself, i.e., She is absolutely inseparable from the universe.\nOr, Dharma indicates the letter of Śakti, and adharma that of Śiva; Vivarjita, great increase. The meaning is that Devī is the tendril of Pañcadaś (vidyā).\nThe two letters (of Śakti and Śiva) are explained in the Nityāhṛdaya and should be learned from the teacher and we have explained them in our Varivasyārahasya.","dev":"धर्माधर्मविवर्जिता","iast":"dharmādharmavivarjitā","pada":[{"dev":"धर्माधर्म","iast":"dharmādharma"},{"dev":"विवर्जिता","iast":"vivarjitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.4,"cpage":147,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":60},{"n":256,"gloss":"Omnipresent","body":"By nineteen names indicating the difference between Jīva and Īśvara, the text proceeds to praise Devī under both forms.\nIn the order of creation, the first manifestation is darkness (Tamas), the next is that of mabat, then that of the threefold egotism (ahankära), next the five subtle elements beginning with sound; among these are the five energies of knowledge (jñāna Śakti) and the five energies of action (Kriyā); in these energies the former five individually generate the five senses (jnanendriyas) and collectively the Antaḥkaraṇa. The latter five generate\nindividually the five organs of action and collectively the Prāṇas. The sound and the others --four subtle forces generate the five gross elements. This is an established doctrine. There the Caitanya manifested through the gross, subtle, and casual vehicles respectively termed, Viśva, Taijasa, and Prājña; these manifested ones (three groups of vehicles) respectively are termed Vaisvānara, Hiraṇyagarbha, and Īśvara. The Supreme Being (Paramatman) manifested in the Antaḥkaraṇa in its casual form is Hiraṇyagarbha; in Prāṇa in its casual form is Saträtman ; when manifested through these two vehicles without differentiation it is Antaryamin. These three alone are termed respectively Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Radra. We\n17*\nhave here explained the three divisions, viz., the individual jīva, (Viśva, Taijasa and Prājña), the collective jīva, (Vais'vänara, Hiraṇyagarbha and Sûträtman), and Paramātman, according to the opinion of those who follow the Upaniṣads. In this theory they accept only three states, viz., waking, dreaming, and sleeping, and three functions, viz., creation, preservation and destruction. In the Tāntric theory the followers of it accept in addition two more states, viz., ecstatic (Turīya) and the state beyond ecstasy, and two functions, viz., Turodhana and Anugraha (for meaning of these two refer names 270 and 273). Those beings endowed with these two states, i.., fourth and the one beyond it and their functions, viz., jīva and Īśvara respectively should also be added. Thus there are\nfive divisions.\nHere there is no contradictory theory as between those who hold that there are three or five causes' (of creation) because this system proceeds from the distinction between the gross and subtle aspects. Since in the sleeping state the Prājña (Jīva is in that state called so) becomes Brahman, he is no longer Jīva, but the Jīva is then not the Supreme One (Paramātman), because it is associated with a trace of avidyā. Hence because the vehicle of the Jīva in the sleeping state is Kāraṇa, the vehicle of the jīva in the fourth is declared to be Mahākarana. Beyond that (i.e., in fifth state) as the Śaiva Sūtra (III, 26) says, One above it becomes equal to Śiva.\" That is Semiequal to Śiva. Beyond that as the Śruti (Mund. Up., III, 1, 3) says, “The spotless one attains the highest equality.' \"\" Here the meaning of highest equality is unity. From this explanation it follows that owing to the absence in those two states (4th and 5th) of the full perfection of jīva or of Śiva, they are not separately enumerated by the followers of the Upaniṣads. Similarly the same reason is given for not taking into account the state of swoon in that work. (Ved. 'Vide Vedānta Sūtra, II, 3, 1 to 12,\nSatra, III, 2, 13), In him who is unconscious (in a swoon, etc.), there is a half union; on account of this remaining.\"\nThe five vehicles of Jīva may be recognised as the five sheaths (Kos'as); the Tirodhāna and Anugraha functions of Is'vara and Sadāśiva are otherwise called the producers of bondage and release. These two states beyond Rudra (4th or 5th) are not wrongly stated since they are supported by the Śaiva Scriptures. “İsvara the visible one (Bahih uniesha may mean manifested clearly) and Sadisiva is momentary (Ni meshontah, uninanifested or partly manifested).\"\nViśvarūpa: Visca, the jīvas in the waking state manifested through the gross elements; and Vaisvānara is he who is the aggregate of the Jīvas. Rūpu, form, i.”., these two are her forms.\nOr, by the preceding name (255) is meant that she is inseparable from the Universe: hence this name expresses that Devī is in the form (Rūpa) of the Universe (Viśva); the meaning is that her form is the Universe itself and she has no other forin except this as the supporter of the universe. The Viṣṇu Pr. says, Just as the plantain tree cannot be seen independently of hark and leaves, likewise the universe, O Lord, is seen, depending on you only.\" The De. Bhag. Pr. also in the first book mentions, “I myself am the universe and there is no other permanency.”\nOr, Vi, separate, S'vurūpu, a dog's form the evolution of human being (jīvabhava) of Brahman directly is the lowest kind of form, comparable to that of a dog; so, inferior service is called Svavritti (dog's service; sec Manu Sinr.); the lower human existence (Pasubhāra) is called Svarūpa (a dog's form of the devotees); Vi, released by means of Devī's grace.\nOr, Tripurasundarī has sixteen parts, of this there is no doubt; this is explained in the Vasanasubhagodaya.\n**There are fifteen parts (Kalās) from the Darsa, to Purnima (the names of the days of bright fortnights), the Sixteenth part is to be known as existenceknowledgebliss (Satchit-ānanda). In the moon's disc there is one Kāla (the sixteenth) called sadā (eternal) devoid of growth or decay. The other fifteen are moving to and fro, ¡.., waxing and waning. Though Devī is not separate from any of them, the Kală called cit (the sixteenth) is termed Tripurasundarī: the others are termed respectively from Kâmes'varī to Chitra, according to the days of the fortnights. In the Pañcadaśī Mantra also which is inseparable from these, there is one syllable which is to be known from the teacher which is of the form of cit and by reason of its being contained in the Pañcadaśī is known as Srividyä. The fifteen syllables known to all the devotees are of the form of Nilyās; thus, the moon's disc, Devī, and the fifteensyllabled Mantra are one. The fifteen syllables belonging to the fifteen Kalās also become one. Hence there are three divisions of fifteen days and of the fifteen syllables, as the three divisions (kūtas) of days, riz., Nandā, Bhadrā, Jaya, Riktā, and Pūrnă, corresponding to the Vāgbhava group (of the Panchalasi Mantra). Hence the followers of Taittariya Sakha repeat the means of the nights of the bright fortnight in three divisions. The Śruti (Tai. Bra. 3-10-1) says, “(1) Darsă, (2) Drashtã, (3) Darsată, (4) Visvarupā, and (5) Sudarsanā: (6) Pyāyamānā, (7) Āpyāyamānā, (8) Pyāyā, (9) Sûnrită, (10) Irâ ; and (11) Apūryamāṇā, (12) Pāryamānā, (13) Pūrayanti, (14) Pārnā, and (15) Parnamisi.\" Thus they repeat the names of the days (of the bright fortnight) also in the three divisions. The Śruti (Tai. Bra. 3-10-1, “ (1) Samjnāna, (2) Vijñāna, (3) Prajnāna, (4) Jānad, and (5) Abhijānad ; (6) Sankalpamāna, (7) Prakalpamāna, (8) Upakalpamāna, (9) Upaklipta, and (10) Klipta ; and (11) Sreya, (12) Vasiya, (13) Ayat, (14) Sambhata and (15) Bhūta.\" The Śruti itself verifies the explanation of day and night of (bright fortnight) of these\ntwo sections (anuvākas) above quoted. The Śruti (Tai. Bra., 3-10-10) says, \"These two sections, Samjnāna, Vijñāna, and Darsā, Drash/ã, are the names of the days and nights of bright fortnights.\" Though the three divisions are here proportioned equally (.., five in each), yet when the 10th day is merged into the eleventh the latter is treated as part of the tenth; as it is said, 66 The pure twelfth day is to be observed intact.\" Consequently the twelfth day takes the eleventh. So in the mantra (of Pañcadaśī) the second division is composed of six letters, whereas the last one has four letters. But as the others (seers) make no such exception (ledha), in the other mantras of Sūrya and others (of Pañcadaśī), the three divisions are equally proportioned. With this idea Lolla says in his commentary on the Subhagodaya, Though the Kāla called Apūryamāṇā (11th one) which belongs to the last group, Candra Khaṇḍa, (i.e., Agni, Sūrya and Candra are respectively the deities of the three divisions of the mantra) yet it is included in the second division (Suryakhanda), and it should be taken that the Kalās, Irā and Āpûryamānā (10) and 11th) are one and the same as the latter is a part of the former.\" As to the fifteen deities (of days and nights) and the manners of worship as well as the use and to the letters for the fifteen syallabled mantra, and as application to Śiva, Śakti, Māyā, Śuddhavidyā, etc., according to Samaya and Kaula schools these practised, should be learned from the Candrakalā (Tantra). So as there is no distinction (between the syllables, the days, deities, etc.), so the word list, though it indicates the fourth night of the bright fortnight, also means the fourth syllable of the first division (called Vāgbhava Küla); for the mantra and the Tattva of Śuddhavidyā, ¡.e., Devī is\nVedha. ie., when the tenth day (Dasami) merges into the eleventh (Ekadasi) even for a few minutes, that day (the 11th) becomes void therefore, the strict observers of vows on that oleventh day, leave out that day and discontinue further ceremony till the 12th day.\ninseparable from these three (the fourth night, the fourth syllable and Śuddhavidyā).\nOr, Viśvarūpa is in the neuter gender, means the fifth day of dark fortnight. The Śruti (Tai. Bra. 3-12-1) says, (1) Prastuta, (2) Viṣṭuta, (3) Samastuta, (4) Kalyāna, and (5) Visvarûpa; (6) Śukra, (7) Amṛta, (8) Tejasvin, (9) Tejas, and (10) Samiddha; and (11) Aruṇa, (12) Bhanumat, (13) Marichimat, (14) Abhitapas, and (15) Tapasvat.\" The nights of the same are (1) Sutā, (2) Sunvati, (3) Prasută, (4) Sayamānā, (5) Abhishůyamānā; (6) Prīti, (7) Prapā, (8) Sampā, (9) Tripti, (10) Tarpayanti; and (11) Kānta, (12) Kāmyā, (13) Kāmajātā, (14) Ayushmati, and (15) Kāmadhughā.” The Prastuta, Viṣṭuta, the Sūta, Sunvatī these two sections are the names of the days and nights of the dark fortnight.\" The meaning is Devī is manifested in the form of all the deities of the nights and of the days (of bright and dark fortnights). Hence one who knows them acquires special merits, so the Śruti declares. (The Sriti Tai, Bra. 3-13-10) also says, One who knows the deities who produce honey and saturate the body with it, in him is produced honey from the Baindava. His sacrifices and good works become effective. To him who is ignorant, they produce no honey and his sacrifices and good works become ineffective.\" Produce honey, the deities of nights, and saturate the body with it, those of the days. For the Śruti (Tai. Bra. 3-10-10) says, Those nights of the bright and dark fortnights are the producers of honey (Madhukrit); their days cause it to flow (Madhuvarshini).\" Sacrifices and good works : devotional work.\nWhen the place of Ḍākinī and other deities (refer for Dakinî, etc., names 484 to 534) is saturated by the waking up of Kuṇḍalinī, by causing the honey to flow (in Sahasrāra), whatever works he does become effective, external actions such as sacrifice, etc., become fruitful. In the absence of that (flow) in which time the actions are performed, that\nja\ntime is productive of death of these actions (i.e.), they become ineffective. This is explained in the Chandrajnana Tr. \"Of those who are able to produce the flow of honey in the Baindava and whose bodies are cool (by the nectar), the sacrifices and good works become fruitful in course of time. Of those who are devoid of the inner flow and whose minds continue scorched, death at once terminates all their actions. The consumer of time alone (Devī) produces honey and causes it to flow. To him who knows, comes the flow of nectar from the Brahmarandhra. Vaidehī (Śrīvidyā belonging to Videha, Manmatha) who is Saragha (lit. bee,) and who is the destroyer of time, has created through the days and nights (of deities of bright and dark fortnights) this Vidyā (indicated) by the fifteen syllables.\"\nKālakarshini, the destroyer of time, is the name of a deity. One who knones, the distinction, viz., that between madhukrit, as the night deities, and madhuvarshini as those of day. Vaidehi, the wife of Videha, Kāmeśa. Vidyā, Pañcadaśī. Śruti (Tai. Bra. 3-10-9) says, 66 'The founder Vaideha, composed the weeks, of the day and night deities by (fifteen syllable, of the mantra).” Saragha, honey bees; Devī is also called a bee because she produces honey. The Śruti (Tai. Bra. 3-10-10) says, She (Devī) is bee, fire is the honey.\"","dev":"विश्वरूपा","iast":"viśvarūpā","pada":[{"dev":"विश्व","iast":"viśva"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":148,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":60},{"n":257,"gloss":"Waking state","body":"It is explained in the fsvarapratyabhijnā ; waking state dear to all, is the source of an external action through the activity of the senses.\" The Jīva is called Jagari, i.e., he who takes upon himself the gross body called Viśva.","dev":"जागरिणी","iast":"jāgariṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":154,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":60},{"n":258,"gloss":"Dreaming state","body":"The explanation of the dreaming state is also given in the Pratyabhijnā; The state of Svapna is objectifying of those visions, which are perceived in the mind, due to the perception of ideas latent in the mind.\" Jīva in the state\nof Svapna is termed Taijasa; its individuality is merged in\nthe subtle body.","dev":"स्वपन्ती","iast":"svapantī","pada":[{"dev":"स्व","iast":"sva"},{"dev":"पन्ती","iast":"pantī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":154,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":60},{"n":259,"gloss":"She is Taijasa itself","body":"Taijasa called Hiranygarbha who is the collective form (62)\nof the Jīvas described above (No. 258).","dev":"तैजसात्मिका","iast":"taijasātmikā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":155,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":60},{"n":260,"gloss":"State of sleep","body":"The description of it is given in the Śiva Sūtra (1, 10). 'The state of sleep is incapacity of discrimination, viz., illusion.\" One says I slept happily, I know nothing,\" thus one says from recollection. By this saying, three modifications of Avidyā are indicated; viz., ignorance, egoism, and happiness; sound sleep is that state in which these three exist; such a person is termed Prājña whose individuality is merged in the causal body.","dev":"सुप्ता","iast":"suptā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":155,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":61},{"n":261,"gloss":"Prājña itself","body":"Prājña, Īśvara who is the collective form of the jīvas above described (No. 260).","dev":"प्राज्ञात्मिका","iast":"prājñātmikā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":155,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":61},{"n":262,"gloss":"State of ecstasy","body":"Turya state is the state in which the experience called Śuddhavidyā is acquired and the result of discrimination of these (lower) three states and enjoyers thereof. The Spanda Śāstra says, He who perceives the object of attainment in the three abodes and the subject thereof, though he participates in it, yet remains unpolluted.” Varadarāja also says, 66 Turīya is the supreme abode, the participation in that state produces astonishment. Though there are different states, riz., waking and others, this (only) is realistic for the Yogin.\" The Śiva Sūtra (I, 7) also says, \"Though there are different states, viz., waking, dreaming, and sleeping, (real) enjoyment exists only in the fourth one. Ecstasy in the fourth state should be allowed like oil to permeate the other three.\" The Jīva is called he who is merged in the great causal body (Mahākārana). Devī is called Turīya, because she is both the individual and the collective form of this state. The means of attaining the state of Turīya is set forth by a Śiva (Sūtra,\nI, 15). He should project his mind by means of his own thought.\" (The commentary on this is as follows) : Abandoning Prāṇāyāma and other uncertain gross means, by his own thought' by his own knowledge, by the internal experience of astonishment, i.e., by the aid of Turīya, extinguishing the notion of body, etc., in consequence of merging himself (into the fourth state), this should he enter.\"\nOr, Turīya is a deity according to the Śakti rahasya as described in the Śruti (Man. Up., 7), \"They consider the fourth calmness, nondual.\" Ācārya (Saun. La. Sl. 97). 64 Thou art Turīya, unlimited mystery, difficult of attainment.\" The Tripurā Siddhānta says, O fairfaced one, as she is a pleasure to Turiyanandanatha (a certain teacher) Devī is known by the name Turīyā.”\n14th verse divides 40 names.","dev":"तुर्या","iast":"turyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":155,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":61},{"n":263,"gloss":"Transcending all the states","body":"There is a fifth state of Jīvas but as it has no special name and as it is beyond the Turīya state, it is simply called, beyond Turīyā'; this name indicates the fifth state; all the states, i.e., those described above. Because when a man transcends the fourth state, he necessarily transcends the other three. Vivarjitā transcending : l'i, entirely, that is one does not return to the other states. This state also has two aspects, the individual and the collective. This fifth state arises from firmness in the fourth one (Turīya). For it is said, The supreme state which is beyond the Turīya is only to be attained through a firm hold on the Turīya.\" Varadarāja, the com. also, The aspirant, by much familiarity with the fourth state attains the state which transcends the fourth one, and becomes equal to Śiva who is the soul of the Universe and who is pure absolute bliss.\" There are three Sūtras (in the Śiva Sūtras, III, 27,28 and 29): describing the nature of the man who\nThere is another reading Kritra then after performing the Prāṇāyāma and other gross means.\n18*\nhas attained the fifth state. \"His physical life is a religious observance; his conversation is Japa; his giving is knowledge of Self.\" His physical life is religious and not worldly (lit. trifling) because it is the means of worshipping Śiva by searching into his own soul. So Bhaṭṭotpala desires physical life in these words: \"Let me have this body invigorated by the nectar of pure Śakti manifested in me, for the sake of worshipping thee.\" His random conversation is Japa, because his mind has grasped the truth. It is explained by two Sūtras (Yoga Sūtra, I, 47 and 48). In the purity of the nondeliberative condition his soul becomes pleased. In that condition knowledge becomes truth supporting (Ritambhara).\" As the Śruti says, Whatever one thinks in his mind, that alone he speaks\"; all his words are in the form of mantras because his thoughts are pure. The Yogavasish/ha says, The wise should always be approached, whether they instruct or not; because their ordinary conversation becomes instruction.\" Gift (or giving): Kṛṣṇadāsa says, “The direct experience of Ātman described above as consciousness (Caitanya) itself, is the knowledge which is his gift and which, with promptness, he freely gives.\" Ācārya, (Sau. La. Slo. 27) considering that these three Sūtras are to be explained as above, says, \"The random talk is Japa, his bodily actions are the mudrās, etc\nThe Vārtika explains, Thus one performing the vow herein described, and repeating mantras, and maintaining (his physical existence), and equal to Śiva, always being in his own Ātman, devoted to the adoration of Śiva, alone he is called the great Yogin devoted to the practice of mighty Mantras, he imparts real knowledge to his disciples.”","dev":"सर्वावस्थाविवर्जिता","iast":"sarvāvasthāvivarjitā","pada":[{"dev":"सर्वावस्था","iast":"sarvāvasthā"},{"dev":"विवर्जिता","iast":"vivarjitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":156,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":61},{"n":264,"gloss":"Creator","body":"Thus after the description of the five divisions of Jīvas who are controlled by Avidyā, he proceeds according to different functions, to describe the five divisions of Īśvara who controls Māya. Ācārya also explains the same\nin the verse (Sau. La. 24).\n\"Brahmā creates the world, etc.\" Creation is the function of Īśvara when the Rajas quality predominates.","dev":"सृष्टिकर्त्री","iast":"sṛṣṭikartrī","pada":[{"dev":"सृष्टि","iast":"sṛṣṭi"},{"dev":"कर्त्री","iast":"kartrī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.933,"cpage":157,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":61},{"n":265,"gloss":"In the form of Brahmā","body":"The Brahmā, the fourfaced one. The Viṣṇu Pr. says, \"O Brahman, Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Śiva, are the chief Śaktis of Brahman.”","dev":"ब्रह्मरूपा","iast":"brahmarūpā","pada":[{"dev":"ब्रह्म","iast":"brahma"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":158,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":61},{"n":266,"gloss":"Protector","body":"Sustaining the universe. This is the function of Īśvara when the Sattva quality predominates.","dev":"गोप्त्री","iast":"goptrī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":158,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":61},{"n":267,"gloss":"In the form of Govinda","body":"In the Harivaṃśa, Nārada says, \"The first portion of Prakṛti the famous Devī called Umā. (The second one) the manifested Viṣṇu, the allpervading, protector of the universe, is known as woman.\"\nConcerning the word Govinda, Vi. Bhag. Pr. says, 1 am the lord (Indra) of the Devas, thou hast attained to the lordship over the cows; hence in the world men praise thee by the name Govinda.”\nAs I recovered\nThe Bhārata, in Mokshadharma, also, the earth which was taken away and rendered inaccessible, I am praised by devas and by words under the name of Govinda.\" The Harivaṃśa says, The gaut, stands for sound, which you (alone) know (vid); hence you are called Govinda by the ascotics.\"\nOr, Govinda, Bṛhaspati (the Guru of the Devas) ; according to the Viśvaprakāśa, “Govinda means Vāsudeva, cowherd, and Bṛhaspati.\"","dev":"गोविन्दरूपिणी","iast":"govindarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"गोविन्द","iast":"govinda"},{"dev":"रूपिणी","iast":"rūpiṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":158,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":61},{"n":268,"gloss":"Destroyer","body":"(63)\nSamhāra, reducing the universe into atoms. This is\nfunction predominates.\nof Īśvara when the Tamas quality","dev":"संहारिणी","iast":"saṃhāriṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":158,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":269,"gloss":"In the form of Rudra","body":"Rudra is the above mentioned İsvara,\nOr, Rudra, who drives away sorrow; or, weep, (Rodati) because the rain which comes at the dissolution\nof the universe originates from his solar eye in the form of tears. The Śruti (Tai. Sam.), \"He wept; because he wept, he is called the weeper (Rudra).\" The Śivarahasya also says, The Lord of Paśus (jīvas) is called Rudra because he drives away sorrow.\"\nOr, according to the Chch. Up. (3-16-3), “The Prāṇas (senses) are the rudras, these cause all this (body) to cry out.\" The Bhārata says, .6 To please, (his parent) the son of Brahmā, the eternal lord of the universe, springing from his forehead wept loudly and terribly.\" The Vayaviyasamhita says, 66 Ru, means pain, or, the cause of pain, he who drives this out, (dra), Śiva, the supreme cause, is called Rudra.\"","dev":"रुद्ररूपा","iast":"rudrarūpā","pada":[{"dev":"रुद्र","iast":"rudra"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.889,"cpage":158,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":270,"gloss":"Disappearance","body":"Tirodhāna, complete destruction, .., the absorption even of the atoms into Prakṛti like the extinction of a light. This is the function of Īśvara when the pure concentrated Sattva quality predominates.\nBecause\nyou\nOr, she is in the form of a Śakti called Tiraskariṇī. The Tripurasiddhānta says, disappearance of all who are not devotees, rightly, 0, cause the fairfaced one, you are called Tiraskariṇī.\"\n271. İs'vari. (Ruler.)\nThe previously mentioned one. Isoara is here used in a special sense just as Māyā is used in different sense. The Supreme individuality (Parāhantā) is the quality of İsvara. This is explained in the Virupakshapanchasika in the Visvasarīra chapter, “Lordship, activity, independence, consciousness itself. These by wisemen as the successive aspects of the Supreme said individuality.\"\nare","dev":"तिरोधानकरी","iast":"tirodhānakarī","pada":[{"dev":"तिरोधान","iast":"tirodhāna"},{"dev":"करी","iast":"karī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":159,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":271,"gloss":"Disappearance. (Tirodhānakarî )","body":"Tirodhāna, complete destruction, .., the absorption even of the atoms into Prakṛti like the extinction of a light. This is the function of Īśvara when the pure concentrated Sattva quality predominates.\nBecause\nyou\nOr, she is in the form of a Śakti called Tiraskariṇī. The Tripurasiddhānta says, disappearance of all who are not devotees, rightly, 0, cause the fairfaced one, you are called Tiraskariṇī.\"\n271. İs'vari. (Ruler.)\nThe previously mentioned one. Isoara is here used in a special sense just as Māyā is used in different sense. The Supreme individuality (Parāhantā) is the quality of İsvara. This is explained in the Virupakshapanchasika in the Visvasarīra chapter, “Lordship, activity, independence, consciousness itself. These by wisemen as the successive aspects of the Supreme said individuality.\"\nare","dev":"ईश्वरी","iast":"īśvarī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":159,"sure":0,"host":270,"v":62},{"n":272,"gloss":"Sadāśiva. When the superlative Sattva quality predominates she is so called","body":"Sadāśiva. When the superlative Sattva quality predominates she is so called.","dev":"सदाशिवा","iast":"sadāśivā","pada":[],"pconf":0.7,"fit":1,"cpage":159,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":273,"gloss":"Conferrer of blessing","body":"Anugraha, when, after complete annihilation, the universe again assumes the form of the primordial atoms\nLA\nthe process is called, Anugraha. This is the function of Sadāśiva.\nOr, Tirodhāna (270) and Anugraha, mean respectively bondage and release. In this meaning Īśvara and Sadāśiva who are respectively the external and the internal manifestations, are the causes of bondage and release.","dev":"ऽनुग्रहदा","iast":"'nugrahadā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":159,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":274,"gloss":"Devoted to the five functions. parayanā). 4. (Panchakritya- According to the Viśva, Parāyaṇa, means, fond of, devoted to, and dependent.\" The Mṛgendra Sam. says, That (Brahman)","body":"is one cause of the origin, preservation, destruction and annihilation of the universe and of the order of the existence of elements and beings.” The other Āgamas also say, The five functions of him are the creation, preservation, destruction, annihilation, and causing the reappearance of the universe which manifests incessantly.\" The De. Bhag. Pr., IV, says, 6: She creates the universe, she protects, she destroys what she protects at the end of the cycle; she it is who in her three forms bewilders the universe. Brahmā united with her creates the universe, Viṣṇu united with her protects, Rudra united with her destroys. She binds the whole universe and holds bewildered by the noose of illusion, O king, by the firm noose of 'I' and ' Mine'. Yogins released from worldly attachinents, desiring liberation and seeking after emancipation, worship the beneficent Devī alone, the ruler of the universe.\" Ācārya also (Saun. La. Sl. 24), Brahmā creates the universe, etc.,” the Śakti Sūtra also, Thus like that (she) exercises the five functions.\" The next Sūtra describes the characteristics of them, viz., Illumination (creation), affection (preservation), dissatisfaction (destruction), planting the seed (causing reappearance), and lamentation (annihilation).\" The Reader is referred to the Pratyabhijnāhridaya for the meaning of them. (64)","dev":"पञ्चकृत्यपरायणा ।।","iast":"pañcakṛtyaparāyaṇā ","pada":[{"dev":"पञ्चकृत्य","iast":"pañcakṛtya"},{"dev":"परायणा","iast":"parāyaṇā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.25,"cpage":160,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":275,"gloss":"Abiding in the midst of the sun's disc","body":"In the Sandhyā time Devī should be meditated upon that. The Vedānta Sūtra (I, 1, 20) explains the Śruti (Chchan. Up. I, 6, 6). \"The golden man seen within the sun, etc.,\" as the supreme Lord. The Kūrma Pr. by the hymns composed to Devī, by Himavat says, I bow down to the form which is in the sun's disc, who is all the Vedas, the one object of knowledge, who fills the different worlds with his brilliance, the cause of the three worlds, known as the supreme Lord.”","dev":"भानुमण्डलमध्यस्था","iast":"bhānumaṇḍalamadhyasthā","pada":[{"dev":"भानु","iast":"bhānu"},{"dev":"मण्डल","iast":"maṇḍala"},{"dev":"मध्यस्था","iast":"madhyasthā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":161,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":276,"gloss":"Bhanumandala' is the Anāhata lotus. Bairavi. Wife of Bhairava, i.e., Paramaśiva. Or, Bhairava, all women taken collectively (Bhīru, women)","body":"The Liṅga Pr. says, Meaning is Śiva, words are Devī; day is Śiva and Night is Devī\"; from that after describing Arundhatī, Anasuya, Śacī, the wife of Indra, etc., each as the manifestations of Devī, thus concludes ; All things indicated by the words in the feminine gender are manifestations of Devī.\"\nOr, in the Tripurachakresvari Mantra, in the middle division, when is eliminated, it is known as Bhairavímantra.\nOr, according to Dhaumya, a girl of twelve years old is called Bhairavī.","dev":"भैरवी","iast":"bhairavī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.6,"cpage":161,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":277,"gloss":"Wearing the garland of prosperity. mălini.) (Bhaga- Bhaga, means six attributes (refer for the meaning No. 279), and Mālinī, possessor. Or, she is the female emblem (Bhaga)","body":"Thus the Liṅga and De. Bhāg. Prs., Those who have the male emblem are the manifestations of Śiva; and those having the female emblem are the manifestations of Devī.”\nOr, Bhagamālini is one of the fifteen Nityā deities. In this mantra the word Bhaga occurs more frequently\n>\naccording to the school of the Dakṣiṇāmūrti than to those of Tantrarāja and Jñānārṇava. The names of surrounding the deities (avaranadevatās) of that (mantra) also are preceded mostly by the word Bhaga; hence she has the garland (Māyā) of Bhaga (words).","dev":"भगमालिनी","iast":"bhagamālinī","pada":[{"dev":"भग","iast":"bhaga"},{"dev":"मालिनी","iast":"mālinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.667,"cpage":161,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":278,"gloss":"Seated on the lotus. The wife of Brahmă","body":"Or, Padma, lotus, of it, the leaves are Prakṛtis, the filaments are the categories, the stalk is knowledge, etc. ; such is her seat.\nOr, Padma, Lakṣmī, the goddess of wealth, Sana, distributing. The great men have said, \"O beneficent mother, in whom thou art pleased that man receives prosperity containing of the enjoyment of supreme bliss ; his house becomes beautiful and contains much gold, and sitting on his couch he fondles his wife; and when thou art angry, that man becomes supremely miserable, his house appears without beauty and in it there is much lamentation and his couch becomes deserted.””\nOr, Padma, the Daitya Surapadma, Sana, to kill, i.e., the slayer of the Daitya, Padmāsura.\nOr, Padma, the bindu, etc. The Rabhasa says, Padma means lotus, numbers called Vytha, Nidhi and bindu,\" they should be applied in this sense.","dev":"पद्मासना","iast":"padmāsanā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":162,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":279,"gloss":"Bhagavatī. The Prs. say, The full supremacy, righteousness, fame, prosperity, wisdom","body":"Bhagavatī.\nThe Prs. say, The full supremacy, righteousness, fame, prosperity, wisdom, discrimination, these six are called Bhaga.\" The Kośa says, Bhaga means supremacy, magnanimity, wisdom, dispassion, womb, fame, power, effort, desire, righteousness, prosperity, and worldly concerns\"; these are in Devī. The De. Bhag. Pr. says,\nAs she knows the origin and dissolution, the going and coming of beings, knowledge and ignorance, she is called Bhagavatī,” The Śaktirahasya says, \"The root Bhaj\nAccording to the peculiar construction of the words 'prosperity' and 'adversity' a double meaning is conveyed.\nderived from the word worship, she who is worshipped by all Devas and she blesses them, hence she is called Bhagavatī.'","dev":"भगवती","iast":"bhagavatī","pada":[{"dev":"भग","iast":"bhaga"},{"dev":"वती","iast":"vatī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":162,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":280,"gloss":"Sister of Viṣṇu","body":"The one Brahman assumed two forms, viz., attributes (Dharma) and the possessor of them; again attributes become two, viz., male and female, the male is Viṣṇu who is the material cause of the whole universe; the female became the spouse of Paramaśiva. The secret of\nthe Śaiva theory is that these three in combination become the one indivisible Brahman. This theory is well mentioned in the Karma and other Prs. and is explained in detail in the Ratnatrayaparīkṣā by Appaya Dīkṣita. Braha. Pr. in the Purushottamakshetra Māhātmya says, \"Subhadrā, who was born from the womb of Menaka in a former birth, in this birth was born along with Kṛṣṇa of Devaki.\" 66 Let there be victory to Viṣṇu, the husband of Subhadrā,\" there Subhadrā, is Lakṣmī and there is no contradiction, or, in reality there is no difference. In an other place, “Brahmă, the grandsire of all the worlds, in order to see Devī, once performed a severe penance in the sacred Kāñcī; Tripurā, standing on a lotus, having a lotus in her hand, with her victorious brother appeared to the ascetic, performing the severe penance, fixed in meditation on the higher Self.\nOr, this contains the Vagbhva (first) division of Kādividyā (Pañcadaśī); Pudmāsana (278) indicates the syllable Ka; Bhaga, E, and vatī, (279) Kāmakalā (¿.e., I) Padmanābha, sa and hoda, Ha and M and make Hrīm. This is the secret meaning.","dev":"पद्मनाभसहोदरी","iast":"padmanābhasahodarī","pada":[{"dev":"पद्मनाभ","iast":"padmanābha"},{"dev":"सहोदरी","iast":"sahodarī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":163,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":62},{"n":281,"gloss":"The series of worlds arise and disappear with (65) the opening and shutting of Her eyes","body":"Since the eyes of Devī never close the expression \"opening and shutting refer to the influence of the destiny of beings, or to her mere wish, The Ajnāvatāra\n\".\nsays, \"By her mere wish the whole universe appears and disappears.' The creation of the supreme is simply the expression of His wish.\" Kālidāsa also says, 66 The universe consisting of the seer, the seen and the seeing, with its parts, existed in you before you thought (of creation). At the manifestation of the universe at your will, your eyes open, at the destruction of the universe (at your will) your eyes shut.\"\n'The\nGorakshanatha in his Mahārthamanjari says, moment occupied by the Highest Teacher in the creation is the same as that occupied in the destruction of the universe.\"","dev":"उन्मेषनिमिषोत्पन्नविपन्नभुवनावलिः","iast":"unmeṣanimiṣotpannavipannabhuvanāvaliḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":163,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":63},{"n":282,"gloss":"Thousand headed and thousand faced","body":"The Śruti (Purushasukta) says, \"Purusha is thousand headed, thousand eyed and thousand footed.\" Thousand means unlimited. The Bh. Gītā (XIII--13) has the same.","dev":"सहस्रशीर्षवदना","iast":"sahasraśīrṣavadanā","pada":[{"dev":"सहस्रशीर्ष","iast":"sahasraśīrṣa"},{"dev":"वदना","iast":"vadanā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":164,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":63},{"n":283,"gloss":"Thousand eyed","body":"","dev":"सहस्राक्षी","iast":"sahasrākṣī","pada":[{"dev":"सहस्र","iast":"sahasra"},{"dev":"अक्षी","iast":"akṣī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":164,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":63},{"n":284,"gloss":"Thousand footed","body":"The De. Bhag. Pr. (Book III), 66 Devī, having thousands of eyes, thousands of hands, thousands of heads and feet, vividly shines forth.\"\nOr, in this (281 to 284) contain the second and last divisions of the mantra (Pañcadaśī).\nBhuvanāvali (281) indicates the two Bhuvanesvarī bījas. Saladsrakshi (283) la, and sahastra (284) ha and (66)\nsa.","dev":"सहस्रपात्","iast":"sahasrapāt","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.833,"cpage":164,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":63},{"n":285,"gloss":"Mother of all from Brahmā to worm","body":"Brahmā, a Jīva called Hiraṇyagarbha who is the gross form of humanity taken collectively; Kita, is the small insect eater of the spider's thread; in the Vaidya Śāstras Kita is called Stamba meaning two kinds of parasitical worms, viz., Kakeru and Kamakeru.","dev":"आब्रह्मकीटजननी","iast":"ābrahmakīṭajananī","pada":[{"dev":"आब्रह्म","iast":"ābrahma"},{"dev":"कीट","iast":"kīṭa"},{"dev":"जननी","iast":"jananī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":164,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":64},{"n":286,"gloss":"She who establishes the castes and orders (of life)","body":"19*\nattaining\nThus she becomes the Vedas, which are her own commands, in order to lead the created Jīvas into the right path. The Vedas are divided into two parts, the Karmakanda and the Brahmā Kāṇḍa; as according to the rule, the devas and lower animals have no share in the Karmakanda, the (divine) Mother established righteousness (Dharma) through Karmakanda after dividing men into four castes and four orders. The Karma Pr. after the praise of Devī by Himavat, says, 'Devī, the life of the Universe having heard his words, remembering her husband (Śiva) answered her father with a smile. O Lord of mountains, hear from me this doctrine, which is supreme, secret, known to Īśvara only, practised by Brahmavādins alone describing the means of by meditation, Karma Yoga, devotion, and wisdom, O Lord of mountains, this is to be attained by these means, not by crores of other actions (Karmas) : thus concluding again she says in order to describe Karma Yoga Karma is ordained by the Śrutis and Smṛtis according to castes and orders; always perform this Karma associated with the knowledge of the Self, for the sake of liberation. Devotion arises from righteousness (Dharma), by devotion the supreme is attained. Dharma means sacrifice, etc., ordained by Śrutis and Smṛtis. Dharma is not to be otherwise known, because from Vedas alone Dharma came into existence; hence the aspirant for liberation should depend on the Vedas which are my form, for the sake of Dharma. By my command the divine Brahmā, for the sake of protecting the Vedas, created the Brāhmaṇas at the beginning and established them in their respective duties.\"","dev":"वर्णाश्रमविधायिनी","iast":"varṇāśramavidhāyinī","pada":[{"dev":"वर्णाश्रम","iast":"varṇāśrama"},{"dev":"विधायिनी","iast":"vidhāyinī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.957,"cpage":164,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":64},{"n":287,"gloss":"The Vedas are the expression of Her command","body":"Åjna, command, is ascertained by the knowers of the Vedas through reflection on the words (of the Vedas) what set forth the object to be attained, the means and the\npractical directions, by giving out the real meaning, meditation and praise; these three belong to the Karmakanda part of the Vedas. Hence Jaimini (1, 1, 22) says,\n(The words which refer to) past things are to be taken with (those which refer to) action.\" The Kūrma Pr., \"The ancient and supreme energy called Vedas, which are my commandments, are manifested at the beginning of creation in the form of Ṛg, Yajus and Sāman.\"\nOr, there are twentyeight Śaiva Tantras beginning with Kamika which follow the Vedas. There are some more scriptures beginning with Kapāla, Bhairava which are opposed to the Vedas. Of these two which follow the Vedas are here meant by Nigama; as they sprung from the mouth of Parameś'vara, they are said to be the form of my command; those (Kāpālika, etc.) which sprang from below the navel (of Śiva) are opposed to the Vedas. This is explained in the De. Bhag. and Skända Prs., \"The five Tantras beginning with kamika have sprung from the Sadyojāta face (of Śiva); the five Tantras Dīptā, etc., from the Vāmadeva face; the five, Vijaya, etc., from the Aghora face; the five, Vairochana, etc., from the east face (Tatpurusha); and the eight Tantras, Prodgita and others from the Īśāna face; these have sprung from the upward current and the others from the current below the navel.\"","dev":"निजाज्ञारूपनिगमा","iast":"nijājñārūpanigamā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":165,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":64},{"n":288,"gloss":"Giver of results, good and bad","body":"When the Veda is established in the form of righteousness through castes and orders, the Mother, in order to confirm that establishment, gives the good and bad results arising from ordained and prohibited actions respectively. The respective results of good and bad actions are heaven and hell. The Karma Pr. says, Those who do not perforin good actions as established, there are hells created by Brahmā into which by my command Yama (the Lord of Death) throws them. Those who are devoted to me and\nperform the duty prescribed by the Vedas, are by my command brought by Indra and others into Svarga and other places.\" It is said in other works also : Led by Īśvara (alone) one goes to heaven or to hell.” The Śivarahasya says also, “When the sacrifice sleeps (is finished) you alone remain awake in order to grant the result to the sacrificer.\" The Śruti also says (Kaush. Up. III, 8). For He makes him perform good actions whom he.wishes to lead upwards, and He alone makes him, whom he wishes to lead downwards, perform bad actions.\" Vedānta Sūtra (III, 2, 38). for that only is possible ; as regards partiality and mercilessness of the Lord the same Ved.Sa. (II, 1, 34) mentions, Partiality and mercilessness cannot be attributed on account of his regarding (merit and demerit).” The Saura Sam. also says, 66 Righteousness and unrighteousness depend on the doer and produce their respective results without Mahādeva exercising any (influence). This is not so The verse beginning with Rightly solicited' till the close of the chapter describing the Anlaryāmi brāhmaṇa (residing in all) concludes thus: \"Śiva is to be attained by reasoning supported by the scriptures. Hence Śiva directly confers the results of righteousness and unrighteousness.”\nThe same is expressed in the From him the fruit (of works),\n(67)","dev":"पुण्यापुण्यफलप्रदा","iast":"puṇyāpuṇyaphalapradā","pada":[{"dev":"पुण्या","iast":"puṇyā"},{"dev":"पुण्यफल","iast":"puṇyaphala"},{"dev":"प्रदा","iast":"pradā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":166,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":64},{"n":289,"gloss":"The dust of Her lotus feet forins the vermilion mark on the parting of the hair on the head of the Vedas (personified as goddesses)","body":"Thus after describing the Karmakända part as devoted to rites (sacrifices and other performances), he now sets forth the explanation of the established fact of Brahmakānda also (ie., Brahman is Siddhavastu) through the Mother's\ncommand alone.\nThe expression dust means that the Vedas are unable to explain the real form of Devī by direct assertions, by complete definition (a rule called Śṛṅga grähika), describe\nHer by the method of negation, like one ashamed and afraid, standing afar, describing inadequately, that (description also) creates no clear idea as to this or that'. This is explained in our Śivastava. \"The allwise scriptures, O Parama Śiva, much beloved by you do not adequately describe you, like a woman silent from shame. If they negative all your attributes by often saying, not this, not that, who else can declare thee to be this or that,' certainly not mere men like myself.\"","dev":"श्रुतिसीमन्तसिन्दूरीकृतपादाब्जधूलिका","iast":"śrutisīmantasindūrīkṛtapādābjadhūlikā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":167,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":65},{"n":290,"gloss":"The pearl (composing nose ring) is enclosed in a shell (composed) of the collected scriptures","body":"The Scriptures when they describe (Brahman) by positive definition, only prove it by characteristics, by the method of approximation (Arundhatinyāya, i.e., arundhatī is a very small star and by locating the surrounding big stars one at last perceives it).\nPearl expression indicates her attributes (Dharma) of creation, etc. The Scriptures describe only her nose pearl and other jewels and not her real nature. Because the Śruti (Tai. Up. II, 9), “Whence all speech with the mind turns away without being able to reach it.\"\nBy the previous name it is indicated that she is to be worshipped by the first three castes. This name indicates that she is to be worshipped by the fourth caste also. For the Rudrayāmala says, The abode indicated by the Vedas is also indicated by other scriptures; hence all Brāhmaṇas, Kṣatriyas, Vaiśyas and Śūdras are fit (to worship).\"\n(68)","dev":"सकलागमसंदोह शुक्तिसंपुटमौक्तिका","iast":"sakalāgamasaṃdoha śuktisaṃpuṭamauktikā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.923,"cpage":168,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":65},{"n":291,"gloss":"Granting the objects of men","body":"Artha, the four objects of human desire. The Brahmā. Pr. says, Those who worship the Supreme Śakti whether properly or improperly, are certainly not in Saṃsāra but are liberated souls. Hence no castes conjointly attain\nAccording to Bhāskarārāya' all speech' means the four kinds of speech, namely, Parā, Paśyantī, madhyamā and Vaikharf.\nprosperity and release without the adoration of Tripurā (Devī).\"\nOr, Purusha, Śiva, as the Śruti (Tai. Sam.) says, \"Purusha is Rudra.\" Arthu, liberation, that is, the worship of the unconditioned Brahman and the results thereof depend on Śakti alone.","dev":"पुरुषार्थप्रदा","iast":"puruṣārthapradā","pada":[{"dev":"पुरुषार्थ","iast":"puruṣārtha"},{"dev":"प्रदा","iast":"pradā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":168,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":66},{"n":292,"gloss":"The fullness. (Pārzā.)","body":"As she is free from the limitations of time, place and circumstance. The Śruti (Bri. Up. V, 1), “That (Brahman) is fullness; this is fullness; this fullness proceeds from that fullness; on grasping the fullness of this fullness, the fullness alone remains.\"\nOr, Pūrna, means the fifth, tenth, fifteenth, Nityā deities, the fourteenth bright night, also the name of a river.","dev":"पूर्णा","iast":"pūrṇā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.667,"cpage":169,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":66},{"n":293,"gloss":"The enjoyer","body":"Bhoginī is the name of a female serpent (Nāgakanyā).","dev":"भोगिनी","iast":"bhoginī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":169,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":66},{"n":294,"gloss":"Ruler of the universe","body":"Bhuvana fourteen worlds, or water. Or, Bhuvane <vari is the deity, indicated by the syllable Hrīm, or she is in the form of Hrīm.\nThe Tripurasiddhānta says, “The great ruler, the wife of Śiva, is called Bhuvanesvarī, because she has blessed Bhuvanānandanātha (a guru) and because she is highly celebrated in all the worlds.\" Bhuvananandanatha is one of the gurus, included under Mänavaugha (vide supru, I, 37 com.).\nOr, Bhuvanesvarī I: n the Chapter Ghalargalayantra on the Dakṣiṇāmūrti Sam. it is said, O ruler of the Devas, in one syllable there are many worlds ... in the syllables Ha, etc., from which are composed the words Hrīm, etc., in the (Ha) the bīja of the (syllables of) ether, Kailāsa and other regions are established,\" and describing the origin of each world, concluded, Hence O great ruler, 66 Again from the\nthou art called Bhuvanādhisvari.” word Hu sprang the ether, from the fourth svara the air...' thus describing the creation of the universe,\nconcludes, Hence thou art called Bhuvanesi.\" Thus two explanations are given.","dev":"भुवनेश्वरी","iast":"bhuvaneśvarī","pada":[{"dev":"भुवन","iast":"bhuvana"},{"dev":"ईश्वरी","iast":"īśvarī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":169,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":66},{"n":295,"gloss":"The mother","body":"Or, Ambikā means\nThe mother of the Universe. Bhārati, the goddess of speech, and earth: Or, Ambikā, the aggregate of Śaktis of lehchā, Jñāna, and Kriyā which are the form of Rudrānī. Or Ambikā means either night or sleep. The Viśva says, Ambikā, means deceit, perfection, sleep, and night.”\nIn the Skanda in the Navaratrapradīpa it is said, Night is the great Devī, and the day is Śiva.\" Harivaṃśa also, Māyā, the great deceiver, known in the world as sleep. Her body is night which creates darkness and destroys the day, the consumer of half the life of beings in the world, being terrible. There is none equal to her. .'. except Viṣṇu, none among the Devas can control her.\" The Mark. Pr. also says, “I praise the divine sleep of Viṣṇu, of unequalled power, the ruler of the Universe, the supporter of the world, and the cause of preservation and destruction.”","dev":"अम्बिका","iast":"ambikā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":170,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":66},{"n":296,"gloss":"Having neither beginning nor end","body":"Pr.\nOr, according to the system of Vararuci nāḍī or adi means eighty (i.e., da=8, and na or a=0); hence she whose worship delivers a man from the eighty causes of death. These (eighty) are divided into two as Påsa and Vadha. The latter is of twentyeight kinds, as the Viṣṇu \"The egoism and selfconceit are of twentyeight kinds.\" The former (Pāśa) is of fiftytwo kinds, as the Liṅga Pr. says, There are fiftytwo Pāśas arising from the knot of avidyā.” Thus eighty. Of these the explanation of the Vadha is given in the Sänkhya Tantra. 66 But disability is of twentyeight kinds. Requiescence is of nine kinds. Perfection is of eight kinds.\" (San. Sūtra, III, 38 to 40.) The meaning of these is given in the Karikas (of Is'vara Kṛṣṇa Slo. 49, 50 and 51), “Depravity\nof the eleven organs, together with injuries of the intellect are pronounced to be disability (Āsakti). The injuries of intellect are seventeen, by inversion of acquiescence and perfectness. Nine kinds of acquiescence are propounded; four internal relating to himself are, viz., Prakṛti (nature), Upādāna (reception), Kāla (time), and Bhāgya (luck), and the other five perfectnesses are relative to abstinence (from enjoyment) of the external objects, reasoning, hearing, studying scriptures, the suppression of three kinds of pain, acquisition of friends and liberality, these are the eight kinds of perfection. The abovementioned three (āsakti, tuṣṭi and siddhi) are the first obstacles to perfection.\" Āsakti means inability of the senses to receive their respective objects, defects caused by blindness, deafness, etc. : they are eleven because there are eleven senses.\nTuṣṭi (elation) is of two kinds, internal and external. The internal is of four kinds. First (when a man says), 'I am liberated by the absorption of mind in the eightfold prakṛti' this is called Prakṛti elation. The second is (when a man says) 'I have succeeded on merely receiving the robes of an ascetic'; this is called Upadāna elation ; the third is (when one says), What is the use of painful meditation success will come with time'; this is called Kāla elation. The last is (when one says), Success will come by the intervention of God: this is called Bhāgya (luck elation). These are all internal because in connection with himself.\nThe external (Tuṣṭi) elation is of five kinds corresponding to the five kinds of objective senses, viz., sound, touch, etc. The faults belonging to each objective sense are the acquiring, keeping, spending, wasting, etc., of earthly wealth. One kind of elation arises when after observing the disadvantages attached to the gratification of objective senses, he ceases their pursuit. Thus there are nine (elations). The separate names of each (of these) from Salila to Uttambha, and the names of each Siddhi\nfrom Tārā to Samudita must be ascertained from proper books.\nThe right kinds of Siddhis: Üha (reasoning) means understanding the meanings without being taught. Śabda (hearing) understanding the meaning by simply hearing the words. Adhyayana (study), understanding real meaning from the Guru's teaching. The other three Siddhis are the reverse of the three kinds of misery, concerning himself, concerning Devas, and concerning the elements. (Adhyātma, Adhibūta and Adhidaivata.) By gaining good friends, he attains Arthasiddhi. And the last (elation) arises from serving ascetics and the learned. To these seventeen there are seventeen reversions (or oppositions). The names of them are beginning with Asteya, and ending with Pramudita which should be ascertained from the proper books.\nThese seventeen are also called incapacity (Āsakti), and the injuries of intellect (Buddhivadha) ; these (seventeen) added to the injuries of the eleven senses they make up twentyeight Vadhas. The arrangement of these is given in the Vāyu Pr., “The eighth creation called Anugraha is fourfold, riz., Viparyaya (invertion), incapacity (Āsakti), Siddhi (perfectness) and Tuṣṭi (elation). The first is by inversion (leading into) the unstable creation; by incapacity, the animal kingdom, by perfectness, human beings, by elation, the Devas.\"","dev":"नादिनिधनो","iast":"nādinidhano","pada":[{"dev":"नादि","iast":"nādi"},{"dev":"निधनस्","iast":"nidhanas"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":170,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":66},{"n":297,"gloss":"Attended by Hari, Brahmā and Indra","body":"In the Śrīcakra (Nagara), in the centre between the seventeenth and the eighteenth walls, is the seat of Viṣṇu ; between the sixteenth and the seventeenth, is of Brahmā ; between the fourteenth and the fifteenth, is of Indra and other Lokapālas (protectors of the world). All these reside (in their respective places) for the sake of worshipping Devī. The De. Bhag. Pr. says, Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Śiva, Indra, Varuṇa, Yama, Vāyu, Agni, Kubera Tvashta, Pasha,\n20*\nAśvins, Bhaga, Ādityas, Vasus, Rudras, Vis'vedevas, Marutganas, all these meditate on Devī, the cause of creation, preservation, and destruction.\"\nBy this expression those who see the Vedas which describe Viṣṇu, Brahmā, etc., alone, do not obtain the real knowledge about Viṣṇu, etc., though they are devoted to them, because only those who see the Vedas in connection with Devī, are marked out by superior excellence; hence there is no use of worshipping other deities as it is opposed to the rule for a beggar to beg from a beggar when a nonbeggar, a (reasonably) rich man is present?\" This is explained in our Sivasthava. \"O Paramaśiva, why should I a beggar beg from Viṣṇu, Brahmā, Indra and other beggars, who possess dominion given by you, saying 'do thou give to me.' Even an ignorant man who already enjoys the result of the twelve days' sacrifice only desires the result of the Jyotiṣṭoma (a superior sacrifice) and not the result of the twelveday's sacrifice.\" (69)","dev":"हरिब्रह्मेन्द्रसेविता","iast":"haribrahmendrasevitā","pada":[{"dev":"हरि","iast":"hari"},{"dev":"ब्रह्मेन्द्र","iast":"brahmendra"},{"dev":"सेविता","iast":"sevitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":172,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":66},{"n":298,"gloss":"Nārāyaṇī. Nārāyaṇa, Viṣṇu or Śiva. The explanation of the word Nārāyaṇa is given in Manu Smṛ. (I, 10): The water is called Narah, because it emanated from Nara","body":"that is his first abode (ayana), hence he is named Nārāyaṇa.\" The Br. Vaivarta Pr. also, Because his abode is among men, hence he is called Nārāyaṇa.\" The De. Bhag. Pr., 'Because Nara means leading, hence, supreme self is called Nara.\" The Bhārata, \"The wise knows that the Tattvas emanated from Nara (Brahman) and form his abode, hence he is called Nārāyaṇa.\" Here Nārāyaṇa is Paramaśiva, because concerning the fourth state, the Kāśīkhaṇḍa says, He is the Husband of Lakṣmī and also of Pārvatī.\" Or this saying the husband of Lakṣmī' may indicate nonseparation between Lakṣmī and Devī. Or, the sister of Viṣṇu is called Nārāyaṇī. For there is a saying, Adoration to Śiva, the husband of Nārāyaṇī.\" Or, because there is no difference between Gaurī and Nārāyaṇa.\nIn the Kūrma Pr. Śiva says, \"I, the supreme Lord, have divided myself into two forms, one is Nārāyaṇa, the other is Gaurī, the mother of the universe. So my supreme nature is known to neither the Devas, nor to the Ṛṣis, because I am one, I am Devī and Viṣṇu.\" The Devī Pr. says, Because she has her abode in the water, not in air, or she has her seat in the ocean, hence she is called Nārāyaṇī, the creator of Nara (men) and women.”\nAccording to the Padma Pr. Nārāyaṇī is the name of the goddess worshipped in the sacred place Supars'va.","dev":"नारायणी","iast":"nārāyaṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":173,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":67},{"n":299,"gloss":"In the form of sound","body":"There are eight notes (varnas) above the bindu of the syllable Hrīm, etc., such as, Ardhchandra, Rodhinī, Nāda, Nadanta, Śakti, Vyapikā Samani, and Unmanī which are subtle, more subtle, and most subtle;' of these the third is Nāda. It's nature is described in the Mahasvachchanda Tantra. Nāda, which resembles the filament of the lotus, shining like crores of suns, surrounded by innumerable cities, is above Rodhinī (the 2nd) of above mentioned . . . The supreme Devī, seated on the lap of that (Nāda), leading upward, should be contemplated.\"\nOr, Nāda simply sound.","dev":"नादरूपा","iast":"nādarūpā","pada":[{"dev":"नाद","iast":"nāda"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":174,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":67},{"n":300,"gloss":"Transcending name and form. vivarjitā.)","body":"(Namaraipa\nThere are five forms of the universe which appear inextricably interwined in the knot of spirit and matter. These are as analysed by great men as \"existence, knowledge, bliss, name and form. The first three belong to Brahman, and the other two belong to the world.\" As the latter two, name and form are illusory, she transcends them. The meaning is that in reality there is no union between being and nonbeing. The Chānd. Up. (VIII, 14, 1) says, Ether (Brahman) separates name and form, between them is Brahman.\" That which remains, when these two\n1 A-U-M. in the syllable Om and Hrīm in the Hrīm, etc., and altogether make eleven in number.\nare\neliminated by the ether which is the energy of consciousness, (Cit Śakti) is Brahman.\nThus in the Saubhagyabhāskara composed by Bhāsurănanda, ends the fourth Kāla called Marīci, with the third hundred of the names.\nTHE FOURTH HUNDRED","dev":"नामरूपविवर्जिता","iast":"nāmarūpavivarjitā","pada":[{"dev":"नामरूप","iast":"nāmarūpa"},{"dev":"विवर्जिता","iast":"vivarjitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.667,"cpage":174,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":67},{"n":301,"gloss":"The syllable Hrīm","body":"Or, Hrīm, shame and Kāri, doer. The meaning of Hrīm is given in the Svatantra Tantra by a Vyākulākshara ' verse 'The lyoma (Ha) indicates the manifestation, Vahni (Ra) involution, I protection, and the dot (1) controlling the three.\" The meaning is Hrīm corresponding to creation, preservation and destruction (with the 4th); Kart doer.\nOr, Hrimkāru, the Bhuvanes'varībīja.","dev":"ह्रींकारी","iast":"hrīṃkārī","pada":[],"pconf":0.7,"fit":1,"cpage":175,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":67},{"n":302,"gloss":"Possessor of Hrīm","body":"Hil, shame. The Śruti says,\ntion, desire, nourishment.\"\n15th verse divides 38 names.\nshame, mind, satisfac-","dev":"हीमती","iast":"hīmatī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.857,"cpage":175,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":67},{"n":303,"gloss":"Abiding in the heart","body":"That is in the heart of ascetics. Or, Hṛdya, delightful.","dev":"हृद्या","iast":"hṛdyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":175,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":67},{"n":304,"gloss":"She has nothing to regret nor to accept","body":"Because the scriptures, which describe injunction and prohibition are confined to the ignorant ones.\n(70)","dev":"हेयोपादेयवर्जिता","iast":"heyopādeyavarjitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":175,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":67},{"n":305,"gloss":"Worshipped by Rājarāja","body":"Rājarāju, Manu and Kubera; or, the Vidyă of Kubera and others (refer No. 238). The Durvāsa (in his\nA most secret meaning of certain mantras was given out in a perplexing verse, with a key also is another verse in the same way, In order that it may not be understood by the uninitiated. Such verses are called Vyakulakshara verse.\n+\nLali-Stha.) when he describes the space between the fourteenth and the fifteenth walls, says, \"I meditate upon Kubera, the bead of a wealthy family, in the Eastern part of that place (of Vāyu), and upon the other Yakṣas, who depend upon the feet of Mother, such as Manibhadra and others,\"","dev":"राजराजार्चिता","iast":"rājarājārcitā","pada":[{"dev":"राजराज","iast":"rājarāja"},{"dev":"अर्चिता","iast":"arcitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":175,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":68},{"n":306,"gloss":"Queen","body":"Because she is the queen of Rījarājesvara (Śiva).","dev":"राज्ञी","iast":"rājñī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":68},{"n":307,"gloss":"The beautiful one. (Rainya.)","body":"","dev":"रम्या","iast":"ramyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.667,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":68},{"n":308,"gloss":"With eyes like those of a deer","body":"Rajiva, according to the Viśva means, deer, fish, lotus, a dependent of a king.\" In the latter sense she looks favourably on those who depend on the king's bounty.","dev":"राजीवलोचना","iast":"rājīvalocanā","pada":[{"dev":"राजीव","iast":"rājīva"},{"dev":"लोचना","iast":"locanā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":68},{"n":309,"gloss":"Delighting","body":"Her devotees. Or, Rānjani, dyeing. By her presence, the rosytinted Devī colours the pure Paramaśiva as the red flower colours the crystal.","dev":"रञ्जनी","iast":"rañjanī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":68},{"n":310,"gloss":"Gladdener","body":"Of her devotees. The Śruti (Chānd. Up. VIII, 12-3),\n“Laughing, playing and rejoicing.\"","dev":"रमणी","iast":"ramaṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":68},{"n":311,"gloss":"She who is to be tasted","body":"The Śruti (Tai. Up. II, 6-1) \"He is alone the essence.\"","dev":"रस्या","iast":"rasyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":68},{"n":312,"gloss":"Having a girdle of tinckling bells. kinkini mekhalā.) 313 Rāma. (Ranat (71)","body":"In the form of Lakṣmī. The Sa. Sam. (IV, 47, 66), \"She appears like a dancer in the form of Lakṣmī, speech (Sarasvatī), etc.”","dev":"रणत्किङ्किणिमेखला","iast":"raṇatkiṅkiṇimekhalā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.429,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":68},{"n":313,"gloss":"Having a girdle of tinckling bells. kinkini mekhalā.) 313 Rāma. (Ranat (71)","body":"In the form of Lakṣmī. The Sa. Sam. (IV, 47, 66), \"She appears like a dancer in the form of Lakṣmī, speech (Sarasvatī), etc.”","dev":"रमा","iast":"ramā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":176,"sure":0,"host":312,"v":69},{"n":314,"gloss":"Having a face like the full moon","body":"","dev":"राकेन्दुवदना","iast":"rākenduvadanā","pada":[{"dev":"राक","iast":"rāka"},{"dev":"इन्दुवदना","iast":"induvadanā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":69},{"n":315,"gloss":"In the form of Rati","body":"The wife of Kāma.\nOr, Ramā (313) indicates 1, rakenduvadana (314) anus'vāra. Thus the Kāmakalā Mantra is indicated here.","dev":"रतिरूपा","iast":"ratirūpā","pada":[{"dev":"रति","iast":"rati"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":69},{"n":316,"gloss":"Beloved of Rati","body":"","dev":"रतिप्रिया","iast":"ratipriyā","pada":[{"dev":"रति","iast":"rati"},{"dev":"प्रिया","iast":"priyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":69},{"n":317,"gloss":"Protector","body":"Or, Raksha, the holy ashes; that is the protector and destroyer.","dev":"रक्षाकरी","iast":"rakṣākarī","pada":[{"dev":"रक्षा","iast":"rakṣā"},{"dev":"करी","iast":"karī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":176,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":69},{"n":318,"gloss":"Slayer of Rākṣasas","body":"","dev":"राक्षसन्नी","iast":"rākṣasannī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.8,"cpage":177,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":69},{"n":319,"gloss":"Rāmā. (Woman.)","body":"Or, Yogins delight in her (Rāma to delight). The Liṅga Pr. says, All men are Śaṃkara, all women, Devī. The names which denote masculine gender are said, to be of Śiva, and those which denote the feminine are the manifestations of Devī. Thus women and men are the manifestations of Devī and Śiva.\" The Viṣṇu Pr. also says,\nThe divine Hari lives in the form of a man among Devas, animals, men, etc., and in the form of women, O Maitreya, Lakṣmī (lives), and there is none higher than these (two).\" The Brah. Vaivarta Pr. also says, Whatever in the three worlds appear in female form all that, O Devī, is thy form; thus is declared in the scriptures.\" Hence the Br. Parāśara Smṛ. says, 66 As displeased, so the Gods also are pleased or displeased. If women are pleased or they are pleased the family increase; if they are displeased the family is spoilt.\"","dev":"रामा","iast":"rāmā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.4,"cpage":177,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":69},{"n":320,"gloss":"Devoted to her husband","body":"As she is the collective form of women, she makes each woman devoted to her husband.\nOr, Ramaṇa, enjoyment, play; lampata, devoted. (72)","dev":"रमणलम्पटा","iast":"ramaṇalampaṭā","pada":[{"dev":"रमण","iast":"ramaṇa"},{"dev":"लम्पटा","iast":"lampaṭā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":177,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":69},{"n":321,"gloss":"To be desired","body":"Those who desire liberation desire to obtain Her by knowledge.\nOr, Kāmya is the deity of the twelfth night of the dark fortnight.","dev":"काम्या","iast":"kāmyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":177,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":70},{"n":322,"gloss":"In the form of Kāmakalā","body":"There are three bindus and the Härdakala; in these, the first bindu is called Kāma and the last Kāla. According to the rule of Pratyahara, Kāmakalā includes all four. The real nature of that (Kāmakalā) is set forth in the Kamakalāvilāsa, from the verse beginning, The Paraśakti\nis in the form of the sprout, expanded out of the union of\nKāma (means) desirable-\nThe two bindus (in the\nŚiva and Śakti,\" till the verse ness and the Kāla the same. middle) represent the sun and the moon.'\nOr, Kāma both Śiva and Devī, and Kāla manifestation. This is explained in the Tripurasiddhānta: “0 Pārvati, Kāla is the manifestation of Kāmeś'vara and Kāmeś'vari, hence she is known as Kāmakalā.”\nOr, she is the manifestation (Kāla) of desire (Kāma). The Kālikā Pr. explains Devī alone is indicated by the word Kamia. Devī is called Kāma because she came to the secret place in the blue peak of the great mountain (Kailāsa) along with me for the sake of desire (Kāma) thus Devī is called Kāma. As she is also fulfilling desire, desiring, desirable, beautiful, restoring the body of Kāma, (manmatha) and destroying the body of Kāma, hence she is called Käma.”","dev":"कामकलारूपा","iast":"kāmakalārūpā","pada":[{"dev":"काम","iast":"kāma"},{"dev":"कलारूपा","iast":"kalārūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":177,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":70},{"n":323,"gloss":"Fond of Kadamba flowers","body":"","dev":"कदम्बकुसुमप्रिया","iast":"kadambakusumapriyā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":178,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":70},{"n":324,"gloss":"Beneficent","body":"Or, according to the Padma Pr. in the Malaya Mountain she is worshipped as Kalyānī.\nOr, kalya, good words, ana, to utter.","dev":"कल्याणी","iast":"kalyāṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":178,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":70},{"n":325,"gloss":"Root of the world","body":"According to the Yadava, Kāṇḍa means filling, root of plants, cloud.”","dev":"जगतीकन्दा","iast":"jagatīkandā","pada":[{"dev":"जगती","iast":"jagatī"},{"dev":"कन्दा","iast":"kandā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":178,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":70},{"n":326,"gloss":"Ocean of the waters of compassion","body":"","dev":"करुणारससागरा","iast":"karuṇārasasāgarā","pada":[{"dev":"करुणा","iast":"karuṇā"},{"dev":"रससागरा","iast":"rasasāgarā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":178,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":70},{"n":327,"gloss":"Possessing sciences","body":"The sciences are sixtyfour.","dev":"कलावती","iast":"kalāvatī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":178,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":71},{"n":328,"gloss":"(73) Her ordinary conversation is science","body":"According to the Amara (verse 86), Kāla means sweetly sounding-ālāpu speech.\nOr, Ka, Brahman; lālā, saliva; ap, attainment. The attainment of the Brahman becomes, through her (grace) as natural as the flows of saliva.","dev":"कलालापा","iast":"kalālāpā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":178,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":71},{"n":329,"gloss":"Beautiful","body":"Or, Ka, Brahman, anta, final. Brahman (unconditioned) is her final form.\nOr, she is the deity (called Kānta) of the eleventh night of the dark fortnight.","dev":"कान्ता","iast":"kāntā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":178,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":71},{"n":330,"gloss":"Fond of mead","body":"The Śruti (Tripurā Up.) says, \"Mead, fish, flesh, cooked serials offered in the triangle on behalf of the great Devatā, the lucky devotee, recovering his self, attains perfection.\"","dev":"कादम्बरीप्रिया","iast":"kādambarīpriyā","pada":[{"dev":"कादम्बरी","iast":"kādambarī"},{"dev":"प्रिया","iast":"priyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":179,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":71},{"n":331,"gloss":"Granting boons","body":"To Brahmā, Viṣṇu and other devotees. This is said by Nārada in the Matsya and Padma Prs. 66 What I told you of this (Devī) about (her) ever uplifted hand; for this uplifted hand of Devī ever confers boons. She will become the giver of boons to all Devas, Daityas and ascetics.\" As in the world the hand is the instrument of giving, here the hand (of Devī) is said to confer boons. In reality, the expression means simply to fulfil the desire of the gods; not having the emblem of granting boons. Ācārya says (Sau La. Slo. 4): \"With the exception of yourself, all the bands of deities by their hands confer (respectively) gift and protection. Thou alone doth not do this.\" The De. Bhag. Pr. also: \"She fulfils the desires of the Devas who seek boons. From the root vri to choose, she is called Varada, conferrer of boons, i.c., giver of the thing chosen.\" In the Varāha Pr. also in the chapter on the Vritrāsuravadha, \"This Devī is always to be worshipped, by concentration on the ninth day; she will certainly become the giver of boons to all worlds.\"","dev":"वरदा","iast":"varadā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":179,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":71},{"n":332,"gloss":"Beautiful eyed","body":"Or, Vāma, fair and nayana lit. eyes, i.e., proofs.\nBashkararaya commenting on Upaniṣad gives injunction to those who are allowed to take mead, this Upaniṣad says this fish, etc., that they should take them first by dedicating them to God and minimise the habit by gradation. The same idea is expressed in the Manu Smṛ. V, 56, Vi, Bhag. Pr. XI, 5-11.\nOr, vāma, the left (path), naya to lead. Or, vāma, the fruit of inaction, because in the Śruti (Chānd. Up., IV, 15, 2 and 3) where the word vāma occurs, it is thus explained.","dev":"वामनयना","iast":"vāmanayanā","pada":[{"dev":"वाम","iast":"vāma"},{"dev":"नयना","iast":"nayanā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":179,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":71},{"n":333,"gloss":"Perturbed by the intoxicating liquor","body":"Varunīmat, belonging to the regions of Varuṇa, i.e., Ādiśeṣa, the thousand hooded snake, because he dwells in that region; avihvala not perturbed or fatigued, i.e., by Her grace Ādiśeṣa is not fatigued, in supporting the universe. The Viṣṇu Pr. says, \"In the Varuṇa regions he (Ādiśeṣa) worships (Devī) with his own glorious body.\"\nVāruṇī: the juice extracted from dates, called Vāruṇī, because the god Varuṇa is fond of it. expression means forgetting external objects and simply enjoying (lit. chewing) Svätmānanda.\nOr, Vārunīmat, those who conquered the l'aruninādi, and became firm (Avihvala). The Yoga Śāstra says, \"Varuninadi is that which is above and below, going everywhere, Pashan is the deity of the quarters, and Vāyu is the deity of this vārunī (nādi).”\n(74)","dev":"वारुणीमदविला","iast":"vāruṇīmadavilā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.875,"cpage":180,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":71},{"n":334,"gloss":"Transcending the Universe","body":"Viva, the categories from earth to Śiva. The Śruti says, Rudra, the great seer, transcends the universe.\"","dev":"विश्वाधिका","iast":"viśvādhikā","pada":[{"dev":"विश्वा","iast":"viśvā"},{"dev":"अधिका","iast":"adhikā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":180,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":72},{"n":335,"gloss":"To be known through the Vedas","body":"Vedas are Ṛg, Yajus, Sāman, and Ātharvaṇa. The Śruti (Kaivalya Up., II, 3), “I alone am to be known from all the Vedas.\" The Smṛti (Bh. Gītā, 15, 15), also repeats the same. Or, there are four gates to the Cintāmaṇi palace (vide name 57); these are the four Vedas; as the deity cannot be seen unless the gates are entered, hence it is said she is to be known, through the Vedas alone. The Śruti (Tai. Br.), “The great quarter, the east is said to be of the Rigs; the endless southern quarter, they say, is that of the Yajus; the west of the\n21*\nAtharva; and the great northern quarter is that of Sāman.\" The meaning is that Devī should be known through the four goddesses, who are the deities of the four Vedas, viz., Śuddhavidyā and others, Saubhāgya and others, Lopāmudrā and others, and Turiyāmba and others, who are the deities of Ṛg, Yajus, Atharva and Sāman respectively.","dev":"वेदवेद्या","iast":"vedavedyā","pada":[{"dev":"वेद","iast":"veda"},{"dev":"वेद्या","iast":"vedyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":180,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":72},{"n":336,"gloss":"Residing in the Vindhya mountain","body":"་་\nThe Padma Pr. in the chapter enumerating the sacred places says, Sit is in the Trikala (mountain) and Vindhyadhivasinī in the Vindya (mountain.).” The Pañcarātra in the Lakṣmī Tr. says, “In the Vaivasvata Manvantara, two Daityas, Sumbha and Nisumbha overelated by their boons, will do injury to the devas. Born in the Nandagopa's family, in the womb of Yasoda, I under the name Nandā, residing in the Vindhya Mountain, shall destroy these two.\" The same story is repeated in\nMark. Pr.","dev":"विन्ध्याचलनिवासिनी","iast":"vindhyācalanivāsinī","pada":[{"dev":"विन्ध्याचल","iast":"vindhyācala"},{"dev":"निवासिनी","iast":"nivāsinī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":181,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":72},{"n":337,"gloss":"Supporter","body":"Nourisher of the universe. The Devī Pr. says, “Dhâtri is known as the mother, because she bears.”\nOr, the wife of Brahmā (Vidhātā). Or, Vi, much, dhātrī, myrabolam, because she is fond of myrabolams.","dev":"विधात्री","iast":"vidhātrī","pada":[{"dev":"विधा","iast":"vidhā"},{"dev":"अत्री","iast":"atrī"}],"pconf":0.92,"fit":1,"cpage":181,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":72},{"n":338,"gloss":"Mother of the Vedas","body":"The Vedas are the\nThe Śruti (Mun. Up., 1, 1, 5) says, breath of this great Being.\" (Purushastikta) also says, * Rigs and Sāmans came out (from Him).\" The Devī Pr., \"Because, there arose from the Kuṇḍalinī, which is triangular in form, the vowels and consonants; hence she is the mother of the Vedas.\"","dev":"वेदजननी","iast":"vedajananī","pada":[{"dev":"वेद","iast":"veda"},{"dev":"जननी","iast":"jananī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":181,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":72},{"n":339,"gloss":"Māyā of Viṣṇu","body":"Viṣṇu, allpervading, unlimited by place, time, etc. The Māyā, conditions or limits Viṣṇu.\nOr, the Māyā, illusion belonging to Viṣṇu. The Bh. Gītā (7--14) says, \"This my divine illusion, consisting\nof Guṇas is very difficult to transcend.\"\nThe Kālikā Pr.\nalso, Vishnumāyā is that which differentiates, everything, into manifested and unmanifested according to the Tamas, Rajas and Sattva qualities.\nD","dev":"विष्णुमाया","iast":"viṣṇumāyā","pada":[{"dev":"विष्णु","iast":"viṣṇu"},{"dev":"माया","iast":"māyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":181,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":72},{"n":340,"gloss":"Playful","body":"Vilāsa that power of projection (Vikshepasakti) which she possesses.\nVilāsa may mean a special power called Pithasakti. Or, Bila, (as there is no difference between the syllable I. and B.), the Brahmarandhra (in Sahasrāra) as to reside. This is explained in the Svacchanda Tr., \"There one should know the cavity of Brahman (Brahmarandhra), surrounded by millions of Rudras . . . This is another Śakti calied Brāhmaṇī, seated on the lap of Brahman, obstructing the door of the path of Salvation.\" (75)\n16th verse divides 38 names.","dev":"विलासिनी","iast":"vilāsinī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":182,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":72},{"n":341,"gloss":"Her body is matter (Kshettra)","body":"(Kshetrasvarupā.)\nKṣetra, Kamarupa and other abodes, or thirtysix categories from earth to Śiva, these are her body. The Liṅga Pr. says, Devī, the wife of the destroyer of the three cities (i.e., Śiva) becomes Kṣetra (matters). The divine and eternal lord, the death of death is the knower of the bodies.\"\n342.","dev":"क्षेत्रस्वरूपा","iast":"kṣetrasvarūpā","pada":[{"dev":"क्षेत्र","iast":"kṣetra"},{"dev":"स्वरूपा","iast":"svarūpā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.667,"cpage":182,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":73},{"n":342,"gloss":"Her body is matter (Kshettra)","body":"(Kshetrasvarupā.)\nKṣetra, Kamarupa and other abodes, or thirtysix categories from earth to Śiva, these are her body. The Liṅga Pr. says, Devī, the wife of the destroyer of the three cities (i.e., Śiva) becomes Kṣetra (matters). The divine and eternal lord, the death of death is the knower of the bodies.\"\n342.","dev":"क्षेत्रेशी","iast":"kṣetreśī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":182,"sure":0,"host":341,"v":73},{"n":343,"gloss":"The wife of the ruler of the matter. (Kshetresi.)","body":"The protector of the matter and of the knower of the matter. (Kshetrakshetrajnapalini.)\nKṣetrajña, is the Jīva. The Viṣṇu Smṛ. says, This body O Earth, is called Kṣetra; one who knows this is called Kṣetrajña. O fair one, know me as the Kṣetrajña in all bodies.\" In the Bh. Gītā Kṛṣṇa teaches the same thing to Arjuna (13, 1 and 2). The Liṅga Pr., \"The wise call the twentyfour categories Kṣetra, and the enjoyer thereof the Kṣetrajña.\" But\n1 Kṣetra lit. field that which is known by the soul, i.e., matter.\nthe Vāyu Pr. says, \"The unmanifested (avyakta) is called Kṣetra, Kṣetrajña.\" Brahmā Pr., Brahman, Kṣetras are the bodies, the soul united with them (Yogatman); the enjoyer, according to his pleasure is the Kṣetrajña.\" The Manu Smṛ. (12, 12 to 15), \"He who is the cause of action of the (embodied) souls they call him the Kṣetrajña. Him, who performs actions, the wise name Bhūtātman (the self consisting of elements). Another internal self that is generated with all bodied, is called Jīva, through which (he, the Kṣetrajña) feels all, pleasure and pain in the bodies. These two, the great one, and the Kṣetrajña, who are closely united with the elements, pervade him who resides in (all) beings great and small. From his body innumerable bodies arise which constantly impel the multiform creatures action.\"\nto","dev":"क्षेत्रक्षेत्रज्ञपालिनी","iast":"kṣetrakṣetrajñapālinī","pada":[{"dev":"क्षेत्र","iast":"kṣetra"},{"dev":"क्षेत्रज्ञ","iast":"kṣetrajña"},{"dev":"पालिनी","iast":"pālinī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.556,"cpage":182,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":73},{"n":344,"gloss":"Free from decay and growth","body":"Growth and decay belong to Kṣetra and changing. Though She controls them, she is free from them. The Bh. Gītā (2, 23), \"Weapons do not cut, etc.\"\nOr, she is free from these two which are the result of actions. The Śruti (Br. Up., IV, 4, 22) says, \"This is the eternal glory of the knower of Brahman. He neither increases by action nor becomes less.\"\n\"Neither becomes more by good actions nor less by bad ones.\"","dev":"क्षयवृद्धिविनिर्मुक्ता","iast":"kṣayavṛddhivinirmuktā","pada":[{"dev":"क्षय","iast":"kṣaya"},{"dev":"वृद्धि","iast":"vṛddhi"},{"dev":"विनिर्मुक्ता","iast":"vinirmuktā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":183,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":73},{"n":345,"gloss":"Worshipped Kṣetrapāla","body":"Kālī was created by Śiva to slay the Daitya Dāruka. Even after killing him the fire of her wrath was not appeased. Seeing the confusion of the world, Śiva in order to dispel her anger assumed the form of a crying infant. She suckled the child who drank up the fire of her anger with the milk. This child which is that incarnation of Śiva, is called Kṣetrapāla. This story occurs in the Liṅga and other Prs. The literal meaning is Kṣetra is the\nprecinct of the sacrificial ceremony, and Palu, the protector.","dev":"क्षेत्रपालसमर्चिता","iast":"kṣetrapālasamarcitā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":183,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":73},{"n":346,"gloss":"Ever victorious","body":"(76)\nOr, Vijayā, knowledge itself.\nAccording to the Devī Pr. she is the deity of Kashmira which is one of the sixtyeight Sacred places. As regards the meaning of Vijaya, the same book says: 46 After conquering the very powerful king of the Daityas named Padma, she is known in the three worlds by the epithet Vijaya (ever victorious) and unconquerable (Aparajitā).”\nOr, Vijaya is the name of a building, mentioned in the Viśvakarmā Śāstra.\nAccording to the Chinthamani, Vijaya is an auspicious hour; \"In the month Åsvina, in the tenth day of the bright fortnight, when the stars appear, that time is known as Vijaya, giving success to all undertakings.\" In the Ratnakośa, we read, 66 'The time, just after the twilight (sandhyā) when the stars begin to shine, is called Vijaya, favourable to all undertakings. The eleventh Muhurtha (eight hours and fortyeight minutes after midday) is named Vijaya. A journey should be begun at that time by all who desire success.","dev":"विजया","iast":"vijayā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":184,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":74},{"n":347,"gloss":"Unsullied","body":"Mālā (impurity) belongs to Avidyā.\nAccording to the Padma Pr. Vimalā is the deity of the sacred place, Purushottama.\nOr, Fimalā is a kind of house. The Viśvakarmā Śāstra enumerates the following houses. “Dhruva, Dhanya, Jaya, Kānta, Vipula, Vijaya Sumukha, Vimalā, Nanda, Nidhana and Manorama.\"","dev":"विमला","iast":"vimalā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":184,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":74},{"n":348,"gloss":"Adorable","body":"","dev":"वन्द्या","iast":"vandyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":184,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":74},{"n":349,"gloss":"Fond of the worshippers","body":"","dev":"वन्दारुजनवत्सला","iast":"vandārujanavatsalā","pada":[{"dev":"वन्दारु","iast":"vandāru"},{"dev":"जन","iast":"jana"},{"dev":"वत्सला","iast":"vatsalā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":184,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":74},{"n":350,"gloss":"The speaker of the word","body":"Or, l'agrădini is a certain deity so called. The Tripurā Siddhānta says: As she always abides in the form of speech on the tongue of all her devotees, she is known in\nthe world as Vāgvādinī.\" The Laghustava also (Slo. 15) says, Since thou art the origin of all words, thou art known in the world as Vagvādini.\"","dev":"वाग्वादिनी","iast":"vāgvādinī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":184,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":74},{"n":351,"gloss":"Having beautiful hair","body":"Or, Vāmaka, men, and Isɑ, lord, i.e., Śiva, his wife is Vāmakesi. According to the Devī Pr. Vāmakesa is the deity of Jada, one of the sixtyeight sacred places, and his wife is Vamakesī.\nOr, Vāmakeś is one (of the twentyeight) Tantras promulgated by Śiva; as she is treated of in that work,` she is l'amakesi.","dev":"वामकेशी","iast":"vāmakeśī","pada":[{"dev":"वाम","iast":"vāma"},{"dev":"केशी","iast":"keśī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":185,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":74},{"n":352,"gloss":"Residing in the circle of fire","body":"Vahnimaṇḍala, is that in the Mūlādhāra, or that in the supreme ether (Paramākās?).\nOr, Vahni means three, hence three mandalas, thus she resides in the three circles of the moon, the sun and the fire.\n(77)","dev":"वह्निमण्डलवासिनी","iast":"vahnimaṇḍalavāsinī","pada":[{"dev":"वह्नि","iast":"vahni"},{"dev":"मण्डल","iast":"maṇḍala"},{"dev":"वासिनी","iast":"vāsinī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":185,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":74},{"n":353,"gloss":"The kalpa creeper of the devotees","body":"The kalpa creeper yields everything desired.\nOr, kalpa means imperfect, i.., she makes perfect (lit.\nluta, spread over) her imperfect devotees.\nOr, Latā, musk, she sheds fragrance on her devotees. According to Rabhasa, Lata means heartpea, musk, jasamine and panigrass.\" The meaning is that she gladdens her imperfect (lit. half) devotees by the gift of all devotion. This is mentioned in the Śaktirahasya : “He who worships Bhavānī irregularly or with imperfect devotion, in the next birth becomes possessed of regular and perfect devotion.\"","dev":"भक्तिमत्कल्पलतिका","iast":"bhaktimatkalpalatikā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":185,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":75},{"n":354,"gloss":"Releasing the ignorant from bondage","body":"The three circles are the moon, in the sahasrāra, the sun, in the anāhata, and the fire in the mūlādhāra. This may mean also the three parts of Pañcadaśī mantra.\nPaśus (lit. cattle) are those who are devoid of the conviction of nonseparation (abhedajnāna). The Br. Up. (1. 4, 10) says, Now, he who worships another deity, thinking the deity is one and himself another individual, he does not know. He is like a Paśu.\" Here the word Yonyām (yah, one and anyām, another deity) means the triangular (Sri) cakra. Because the Śruti (Tai.) says, ** In that golden_threepetalled bud_three (syllabled mantra, ¿e., of Devī) is established.\" Hence the paśus are those who are devoid of wisdom.\nPas (bondage): pū, thirst, Aw, hunger. (hunger and thirst) belongs only to the ignorant, and she 17, completely manhinī, releases. The ignorants are called Paśus because they possess the desire of eating and drinking only. The Śruti says, Now the other pas'us understand only hunger and thirst; they do not speak of the known (Brahman); they do not see the known, they do not know the future, nor this nor the other world.”\nOr, Paśu (avyaya indeclinable) means completely, as in the Śruti Samyagarthe, etc.,\" the word Paśu is thus explained, Paśu the noose of Varuṇa, from which she frees her devotees.\nPaśu, dice, ri, much, mochinī, throwing. The meaning is that in gambling with Śiva, while throwing the dice on the board, she conquered him.\nPasupa, the protector (pa) of paśus, i.e., the supreme Śiva; as, those who desire to obtain Śiva, such ones she emancipates (mochinī), entirely (vi).\nParus, all things from Brahmā, to a tree, because they have the quality of parus (i.e., the desire of eating and drinking); Paa avidyā because it is the means of bondage. The Saura Sam. explaining the word, avidyā says, “It is the cause of all bondage.\"\nOr, Paras, the modifications (seven) or the categories (sixteen) of it (avidyā); Vimochini she releases them by (means of) devotion to Śiva. The Liṅga Pr.: \"From\nBrahmā to trees are the Paśus of the tridentbearer, the god of gods, because they all have the nature of beasts (Paśus).\" The twentyfour categories, (incluling) illusion (Māyā) action (Karma) and the qualities (guṇas), and the objects also are called Paśus because they bind the Jīvas. Bound by these, all embodied souls are emancipated by devotions to Śiva alone.\"\nThere are fivefold afflictions, viz., nescience, egoism, desire, anger, ardent attachment (to life). Nescience is the absence of discrimination between the Self and the nonself. Egoism is the assumption that body, etc., which are not Self are Self. Desire is the craving after flowers, scents, etc., which are the means of bodily enjoyment. Anger is aversion to whatever obstructs the attainment of them. Ardent attachment is not renouncing a thing even though, one knows it is not beneficial. The same is explained in the Yoga Sūtra, (III, 3 to 9). But the De. Bhag. Pr. says, 66 (1) nondiscrimination is darkness (Tamas); (2) bewilderment of mind is confusion (Moha); (3) desire of worldly pleasures is great confusion (Mahi moha); (4) death is deep indignation (Andhatāmisra); and (5) anger is called indignation (Tāmisra). Thus nescience which sprung from the great Ātman (Brahman) is fivefold.\" But here words Tamas, etc., are only different names for the five afflictions mentioned above. Death here corresponds to ardent attachment (of yoga sūtra division). This is explained in the Liṅga Pr. : O best of men, the wise say that nescience, egoism, desire, hatred and ardent attachment, these five are the Pāśas. The learned say, the afflictions are five, namely, darkness, confusion, great confusion, deep indignation and indignation. Nescience, they say is darkness; egoism is confusion; desire is great confusion, attachment (to life) is deep indignation and hatred is indignation.\" The divisions of these five afflictions are given in the same work : There are eight kinds of darkness, eight of confusion, ten of great\nconfusion, eighteen of deep indignation and eight of indignation.\" These are in all fiftytwo. Some say that indignation has ten more, making sixtytwo in all. These (sixtytwo) afflictions differ, according to the three kinds of men (Paśus), viz., the worldly men, the ascetics (yogin) and the knowers of truth (Tatvajna). The De. Bhag. Pr. says, In the knowers of truth these afflictions sleep, in tho yogins, they are burned up, in those attached to worldly objects they arise unimpeded.\"\nThe Kulirava says, The Pāśas are eight, viz., pity, doubt, fear, shame, disgust, family, custom and caste.\" The above enumerations of the afflictions are not contradictory, the larger are simply elaborators of the smaller enumerations. These larger divisions are not here explained in letail for fear of prolixity.\nThus in the text the word Pāśa according to the rule (of Vararuci's system) though it indicates the number iftyone, (Sa five and pʊ, one) is here taken, according to the rule of approximity as fiftytwo. Thus we see in the (first) verse Nityananda vapuh, etc., of the Saradītilaka when the word for fifty occurs, explained as fiftytwo by Harṣa Dīkṣita and others. Therefore Paśu means fiftytwo; Pāśas, afflictions, vimocinī, remover. The Śiva Rahasya says: He alone who binds the Paśus by the five afflictions and fiftytwo Pašus, is able, being worshipped with much devotion, to free them.\" So much for Pauranic interpretation.\nOr, atoms (anu), difference (Bheda), and action (Karma), are the three (Paśus). Of these ignorance is anu; this is of two kinds; the one is the absence of the congnition of the nature of the Self which is Intelligence itself; the second is the (erroneous) cognition of the self in body, etc. These two are together called the impurity of anu (Ānava Mālā).\nAnu is so called because it limits the unlimited Self. This is said in the Saura Sam.: \"It is called anu because\n22*\nit causes the limitation of the Self; as it is impure it is\ncalled impurity (Mālā).\"\nOf\nBheda means division of the one self into many. this, the root cause is called Māyā, that is the sixth affliction to be added (to the five). This is one (group). The group of the categories, the products of these (six) from the seventh to thirtysixth, is another one. These two together are called the impurity of illusion (Māyā Mālā).\nKarma (actions) means the product of ordained or prohibited actions, capable of giving bodies, and invisible (adṛṣṭa). This is of two kinds, good and bad; together these are called the impurity of action (Karma Mālā).\nThus in these three, the latter have the qualities of the former. These are all explained in the Pratyabhijnā Śāstra: \"The loss of the perception of independence, and not understanding the independence, thus there are two kinds of impurities belonging to anu. Because this takes away one's own nature. This impurity of illusion is the idea of the duality of the Self which gives birth and enjoyment, The impurity of action is leading one to the perception of necessary actions. Thus the three (Pāśas) (exist) by the power of Māyā only.\"\nThis is more clearly explained in the Śaiva Sūtra Vārtika: \"If, of the waking soul (Vis'va) as above described, intelligence is the body, how is it connected with bondage? To remove this doubt, the great Îsvara replies with the Sūtras, treating of the contact and noncontact with the Pāśas\" (Sūtra, I, 2). “Ignorance (is) the bondage.” (Vārtika): \"The first ignorance is cognition in the nonself of the Self, which is the source of intelligence; the second is thinking of body, etc., which are notself, as Self. These two belong to anu. Mālā, the impurity, the man attributes to himself, bringing about bondage by his own will. But the anu impurity is not the sole bond.\" (Sūtra, I, 3) : Birth (yognih) is the group (varga). Kāla (Part) is the body. Yoni means difference (Bheda),\nthe cause of extension, i.e., Māyā. Varga the group of the categories, sprung from that (Māyā), from time to earth this is the impurity of Māyā, which is (the cause) of the perception of duality.\nKāla (lit. with part, i.e., embodied souls) separates men after entering into the body, limited, and in the nature of good, and bad. Body is that which is called the impurity of action. In these (last) two the first impurity is included.\nThus there are three bonds, the second includes the first, the third the other two. The word paśu indicates, the three kinds of Jīvas resulting from the three bonds. Though, by the combinations of all possible ways with the three bonds, there are seven kinds of Jīvas, yet in reality, as the second bond includes the first and the third the other two, there are only three kinds. These paśus in the Nityāhṛdaya are termed respectively, pure, mixed, and impure (for Anu Bheda, and Karma Malas). But in the Svacchanda Tr. and other works they are called respectively Vijñānakevala (mere discrimination), Pralayākala (lit. until the dissolution with parts temporarily) and Sakala (with parts always). So it is described (in the above books), “There are three kinds of Paśus : among these some are Sakalas, some are called Pralayākalas, O, Maheśvarī, the remaining ones are the Vijnānakevalas. Hear their respective natures.\" Those bound by the three Pāśas are called Sakalas. It is said, Covered with impurity from the beginning, covered with illusion and action, though pure (in himself), regarding the body as Śiva (Self), ever tainted with separateness, weak, continually active, enjoying the fruits of his own actions, ever enamoured with pleasure, this is the Sakala Paśu.\" In the above quotation impurity, illusion, mean respectively Anu and Bheda. The Sakala Paśus are of two kinds, one who has burned his impurities, and the other whose impurities are unburned; of those, who have burned the\nmantras.\nthree impurities (Anu, Bheda and Karma) are the one hundred and eighteen sages (Siddhas); they, by power of the full grace of Śiva obtained the dominion over the Of these the hundred are called Rudras, eight, Satamandalins, eight Krodhabhattāraka (Durvāsa) and others, one is Viresvara and the last is Srikan/ha. The others also, Śiva himself in the form of a teacher, blesses. Those who have not burned the three impurities, are appointed to lifferent births in order to burn their impurities through fruition. That itself is a sign of graciousness to (them). For it is said, 'When the impurities are not come to fruition, they can be the recipients of ordinary grace; thus ( Mahesvarî, the three kinds of Paśus obtain mastery and dominion by the grace and he out of compassion appoints them to different births in order to burn (their impurities).\"\nof Śiva alone\nThe mixed\nThose who are bound by the two Pakus anu and action, are Pralayākalas (temporarily bound); of these there are two divisions, those who have and those who have not burned their impurities; among these the latter, by the power of action are born in superior (from a religious standpoint) positions. The former partaking of the grace of the Lord become the lords of the universe. This is said in the Svacchanda Sangraha. (class) who have right notions, are called Pralayākalas ; they have the body of eight cities (Puryash/aka) ; according to their different actions, O beloved one, they obtain many births; and having enjoyed the fruits of their own actions, when their actions are in equilibrium they become Śiva himself; they, having completely burned the impurity of action, having retained become the lords of the world, ruling the Tattvas, Jala something (of action) they (water), etc. These (lords) are of three kinds.”\nThose who are bound by the impurity of anu only are the Vijninakovalas (those mere discriminators). For it is\n173 said The Paśu who is bound by one impurity (of anu) alone is called Vijñānakevala; O beloved one as he, completely burned the impurity (of anu) became the mere discriminator.\" These also are of two kinds, i.c., one has and the other has not finished with sin. The former become the lords of knowledge (Vidyesvaras), the latter are the spirits of the seven crores of powerful mantras. But these must not be considered inanimate (sound); they like ourselves possess Caitanya. So the Mṛgendra Sam. says, Then who has got rid of the beginningless impurity (of anu) becomes omnipotent, omniscient, Śiva himself, and throws off the meshes of the net of anu, which before held him down.\" The Agamikas also say, Śambhu who blesses all, gives happiness and salvation to all those who are bound by anu and persevere in their work (i.c., burning impurity)\". The mantras are called anu because they belong to the soul, who has not burned impurity. Thus there are endless divisions of the six kinds of Paśus according to their progress in burning the impurities. As the births and dominion attained by these, according to their progress, are for the sake of burning the impurities by fruition; they are (intended as the) means of salvation.\nBut if release is the work of Śiva, how does Devī become an agent? Without possessing the power (Śakti) of release Śiva can do nothing; therefore the agency of release should be acknowledged to belong to Śakti alone by (the rule) of agreement and continuity. This is attested to by great men: “I adore with all my heart that Śakti which is Intelligence, primeval through whom Śiva is able to confer happiness and salvation on all Paśus.\" Moreover the agency is independence, because says Pāṇini (1,4,54) \"The agency is independence.\" That belongs to Śakti sø the Śakti Sūtra says: The independent intelligence (cit) is the cause of the establishment of the Universe:\" Śaiva Sūtra (1, 1) says, Intelligence is the self (Śiva),”\nBut a\nAs there is no expression (in the above Śaiva Sūtra) of independence and from the force of the neuter gender (i.e., the word Caitanya is neuter) Śiva has not the quality of agency, etc. But Śakti and Śiva Sūtras of above (i.e., Intelligence is Devī and Intelligence is Śiva) and the saying of the wise \"intelligence (cit) applies to Śiva as well as to Śakti,” mean in such cases (that Śiva and Śakti) are inseparable (i.e., both are one and same). is the doctrine of the Śaiva schools.\nSuch\nThus Paśus means those above discribed, Pāśa, impurities vi, according to their deserts, Mcchini releases.","dev":"पशुपाशविमोचिनी","iast":"paśupāśavimocinī","pada":[{"dev":"पशुपाश","iast":"paśupāśa"},{"dev":"विमोचिनी","iast":"vimocinī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":185,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":75},{"n":355,"gloss":"Destroyer of all heretics","body":"The Liṅga Pr. describes the nature of the heretics 'thus: Observers of vows not enjoined in the Vedas and those who are excluded from the ceremonies enjoined by the Śruti and Smṛtis are called heretics, they are not to be spoken with by the Twiceborn. The Brahmā Vaivarta Pr. also 66 ‘(1) Purāṇas, (2) Nyāya, (3) Mīmāṃsā, (4) Dharma Śāstras, (5 to 10) the Angas (i.e., the supplements Śikṣā to Jyotiṣa), (11 to 14) the Vedas (four) which are the source of knowledge, these fourteen are the seat of righteousness (Dharma sthāna).” teen) Taking these (four- These are the true, what is beyond these imagined by men is called heresy (Pashnanda); this is created by the great confusion for the bewilderment of the Daityas.” If the reading is Pākhanda, it gives the same meaning. The explanation of the word is, of the Vedas and Khaṇḍa cutting off.” Pa means the meaning","dev":"संहृताशेषपाषण्डा","iast":"saṃhṛtāśeṣapāṣaṇḍā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":193,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":75},{"n":356,"gloss":"Inciting to right action","body":"Because she destroys the wicked, and she incites to right action. The expression means: She gives the inclination to observe the precepts enjoined in the Karma Kāṇḍa and the Advaita Brahman in the Jnanakanda, through the means laid down in the Purāṇas. It is said in the Karma Pr. by Devī herself. \"The eighteen Purāṇas, O\n175 King, were promulgated by Vyāsa, by the command of Brahman; in them righteousness (Dharma) is established. The other supplementary (upa) Purāṇas were promulgated by his pupils. In every age (yuga) he, (Vyāsa) the knower of the science of righteousness, is the promulgator of all (Purāṇas, etc.). Śikṣā, Kalpa, Grammar, Nirukta, Chandas, and Jyotiṣa, and logic etc., (four) and the four Vedas, thus the fourteen (scriptures) are the sources of knowledge. The righteousness (Dharma), O best one, is not to be found elsewhere. Thus the supreme Dharma, which has come down from the grandsire, Manu, Vyāsa and others, by my command, is established till the dissolution of the universe.\" (78)","dev":"सदाचारप्रवर्तिका","iast":"sadācārapravartikā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":193,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":75},{"n":357,"gloss":"She is the moonlight delighting those burned by the triple fire of misery","body":"The three miseries are, that belong to body, to elements, and to deities. Fire, Saṃsāra, (The man free from it becomes) as the Śruti (Br. Up., IV, 4-25) says, Without decay and immortal.\"","dev":"तापत्रयाभिसंतप्तसमाह्लादनचन्द्रिका","iast":"tāpatrayābhisaṃtaptasamāhlādanacandrikā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.913,"cpage":194,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":76},{"n":358,"gloss":"Ever young","body":"","dev":"तरुणी","iast":"taruṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":194,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":76},{"n":359,"gloss":"Worshipped by ascetics","body":"Or, Tapa, Saṃsāra the father of misery, Sara, essence, a, deep, dhya meditation, i.e., Devī is the essential object of meditation amidst the earthly misery.","dev":"तापसाराध्या","iast":"tāpasārādhyā","pada":[{"dev":"तापस","iast":"tāpasa"},{"dev":"आराध्या","iast":"ārādhyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":194,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":76},{"n":360,"gloss":"Slender waisted","body":"Tanumadhyā is the deity worshipped in the country of Kāñcī; for it is said, \"May Devī, Tanumadhyā, the wife of Bilvesvara residing on the bank of the Niva (river), protect me.\"\nTanumadya is the name of a certain metre. The Piṅgalā Sūtra, says, \"If there is one Tagana and yagana in each foot it is called Tanumadhyā.\"\n66 They","dev":"तनुमध्या","iast":"tanumadhyā","pada":[{"dev":"तनु","iast":"tanu"},{"dev":"मध्या","iast":"madhyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":194,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":76},{"n":361,"gloss":"Remover of darkness","body":"Tamas, ignorance. The Īśa. Up. (9) says, who worship ignorance enter blind darkness.\" The Brahmā.\nPr. also, The Aranyaka Śruti (Tai. Ar.) blames all worship except that of Vilya.\"","dev":"तमोऽपहा","iast":"tamo'pahā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":194,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":76},{"n":362,"gloss":"Intelligence","body":"(79)\nCit is wisdom opposed to avidyā. The Śakti Sūtra, Cit is independent and the cause of the establishment of the Universe.\" The Mahavasishtha, \"She (Devī) is called cit, because she is the life of those who desire life.\"","dev":"चिति","iast":"citi","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.8,"cpage":195,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":77},{"n":363,"gloss":"She is what is denoted by the term Tat","body":"Tat, the word which occurs in the transcendental phrases means the conditioned Brahman endowed with the work of creation, etc., of the Universe. The same word Tut also indicates, (indirectly, Lakshya) unconditioned Brahman, who is without attributes. The relation between the two Brahmans (unconditioned and conditioned) is the sameness (Tādātmya).","dev":"स्तत्पदलक्ष्यार्था","iast":"statpadalakṣyārthā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.909,"cpage":195,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":77},{"n":364,"gloss":"The one essence of intelligence. rūpiṇī.)","body":"(Chidekarasa-\nBut even the unconditioned (Brahman) has the attributes of bliss, etc. (To answer this question) this name is given, Bliss consciousness and other attributes'; the expression means, that those attributes are inseparable from her. This is explained in the Panchapadika (of Padmapādācārya): \"Bliss, the perception of the objects and eternity are attributes which although inseparable from consciousness appear as different.\"\nOr, the unconditioned Brahman is not the (real) nature of Īśvara (conditioned one), because both are different, hence the relation (between the two) just described above (No. 363) is not possible. To remove this doubt this name is introduced. Chidekarasarupini, i.e., She is ever nonseparated from the cit. The relation of these two is possible as the two are one and the same. Though the two appear as different (by attributes) yet at the same time they are one.","dev":"चिदेकरसरूपिणी","iast":"cidekarasarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"चित्","iast":"cit"},{"dev":"एकरस","iast":"ekarasa"},{"dev":"रूपिणी","iast":"rūpiṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.545,"cpage":195,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":77},{"n":365,"gloss":"The totality of the bliss of Brahmā, etc., is but a minute portion of her own bliss","body":"The bliss of Indra and others is but a drop in the Ocean, in comparison with the bliss of Devī. Brahmā, etc.; Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Rudra, who possess the quality of creation, preservation and destruction. The Śruti (Tai. Up., II, S) says, Other beings live on a mere part of\nthis bliss.\"\nAgain the same Śruti, from man's bliss more and more in the higher grades, describing, includes (among the bliss of Devas) the bliss of Prajāpati, also, as it is limited and as it is less than the bliss of unconditioned Brahman, and consequently unfit for human aspiration, and establishes that the bliss of Brahman (alone) is unlimited. The meaning is that the word Tat (863) indicates the unconditioned Brahman, in order to agree with the authorities (scriptures) which establish the meaning of them by (the rule) of beginning, etc. (Upakrama, etc.) as the knowledge as the means of Puruṣārthas.\nsport.\nLava according to the Viśva means, particle and","dev":"स्वात्मानम्दलवीभूतब्रह्माद्यानन्दसंततिः","iast":"svātmānamdalavībhūtabrahmādyānandasaṃtatiḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.936,"cpage":196,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":77},{"n":366,"gloss":"Pară. (80) Since the power of expression depends upon the correspondence of the words and their meanings, as the unconditioned Brahman also is included in the sound (Śabdabrahman)","body":"why then is Brahman indirectly (Lakṣaṇa) indicated (as the Vedāntins say) by the words \"existence, etc.\"? Because the words which are the physical forms of speech (Vaikhārī) only express (identify) the physical form of Brahman (the Virāṭ) and do not correspond to the pure (unconditioned) Brahman. Thus replying he divides speech (into four forms).\nThe following should be noted. During Pralaya, when the actions (Karma) of beings about to be manifested are not yet ripe, the Brahman, bound my Māyā and by those actions\n*\n(Karmabhinnamaya lit. Māyā itself in the form of actions) is called Ghanibhûte (the congealed one).\nWhen, in course of time, the actions begin to ripen and the former state of unripeness disappears, that state is called Vichikirsha (longing for action). At the moment of ripeness, when the modification of Māyā appears, Brahman, endowed with May in the form of ripened actions, is called Avyakta (unmanifested). Hence creation is attributed in the Smṛtis to the Avyakta: \"O best of twiceborn, from that is sprung the Avyakta with the three qualities.\" That (Avyakta), as it is the sprouting root of the universe, is termed as the Kāraṇabindu (the cause dot). The Prapanchasāra says, That Intelligence, the congealed one, desirous of action, attains the state (of Kāraṇa) bindu.\" From this Kāraṇa bindu proceeds in order the Kārya binilu (effect dot). From the latter Nāda (sound); thence the Bīja; thus the three came into being. These (three) are also called by the words (the supreme) subtle (Sakshama) and physical (Sthala). In their nature they partake respectively of intelligence (cit), combined of intelligence and nonintelligence (chidachit), and of nonintelligence (achit). So the Rahasyagama says, That Bindu (the cause dot) when in the course of time it sprouts, becomes three. Its three divisions are gross, subtle, and supreme. These are called, Bindu Nāda and Bīja.' These four, including the Kāraṇa bindu, in their divine aspect (Adhidaivata) are Avyakta, Īśvara, Hiraṇyagarbha, and Virāṭ, also the four Śaktis, viz., Santa, Vāma, Jyeshta and Raudrī, and also, Ambikā, Icchā, Jñāna, and Kriyā. In the elemental aspect (Adhibhata) these are the four seats, viz., Kāmarūpa, Pārṇagiri, Jālandhara, and Oḍyāna. So the Nityāhṛdaya says.\nIn the bodily aspects (Adhyātma) the Kāraṇabindu, resides in the Mūlādhāra and is known by the words as Śakti Piṇḍa (monosyllabled mantra), Kundalini, etc. The Ācārya (Laghustava, Slo. 2) says by the following\nwords about the Kundalini : 44 There is a Śakti, called Kundalini having known her who is ever engaged in the work of the creation of the universe, a man never again enters his mother's womb as a child (ie., he has no Saṃsāra).\" This is the Kāraṇabindu in its nondifferentiated condition. When it sprouts, in order to create the three, viz., Karyabindu, etc., then the anmanifested sound called Śabdabrahman (Brahmanword) arises in it. It is said also, \"From this differentiation of that (Kiraṇa) bindu comes the unmanifested sound; that sound is termed Śabdabrahman by those learned in sound.”\nThis sound, since it is one with the Kāraṇabindu and is therefore allpervading, yet first appears in the Mûladhara of persons by the power of air acted upon by the effort of manifestation. It is said, “In the Mūlādhāra in the body the air first appears; that air acted upon by the effort of a person desiring to speak, produces the allpervading Śabdabrahman.\" That Śabdabrahman which is in the Kārana bindu, when it is manifested, remaining motionless (nishspanda) in its own place, is called Parā speech.\nThe same (Śabdabrahman), produced by the same air proceeding as far as the navel, joined with the reasoning intellect, (Manas) possessing the nature of the manifested Kāryabindu with simple motion (Samīnyaspanda) is named Paśyantī speech.\nNext the same Sabdabrahmau, produced by the same air proceeding as far as the heart, joined with the determining understanding (Buddhi), in the manifested Nāda, endowed with special motion (Viseshaspanda) is called Madhyami speech.\nNext the same (Sabila brahman), produced by the same air, proceeding as far as the month, devoloped in the throat, etc., in the form of articulation, capable of being heard by the ears of others' possessing the nature of the manifested Bīja, with the universal motion (Spashfatara) is called Vaikhārī speech. It is explained by the Ācārya :\n'That (sound) which first arises in the Mūlādhāra is called Pară; next the Paśyantī; next when it goes as far as the heart and is joined to the understanding (Buddhi) it is called Madhyamā; and the full manifestation is Vaikhārī ; . . . Thus, articulated sound is produced by air.\" The Nityā Tr. also says, The Parā form rises in the Mūlādhāra produced by air; the same (air) rising upwards, manifested in the Svādhiṣṭhāna, attains the Pas'yanti state. The same slowly rising upwards and manifested in the Anāhata, united with the understanding is termed Madhyamā. Again rising upwards appearing in the Visudhi and comes out from the throat as Vaikhārī.\" Thus, though there are four kinds of speech gross minded men who do not understand the firstthree, viz., Parā, etc., think speech to be Vaikhārī alone. The Śruti says, \"Hence men think that alone to be speech which is imperfect.\" That is to say, imperfect by not possessing the firstthree forms. Another Śruti also (Ṛg Veda 1, 164, 45): \"Four are the definite grades of the speech; those Brāhmaṇas who are wise know them : three deposited, are in secret and motionless; men speak the fourth grade of speech.\nThe Su. Sam. (IV, 4-23) also says, Apada the motionless (Śabda brahman) became pada (the four forms of speech) and pada may become apada. He who knows the distinction between pada and apada, he really sees, (i.e., himself becomes Brahman).”\nThus by the Śrutis and Smṛtis it is concluded that the word Tat has four divisions (parā, etc.). The characteristic, of the words (pada) according to Gautama. (Sūtra, II, 2-50) is, \"The pala is that which has a termination.” Pāṇini also (Sūtra, I, IV, 14) : That which ends in Sup (nominal) and in Tip (verbal) is called Pada.\" Again the Sup (termination) has four divisions.\nEven the word Tat in the parā form only denotes the unmanifested form (Avyakta, the cause of Pară, etc.)\nwhich has the three qualities, and does not indicate the unconditioned Brahman which is above that (Avyakta) ; how can the word Tat in the Vaikharf form indicate Brahman? It does not. But even if the word Tādātmya (identity) has the power to denote (Brahman) yet the word Tat can only indicate it indirectly.\n•Or, Parā (highest): She is called Parā because she is the highest of the three (lower) speeches: The Tripurā Siddhānta explains the word Parā by different ways, As she is pleased with Paranandanatha (a guru) she is called Parā. As she is celebrated in the work called Parānanda, she is Parā, and as she is the supreme source of grace, she is Parā. Thus Śāmbhavī is called Pară.”","dev":"परा","iast":"parā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.2,"cpage":196,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":78},{"n":367,"gloss":"Inner consciousness","body":"Pratyak, inwardly, one who approaches the Self, rūpa, form, i.e., the unmanifested Brahman, as her form.","dev":"प्रत्यक्चितीरूपा","iast":"pratyakcitīrūpā","pada":[{"dev":"प्रत्यक्","iast":"pratyak"},{"dev":"चिती","iast":"citī"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":200,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":78},{"n":368,"gloss":"Paśyantī. She has another name, namely, Uttīrṇa","body":"The Saubhāgya Sudhodaya says, As she sees all in herself, and as she rises Uttīrṇa) above the path of action, hence this Mother is called Paśyantī, and Uttirnā.”","dev":"पश्यन्ती","iast":"paśyantī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":200,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":78},{"n":369,"gloss":"Supreme deity","body":"She is to be worshipped as the Supreme deity.","dev":"परदेवता","iast":"paradevatā","pada":[{"dev":"पर","iast":"para"},{"dev":"देवता","iast":"devatā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":200,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":78},{"n":370,"gloss":"Madhyamā. Because she abides in the midst. It is said: \"She is neither like Pas'yant!","body":"Madhyamā.\nBecause she abides in the midst. It is said: \"She is neither like Pas'yant!, nor does she proceed outwards like Vaikhārī, with articulation fully developed; but she is in the middle between these two.\"","dev":"मध्यमा","iast":"madhyamā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":200,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":78},{"n":371,"gloss":"In the form of Vaikhārī","body":"Vaikhārī: Vi, much, Khara, hard, i.e., speech in its physical form.\nAccording to the Saubhāgya Sudhodaya, l'ai, certainly Kha, the cavity (of the ear) ra to go, or to enter.\nBut according to the saying of the Yoga Śāstras, \"She is called Vaikhārī because she was produced by the Prāṇa called Vikhara.”","dev":"वैखरीरूपा","iast":"vaikharīrūpā","pada":[{"dev":"वैखरी","iast":"vaikharī"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":200,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":78},{"n":372,"gloss":"The swan in the minds of the devotees","body":"[As the mythical swans live in the celestial lake called Mānasa so she lives in the minds (Mânasa) of her devotees.]","dev":"भक्तमानसहंसिका","iast":"bhaktamānasahaṃsikā","pada":[{"dev":"भक्त","iast":"bhakta"},{"dev":"मानस","iast":"mānasa"},{"dev":"हंसिका","iast":"haṃsikā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":201,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":78},{"n":373,"gloss":"The vital current of Kāmeśvara, prānanadi.)","body":"(81) (Kāmesvara-\nThe Achirya (Sau. La. S1. 28), **When Śiva drank the virulant poison, his days did not end owing, O mother, to the power of your ear ornaments.\"\n\" I","dev":"कामेश्वरप्राणनाडी","iast":"kāmeśvaraprāṇanāḍī","pada":[{"dev":"कामेश्वर","iast":"kāmeśvara"},{"dev":"प्राण","iast":"prāṇa"},{"dev":"नाडी","iast":"nāḍī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.615,"cpage":201,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":79},{"n":374,"gloss":"Judge of actions","body":"Kṛta good and bad (actions).\n\"The Sun, the moon, the god of death, time, and the five elements, these nine are the witnesses of good and bad actions,\" she is not separated from these, she is called Kṛtajñā.\nOr, Kṛta, the actions done, jnă, i.c., she rewards them by (imparting) knowledge.\nOr, Kṛta (vat) alreadyacquired, jn, knowledge, she\npossesses.\nKṛta; Kṛta Yuga, the golden age, as then righteousness was perfectly established ; knowledge (juu̸), also was perfect, i.e., she has perfect knowledge (as the golden age had).\nAccording to the science of dice there are four Dyûtas which are called Kṛta, Treta, Dvāpara. and Kālī, and numbered respectively four, three, two and one ; but they count ten, six, three and one, respectively: because in the higher number the lower ones are included. So the Sruți (Chānd. Up., IV, 3-8) says, “Now these five and other fivë, make ten, that is (represents) the Kṛta cast.\" Hence, according to the science of Dyuta, he who conquers the Kṛta, is understood to have conquered the other three. For the Śruti (Chānd. Up., IV, 1-4) says, As in the game\nSuch ornaments are worn by a married woman only during her coverture.\nof Dice all the lower casts belong to him who has conquered with the Kṛta cast.\" Kṛta cast, her knowledge is embracing.\nThe meaning is like the allpervading and all-\nOr, she knows the Kritu cast. The expression is that she always conquers when she casts the dice with Śiva.","dev":"कृतज्ञा","iast":"kṛtajñā","pada":[{"dev":"कृत","iast":"kṛta"},{"dev":"ज्ञा","iast":"jñā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":201,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":79},{"n":375,"gloss":"Worshipped by Kāma","body":"The Arunopanishad (Tai. Ar. I, 11) says, (There is a) bodiless son of Lakṣmī; though he is without mind, he has life. He acquired that jewel (the Pañcadaśī mantra).\"","dev":"कामपूजिता","iast":"kāmapūjitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":202,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":79},{"n":376,"gloss":"Filled with the essence of love","body":"Or, as he refers below to the Jālandhara and Odhyān: centres (Pi/has) these two (375 and 376) represent the Pīṭhas called Kāmarūpa and Pārṇagiri. Śṛṅga, two (i.e. two horns), petal, rásu, six (i.e., six flavours), so Sringārurusu means, the centre called Anāhata which has twice six petals. Sum, frequently, pūrna, remains, ¿.e.. she frequently remains in that centre. According to the Tantras, the well known four centres in the elements (Adhibhūta, viz.. Kamarûpa, etc.,) remain in the body in the Mūlādhāra, Anāhata, Viśuddhi, and in the Äjna respectively. Hence this name (indicates) Anāhata and Pārṇagiri centres (in the body and in the world).\nOr, Śṛṅga chief, arara, covering, that is to say the Avidyā which veils Sa, with sampūrṇa, Brahmā (lit. perfect), i.e., she is both the conditioned and unconditioned Brahman.","dev":"शृङ्गाररससंपूर्णा","iast":"śṛṅgārarasasaṃpūrṇā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":202,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":79},{"n":377,"gloss":"Victorious. (Jaya.)","body":"According to the Padma Pr. \"Jaya is the deity worshipped in the Varāha mountain.\"","dev":"जया","iast":"jayā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":202,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":79},{"n":378,"gloss":"Remaining in the Jālandhara","body":"According to the Padma Pr. Jālandhara is a place where she is worshipped under the name of Vishnumukti.\n(82)\n17th verse divides 44 names.","dev":"जालन्धरस्थिता","iast":"jālandharasthitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":202,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":79},{"n":379,"gloss":"Abiding in the centre called Oḍyāna","body":"","dev":"ओड्याणपीठनिलया","iast":"oḍyāṇapīṭhanilayā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.947,"cpage":203,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":80},{"n":380,"gloss":"Dwelling in the centre of Bindu","body":"Bindu mandala, the centre called Sarvānandamaya. Some explain that Bindu means white, Mandala, circle, i.e., the Brahmarandhra.","dev":"बिन्दुमण्डलवासिनी","iast":"bindumaṇḍalavāsinī","pada":[{"dev":"बिन्दु","iast":"bindu"},{"dev":"मण्डल","iast":"maṇḍala"},{"dev":"वासिनी","iast":"vāsinī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":203,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":80},{"n":381,"gloss":"To be worshipped in the secret by sacrificial rites","body":"The Ācārya (San. La. Sl. 9) says: In the Sahasrāra lotus, in the secret place, you sport with your husband.” Raho, in the secret (in the Sahasrāra), yāga, the sacrifice of the eight oblations of good actions, etc., in the fire of cit supported by the golden Kundalini; or Rahoyaga means the meeting (of Śiva and Śakti) in a lonely place (in the Sahasrāra); Krama, stepping into (that place); ārúdhyā is to be attained.\nĀpastamba says,\nSalvation is not for him who confines himself to scriptural words alone, nor for him who delights in fine houses, or in rich food and clothes or in worldly ideas; but the man of firm vow, who leads a lonely life and has ceased from (worldly) pursuits, attains salvation, and he who constantly rejoices in meditation on the Self and who never injures anything, attains salvation.\"","dev":"रहोयागक्रमाराध्या","iast":"rahoyāgakramārādhyā","pada":[{"dev":"रहस्","iast":"rahas"},{"dev":"याग","iast":"yāga"},{"dev":"क्रम","iast":"krama"},{"dev":"आराध्या","iast":"ārādhyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":203,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":80},{"n":382,"gloss":"Gratified by the Secret oblations","body":"If one\nThe Aruṇa. Up. also (Tai. Ar., I, 11) says, would enter, let him enter, having practised lonely communion.\" According to the method of devotion to the Śrīvidyā one should only adopt the path after having recognised the higher self. Rahastarpaṇa: A Mantra describes it thus: \"Holding in both hands, representing brightness and darkness (Prakāśa and Vimarśa) the sacrificial ladle of Unmanī (a Yogic state), pouring out the ghee of righteousness and unrighteousness and the senses (Kalās)\nin the fire of consciousness, I sacrifice.\" Another description is, I sacrifice the Universe from Earth to Śiva in the fire of saṃvit, ever burning without fuel, and everincreasing, dispelling the darkness of illusion, the centre from which ever emanates beautiful rays.' (83)","dev":"रहस्तर्पणतर्पिता","iast":"rahastarpaṇatarpitā","pada":[{"dev":"रहस्","iast":"rahas"},{"dev":"तर्पण","iast":"tarpaṇa"},{"dev":"तर्पिता","iast":"tarpitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":203,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":80},{"n":383,"gloss":"Conferring immediate grace","body":"She is at once gratified by the sacrifice and oblation mentioned above (381 and 382).","dev":"सद्यः प्रसादिनी","iast":"sadyaḥ prasādinī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":204,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":81},{"n":384,"gloss":"Witness of the universe","body":"","dev":"विश्वसाक्षिणी","iast":"viśvasākṣiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"विश्व","iast":"viśva"},{"dev":"साक्षिणी","iast":"sākṣiṇī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":204,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":81},{"n":385,"gloss":"Herself unwitnessed","body":"There is no witness to the one who is the witness of\nall things.","dev":"साक्षिवर्जिता","iast":"sākṣivarjitā","pada":[{"dev":"साक्षि","iast":"sākṣi"},{"dev":"वर्जिता","iast":"varjitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":204,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":81},{"n":386,"gloss":"Accompanied by the deities of six limbs","body":"The Angas (limbs) are heart, head, tuft of hair, eyes, armour and weapons. She is accompanied by the Śaktis belonging to these. The Jñānārṇava and other works say, Next he should perform the consecration of the limbs which belong to the Srividyamantra. This, one should perform externally in order, afterwards, he should worship the surrounding deities (which are three in number). By these (Āvaraṇa) deities the number nine is completed.\nOr, Ṣaḍaṅgas devatā is Śiva who presides over the six angas. The De. Bhag. Pr. says, \"Omniscience, contentment, nonoriginal wisdom, independence, unfading power and infinity, these, say the learned, are the six angas of the Lord Maheśvara.\"\nOr, Ṣaḍaṅgas are phonetics, ceremonial (kalpa), grammar, lexicography, prosody, and astronomy. Surrounded by the deities of these six, means the Vedas are her form.","dev":"षडङ्गदेवतायुक्त।","iast":"ṣaḍaṅgadevatāyukta","pada":[{"dev":"षडङ्ग","iast":"ṣaḍaṅga"},{"dev":"देवता","iast":"devatā"},{"dev":"युक्त","iast":"yukta"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":204,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":81},{"n":387,"gloss":"Possessing the six qualities","body":"The six guṇas, according to the Kāmandaka (ethical code) are, peace, war, marching, sitting encamped, dividing his forces and allies. The Purāṇas, give out the following: 24*\n\"Prosperity, righteousness, fame, wealth, wisdom and dispassion.\"","dev":"षाड्गुण्यपरिपूरिता","iast":"ṣāḍguṇyaparipūritā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":204,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":81},{"n":388,"gloss":"Ever compassionate","body":"(84)\nOr, Nityaklinną is the deity of the third day of the lunar fortnight. This deity has been described in the Garuḍa Pr. beginning with the verse, \"Next I describe Nityaklinną\nshe is Tripurā herself giving happiness and salvation.\"","dev":"नित्यक्लिन्ना","iast":"nityaklinnā","pada":[{"dev":"नित्य","iast":"nitya"},{"dev":"क्लिन्ना","iast":"klinnā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":205,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":82},{"n":389,"gloss":"Without an equal","body":"The Śruti (Sve. Up., 4, 19): “None is equal to him.\"","dev":"निरुपमा","iast":"nirupamā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":205,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":82},{"n":390,"gloss":"Conferring the bliss of Nirvāṇa","body":"Nirvāṇa: nir, freed; vāna (or Bana) body; because in the Śruti (Pra. Up., II, 2), “ Holding this bow (body),\" and in the word “Girvāna \" by the Vedāntins and Mîmāmsakas this word is explained as body. The Amaras'esha also says, “ Bāna means body and bow.\" So here Nirvāna means without body, i.e., indescribable bliss. In the Karma Pr. Devī says to Himavat: \"If you neglect me, O king of mountains, you cannot attain the pure rest of the supreme Nirvāṇa, therefore seek refuge in me (regarding me) as one, as many, or as combined; thus worshipping me, O great king, you will attain that state.\"","dev":"निर्वाणसुखदायिनी","iast":"nirvāṇasukhadāyinī","pada":[{"dev":"निर्वाण","iast":"nirvāṇa"},{"dev":"सुख","iast":"sukha"},{"dev":"दायिनी","iast":"dāyinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":205,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":82},{"n":391,"gloss":"In the form of sixteen eternal deities","body":"The sixteen deities are from Kāmeśvarī to Tripurasundarī.' The Tantrarāja says, \"The fifteen deities are the limbs of Lalitā who is the first (of the sixteen); as she is endowed with these limbs she is the body, of which they are parts.\"\nOr, nitya, compulsory; a frequent; shodasika, a house where graha sacrifice is performed; rūpa pleased,\nThe names of the fifteen Nityā deities are as follows: (1) Kāmeśvarī, (2) Bhagamalini, (3) Nityaklinua, (4) Bheranda, (5) Vahnivasini, (6) Mahāvajresvari, (7) Sivadūti, (8) Tvarită, (9) Kulasundari, (10) Nityā, (11) Nilapatakini, (12) Vijaya. (13) Sarvamaṅgalā, (14) Jvalamálini, (15) Chitra, and (16) Tripurasundarī. See the Bhavānī Up. Prayogavidhi by Bhāskarārāya.\ni.e., she is pleased by the compulsory sacrifices. The Saksi Rahasya says, 'The mother is as much pleased by a single repetition of the Ṣoḍaśī (sixteensyllabled mantra) as by crores of Vājapeya and of Shodast sacrifices.\"","dev":"नित्याषोडशिकारूपा","iast":"nityāṣoḍaśikārūpā","pada":[{"dev":"नित्या","iast":"nityā"},{"dev":"षोडशिका","iast":"ṣoḍaśikā"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":205,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":82},{"n":392,"gloss":"Possessing half the body of Śrīkaṇṭha","body":"Śrīkaṇṭha : Sri, poison, Kaṇṭha throat, as the poison is fixed in his throat, Śiva is called Śrīkaṇṭha. She forms one body with Śiva. Hence the Br. Up. (1, 4, 1 to 3) says, In the beginning this was Self alone... He then divided himself into two, and thence arose husband and wife.\"\nOr, like Śiva, she has a body of two colours, one half of the body is blue and the other half is white. The Vāyu Pr. says, \"That beneficent one, having the half of the body of Śaṃkara and the right half of her body is white, and the left is blue; thus divide my body, said Brahmā to Devī. O twiceborn ones, thus of the two forms one became Gaurī (white) the other Kall (blue).\"\nAccording to the Mātṛkākośa A means Śrīkaṇṭha, Indra, forehead, and Viṣṇu.\" Thus Śrīkaṇṭha, is A. The letter A is half the body of the other letters in speech. Thus it is said in the Śruti,—“ A is all the speech, that (4) takes on different forms in combination with the different consonants (lit. with Sparsa, i.e., ka to ma and with Ushma, S'a, etc.). The meaning is, that A, the first utterance, is the Parā form which becomes (later on) the Vaikari. So the St. Sam. (IV, 47, 59) : 64 The supreme Śakti, Devī, the form of cit, arises as speech, that (speech) is called Parā. I ever bow down in devotion to that Śakti who is the half body of Śiva.\"\nOr, Devī who has only half a body, the other half is called Śrīkaṇṭha, i.e., the fulfiller (of the half). This is also explained in the same book (Sa. Sam., IV, 47, 60).\nThe Śakti called desire is filled in the belly of Śiva.\" This means in the Śaiva school in the performance of Nyāsa (meditating certain deities on different parts of the\nbody) half the body is filled by the Purnodarī and other Śaktis and the other half by Śrīkaṇṭha of Śiva.\nOr, when we write the letter A (Śrīkaṇṭha) half (ardha) of it is like the Kāmakala (i.e., 3+§=3).","dev":"श्रीकण्ठार्धशरीरिणी","iast":"śrīkaṇṭhārdhaśarīriṇī","pada":[{"dev":"श्रीकण्ठ","iast":"śrīkaṇṭha"},{"dev":"अर्ध","iast":"ardha"},{"dev":"शरीरिणी","iast":"śarīriṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":206,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":82},{"n":393,"gloss":"Luminous","body":"(85)\nPrabhā, the surrounding Āvaraṇa Devatās, viz., animă, etc., because there is a saying, “(Devī) is surrounded by Anim and other luminaries.\" Some learned Jains, Haribhadra and others record in their works Dharmasangrahini, etc., The rays are the qualities, they are not the substratum; among these even, the quality of brightness is not the substratum.\"","dev":"प्रभावती","iast":"prabhāvatī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":207,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":83},{"n":394,"gloss":"In the form of brightness","body":"This indicates the identity of the quality and the possessor of it. Prabhā, Anima and other Devatās. Śruti (Chānd. Up., 3-14-2): Mind itself, brightness itself.\"\nDe. Bhag. Pr. says,","dev":"प्रभारूपा","iast":"prabhārūpā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":207,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":83},{"n":395,"gloss":"Celebrated","body":"Because Devī is known by all in the shape of 'I ’. That (Devī) all men recognise under the form of 'I'.\" The first verse of the same book also says, \"We meditate upon the primeval Vidyā in the form of the Universal Caitanya.\"\n396.","dev":"प्रसिद्धा","iast":"prasiddhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":207,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":83},{"n":396,"gloss":"Celebrated. (Prasiddha.)","body":"Because Devī is known by all in the shape of 'I ’. That (Devī) all men recognise under the form of 'I'.\" The first verse of the same book also says, \"We meditate upon the primeval Vidyā in the form of the Universal Caitanya.\"\n396.","dev":"परमेश्वरी","iast":"parameśvarī","pada":[{"dev":"परम","iast":"parama"},{"dev":"ईश्वरी","iast":"īśvarī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0,"cpage":207,"sure":0,"host":395,"v":83},{"n":397,"gloss":"Supreme ruler. (Parameśvarī.)","body":"Malaprakritih. (Primary cause.)\nMūla, root, i.e., the Śrīvidyā mantra, prakṛti (origin) ; She is in the form of two letters Prakāśa (a) which is the origin (of all other letters) and Vimarsal (ha, the last letter). Or, Mālaprakṛti is the matter of the Sāṃkhya School for they say (Is'vara. Kā., production. The seven, Mahat, etc., are productions and Mālaprakṛti is no productive. The sixteen are the modifications. Purusha is neither Prakṛti nor (it's) modifications.”\nIII) :\nAccording to the Mṛgendra Sam., the Kundalini is called Mālaprakṛti with its seven productions (Mahat, etc.); it (the Kundalini) is surrounded by Suṣumnā which is in the form of seven (productions), viz., Mahat, etc.\nOr, of the five elements from ether to earth, each is the Prakṛti (origin) of succeeding one; of ether Brahman is the Prakṛti; for the Śruti (Tai. Up., II, 1) says, “From the Self arose ether.\" That Brahman has no Prakṛti. So be is the root (Mūla), the first cause. In the Pañcarātra Ag. Śiva says, There is one mother of the Universe, the mother of the Scriptures, Sasasvati, who has no origin: hence she is called Mālaprakṛti. In her, I took birth with the categories, viz., Mahat, etc.\"\nThis is to be understood here. Amongst the actions (Karma) which are to become ripe in a certain time, those that ripen are exhausted by fruition, the others which are not ripe and have not come (consequently) to fruition, a new creation for their sake being useless, a Prākṛta (temporary) pralaya (dissolution) takes place. Then Māyā, consuming all the world, and absorbed into the independent Paramaśiva, who is without attributes: it (Māyā) abides thus, till the ripening of the remaining actions. It is said, \"In the dissolution, this animate and inanimate universe is merged in that (Māyā).\" The Viṣṇu Pr. also says, The earth, the basis of all, O divine Ṛṣi, becomes dissolved into water, water is absorbed by fire, fire ís absorbed into air, air into ether, this into the unmanifested (Avyakta), this ✪ Brāhmaṇa, is entirely absorbed in the unconditioned Purusha.\" The Avyakta is Māyā. The dissolution of it (Māyā) is not absolute annihilation as in the salvation (Nirvāṇa); but like the mental modifications in the state of sleep, as there is no appearance of the modifications of Māyā (during pralaya). Though (at that time) by the power of the illumination of the unconditioned supreme Self, it (Māyā) receives light, yet it remains as if it has no light. If there is no light at all (to the Māyā) then there is no existence of it. If you accept this theory, there will be no succeeding creation.\nThe modification of Māyā in the form of desire of creation arises in Paramaśiva for the sake of bestowing the\nfruit on those whose unripened actions are absorbed in Māyā; when their actions become ripe in course of time, that state of Māyā is variously named by the words 'sight,' desire,' thought,' desire of action,' etc. The Aitareya Up. (I-I) says, 'He thought, let me create the worlds.\" The Chānd. (Up., VI, 2-3) : It thought, let me become many. The Tai. (Up. II, 6): \"He desired, let me beconïe many.\" The Mun. (Up., I, 1, 8): “By penance Brahman is increased.\" This manifestation of Māyā, characterised by objective distinctions, is the first creation, the creation of darkness (Tamassarga), void of consciousness. Śruti (Ṛgveda, 10-129) beginning with 66 There was no being, in that time, nor, was no being. . . . In the beginning darkness was hidden by intelligence,\" and also confirm the above. \"From that, the Avyakta arose, etc.\"\nFrom this (creation) called Tamas, in which the three qualities (guṇas) were differentiated, arose the creation of partially manifested, Mahat. This is the second creation. It is said, From Ayakta comes the category of mahat in which are distinctly manifested the three guṇas; so also from mahat ahaṃkāra.\" From that (creation of Mahat) arises the third, the creation of Ahaṃkāra in which the 'three guṇas are manifested objectively. It is said, Mahat arose the threefold egoism, viz., Vaikārika (pure), Taijasa (passional) and Tāmasa (dark), which last is the origin of the elements.\" the elements,' it follows, that the rest of the creation belong Here, as Tāmasa is the origin of to Sattva and Rajas.\nFrom\nFrom that Tāmasa egoism which is called the origin of the elements, arose, with the aid of the Rajas, the creation of the five subtle elements (Tanmatras). This is the fourth\ncreation.\nFrom the pure egoism called Vaikārika, with the aid of Rajas, arose the creation of the aggregate of eleven This is the fifth creation.\nsenses.\nག\nFrom the Rajas egoism, the deities, Dik, Vāta, Arka. Prachetas, Aśvins, etc., who are the deities of the above two (fourth and fifth creations). This is the six creation.\nFor the Sāṃkhyas say (Is. Ka., 25): From the pure egoism arose the eleven Sāttvic creation (senses); from the Bhútadi (Tāmasa egoism) the subtle elements, and from Rajas egoism (the deities presiding over both).”\nBut in the Śaiva school it is said, from pure egoism comes mind, from passional egoism, the ten senses; thus there is a difference. For the Śaivas say, It (mahat) becomes threefold by differentiation of Sattva, Rajas and Tamas; that is called by names Vaikārika, Taijasa and Bhatadi; from Taijasa mind comes, from Vaikārika senses, from Bhutadi the subtle elements. Thus are the creations from that (mahat).”\nThese six creations are called Prākṛta (belong to Prakṛti).\nThe Vaikṛta (belong to products, muhat, etc.,) creation, comprises trees, etc., whose life current tends upwards, animals, whose lifecurrent is hoizontal, and Batha, Preta, etc., whose lifecurrent tends down wards.\nThese Prākṛta and Vaikṛta creations, taken together, are called the Kaumāra creation. The Vi. Bhag. Pr. says,\nThe first is the creation of mahat, therein the inequality of the guṇas arises. The second is egoism, therein arises object, (Dravya) knowledge, (Jñāna) and action (Kriyā). The third is the creation of the elements, therein arise the subtle elements, having the energies of Dravya. The fourth is the creation of the senses, which consists of knowledge and action. The fifth is the Vaikārika, the creation of the Devas, which consists of mind. The sixth is the creation of Tamas which is the creation of the allpervading (Māyā) devoid of knowledge. These six are called Prākṛta creations. Hear frou me the Vaikṛta creation, etc.\"\nHere the creation of Tamas called Avyakta, is the sixth in the order, but according to its meaning, it should\nbe taken as first.\nIn this connection the Vāyu and other\nPrs. should be consulted.\nIn these creations beginning with the Avyakta creation, the previous one is the origin of succeeding ones. Brahman alone is the origin of the Avyakta. So as he is the root of all creations, and as he has no root, he is the root matter. (Mālaprakṛti.) Hence the Śruti (Katha Up.3-10 and 11) says, Beyond the senses there are the objects\nThis is\nBeyond the Mahat, the Avyakta, beyond Avyakta there is the Person. Beyond the Person, there is nothing. the goal, and the supreme abode.\"\nOr, ma, five, i.e., the five subtle elements and la, three, i.e., Avyakta, Mahat and Ahaṃkāra, therefore Mila, eight, Prakṛti, causes. The Samasa Sūtra says:\neight Prakṛtis.\"","dev":"मूलप्रकृति","iast":"mūlaprakṛti","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.462,"cpage":207,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":83},{"n":398,"gloss":"Undeveloped","body":"There are\nHe now proceeds to praise Devī under the form of the creations in order.\nAvyakta is the manifestation of Māyā.\nAccording to the Sāṃkhya school, it is expressed by the words, Pradhana (foundation), Prakṛti (matter) and other words. It is said in the Sāṃkhya Saptati \"That is the Avyakta which is subtle, without characteristics, inanimate, without beginning or end, capable of production, without parts, one and universal,\" Panchasikhacharya also Pradhana, say the wise, is the Avyakta, which is without beginning or middle, beyond Mahat, and permanent.\" It (Avyakta) is the collective form of the three qualities. The Sāṃkhya Sūtra (VI, 39) says, \"S'atva and the rest are not qualities of that (Brahman), because they are the qualities of that (Avyakta).\"\nOr, Avyakta means Brahman for in the place (Ved. St.) III, 2-23) it is said, \"That (Brahman) is Avyakta for the scriptures say so. The Śruti (Mun. Up8), \"He is not to May mean the abridged Sūtras of Sāṃkhya and we get some MSS. of the same.\n$\nbe felt by the eyes, nor by the speech, nor by the other deities (senses), nor by penance, nor by action.\"\nOr, Avyakta means Viṣṇu. The Liṅga Pr. says, “The names of Viṣṇu, who is ever capable of creation, are Pradhāna, Avyaya, Yoni (origin), Avyakta, Prakṛti and Tamas.\"","dev":"व्यक्ता","iast":"vyaktā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":211,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":83},{"n":399,"gloss":"In the form of manifested and unmanifested","body":"Vyakla, the category Mahat, it is called so because it was first manifested and also from its greatness. Avyakta : A, on all sides, Fyakta, manifested, i.e., egoism, the product of the above.\nThis (name) may be taken as two names, if the naine Brahmā jananī (822 and 823) is taken as one. According to this view, vyakta is the supreme egoism (Parāhantā), for supreme egoism is in the form of Tripurasundarī, because she is manifested in the category of egoism.\nOr, she is in the form of both manifested and unmanifested. That is, she is and is not subject to the modifications of the elements. The Liṅga Pr. says, 66 Vyakta is called Sat (existence), as it is the second modification of the elements; Avyakta is called Asat because it is devoid of that (modification).”\nOr, Vyakta, perishable and Avyakta, imperishable. The Matsya Pr. says, \"Vyakta is said to be perishable and Avyakta, imperishable.”\nOr, Vyakta, individual, and avyakta, collective form. The Nṛsiṃha Pr. says, The Avyakta is known as collective, and cyaklu, O Lord of ascetics, is individual.”\nAccording to the Brahmā. Pr., \"The wise say the seventythree categories are meant by the word. Vyakta and by the word Avyakta, the supreme Prakṛti.”\nOr, vyakta, uvyakta um vyaktāryakta ; thus there are three kinds of Liṅgas (of Śiva). She is in all the forms. The three kinds of Liṅgas are described in the Br. Vaivarta Pr. \"There are three Liṅgas, ei., Svāyambhuva\n25*\n(selfexisting), Banalinga (got from a certain river) and Śaila Liṅga (made of stone), these are respectively called Vyakta, Avyakta, and Vyaktāvyakta. Vyakta, they say, gives salvation, the Avyakta, gives (worldly) happiness, and Vyaktāvyakta gives both happiness and salvation. The Bana Liṅga is said to be that which outweighs even two or three Tulas (a weight). The rest are called Śaila (mere stone) by the learned.”\nOr, She is manifested (Vyakta) in those whose deeds are ripened and is not manifested (Avyakta) in those who are bound by the noose (of Māyā). The Śaktirahasya says when it describes the descent of the Śakti (into man) :\nHow do you say that the supreme Śakti which is allpervading descends, the descent is from above downwards; only that which has form and is not allpervading can descend? True, she is all pervading, eternal, coexistent with Śiva; yet she is hidden in those who are bound by the noose of the impurity, action, etc., and manifested in them whose sins are burned out, in this way she is said to\ndescend.\"","dev":"व्यक्ताव्यक्तस्वरूपिणी","iast":"vyaktāvyaktasvarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"व्यक्त","iast":"vyakta"},{"dev":"अव्यक्त","iast":"avyakta"},{"dev":"स्वरूपिणी","iast":"svarūpiṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":212,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":83},{"n":400,"gloss":"Allpervading","body":"(86)\nShe is called allpervading, because she assumes the forms of the three creations of the threefold egoism (vide name 379).\nThus ends the fifth Kālī, called Jvälin with the fourth hundred in the Saubhāgyabhāskara composed by Bhāskarārāya.\nTHE FIFTH HUNDRED","dev":"व्यापिनी","iast":"vyāpinī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":213,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":84},{"n":401,"gloss":"Multiform","body":"The different forms of the Vaikṛta creation, or of the Kaumāra creation (for Vaikṛta and Prākṛta, vide name 397).","dev":"विविधाकारा","iast":"vividhākārā","pada":[{"dev":"विविध","iast":"vividha"},{"dev":"आकारा","iast":"ākārā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":213,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":84},{"n":402,"gloss":"In the form of knowledge and ignorance","body":"Vidyā and aridyă are explained in the Īśa (Up., 11) : One who knows both knowledge and ignorance having crossed over death by ignorance and attains immortality by knowledge.\"\nOr, Vidyā, knowledge of the Self, Avidyū, the knowledge in the form of the last modification (of duality, i.e., just before realisation). The Br. Nāradīya Pr. says, The supreme Śakti of that Viṣṇu, capable of absorbing the universe, in the form of existence and nonexistence, is sung as Vidyā and Aridya.\" The De. Bhag. Pr. also, She is Brahman himself, very difficult to attain and in the form of Vidyā and Avidyā.\" In another place, “O King, know, that Vidyā and Avidyā are two forms of Devī; by one men are freed, by the other they are\nbound.\"\nOr, Vidyā, the knowledge in the forin of the last modification of duality or of the false knowledge (Charamavritti). Arīdyā, confused knowledge of separateness; Sva, the knowledge belonging to supreme Brahmin; because according to a Lexicography, Sva means, relations and Self\" her form. The Liṅga Pr. Bhrānti, Vidyā and Parā.\n; rūpu, these three are\nsays, Śiva has three forms, The knowledge of different objects is called Bhrānti (confused) by the learned. Knowledge in the form of Self, is called Vidyā. Knowledge concerning Brahman without ambiguity is called Parā (supreme).”","dev":"विद्याविद्यास्वरूपिणी","iast":"vidyāvidyāsvarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"विद्या","iast":"vidyā"},{"dev":"विद्या","iast":"vidyā"},{"dev":"स्वरूपिणी","iast":"svarūpiṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.909,"cpage":214,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":84},{"n":403,"gloss":"The moonlight which gladdens the kumuda flowers of the eyes of Mahakamesa","body":"Kaumudī (moonlight) may mean the full moon of the month, Kartiku. The Yadava says, Kumuda (neu.) means waterlily, red lotus, and (in masc.) ape. Kaum uda means the month Kārtika and Kaumudī, the moon of that month.\"\nOr, ku, inferior, mud, pleasure, Kaumuda are those who long for worldly pleasures. They are called inferior because (their pleasures) are mixed with pain and for other reasons. So Kaumuda means a miserable one. Śāśvata and Viśva say, \"Kumut means inferior men and waterlily.\" Ā, full, Hlāda, joy, i.e., salvation. A man attains that stage by being led (nayana) to Mahākāmesa. Kaumudī, light. The meaning is she becomes, out of pity, light, as it were, to them who are blind in worldly pursuits. (87)","dev":"महाकामेशनयनकुमुदाह्लादकौमुदी","iast":"mahākāmeśanayanakumudāhlādakaumudī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":214,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":84},{"n":404,"gloss":"She is the sunbeam which drives out the darkness from the heart of her devotees","body":"","dev":"भक्ताहार्दतमोभेदभानुमद्भानुसंततिः","iast":"bhaktāhārdatamobhedabhānumadbhānusaṃtatiḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.917,"cpage":215,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":85},{"n":405,"gloss":"Śiva is her messenger","body":"The Mark. Pr. says, As Śiva himself was made a messenger by that Devī, she was known in the world as Śivadūtī.\" Some commentators think that she was made a messenger by Śiva. But this (interpretation) is without\nauthority.\nThis is the deity of the Puṣkaratīrtha. The Padma Pr. says, 66 Now I will tell you of other deities.\nlives in the Puṣkara\never conferring happiness.'\nShe who\nŚivadūtī, the beneficent,","dev":"शिवदूती","iast":"śivadūtī","pada":[{"dev":"शिव","iast":"śiva"},{"dev":"दूती","iast":"dūtī"}],"pconf":0.92,"fit":1,"cpage":215,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":85},{"n":406,"gloss":"Worshipped by Śiva","body":"The Brahmā. Pra. says, Even Śiva by worshipping whom, by meditation, by the power of Yoga, becomes the lord of all Siddhis, and becomes halffemale (Ardhnarisvara).\"\nŚivadūtī is a certain mantra of four parts worshipped\nby Śiva.\n(Sivamûrtih.)","dev":"शिवाराध्या","iast":"śivārādhyā","pada":[{"dev":"शिवा","iast":"śivā"},{"dev":"राध्या","iast":"rādhyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":215,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":85},{"n":407,"gloss":"In the form of Śiva. Because there is no separation between Śiva and Śakti. The Śruti","body":"In the form of Śiva. Because there is no separation between Śiva and Śakti. The Śruti says, One Rudra hidden in all beings, he is with Māyā, with parts and without parts. He is Devf himself, and is not separated from her. By knowing this, one attains immortality.\" Or Śiva, beneficent,\nMurti form; Or Śiva, Salvation, Mūrti, form. Because salvation is the realisation of the very Self. The Saura. Sam. \"Now I shall explain concisely to you the nature of salvation Hence the supreme salvation is the realisation of the Self, those who were bound by Avidyă, are freed by Vidyā.\"","dev":"शिवमूर्तिः","iast":"śivamūrtiḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":215,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":85},{"n":408,"gloss":"Giver of happiness","body":"She makes her devotees happy (Śiva) by removing the noose of Avidyā and leads them to Brahman which is to be attained by liberated souls. Or, Śiva means auspiciousness and Kari giver.","dev":"शिवंकरी","iast":"śivaṃkarī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.909,"cpage":216,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":85},{"n":409,"gloss":"Beloved of Śiva","body":"Or Śiva is loved by her.","dev":"शिवप्रिया","iast":"śivapriyā","pada":[{"dev":"शिव","iast":"śiva"},{"dev":"प्रिया","iast":"priyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":216,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":86},{"n":410,"gloss":"Beyond Śiva","body":"(88)\nBecause the position of Śiva depends upon Śakti. Or, to whom Śiva is the supreme. (Parā); or she reveals Śiva (to her devotees).","dev":"शिवपरा","iast":"śivaparā","pada":[{"dev":"शिव","iast":"śiva"},{"dev":"परा","iast":"parā"}],"pconf":0.92,"fit":1,"cpage":216,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":86},{"n":411,"gloss":"Desired by the wise","body":"S'iṣṭa ordained ceremonies which are dear to her (Iṣṭa).\nworshipped by the ordained cere-\nS'iṣṭa : she is monies. It is said, The Lord should be worshipped by means of ceremonies according to one's caste and order, and not by (mere) offering scent, flower, etc.\" The Bhārata and Br. Nāradīya Pr. say: Righteousness arises from conduct, the Lord of righteousness is Viṣṇu.”\nAccording to the Vashistha Sūtras The S'ishtas are those who always control their limbs, eyes, speech, and whole body, who receive the Vedas with their supplements by tradition, who are devotees to Brahman, and whose every motive directly depends on the scriptures.\"\nOr, she is worshipped (Iṣṭa) by the wise (Sishta).","dev":"शिष्टेष्टा","iast":"śiṣṭeṣṭā","pada":[{"dev":"शिष्ट","iast":"śiṣṭa"},{"dev":"इष्टा","iast":"iṣṭā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":216,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":86},{"n":412,"gloss":"Worshipped by the great ones","body":"This name clearly sums up the meaning of the previons one.","dev":"शिष्टपूजिता","iast":"śiṣṭapūjitā","pada":[{"dev":"शिष्ट","iast":"śiṣṭa"},{"dev":"पूजिता","iast":"pūjitā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":216,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":86},{"n":413,"gloss":"Immeasurable","body":"Or, A, Brahmā, Viṣṇu, etc., Prameya, to be known, or measured (i.e., she is to be known by them only).\nOr, ap, water, prameya abiding in. The Śruti says. My origin is in the water of ocean.'","dev":"अप्रमेया","iast":"aprameyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":216,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":86},{"n":414,"gloss":"Selfilluminated","body":"The Śruti (Br. Up., 4-3-9) says, becomes selfilluminated.\"\nHere this Person\nOr, su, much, ap, water, prakāśa, manifested.","dev":"स्वप्रकाशा","iast":"svaprakāśā","pada":[{"dev":"स्व","iast":"sva"},{"dev":"प्रकाशा","iast":"prakāśā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":217,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":86},{"n":415,"gloss":"Beyond mind and speech","body":"Manas includes the functions of mind. The Śruti (Tai. Up., II, 9—1). From whence speech and mind turn away unable to reach (it).\" In the Viṣṇu Pr., the Prahlāda's saying is, \"I bow down to the Supreme Isvar! who transcends speech and mind and who can be grasped by the wisdom of the wise alone.\"\nOr, in whom is not to be found any object of thought or speech.\nOr, A is to be prefixed to the name, then, Manas, mind, Vacha, speech, ama, not purified (lit. unbaked, e.g., a clay vessel). A, (not), she is beyond those whose mind and speech are not purified and therefore cannot be known by them. How is it, the Śruti in one place says, The mind turns away, etc.,\" and again (in another place Katha. Up., 4-11), \"By mind alone it should be perceived, etc.\" This contradiction is removed in the Bhāmatī (the commentary on Śaṃkara's commentary on Ved. Sa. by Vachaspati Miśra) by adding 'Not purified and purified' respectively to the word 'mind'.'","dev":"मनोवाचामगोचरा","iast":"manovācāmagocarā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":217,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":86},{"n":416,"gloss":"Chitsaktih. (89) Cit, according to the Yāska Smṛti and Pāṇini (8, 2, 101) means comparison. Or, Chitrakti, the power of removing Avidyā and is also termed Caitanya; according to the (Sve. Up. V. I)","body":"The Com. discusses here about the division of some according to Chchatakshara Sūtras.\nnames\nIn imperishable and infinite highest Brahman, wherein the two, Vidyā and Avidyä are hidden, one is imperishable and the other is perishable; he who rules these two is different from either.\" The De. Bhag. Pr. (Book V), “O king, in all beings there is the Śakti with all its power, any being devoid of that Śakti becomes like a corpse. That Citśakti is in all beings, that is but her form.\"","dev":"चिच्छक्ति","iast":"cicchakti","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.667,"cpage":217,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":87},{"n":417,"gloss":"In the form of Consciousness","body":"Sankshepa Sarirakacharya says, The pure Citśakti of the supreme Lord is called Caitanya.' The Gauḍapāda Sūtra (3) also, \"Śakti is Caitanya itself.\" The De. Bha. Pr., \"We meditate upon that primeval Vidyā which is in the form of Caitanya of all,`and which guides our senses. This means the tripartite Gāyatrī. Hence the Matsya Pr. says, They know the Bhagavata (Pr.) which begins with Gāyatrī.\" In the Śruti (Tai. Up., II, 8-1): \"That of bliss (Brahman) is to be discussed.\" That, the energy in the form of consciousness. Thus the holy Sankararanya explains it in his commentary called Vidyāratna, as the Vimarśa form.","dev":"चेतनारूपा","iast":"cetanārūpā","pada":[{"dev":"चेतना","iast":"cetanā"},{"dev":"रूपा","iast":"rūpā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":218,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":87},{"n":418,"gloss":"The energy of the inanimate creation","body":"A certain modification of Māyā which is correlative to the power which creates the (animate) world, that is this (name) simply denotes the creative energy. The Viṣṇu Pr. says, The energies of all beings are not to be grasped by our intellects. The creative energies of Brahman are a hundred times more (difficult to grasp); they are in him like heat is in fire, O best of ascetics. He (Brahman) is only the instrumental cause of the creation of the world. The creative energies are the material cause. Except the instrumental cause, the creative energies depend on nothing. O best of ascetics, an object becomes itself by its own energy.\"","dev":"जडशक्ति","iast":"jaḍaśakti","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.909,"cpage":218,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":87},{"n":419,"gloss":"The objective world","body":"","dev":"र्जडात्मिका","iast":"rjaḍātmikā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.909,"cpage":218,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":87},{"n":420,"gloss":"Gāyatrī. The metre consisting of twentyfour syllables is called Gāyatrī. The Karma Pr","body":"Gāyatrī.\nThe metre consisting of twentyfour syllables is called Gāyatrī. The Karma Pr.: “I am Gāyatrī among the metres (vide Bh. Gītā, 10-35).\nOr, a cowherd's daughter is called Gāyatrī, who is the younger wife of Brahmi. This story occurs in the Padma Pr. Once in the Puṣkara, when Brahmā was performing a sacrifice and called Sāvitrī (his wife) who was with Lakṣmī and others. She did not come, but replied, I will come with the others; enraged at this speech he substituted a cowherd's daughter bestowed by the hand of Indra, and thus addressed Viṣṇu. 66 Then Brahmā said to Viṣṇu. let us hasten for the sacrifice, there is here, O Lord, a very virtuous woman called Gāyatrī. Thus addressed. Viṣṇu replied to Brahmā, do you wed this (woman) by the Gandharva (form of) marriage. I give her to you, () Lord. do not hesitate. Take the hand of this cowherd's daughter : and thus the grandsire wedded (her) by the Gandharva marriage, etc.\"\nOr, Gāyatrī, the mother of the Vedas. According to the Bharadvāja Smṛ., Because she protects (tra) the singer, (Ga) she is called Gāyatrī.\" As mother is inseparable from Gāyatrī, she is thus named. The Padma Pr., 66 After bathing in the Puṣkara, one should repeat me (ie., Gāyatrī) the mother of the Vedas. Though I abide in the eight syllables (of each part), yet this universe is pervaded by me.' The Devī Pr., As (everyone) sings (Gāyana) (her), and approach (Gamana), she is called Gāyatrī, worshipped by Devas.\" The Chchi. (Up.. III, 12-1) : Sings forth (Gayati) and protects (Trāyate).” Va. Ram..\nsong","dev":"गायत्री","iast":"gāyatrī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":218,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":87},{"n":421,"gloss":"Gāyatrī, because she is in the form of Vyahritih. The utterance, or a certain Mantra","body":"Gāyatrī, because she is in the form of\nVyahritih.\nThe utterance, or a certain Mantra. The Vāyu Pr. Addressed by me (vyaharana), you approached. hence you are known by the name lyähriti.”","dev":"व्याहृतिः","iast":"vyāhṛtiḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.4,"cpage":219,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":87},{"n":422,"gloss":"Sandhyā. Sandhyā, means, junction also meditation, .., the idea of the nonseparation of ourselves and that of Caitanya which is in the sun. The explanation (of the word) is men meditate","body":"wholly Sam on her. She is called (Sandhyā),' The Bhārata says, Sandhyā, means the Brahman which abides in the Sun and she is meditated as one, by Brahmā and allbeings who are his rays.\" Thus those who know the Vedas say, \"The aspirant should think I am the servant of that Brahman, who is existence and consciousness, and I am He.\" Vyāsa Smṛ. also, \"Do not distinguish Gāyatrī from Brahman, in any case one should worship her (saying) 'I am She.' The Bharadvāja Smṛ., She, who is differentiated as Brahman, etc., the witness of action, radiant, the Śakti of Īśvara, is called by the wise Sandhyā.” The great men's saying also, Gāyatrī with her head (the Mantra Omapah, etc.) and with the fourth part is described in the Vedas as Sandhyā. O Tripurā, thou alone dost decree the result of (even) great actions.\" So (the great com.) Mādhava says, \"The word Sandhyā means the deity who is to be worshipped at the twilights.\" The Parijatakara also says, As she is alone to be meditated upon, she is called Sandhyā.\"\nThis (Sandhyā) is the mind born daughter of Brahmā. The Kālikā Pr., Born from his mind, beautiful in form, having beautiful limbs, named Sandhyā. She is the victorious deity of the twilights. Because she was born to Brahmā, while he was engaged in meditation, she is known as Sandhyā.\" The Bhagavatī Pr. : Sandhyā, who is the mind born daughter of Brahmä, after performing penance and giving up her body, she became Arundhatī (wife of Vasiṣṭha).\"\nBut in the Reṇukā Pr. it is said, Ida is Kālī, Pinga la is Lakṣmī. Sushumnā is Ekavīrā, thus the Sandhyā is\nthreefold.\"\nAccording to Dhaunya a year old girl is called Sandhyā. 18th verse divides 38 names.\n26*","dev":"सन्ध्या","iast":"sandhyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":219,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":87},{"n":423,"gloss":"Worshipped by all the twiceborn. brindanishevita.)","body":"(Dvija-\nShe is so worshipped because she is Sandhyā. The Reṇukā Pr., This one Sandhyā is to be worshipped by Devas, the twiceborn and by great souls, in sitting down, in lying down, in moving about, in eating and she is Reṇukā herself.\"\nOr, the three names (421, 422 and 423) Vyahriti,' etc., mean the following three states. Fyahriti is the operation of speech, indicates the waking state.\nSandhyā, because it is between the other two, indicates the dreaming state; since the Veil. Sūtra (III-2-1) says, \"In the intermediate place there is a creation, for the scriptures says so.\" And it means the dreaming state.\nDvija means birds and hence the Jīvas; by all these three states she is worshipped (Sevita) by realising union (ni) with her. The Sleeping State: As birds fatigued with flight, fold their wings and enter their nests, so the tired Jīvas quitting the waking and dreaming states, are absorbed in the supreme Brahman. The Br. Up. (4-3-19) says, “Just sa in this sky a hawk or an eagle bird after a long flight closing its wings goes to its nest, so this person also rushes to that goal, where sleeping he neither desires anything, nor sees any dream.\" Chānd. Up. (6-8-1) also, “O child. there he becomes possessed of Brahman.\"\n(90) (Tatvasana.)","dev":"द्विजबृन्दनिषेविता","iast":"dvijabṛndaniṣevitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.842,"cpage":221,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":87},{"n":424,"gloss":"Having the categories as her seat. Tattvas, the thirtysix categories from Earth to Siv","body":"Having the categories as her seat. Tattvas, the thirtysix categories from Earth to Śiva, Āsana a posture called Yogapitha.\nOr Tattvas, the categories, āsana she rejects (as to\nreject).","dev":"तत्त्वासना","iast":"tattvāsanā","pada":[{"dev":"तत्त्व","iast":"tattva"},{"dev":"आसना","iast":"āsanā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.5,"cpage":221,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":88},{"n":425,"gloss":"That. (Tat.)","body":"The word Tat signifies the revolution of mind; as Devī revolves in all Buddhis she is signified by the word Tat.","dev":"तत्त्वमयी तत्","iast":"tattvamayī tat","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":221,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":88},{"n":426,"gloss":"Thou","body":"The word Team also signifies Devī.","dev":"त्वं","iast":"tvaṃ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":221,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":88},{"n":427,"gloss":"Oh. (Ayi.)","body":"1\nThis word is used in interrogation, Devī is addressed\nby this word as she is the mother of all.\nOr, ayi, fortunate one.","dev":"अयी","iast":"ayī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":222,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":88},{"n":428,"gloss":"Residing in the five sheaths. rasthitha.)","body":"(Pañca kosanta-\nThese five (kośas) are the five employed in the worship of the five (deities). These five are, according to the Jñānārṇava, Śrīvidyā Paraṃjyotis, Parā, Nishkalasāmbhavi, and the Ajapamantra, and these are called the five sheaths.\" When we worship these five in the Śrīcakra, the last four Paraṃjyotis, etc., are to be worshipped individually and collectively in the Srishti and other Cakras which are surrounding (the Śrīcakra). But the Thus she is in the Śrīvidyā is the Bindu in the centre.\nmidst of the five sheaths.\nThere are in our bodies the five sheaths called Annamaya, Pranamaya, Manomaya, Vijnānamaya and ́Ãnandamaya, each involved in the preceding one, of these five, the innermost last is the Ânandamaya, with that she is identified. In the Ved. Sa. (I-1-12 to 19) by the Vrittikara it is But our holy explained that ānandamaya is Brahman. Ācārya says, that ānandamaya sheath is not Brahman because it is impure and is included among the annamaya and other sheaths which are to be abandoned (lit. purified). In that case, according to Śrīkaṇṭha Bhāṣya and its commentaries, the ānandamaya is in the form of Cit Śakti which is the body of Brahman, who is the Parākasa itself; as we see (in the Tai. Up., II-7-1), “This ether is Ānanda (Brahman).\"\nIn the opinion that Brahman is the support of the Ānandamaya,” the meaning is that she is in the centre of these five (sheaths). This (we think) is correct, because the Brahmā Gītā (3-45 and 46 of St. Sam.) confirms this verse. So the Ānandamaya also is filled by another Brahman, Bh. Nārā. makes the three names (425-127) as one Tatramayî.'\nwhich is the witness, omnipresent. That Brahman is called the support which is existence, wisdom, and unity. That is the one ever remaining essence. O great Devas, do not think otherwise.\"\nThe Krodhabha//āraka (Durvāsa) also says, O mother, blazing with radiant light, he, who knows you as surrounded by the five sheaths, Anna, Prāṇa, Manas, Buddhi and Ānanda, with the marks of head, wings, support (lit. tail) and trunk, described by the words of the Mahopanished, indeed is the knower of Brahman.\"\n420 Illimitable Majesty.\n(Nissima mahima.)","dev":"पञ्चकोशान्तरस्थिता","iast":"pañcakośāntarasthitā","pada":[{"dev":"पञ्च","iast":"pañca"},{"dev":"कोश","iast":"kośa"},{"dev":"अन्तरस्थिता","iast":"antarasthitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.556,"cpage":222,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":88},{"n":429,"gloss":"Residing in the five sheaths. rasthitha.)","body":"(Pañca kosanta-\nThese five (kośas) are the five employed in the worship of the five (deities). These five are, according to the Jñānārṇava, Śrīvidyā Paraṃjyotis, Parā, Nishkalasāmbhavi, and the Ajapamantra, and these are called the five sheaths.\" When we worship these five in the Śrīcakra, the last four 