young","body":"Because she is devoid of old age in the three periods (present, past and future.)","dev":"नित्ययौवना","iast":"nityayauvanā","pada":[{"dev":"नित्य","iast":"nitya"},{"dev":"यौवना","iast":"yauvanā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":223,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":88},{"n":431,"gloss":"Shining with rapture","body":"Mada, a kind of bliss, untainted by worldly things. (91)","dev":"मदशालिनी","iast":"madaśālinī","pada":[{"dev":"मद","iast":"mada"},{"dev":"शालिनी","iast":"śālinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":223,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":88},{"n":432,"gloss":"With reddened eyes rolling with rapture","body":"Gharnana (rolling) turned away from worldly things.","dev":"मदघूर्णितरक्ताक्षी","iast":"madaghūrṇitaraktākṣī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":223,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":89},{"n":433,"gloss":"With cheeks blushing with rapture. pātāla gandabhûh.)","body":"Pātāla, rose colour.\n(Mada\nOr Mada, musk, Pātala, a kind of flower, by these her cheeks are painted and perfumed. Mada, according to the semen, musk, pride, joy, rut of an elephant,\nViśva means,\nand liquor.'","dev":"मदपाटलगण्डभूः","iast":"madapāṭalagaṇḍabhūḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.667,"cpage":223,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":89},{"n":434,"gloss":"Her body smeared with sandal paste","body":"","dev":"चन्दनद्रवदिग्धाङ्गी","iast":"candanadravadigdhāṅgī","pada":[{"dev":"चन्दन","iast":"candana"},{"dev":"द्रव","iast":"drava"},{"dev":"दिग्धाङ्गी","iast":"digdhāṅgī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.963,"cpage":223,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":89},{"n":435,"gloss":"Fond of the Champaka flower","body":"","dev":"चाम्पेयकुसुमप्रिया","iast":"cāmpeyakusumapriyā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":223,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":89},{"n":436,"gloss":"Skilful","body":"(92)\nAs she exhibits skill in the creation, etc. Or Kūṭa, water, la, receiver.\nOr, ku, inferior, sala, moon, i.e., as she is possessed of greater brightness, the moon is inferior in comparison.","dev":"कुशला","iast":"kuśalā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":223,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":90},{"n":437,"gloss":"Of graceful appearance","body":"","dev":"कोमलाकारा","iast":"komalākārā","pada":[{"dev":"कोमला","iast":"komalā"},{"dev":"कारा","iast":"kārā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":223,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":90},{"n":438,"gloss":"Kurukullā. This deity is located in the tank, called Vimarsamaya, in the Śrīcakra, i","body":"Kurukullā.\nThis deity is located in the tank, called Vimarsamaya, in the Śrīcakra, in the space between the walls of Cit and Ahaṃkāra; concerning that tank, the Lalitãstavaratna (Sl. 97) says, \"I ever meditate on that Kurukullā, who resides in the ruby, Kuruvinda, whose waist is bent by the weight of her breasts which put mountains to shame and whose body is smeared with red paste.\" This deity is described in the twentysecond chapter of the Tantrarāja.","dev":"कुरुकुल्ला","iast":"kurukullā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":224,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":90},{"n":439,"gloss":"The ruler of the triad","body":"Kula, the triad, viz., the measurer, the measurement, and the thing measured.","dev":"कुलेश्वरी","iast":"kuleśvarī","pada":[{"dev":"कुल","iast":"kula"},{"dev":"ईश्वरी","iast":"īśvarī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":224,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":90},{"n":440,"gloss":"Abiding in the Kulakuṇḍa","body":"Kulakuṇḍa is the bindu, which is in centre of the pericarp of the Mūlādhāra. It is like the small cavity in the centre of the pericarp of a lotus.\nOr, Alaya, the place of rest (in the sleep), that is the place of rest of Kundalini. Ācārya (Sau. La. Sl. 10), After reaching your own place assuming the form of a coiled serpent you sleep in the cavity of the Kulakuṇḍa.”","dev":"कुलकुण्डालया","iast":"kulakuṇḍālayā","pada":[{"dev":"कुल","iast":"kula"},{"dev":"कुण्डा","iast":"kuṇḍā"},{"dev":"लया","iast":"layā"}],"pconf":0.93,"fit":1,"cpage":224,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":90},{"n":441,"gloss":"Worshipped by those, devoted to the Kaula path","body":"Marga, the path handed down by family tradition. Kula, it is so called because it is connected with the Mūlādhāra. The Vratakhanda says, That Devī who is established by family custom, should be worshipped in that (customary) way by offerings, scents, etc.. She is to be worshipped by oblations, etc., in the family customary way.\"\nOr, there are three modes in the worship of Devī, viz., Samaya, Miśra, and Kaula. The first is the path laid down in the Vedas and also described in the five Āgamas of Śuka, Vasiṣṭha, etc. The second is that which is: described in the eight Tantras, Chandrakala and others. It is called mixed because it partakes of both (modes). The Kaula mode which differs from the above two is\ndescribed in other Tantras.\nKaula is so called because The characteristics of these\nit is sought for by the Kaulas. modes of worship and of their devotees are described in the corresponding Tantras. (Vide Ānanda Lahari Sl. 31 Com.)\n(93)","dev":"कौलमार्गतत्परसेविता","iast":"kaulamārgatatparasevitā","pada":[{"dev":"कौलमार्ग","iast":"kaulamārga"},{"dev":"तत्पर","iast":"tatpara"},{"dev":"सेविता","iast":"sevitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":224,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":90},{"n":442,"gloss":"Mother of Kumara and Gaṇanātha","body":"Kumāra, Skanda : Gaṇanātha, Gaṇeśa.\nAmbu,\nOr Ku, inferior, Maragana, the aggregate of passional ideas (Vikāra), Nathas, the lords, of the above. the binder (or destroyer).\nKumāra, the Ahankira (egoism) whose deity is Kumara. The Varāha Pr. says, Viṣṇu is the Person, or Śiva by that name is called. Avyakta is Umå or Lakṣmī, the lotus eyed. Ahunkāru arises from the interaction of these (two). This (egoism) is the Guha (Skanda), the leader of the army.\" She binds those who possess the qualities of egoism.","dev":"कुमारगणनाथाम्बा","iast":"kumāragaṇanāthāmbā","pada":[{"dev":"कुमार","iast":"kumāra"},{"dev":"गणनाथ","iast":"gaṇanātha"},{"dev":"अम्बा","iast":"ambā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":225,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":443,"gloss":"Contentment","body":"There are seven names, viz., contentment, nourishment, wisdom, might, tranquility, benevolence, beauty. He procoeds to praise Devī under these names.\nThe Mark. Pr. says, \"The goddess who resides as a contentment in all beings, adoration to her.\" The same is given in the Mallāri Māhātmya, The De. Bhi. Pr. (Book III), The god who, etc.\" known, is in all beings in the form of understanding, That mother as is wellfame, firmness, prosperity, energy, faith, intelligence and memory.\" According to Padma Pr. Tush/i is a deity in the Vasrapatha Tīrtha.","dev":"तुष्टिः","iast":"tuṣṭiḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":225,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":444,"gloss":"Nourishment. (Push/ih.)","body":"This deity is in the Devadaruvana.","dev":"पुष्टिर्मतिर्धृतिः","iast":"puṣṭirmatirdhṛtiḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.4,"cpage":225,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":445,"gloss":"Intelligence. (Matih.)","body":"Mati is explained in the Vāyu Pr. thus, “Purusha bears the measuring (rod), understands division and thinks himself composed of parts, hence he is known as Mati.\"\nThe St. Sam. (Sa. Gītā S-89) says, Mati is the form of Devī : Let us adore that Śiva who is worshipped by Viṣṇu and others, who is the bestower of bliss, who is known as Mati derived from experience, supreme, who knows (lit. measures) the Vedas, beneficent.\"","dev":"शान्तिः","iast":"śāntiḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.571,"cpage":225,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":446,"gloss":"Fortitude. (Dhritih.)","body":"This is the deity worshipped in the Pindṛraka place.","dev":"स्वस्तिमती","iast":"svastimatī","pada":[{"dev":"स्वस्ति","iast":"svasti"},{"dev":"मती","iast":"matī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.2,"cpage":226,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":447,"gloss":"Tranquility. (Santih.)","body":"Or, S'anti, a certain Kalâ (part) belonging to Vāyu. The Śaivāgama says, “That which gives place to a man (struggling with) the flood of impurity (of anu), illusion (of Māyā), and change (of Karma), that Kāla is called Śānti. That is the abode which is the seat of dominion.” The Br. Parisara Smṛ. also, \"Beyond the fifteen finger's breadth from the end of the nose where the Jīva is purified, is the sixteenth part . . . that Kālī is called Sänti.”","dev":"कान्ति","iast":"kānti","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.571,"cpage":226,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":448,"gloss":"Ever true. (Svastimatī.)","body":"Su, exceedingly, asti, being, mat, having. Because her being is supreme reality and not what ordinary people think to be reality. The Śruti (Br. Up., 2-1-20) says, The senses are truth, this (Brahman) is their reality.\" That (Brahman) is the real of the real.”\nSvasti, according to the Ratnakośa means, “* Benediction, benevolence, sinless, holy, auspiciousness.\" Yāska Svasti means immortality.\"\nalso,","dev":"र्नन्दिनी","iast":"rnandinī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":226,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":449,"gloss":"Effulgent. (Kantih.)","body":"Or Kanti, is the Icchā Śakti..","dev":"विघ्ननाशिनी","iast":"vighnanāśinī","pada":[{"dev":"विघ्न","iast":"vighna"},{"dev":"नाशिनी","iast":"nāśinī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.364,"cpage":226,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":450,"gloss":"Daughter of Nanda. (Nandinī.} Or, Nandinī, a certain cow born in the family of the ce","body":"Daughter of Nanda. (Nandinī.}\nOr, Nandinī, a certain cow born in the family of the celestial cow (Kāmadhenu),; or nandinī, Gaṅgā.","dev":"तेजोवती","iast":"tejovatī","pada":[{"dev":"तेजस्","iast":"tejas"},{"dev":"वती","iast":"vatī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.222,"cpage":226,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":451,"gloss":"Destroyer of obstacles. (Vighnanāsini.)","body":"Vighna, hindrances to knowledge,","dev":"त्रिनयना","iast":"trinayanā","pada":[{"dev":"त्रि","iast":"tri"},{"dev":"नयना","iast":"nayanā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.545,"cpage":226,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":452,"gloss":"Splendour. (Tejováti.)","body":"(94)\nBecause she is the support of the Sun and other luminaries. The Śruti (Br. Up., 3-8-11), 66 In this indestructible (Brahman) the Sun and Moon were established.\"","dev":"लोलाक्षीकामरूपिणी","iast":"lolākṣīkāmarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"लोलाक्षी","iast":"lolākṣī"},{"dev":"कामरूपिणी","iast":"kāmarūpiṇī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.429,"cpage":226,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":453,"gloss":"Threeeyed. (Trinayanā.)","body":"The three eyes are the moon, the sun and the fire. Or, Trinayanā, is another name for the word vaushat (particle of invocation of gods). Or, Nayana, indicates proofs, Naya, to lead, i.e., leads one to proof. The Śāṇḍilya Sūtra (99) says: There are three proofs, viz., word, inference, and observation like Rudra.\" (Rudra has three eyes, neither more nor less, similarly there are three proofs.) Here Śabda (vocal authority) corresponds to Śravaṇa (Hearing, etc., of Br. Up., 2-4-5); inference, as it is mental, to Manana (perception) and observation to Nidhidhyasana (reflection). But occasionally different ideas may intrude this (reflection), hence the ascetic Śāṇḍilya (St. 99) takes Śabda first (because the last is more important). Upamāna (illustration, the third proof) is omitted here because it is not required. Hence Manu (Smṛ. Slo. 12, 105) enumerates three only. Perception, inference, and scriptures, consisting of different branches, these three should be used by those who desire to practise righteousness (Dharma).\" So Sāṃkhya (I, 88) and Yoga Satras (I, 7), repeat the above.\nOr Tri, three (paths), i.e., south, north and the path of Brahman, Naya to lead. She leads those who are entitled to have one of the three paths. The Devī Pr. says, are the Southern and Northern worlds, and the supreme There abode of Brahman, as she leads those who follow the right way, she is called Trinayanā.","dev":"मालिनी","iast":"mālinī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.25,"cpage":227,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":454,"gloss":"In the form of the desire of women. (Lolakshikamarupini.)","body":"To show her love is not limited to Śiva, she is so called.\nOr, she is in the form of Yogesvarī, who is the deity of desire. The Varāha Pr., \"(1) desire, (2) anger, greed, (4) passion, (5) bewilderment, (6) envy, (7) calumny, (3) (8) scorn, thus there are eight mothers. You should recognise desire as Yogeśvarī, anger as Maheśvarī, greed is\nsaid to be Vaiṣṇavī, passion as Brāhmaṇī, bewilderment is the selfexistent Kalyāṇī, envy as Indraja, the Devī, holding in her hand the death rod (Yamadanda) is herself calumny, and scorn is Varāha. Thus they are mentioned.\"","dev":"हंसिनी","iast":"haṃsinī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.333,"cpage":227,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":455,"gloss":"Wearing garlands. (Mālinī.)","body":"Malint is the name of the deity of fiftyone letters. Or, mālinī is a companion of Devī, mentioned in the Vāmana Pr. in the section on the marriage ceremony of Pārvatī. \"Then Mālinī clasped the foot of Hara. Hara said whatever you ask I will give, release my foot. Mālinī, then replied to Śiva, O Śiva, bestow your prosperity on my friend, then you will be released. Then Śiva said, I have already given, release me, etc.\"\nMālinī is a certain metre or the name of Gangā, According to the Viśva, Malin! means, A certain metre, a woman of the florist caste, the city of Champa, Gaurī, and Gaṅgā.\"\nMālinī, according to Dhaumya, is a girl of seven years\nof age.","dev":"माता","iast":"mātā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.4,"cpage":228,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":456,"gloss":"Swan. (Hamsini.)","body":"Or, Haṃsa means the Ajapā mantra.","dev":"मलयाचलवासिनी","iast":"malayācalavāsinī","pada":[{"dev":"मलयाचल","iast":"malayācala"},{"dev":"वासिनी","iast":"vāsinī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.5,"cpage":228,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":91},{"n":457,"gloss":"Mother. (Mata.)","body":"Because she is the mother of all. Or Mata, in the form of letters (matrikās). The St. Sam. (4-4-18) says : 66 As she is the mother of all Mantras, hence the Supreme Īśvarī is called Mātrikā.” Or Mata, is the mantra of the Nityā deity of the tenth day (of lunar fortnight). Mata is the deity, according to the Padma Pr., of the Kayavarohana place.\nMata is also the name of the Lakshmibija. The Viśva says, “Sri, rāma and mātā, mean Lakshini.\"","dev":"सुमुखी","iast":"sumukhī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.5,"cpage":228,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":92},{"n":458,"gloss":"Residing in the malaya mountain. (Malayachala vāsinī.)","body":"This deity is described in the Sabara Cintāmaṇi as the Malayalaya bhagavatī.\n27*\n(95)\n19th verse dívides 35 names.","dev":"नलिनी","iast":"nalinī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.364,"cpage":228,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":92},{"n":459,"gloss":"Handsomefaced. (Sumukhī.)","body":"Because by wisdom, the beauty of the face increases. The Śrutis say, One who knows this, his face shines.\" (Chānd. Up., 4-14-2) also, O child, your face shines like that of a sage (Brahmavit).\"\nOr, Sumukhī is a certain deity to be worshipped as a part of the Ṣoḍaśī (Mantra).","dev":"सुभ्रूः","iast":"subhrūḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.444,"cpage":229,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":92},{"n":460,"gloss":"Lotus. (Nalinī.)","body":"Her hands, feet, face, eyes and other limbs are like the lotus, according to the rule of creation (vide the com. on name 278)..\nOr, Nalinī, Gaṅgā. Or Nalinī: Nala, & king by that name, as he was identified with Devī by devotion, she is called Nalinī.\n461. 462.","dev":"शोभना","iast":"śobhanā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.286,"cpage":229,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":92},{"n":461,"gloss":"Lotus. (Nalinī.)","body":"Her hands, feet, face, eyes and other limbs are like the lotus, according to the rule of creation (vide the com. on name 278)..\nOr, Nalinī, Gaṅgā. Or Nalinī: Nala, & king by that name, as he was identified with Devī by devotion, she is called Nalinī.\n461. 462.","dev":"सुरनायिका","iast":"suranāyikā","pada":[{"dev":"सुर","iast":"sura"},{"dev":"नायिका","iast":"nāyikā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0,"cpage":229,"sure":0,"host":460,"v":92},{"n":462,"gloss":"Lotus. (Nalinī.)","body":"Her hands, feet, face, eyes and other limbs are like the lotus, according to the rule of creation (vide the com. on name 278)..\nOr, Nalinī, Gaṅgā. Or Nalinī: Nala, & king by that name, as he was identified with Devī by devotion, she is called Nalinī.\n461. 462.","dev":"कालकण्ठी","iast":"kālakaṇṭhī","pada":[{"dev":"काल","iast":"kāla"},{"dev":"कण्ठी","iast":"kaṇṭhī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0,"cpage":229,"sure":0,"host":460,"v":92},{"n":463,"gloss":"With beautiful eyebrows. (Subhrüh.)","body":"Handsome. (Sobhanā.)\nLeader of the Devas. (Suranāyikā.)\nThe Śruti says, 66\nDevas.\"\n(She is) the supreme power of the","dev":"कान्तिमती","iast":"kāntimatī","pada":[{"dev":"कान्ति","iast":"kānti"},{"dev":"मती","iast":"matī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0,"cpage":229,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":92},{"n":464,"gloss":"Wife of Kālakaṇṭha. (Kalakhan/hi.)","body":"Kālakaṇṭha: Śiva is called so because his throat is\ncoloured by poison. Vāyu Pr. says,\nIn the presence of multitude of Devas, Piśācas, serpents, and Rākṣasas, the virulant poison became transfixed in my throat ; hence I am called Kälakantha.”\nAccording to the Devī Pr. among the sixtyeight sacred places, Kalanjara is a place where Kālakaṇṭha worshipped.\nis\nOr, Kāla a soft low sound, Kaṇṭha, throat, .e., a soft low sound proceeds from her throat.\nOr. there is a story in the Liṅga Pr. that in order to destroy the Daitya Dāruka, \"Śiva created Kālī as well as Kapardinī and Kalakanṭhi.”","dev":"क्षोभिणी","iast":"kṣobhiṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.545,"cpage":229,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":92},{"n":465,"gloss":"Radiant. (Kantimahih.)","body":"","dev":"सूक्ष्मरूपिणी","iast":"sūkṣmarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"सूक्ष्म","iast":"sūkṣma"},{"dev":"रूपिणी","iast":"rūpiṇī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.364,"cpage":229,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":92},{"n":466,"gloss":"Causing emotion. (Kshobhini.)","body":"She excites (Kshub to excite) Śiva to create. The Viṣṇu Pr. says, The Lord Viṣṇu entering the Prakṛti and Purusha, by his own will excited (both) at the time of creation.\"\nOr, Kshobhin by the agitation of (her) mind she created multitudes of deities. The Varāha Pr. describing the Trimaty's says, 'Vaiṣṇavī (the wife of Viṣṇu) once went to the Mandara mountain to perform penance; after she had performed penance for a long time she became excited (by passion); from that excitement sprang young women of fair appearance, with blue curling hair, with lips red as the Bimba fruit, lotuseyed, with body like the red lotus wearing an anklet, beautiful, when the mind of Devī was agitated, hundreds and millions of such women arose with different faces.\"\n16):","dev":"वज्रेश्वरी","iast":"vajreśvarī","pada":[{"dev":"वज्र","iast":"vajra"},{"dev":"ईश्वरी","iast":"īśvarī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.2,"cpage":229,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":93},{"n":467,"gloss":"Of subtle form. (Sakshmarupini.)","body":"Sūkṣma, difficult to perceive. The Śruti (Kai. Up.\nSubtler than the subtle, eternal.\" (Katha. Up., 2-20): 'More minute than an atom.\"\nOr, Sūkṣma is the name of a kind of Homa (oblation). The Tantrarāja says, 'In the eternal and perishable centre of the Mūlādhāra, there is a fire. Thus there are twelve kinds of Homa corresponding to the letters, as mentioned.\"\nOr, as said above (vide No. 6, Com.) there are three forms, physical, subtle and supreme (and this refers to the second one).","dev":"वामदेवी","iast":"vāmadevī","pada":[{"dev":"वाम","iast":"vāma"},{"dev":"देवी","iast":"devī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.182,"cpage":230,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":93},{"n":468,"gloss":"Vajres'vari. (96)","body":"She is the sixth Nityā deity, or the tutelary deity of the Jālandhara, Pīṭha.\nOr, the Śrīcakra has twelve walls, all built of diamond; in the centre of the eleventh, there is a river called vajramayi and she is its deity. Durvāsa (Lalitā stavaratna, 44 and 45) says, \"There let the everflowing river called Fajra, be everlasting, filled with the sound of the sweet notes of the swans gliding on the beautiful waves ;\non the pleasant bank of that river, Vajresī flourishes decked with diamond ornaments praised by the Devas headed by Indra, the hurler of the thunderbolt.\"\nOr, Devī herself gave Indra the bow, vajra. The Brahmā. Pr. says when Indra performed penance in the water, From that water Devī arose and gave Indra the bow called Vajra. Again she disappeared and Indra satisfied returned to Svarga (heaven).”","dev":"वयो","iast":"vayo","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.25,"cpage":230,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":93},{"n":469,"gloss":"Vāmadevī. (The wife of Vāmadeva.)","body":"l'ama, to be worshipped, deva, the deity. The Aitareya The Devas addressed him; he indeed is to be worshipped by all of us, hence he is called Vāmadeva.”\nŚruti says,\nOr, vāma, by left side, Deva, he shines, i.e., Ardhanarisvara (half body is Śiva and other half is Devī) is\ncalled Vāmadeva.\nOr, Vāmadeva is one of the (five) forms Vyaha of Śiva as described in the Śiva Pr., Of beautiful appearance, red as the red paste (kuṃkuma), the north face of the Lord called Vāma is firmly established.”\nOr, Fame, fair; Vāma, the fruits of actions, Devī, the presiding deity. Or, Vāma, those devoted to the lefthand path (Vamachara). The Kālikā Pr. says, The worshipper, who is ever devoted to that path, is called l'amu.” She is their goddess. The Devī Pr. says, Vāma, opposite, inverted ; Devī gives bliss through that path she is called\nVāmadevī.","dev":"वस्थाविवर्जिता","iast":"vasthāvivarjitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.333,"cpage":231,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":93},{"n":470,"gloss":"Exempt from the states of life. vivarjitā.)","body":"(Vayovastha-\nVayas, the states of childhood, boyhood, youth, etc. : because she is eternal.","dev":"सिद्धेश्वरी","iast":"siddheśvarī","pada":[{"dev":"सिद्ध","iast":"siddha"},{"dev":"ईश्वरी","iast":"īśvarī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.4,"cpage":231,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":93},{"n":471,"gloss":"Queen of the Siddhas. (Siddhesvari.)","body":"The Siddhus, are Goraksha and others.\ndeity of this name at Benares.","dev":"सिद्धविद्या","iast":"siddhavidyā","pada":[{"dev":"सिद्ध","iast":"siddha"},{"dev":"विद्या","iast":"vidyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.667,"cpage":231,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":93},{"n":472,"gloss":"Eternal Science. (Siddhavidyā.)","body":"There is also a\n(ie., the Pañcadaśī.) Hence the Pañcadaś is exempted from the precautions necessary for other Mantras\n(Siddhārikosha). The Kādimata says, The (astrological) calculations are unnecessary as it is the eternal and universal (Mantra).\"","dev":"सिद्धमाता","iast":"siddhamātā","pada":[{"dev":"सिद्ध","iast":"siddha"},{"dev":"माता","iast":"mātā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.545,"cpage":231,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":93},{"n":473,"gloss":"The mother of Siddhas. (Siddhamätä.)","body":"Mālā, protector.","dev":"यशस्विनी","iast":"yaśasvinī","pada":[{"dev":"यशस्","iast":"yaśas"},{"dev":"विनी","iast":"vinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.222,"cpage":232,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":93},{"n":474,"gloss":"Famous. (Yasasvinī.)","body":"The Śruti (Mahānāra. Up., 7-10) says,\nBecause his\nname is the great fame.\"\n(97)\n(Viśuddhi·","dev":"श्रीललितासहस्रनामस्तोत्रम","iast":"śrīlalitāsahasranāmastotrama","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.235,"cpage":232,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":94},{"n":475,"gloss":"Abiding in the Viśuddhi centre. chakranilaya.)","body":"Now he proceeds to praise Devī, by sixtytwo names, according to the Yoginīnyāsa, under the forms of the seven Yogini deities who preside over the seven cakras, Vis'uddhi, etc., whose names begin with the syllables Da, ra, la, ka, sa, ha, ya (respectively). The meditation on the Ḍākinī is, In the Viśuddhi centre which is in the cavity of the throat, in the sixteenpetalled lotus, I adore Ḍākinī, the rosy, threeeyed, armed with club, the sword, the trident and a large skin, having one face, striking the ignorant with terror, ever fond of sweet food (Pāyasa), the deity presiding over the organ of touch, whose form is surrounded by Amṛta and other deities, and wo shipped by warriors.\"\nViśuddhi chuckra ; her abode is in the pericarp of the sixteenpetalled lotus.","dev":"विशुद्धचक्रनिलया","iast":"viśuddhacakranilayā","pada":[{"dev":"विशुद्ध","iast":"viśuddha"},{"dev":"चक्र","iast":"cakra"},{"dev":"निलया","iast":"nilayā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.75,"cpage":232,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":94},{"n":476,"gloss":"Of rosy complexion","body":"","dev":"रक्तवर्णा","iast":"raktavarṇā","pada":[{"dev":"रक्त","iast":"rakta"},{"dev":"वर्णा","iast":"varṇā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":232,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":94},{"n":477,"gloss":"Threeeyed","body":"478.\npraharana.)\nArmed with the club, etc.\n(Khadvāngādi-\nKhade&nga, (lit.) the foot of a bed, or a club with a human skull at its end.","dev":"त्रिलोचना","iast":"trilocanā","pada":[{"dev":"त्रि","iast":"tri"},{"dev":"लोचना","iast":"locanā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":232,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":94},{"n":478,"gloss":"Threeeyed. (Trilochana.)","body":"478.\npraharana.)\nArmed with the club, etc.\n(Khadvāngādi-\nKhade&nga, (lit.) the foot of a bed, or a club with a human skull at its end.","dev":"खटाङ्गादिप्रहरणा","iast":"khaṭāṅgādipraharaṇā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":232,"sure":0,"host":477,"v":94},{"n":479,"gloss":"Possessed of one face","body":"(98)","dev":"वदनैकसमन्विता","iast":"vadanaikasamanvitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":232,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":94},{"n":480,"gloss":"Fond of milk food","body":"481.\n482. yankari.)\nThe deity of organ of touch. (Tvaksthā.) Filling the ignorant with fear.\n(Pasulokabha-\nPaśus are those devoid of the knowledge of nonduality. The Śrutis (Br. Up., 1-4-10) say, \"Now one who worships another deity (saying) the deity is different, and I am different, he knows not, he is like a beast (Pas'u).\" \"Fear comes when there is duality\" (Br. Up., 1-4-2). To him, who makes a small hole (difference), next fear comes (Tai. Up., II, 7-1.)","dev":"पायसान्नप्रिया","iast":"pāyasānnapriyā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":232,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":95},{"n":481,"gloss":"Fond of milk food. (Payasānnapriyā.)","body":"481.\n482. yankari.)\nThe deity of organ of touch. (Tvaksthā.) Filling the ignorant with fear.\n(Pasulokabha-\nPaśus are those devoid of the knowledge of nonduality. The Śrutis (Br. Up., 1-4-10) say, \"Now one who worships another deity (saying) the deity is different, and I am different, he knows not, he is like a beast (Pas'u).\" \"Fear comes when there is duality\" (Br. Up., 1-4-2). To him, who makes a small hole (difference), next fear comes (Tai. Up., II, 7-1.)","dev":"त्वक्स्था","iast":"tvaksthā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":232,"sure":0,"host":480,"v":95},{"n":482,"gloss":"Fond of milk food. (Payasānnapriyā.)","body":"481.\n482. yankari.)\nThe deity of organ of touch. (Tvaksthā.) Filling the ignorant with fear.\n(Pasulokabha-\nPaśus are those devoid of the knowledge of nonduality. The Śrutis (Br. Up., 1-4-10) say, \"Now one who worships another deity (saying) the deity is different, and I am different, he knows not, he is like a beast (Pas'u).\" \"Fear comes when there is duality\" (Br. Up., 1-4-2). To him, who makes a small hole (difference), next fear comes (Tai. Up., II, 7-1.)","dev":"पशुलोकभयंकरी","iast":"paśulokabhayaṃkarī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":232,"sure":0,"host":480,"v":95},{"n":483,"gloss":"Surrounded by amṛta and other Śaktis","body":"Amṛta, etc., are the sixteen great Śaktis from Auritakarshini to Akṣara, each seated in each of the sixteen petals. The Svacchanda Tr. says, One finger above that (Anāhata) is the Viśuddhi with sixteen petals. Ḍākinī, the supreme ruler is in the centre. Amṛta to Akṣara are in the sixteen petals. Above that is Chandrabimba.\"","dev":"अमृतादिमहाशक्तिसंवृता","iast":"amṛtādimahāśaktisaṃvṛtā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":233,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":95},{"n":484,"gloss":"Dakinis'vari. Thus she possesses the nine","body":"attri- (99)\nbutes.","dev":"डाकिनीश्वरी","iast":"ḍākinīśvarī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":233,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":95},{"n":485,"gloss":"Abiding in the Anāhata lotus","body":"In the heart, in the twelvepetalled lotus, called Anāhata cakra, the Yogini named Rākinī, resides. Her meditation is as follows: \"We meditate upon Rākinī who is in the twelvepetalled lotus of the heart, having two faces, with protruding tusks, black coloured, bearing the disc, the trident, the skull, and the drum in her hands, threeeyed, presiding over the blood, attended by Kālarātri and other deities, fond of greasy food, worshipped by warriors, and the giver of desired objects.\"","dev":"अनाहताब्जनिलया","iast":"anāhatābjanilayā","pada":[{"dev":"अनाहत","iast":"anāhata"},{"dev":"अब्ज","iast":"abja"},{"dev":"निलया","iast":"nilayā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":233,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":96},{"n":486,"gloss":"Black in hue","body":"Śyāmā, a girl of sixteen years of age and Abha, like.","dev":"श्यामाभा","iast":"śyāmābhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":233,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":96},{"n":487,"gloss":"Twofaced","body":"","dev":"वदनद्वया","iast":"vadanadvayā","pada":[{"dev":"वदन","iast":"vadana"},{"dev":"द्वया","iast":"dvayā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":233,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":96},{"n":488,"gloss":"With shining tusks","body":"","dev":"दंष्ट्रोज्ज्वला","iast":"daṃṣṭrojjvalā","pada":[{"dev":"दंष्ट्र","iast":"daṃṣṭra"},{"dev":"उज्ज्वला","iast":"ujjvalā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":233,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":96},{"n":489,"gloss":"Wearing a garland of Akṣa beads, etc","body":"The Akṣamālā here stands for the disc in the above (485). Akṣa, the wheel of a chariot, ma, resemblance, la, bears, ie, resemblance of a chariot.","dev":"ऽक्षमालादिधरा","iast":"'kṣamālādidharā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":233,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":96},{"n":490,"gloss":"Residing in the blood","body":"(100)","dev":"रुधिरसंस्थिता","iast":"rudhirasaṃsthitā","pada":[{"dev":"रुधिर","iast":"rudhira"},{"dev":"संस्थिता","iast":"saṃsthitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":234,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":96},{"n":491,"gloss":"Attended by hosts of Śaktis, Kālarātri, etc","body":"There is a certain Śakti called Kalarātrī.\nConcerning\nthe three forms (of Devī) says the Varāha Pr. : 66 The Śakti of Rudra (Raudrī) born from darkness, who went to the blue mountain determined to perform penance, O earth, hear her vow That Raudrī, the supreme Śakti ever devoted to penance, causing destruction, is called Kalarātrī.”\nAdi (ete.): twelve Śaktis from Kālarātri to Tankārī, one in each petal (of Anāhata).","dev":"कालरात्र्यादिशक्त्यौघवृता","iast":"kālarātryādiśaktyaughavṛtā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":234,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":97},{"n":492,"gloss":"Fond of greasy food","body":"","dev":"स्निग्धौदनप्रिया","iast":"snigdhaudanapriyā","pada":[{"dev":"स्निग्ध","iast":"snigdha"},{"dev":"ओदन","iast":"odana"},{"dev":"प्रिया","iast":"priyā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.952,"cpage":234,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":97},{"n":493,"gloss":"Granting boons to great warriors","body":"Vi, many, ira or ira, excited or intoxicated; the meaning is the Viras are the (trained orators) gifted with eloquence. Or, Mahūvīra, a kind of vessel used in the Soma sacritice, as it is explained in the Śruti, Mahavira. The meaning is the Mahāvīras are those who are continually drinking the nectar of Brahman. Indras, those who know Brahman. Ida, those who directly realise (dra) Brahman, which is the Self, the witness of all, saying 'I ain He.' The Śruti says, He perceived, hence he is called Indra.\"\nOr, Virendras are those who realise the fourth state (Turīya) in the other three states. The Śiva Sūtra (I, 11) says, He is Viresa, who enjoys in a threefold way.\" The commentator Varadarāja interprets the Sūtra thus, Vīras, senses causing the miseries of separateness, extending within and without, and I is the lord of the senses.\"\nOr, Mahāvīru, is Prahlāda, and Indra, the lord of the Devas. The De. Bhag. Pr. (Book IV) says, That Indra and\nPrahlāda praised Devī after their fight which lasted a hundred divine years, and she granted them boons.\n20th verse divides 39 names.","dev":"महावीरेन्द्रवरदा","iast":"mahāvīrendravaradā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"वीरेन्द्र","iast":"vīrendra"},{"dev":"वरदा","iast":"varadā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":234,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":97},{"n":494,"gloss":"Assuming the form of the Mother Rākinī","body":"(101)","dev":"राकिण्यम्बास्वरूपिणी","iast":"rākiṇyambāsvarūpiṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":235,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":97},{"n":495,"gloss":"Abiding in the Maṇipūra lotus","body":"Here\nMaṇipūra is the tenpetalled lotus in the navel. the Yogini named Lākinī resides. Her meditation is: \"Let us meditate on Lākinī in the eightpetalled lotus of the navel, threefaced, tusked, redcoloured, bearing in her hands, the dart (Śakti), the thunderbolt, club, and abhaya (a weapon), most terrible, attended by Damari and other Śaktis, inspiring the ignorant with terror, presiding over the flesh, fond of sweetmeat, doing good to all.\"","dev":"मणिपूराब्जनिलया","iast":"maṇipūrābjanilayā","pada":[{"dev":"मणिपूर","iast":"maṇipūra"},{"dev":"अब्ज","iast":"abja"},{"dev":"निलया","iast":"nilayā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":235,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":98},{"n":496,"gloss":"Threefaced","body":"","dev":"वदनत्रयसंयुता","iast":"vadanatrayasaṃyutā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":235,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":98},{"n":497,"gloss":"Armed with the thunderbolt and other weapons","body":"","dev":"वज्रादिकायुधोपेता","iast":"vajrādikāyudhopetā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":235,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":98},{"n":498,"gloss":"Attended by Dâmari and others (Śaktis)","body":"etc. (Damaryadibhirävrita.)\nÅdi (etc.), are the ten Śaktis from Dāmari to Phatkarini.","dev":"डामर्यादिभिरावृता","iast":"ḍāmaryādibhirāvṛtā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.143,"cpage":235,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":98},{"n":499,"gloss":"Bloodcoloured","body":"","dev":"रक्तवर्णा","iast":"raktavarṇā","pada":[{"dev":"रक्त","iast":"rakta"},{"dev":"वर्णा","iast":"varṇā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":235,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":99},{"n":500,"gloss":"Presiding over flesh","body":"(102)\nThus ends the sixth Kāla called Ruchi with the fifth hundred in the Saubhāgyabhāskara composed by Bhăsurănanda.\nSIXTH HUNDRED","dev":"मांसनिष्ठा","iast":"māṃsaniṣṭhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":235,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":99},{"n":501,"gloss":"Fond of sweetmeats","body":"","dev":"गुडान्नप्रीतमानसा","iast":"guḍānnaprītamānasā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":235,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":99},{"n":502,"gloss":"Conferring happiness on all her devotees","body":"","dev":"समस्तभक्तसुखदा","iast":"samastabhaktasukhadā","pada":[{"dev":"समस्त","iast":"samasta"},{"dev":"भक्त","iast":"bhakta"},{"dev":"सुखदा","iast":"sukhadā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":235,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":99},{"n":503,"gloss":"Assuming the form of the mother Lākinī","body":"(103)","dev":"लाकिन्यम्बास्वरूपिणी","iast":"lākinyambāsvarūpiṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":235,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":99},{"n":504,"gloss":"Residing in the Svadhisana lotus. thin mbujagatā.)","body":"(Svādhiṣṭh-\nThe Yogini called Käkini resides in the sixpetalled Svādhiṣṭh/häna lotus; her meditation is: \"We meditate upon Kākinī who resides in the sixpetalled Svādhiṣṭh/hana lotus, fourfaced, threeeyed, bearing in her hands the trident, the noose, skull and abhaya (weapon), ever proud, presiding over the fat (or lymph), fond of mead, attended by Bandhinī and others, yellow, fond of food mixed with curd, giver of desired objects.\"","dev":"स्वाधिष्ठानाम्बुजगता","iast":"svādhiṣṭhānāmbujagatā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":0.556,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":100},{"n":505,"gloss":"Fourfaced and fascinating. manohara.)","body":"(Chaturvaktra-","dev":"चतुर्वक्त्रमनोहरा","iast":"caturvaktramanoharā","pada":[{"dev":"चतुर्वक्त्र","iast":"caturvaktra"},{"dev":"मनोहरा","iast":"manoharā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.533,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":100},{"n":506,"gloss":"Armed with the trident and other weapons","body":"","dev":"शूलाद्यायुधसंपन्ना","iast":"śūlādyāyudhasaṃpannā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":100},{"n":507,"gloss":"Yellow","body":"","dev":"पीतवर्णा","iast":"pītavarṇā","pada":[{"dev":"पीत","iast":"pīta"},{"dev":"वर्णा","iast":"varṇā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":100},{"n":508,"gloss":"Very proud","body":"Caused by her excessive beauty, etc.","dev":"ऽतिगर्विता","iast":"'tigarvitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":100},{"n":509,"gloss":"Presiding over fat","body":"","dev":"मेदोनिष्ठा","iast":"medoniṣṭhā","pada":[{"dev":"मेदस्","iast":"medas"},{"dev":"निष्ठा","iast":"niṣṭhā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":101},{"n":510,"gloss":"Fond of mead","body":"(104)\nMadiun, honey or spirituous liquor. The Śruti says, One who presents an oblation with hony (Madhu), pleases the great Devī.\"","dev":"मधुप्रीता","iast":"madhuprītā","pada":[{"dev":"मधु","iast":"madhu"},{"dev":"प्रीता","iast":"prītā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":101},{"n":511,"gloss":"Attended by Bandhinī and others","body":"These are the six deities from Bandhinī to Lamboshthi.","dev":"बन्दिन्यादिसमन्विता","iast":"bandinyādisamanvitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.957,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":101},{"n":512,"gloss":"Fond of food mixed with curd","body":"","dev":"दध्यन्नासक्तहृदया","iast":"dadhyannāsaktahṛdayā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":101},{"n":513,"gloss":"Assuming the form of Kākinī","body":"","dev":"काकिनीरूपधारिणी","iast":"kākinīrūpadhāriṇī","pada":[{"dev":"काकिनी","iast":"kākinī"},{"dev":"रूपधारिणी","iast":"rūpadhāriṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":101},{"n":514,"gloss":"Ascending the Mūlādhāra lotus. (Müllhārām- (105)","body":"bujārūdha.)\nThe Yogini called Śākinī resides in the fourpetalled Mūlādhāra lotus. Her meditation is: \"We meditate upon Sikini who resides in the fourpetalled Mūlādhāra lotus, fivefaced, threeeyed, smokecoloured, presiding over the Lones, bearing in her hands the elephanthook, lotus, book,\n28*\nand Jñānamudrā (a gesture), attended by gentle Varada and other deities, fond of eating mulgabeams, intoxicated with mead.\"","dev":"मूलाधाराम्बुजारूढा","iast":"mūlādhārāmbujārūḍhā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":0.625,"cpage":236,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":102},{"n":515,"gloss":"Five faced","body":"","dev":"पञ्चवक्त्रा","iast":"pañcavaktrā","pada":[{"dev":"पञ्च","iast":"pañca"},{"dev":"वक्त्रा","iast":"vaktrā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":102},{"n":516,"gloss":"Presiding over bones","body":"","dev":"ऽस्थिसंस्थिता","iast":"'sthisaṃsthitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":102},{"n":517,"gloss":"Armed with elephant hook, etc","body":"","dev":"अङ्कुशादिप्रहरणा","iast":"aṅkuśādipraharaṇā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.889,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":102},{"n":518,"gloss":"Attend by Varada and others","body":"There are four Śaktis from Varală to Sarasvatī. (106)","dev":"वरदादिनिषेविता","iast":"varadādiniṣevitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":102},{"n":519,"gloss":"Fond of food mixed with mudgabeams","body":"","dev":"मुद्गौदनासक्तचित्ता","iast":"mudgaudanāsaktacittā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":0.9,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":103},{"n":520,"gloss":"Assuming the form of Śākinī","body":"","dev":"साकिन्यम्चास्वरूपिणी","iast":"sākinyamcāsvarūpiṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.909,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":103},{"n":521,"gloss":"Residing over the Ājñā centre lotus","body":"There is a twopetalled lotus in the Ãjna centre between the eyebrows; there resides the Yogini, called Hākinī. (The meditation on her is) : We meditate upon Hākini, residing between the eyebrows, in the twopetalled Bindu lotus, white, holding the Jnānamudrā, drum, lotus, Rudraksha bead, and skull, residing in the marrow, sixfaced, threeeyed, attended by Haṃsavatī and other Śaktis, fond of saffronflavoured food, doing good to all.”","dev":"आज्ञाचक्राब्जनिलया","iast":"ājñācakrābjanilayā","pada":[{"dev":"आज्ञाचक्र","iast":"ājñācakra"},{"dev":"अब्ज","iast":"abja"},{"dev":"निलया","iast":"nilayā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":103},{"n":522,"gloss":"White","body":"","dev":"शुक्लवर्णा","iast":"śuklavarṇā","pada":[{"dev":"शुक्ल","iast":"śukla"},{"dev":"वर्णा","iast":"varṇā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":103},{"n":523,"gloss":"Sixfaced","body":"(107)","dev":"षडानना","iast":"ṣaḍānanā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":103},{"n":524,"gloss":"Presiding over the marrow","body":"","dev":"मज्जासंस्था","iast":"majjāsaṃsthā","pada":[{"dev":"मज्जा","iast":"majjā"},{"dev":"संस्था","iast":"saṃsthā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":104},{"n":525,"gloss":"Attended by Haṃsavatī and other śaktis","body":"The two Śaktis are Haṃsavatī and Kshamavati.","dev":"हंसवतीमुख्यशक्तिसमन्विता","iast":"haṃsavatīmukhyaśaktisamanvitā","pada":[{"dev":"हंसवती","iast":"haṃsavatī"},{"dev":"मुख्य","iast":"mukhya"},{"dev":"शक्ति","iast":"śakti"},{"dev":"समन्विता","iast":"samanvitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":104},{"n":526,"gloss":"Fond of saffron flavoured food","body":"","dev":"हरिद्रान्नैकरसिका","iast":"haridrānnaikarasikā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":104},{"n":527,"gloss":"Assuming the form of Hākinī","body":"(108)","dev":"हाकिनीरूपधारिणी","iast":"hākinīrūpadhāriṇī","pada":[{"dev":"हाकिनी","iast":"hākinī"},{"dev":"रूपधारिणी","iast":"rūpadhāriṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":104},{"n":528,"gloss":"Residing in the thousandpetalled lotus","body":"In the Brahmarandhra in the thousandpetalled lotus, resides the Yogini named Yākinī. (Her meditation is): \"We meditate upon the beneficent Devī, Yākinī, residing in the moon of the pericarp of the thousandpetalled lotus in the Brahmarandhra, presiding over the semen, armed with all kinds of weapons, facing on every side, attended by the host of the Śaktis of the letters from A to Kṣa (fiftyone), all coloured, fond of all kinds of food, and devoted to the supreme Śiva.","dev":"सहस्रदलपद्मस्था","iast":"sahasradalapadmasthā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":237,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":105},{"n":529,"gloss":"Brilliant with all colours","body":"Or, Sarva, all, Varna (the fiftyone) letters from A to Kṣa, that is the fifty Śaktis from Amṛta to Kshmavatī ; upa, in the petals. Counting these (fifty) backwards and forwards we obtain a hundred Śaktis; these each reside in ten petals. Upa means ten (according to the Vararuci's rule pa one and u, ciphor), i.e., each residing in ten petals. Hence some repeat in the Yoginīnyāsa ten times each of those Śaktis to make up a (thousand).","dev":"सर्ववर्णोपशोभिता","iast":"sarvavarṇopaśobhitā","pada":[],"pconf":0.56,"fit":1,"cpage":238,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":105},{"n":530,"gloss":"Armed with all weapons","body":"The Śruti (Rudrādhyāya) says, You have thousands\nof arms and thousands of weapons.\"","dev":"सर्वायुधधरा","iast":"sarvāyudhadharā","pada":[{"dev":"सर्वायुध","iast":"sarvāyudha"},{"dev":"धरा","iast":"dharā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":238,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":105},{"n":531,"gloss":"Residing in the Semen","body":"According to the Bha. Uttara Pr. Śukla is the name of a kind of meditation at the time of sexual enjoyment.","dev":"शुक्लसंस्थिता","iast":"śuklasaṃsthitā","pada":[{"dev":"शुक्ल","iast":"śukla"},{"dev":"संस्थिता","iast":"saṃsthitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":238,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":105},{"n":532,"gloss":"Facing on every side","body":"(Bh. Gītā, 13-13): Everywhere eyes, heads, and\nmouths.\"\n21st verse divides 31 names.\n(109)","dev":"सर्वतोमुखी","iast":"sarvatomukhī","pada":[{"dev":"सर्वतस्","iast":"sarvatas"},{"dev":"मुखी","iast":"mukhī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":238,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":105},{"n":533,"gloss":"Fond of all kinds of food","body":"Assuming the form of the mother yākinī. (Yakinyambasvarapinī.)\n534.","dev":"सर्वोदनप्रीतचित्ता","iast":"sarvodanaprītacittā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":238,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":106},{"n":534,"gloss":"Fond of all kinds of food. (Sarvaudanapritachitta.)","body":"Assuming the form of the mother yākinī. (Yakinyambasvarapinī.)\n534.","dev":"याकिन्यम्बास्वरूपिणी","iast":"yākinyambāsvarūpiṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":238,"sure":0,"host":533,"v":106},{"n":535,"gloss":"Svāhā","body":"Svāhā.","dev":"स्वाहा","iast":"svāhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":238,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":106},{"n":536,"gloss":"Svadhi. Thus having described","body":"under the forms of Yoginis, he proceeds to describe (Her) with other qualities.\n'6.\nThe words Svākā and Srudha are used when oblations are offered to gods. The Kośa says, The words Svähi Sraushaf, Vausha, Vasha and Svadhã are used when the oblations are offered to Devas.\" Hence these two words indicate Devī herself. The Mark. Pr. says, \"There are seven words which are used in the Soma (sacrifice), in the oblation and in the cooking. By repeating (or uttering) your name (only), O Devī, the merit of repeating these names are obtained by Brahmavādins.\" In another place (DeviMiha.): By uttering your name all the host of gods, O Devī, is satisfied in all sacrifices, for thou art Srāhā and thou art named Svudhã, the word which gives satisfaction to the hosts of Pitṛs.\" The Prapañcasāra explains the meaning of these words thus: \"Sea means heaven and also the Self, aha the attainment. and Srākā is the\nwife of fire.\"\nOr Sva\nAccording to the Tai. Śruti, “Your own (Sra) speech (ahā) means one's own speech. The Sāmaveda Bra. the Yāska Nirukta also explain 'Svāhā' thus, Su well aha, speech, or Sva, Self, āha, to speak.\" Some others explain this, Su, good, aha, to be given as an oblation. one's own people, āha, to go, i.c., she recognises the people as her own Self. Or su, well, A, Brahmā, bu, to go. Svāhā is the wife of Śiva in the form of fire; she is the mother of Skanda. The Liṅga Pr. says, 66 The wife of Śiva in the form of fire is said to be Svākā. faced one (Skanda) is called by the wise the son.\" The same confirms the Vāyu Pr. also. According to the Padma Pr. this is the presiding deity of the city of Maheś'vara.\nThe divine six-\nSvadha : 814, well, A, Viṣṇu, dha nourishes (or protects).","dev":"स्वधा","iast":"svadhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":238,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":106},{"n":537,"gloss":"Devoid of intelligence","body":"Amati, is Avidyā or, A, little, muti, knowledge, i.e., she is in the form of the knowledge of modifications. Durgā. charya in his commentary (on Śukla Yajus) explains the word amati, as the knowledge of the Self.\nOr amaiti, as said before (vide No. 397) refers to the first creation which was devoid of intelligence.","dev":"ऽमति","iast":"'mati","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.8,"cpage":239,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":106},{"n":538,"gloss":"Intelligence","body":"The next creation was accompanied by intelligence. Or, Medhā, individual intelligence. The Dev! Maha. says, \"Devī who resides in all beings in the form of intelligence\"; and “O Devī, thou art intelligence, thou art the essence of all the scriptures.\"\nAccording to the Padma Pr. the deity called Medhā is in the country of Kasmira.","dev":"र्मेधा","iast":"rmedhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.857,"cpage":240,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":106},{"n":539,"gloss":"Srutih","body":"","dev":"श्रुति","iast":"śruti","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.857,"cpage":240,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":106},{"n":540,"gloss":"Smritih. The Vedas and the Smṛtis of Manu, etc., are but her form. The Karma Pr. say","body":"Smritih.\nThe Vedas and the Smṛtis of Manu, etc., are but her form. The Karma Pr. says, Rigs, Yajus, Sāman, and Ātharvaṇa are the inherent form of Brahman, thus is the eternal Śakti.\"\nOr, Śruti and Smṛti, the knowledge in the form of hear; ing and recollection. The Vāyu Pr. says, As She recollects all actions, present, past, and future, she is called Smṛti.\" The Devī Pr.,“ (She is the Smṛti) because she recollects.\"","dev":"स्मृतिर","iast":"smṛtira","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.8,"cpage":240,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":106},{"n":541,"gloss":"The best","body":"Because there is none superior to her. The Śruti and Smṛti (Sve. Up., 6, 8, Bh. Gītā, 11, 43), \"Nothing is to be found equal or superior to him.” The De. Bhag. Pr. (Book III): People never say of a man devoid of strength ‘he is deprived of Rudra or of Viṣṇu,' but say, 'deprived of Śakti' (energy).”\nOr, Anutta, not depending, ma, intelligence or dominion, i.e., whose intelligence or dominion is not derived from others.\n(110)","dev":"नुत्तमा","iast":"nuttamā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":240,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":106},{"n":542,"gloss":"Famed for righteousness","body":"Whose fame sanctifies.","dev":"पुण्यकीर्त्तिः","iast":"puṇyakīrttiḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":240,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":107},{"n":543,"gloss":"Attained through righteousness","body":"Puṇya, good actions performed in previous births. The De. Bhag. Pr. says, \"Only those ever righteous\nascetics, who are devoted to wisdom, see; but the men of desire see not the holy and beneficent Devī.\"","dev":"पुण्यलभ्या","iast":"puṇyalabhyā","pada":[{"dev":"पुण्य","iast":"puṇya"},{"dev":"लभ्या","iast":"labhyā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":240,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":107},{"n":544,"gloss":"Hearing of and praising (thee)","body":"are holy.","dev":"पुण्यश्रवणकीर्तना","iast":"puṇyaśravaṇakīrtanā","pada":[{"dev":"पुण्य","iast":"puṇya"},{"dev":"श्रवण","iast":"śravaṇa"},{"dev":"कीर्तना","iast":"kīrtanā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":241,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":107},{"n":545,"gloss":"Worshipped by Indra's wife","body":"This story occurs in the De. Bhag. Pr. (Book VI) : when Nahusha was ruling the heaven, the wife of Indra worshipped Devī that Indra might be restored. Thus addressed, the wife of Indra with cheerful mind received the powerful mantra of Devī, from the teacher (Bṛhaspati) and worshipped earnestly Tripurasundarī, by oblations of food, flowers, etc.","dev":"पुलोमजार्चिता","iast":"pulomajārcitā","pada":[{"dev":"पुलोमजा","iast":"pulomajā"},{"dev":"अर्चिता","iast":"arcitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":241,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":107},{"n":546,"gloss":"Remover of bonds","body":"Bandha, bonds of ignorance or prison.\nIn the Harivaṃśa, Aniruddha says, 'O Śaṃkarī, thou art praised by these and other names by me; by thy grace, let me be soon freed from prison. O largeeyed one, behold, I take refuge in thy feet. You are to liberate me from every bond Durgā, the powerful one, thus praised, set free the brave Aniruddha, who was confined in the town of Bana.\" Thus the De. Bhag. Pr. also (Book VI) says, Ekavali was imprisoned by a Daitya called Kālaketu, was Once a princess named freed by Devī who was worshipped by Yasovati, the friend of the princess.\"\n(111)","dev":"बन्धमोचनी","iast":"bandhamocanī","pada":[{"dev":"बन्ध","iast":"bandha"},{"dev":"मोचनी","iast":"mocanī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":241,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":107},{"n":547,"gloss":"Wavy haired","body":"","dev":"बर्ब बंधु रालका","iast":"barba baṃdhu rālakā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.75,"cpage":241,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":107},{"n":548,"gloss":"In the Vimarśa form","body":"Vimarśa is the (first) inherent vibration of Parabrahman who is in the Prakāśa aspect.\nThe Saubhāgya\nSudhodaya says, In that (Brahman) there is an inherent Śakti in the form of vibration called Vimarśa. (Śakti) produces the animate and inanimate universe, the same also destroys it.”\nOr Vimarśa means significant words (i.e., speech). The Mātṛkāviveka says, \"Without Vimarśa (speech) how is thought (Prakāśa) manifested? Without thought to be\nF\nspoken of, how can there be speech? Therefore thought is required for the existence of speech, and thought, even when there is selfconsciousness (for its own existence) required speech.\"","dev":"विमर्शरूपिणी","iast":"vimarśarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"विमर्श","iast":"vimarśa"},{"dev":"रूपिणी","iast":"rūpiṇī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":241,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":108},{"n":549,"gloss":"Vidyā. Because she is the Vidyā which confers salvation. The Devī Maha. says, The hol","body":"Vidyā.\nBecause she is the Vidyā which confers salvation. The Devī Maha. says, The holy supreme Devī is Vidyā.\" The Gauḍapāda Sūtra says, She herself 18 Vidyā.\" She means Devī as described in the previous Sūtra as in the form of absolute consciousness\".\nOr Vidyā, a certain kind of Kāla, which is in the light (Tejas) and its nature is described in the Śaiva Tantras. \"By the discrimination of the products of Māyā, one recognises the state of wisdom, that supreme Kāla is known as Vidyā, consisting of knowledge and action.”","dev":"विद्या","iast":"vidyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":242,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":108},{"n":550,"gloss":"The mother of the universe (consisting) of ether, etc","body":"The Śruti (Tai. Up., II, 2), Ether arose from Ātman.\"","dev":"वियदादिजगत्प्रसूः","iast":"viyadādijagatprasūḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.952,"cpage":242,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":108},{"n":551,"gloss":"Alleviating all diseases","body":"Vyaddi, old age, etc.","dev":"सर्वव्याधिप्रशमनी","iast":"sarvavyādhipraśamanī","pada":[{"dev":"सर्व","iast":"sarva"},{"dev":"व्याधि","iast":"vyādhi"},{"dev":"प्रशमनी","iast":"praśamanī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":242,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":108},{"n":552,"gloss":"Dispelling all death","body":"Mṛtyu, untimely death, fatal disease, etc. The Śrutis (Sve. Up., 4-15, Katha. Up., 3—15) say : 56 Knowing Him thus, he breaks the nooses of death freed from the power of death.\"\n;\"He is (112)","dev":"सर्वमृत्युनिवारिणी","iast":"sarvamṛtyunivāriṇī","pada":[{"dev":"सर्वमृत्यु","iast":"sarvamṛtyu"},{"dev":"निवारिणी","iast":"nivāriṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.957,"cpage":242,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":108},{"n":553,"gloss":"The first","body":"As she is the root cause of the whole universe she is the first to be enumerated.\nOr, Ganya, the hosts (of Maruts, etc.), she is the first commander of these.","dev":"अग्रगण्या","iast":"agragaṇyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":242,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":109},{"n":554,"gloss":"Of unthinkable form","body":"Because she is free from connection with the qualities, her nature is unthinkable.","dev":"ऽचिन्त्यरूपा","iast":"'cintyarūpā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.923,"cpage":242,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":109},{"n":555,"gloss":"Destroyer of sin in the Kālī (age)","body":"She destroys the sin which necessarily predominates in the Kālī age and which cannot be destroyed by others. The Karma Pr. says, Water is able to quench the fire, the presence of the sun to dispel darkness, and the repetition of the names of Devī, to destroy the multitude of sins in the Kālī age.\" The Brahmă. Pr. : “The remembrance of the feet of Supreme Śakti is said to be the highest expiation for sins consciously or unconsciously committed.\"","dev":"कलिकल्मषनाशिनी","iast":"kalikalmaṣanāśinī","pada":[{"dev":"कलि","iast":"kali"},{"dev":"कल्मष","iast":"kalmaṣa"},{"dev":"नाशिनी","iast":"nāśinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.947,"cpage":242,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":109},{"n":556,"gloss":"Kātyāyanī. The daughter of a Ṛṣi named Kata. This is the name of the deity in the collective form of the brightness","body":"of all the Devas. The Vāmana Pr. says, That brightness which is the best, and the greatest, is known in the world by the name of Kātyāyanī. Under that name she shines and is celebrated in the world.\"\nAccording to the Kālikā Pr. this is the deity at Odh yana. The Devī Pr. says, Ka, means Brahmā, head and the philosopher's stone; as she supports or rests upon them, she is called Kātyāyanī.\"","dev":"कात्यायनी","iast":"kātyāyanī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":243,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":109},{"n":557,"gloss":"The destroyer of time","body":"Kālu, death. The Śruti (Sve. Up., 6-2) says, 66 Omniscient, time of time, possessed of all qualities, allknowing.\"","dev":"कालहन्त्री","iast":"kālahantrī","pada":[{"dev":"काल","iast":"kāla"},{"dev":"हन्त्री","iast":"hantrī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":243,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":109},{"n":558,"gloss":"Worshipped by Kamalaksha. sheviti.)","body":"(Kamalakshani-\nThe Padma Pr., \"Viṣṇu ever worships the sapphire Devī, hence he attained his own state.\"\n(113)","dev":"कमलाक्षनिषेविता","iast":"kamalākṣaniṣevitā","pada":[{"dev":"कमलाक्ष","iast":"kamalākṣa"},{"dev":"निषेविता","iast":"niṣevitā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.5,"cpage":243,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":109},{"n":559,"gloss":"Her mouth is full of betel","body":"","dev":"ताम्बूलपूरितमुखी","iast":"tāmbūlapūritamukhī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":243,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":110},{"n":560,"gloss":"Of the hue of pomegranate flower","body":"The pomegranate (Dadima) tree is a kind of tree which bars no frait but only flowers.","dev":"दाडिमीकुसुमप्रभा","iast":"dāḍimīkusumaprabhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":243,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":110},{"n":561,"gloss":"Fawneyed","body":"","dev":"मृगाक्षी","iast":"mṛgākṣī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":243,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":110},{"n":562,"gloss":"Enchanting","body":"The Laghu Nāradīya Pr.. ** Because, O beautiful one, this whole universe is enchanted with thee, thy name shall be the enchanting one, which is derived from its own qualities.\"\nOr, at the churning of the ocean, Viṣṇu assumed the form of Mohinī and that deity resides on the bank of the river Pratara. The Brahmā. Pr. says: As the result of his meditation there first appeared to Brahmā, the Śakti who is called Prakṛti, who fulfils the desires of the gods. Her second appearance causing enchantment of all, transcending mind and speech, was during the churning (of the ocean) for nectar, seeing which even the omniscient Śiva was enchanted The form obtained as the result of meditation, unequalled, marvellous, meditating with one mind on that (form) you will be emancipated.”","dev":"मोहिनी","iast":"mohinī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.857,"cpage":243,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":110},{"n":563,"gloss":"The first","body":"The Śruti (Tai. Up., 3-10-6), “I am the first born\nof truth.\"","dev":"मुख्या","iast":"mukhyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":244,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":110},{"n":564,"gloss":"The wife of Mṛḍa","body":"Mṛḍa, the giver of happiness, i.e., Paramas'iva.\n22nd verse livides 31 names.","dev":"मृडानी","iast":"mṛḍānī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.889,"cpage":244,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":110},{"n":565,"gloss":"The friend","body":"Or Mitru (the twelve) suns.","dev":"मित्ररूपिणी","iast":"mitrarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"मित्र","iast":"mitra"},{"dev":"रूपिणी","iast":"rūpiṇī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":244,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":110},{"n":566,"gloss":"Eternally pleased","body":"Or Nityā, happiness itself.","dev":"नित्यतृप्ता","iast":"nityatṛptā","pada":[{"dev":"नित्य","iast":"nitya"},{"dev":"तृप्ता","iast":"tṛptā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":244,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":111},{"n":567,"gloss":"Treasure of the devotees","body":"Nidhi, which fulfils desire.","dev":"भक्तनिधि","iast":"bhaktanidhi","pada":[{"dev":"भक्त","iast":"bhakta"},{"dev":"निधि","iast":"nidhi"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":244,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":111},{"n":568,"gloss":"The guide","body":"Because she is the guide of the universe.","dev":"नियन्त्री","iast":"niyantrī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":244,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":111},{"n":569,"gloss":"Ruler of all","body":"(114)","dev":"निखिलेश्वरी","iast":"nikhileśvarī","pada":[{"dev":"निखिल","iast":"nikhila"},{"dev":"ईश्वरी","iast":"īśvarī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":244,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":111},{"n":570,"gloss":"To be attained by the idea of cheerfulness, etc","body":"There are four\nkinds of ideas, riz., friendship, compassion, complacency and indifference: these apply respectively to the happy, the miserable, the virtuous, and the sinful. These are explained in the Vi. Bhag. Pr. These\n29*\npurify the mind. The wise say (Māgha 4-55), “They, whose minds are purified by friendship, etc., removing their sorrow and who obtaining here (in this body) the seed of Yoga, and attaining glory by discriminating Purusha from Sattva (Matter) and who remaining in Samādhi (the highest state of meditation), desire to transcend even that state.\" Refer to Yogasútra also (I, 33). The commentator Rajamärtända says, ' happiness, etc.,' indicates the persons who are happy, etc.","dev":"मैत्र्यादिवासनालभ्या","iast":"maitryādivāsanālabhyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":244,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":111},{"n":571,"gloss":"Witness of the great dissolution","body":"As Brahmā, Viṣṇu, etc., perish at that time of the dance of Śiva at the great dissolution, she alone is the witness (of that dance). It is said, Bearing the noose, the elephant hook, the bow of sugarcane, and the arrow of flowers, your form alone remains victorious, witnessing the dance of the divine Parabhairava, bearing the axe at the time of dissolution of the universe.\"\n(Parasaktih.)\n(115)","dev":"महाप्रलयसाक्षिणी","iast":"mahāpralayasākṣiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"महाप्रलय","iast":"mahāpralaya"},{"dev":"साक्षिणी","iast":"sākṣiṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":245,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":111},{"n":572,"gloss":"The Supreme energy. In the body the tenth elementary substance (Dhātu)","body":"is called Parā Śakti. The Kamikagama says, Skin, blood, flesh, fat and bone, these (five) substances are derived from Devī; marrow, semen, breath, vitality (JIva), these (four) substances are derived from Śiva. Thus this body consists of nine substances derived from nine origins. The tenth substance alone is called Parā Śakti.\"\nOr, the Śruti (Sve. Up., 6-8) says, His supreme Śakti is known in different forms.\" The Liṅga Pr. : 66 Whatever energy is attributed to any substance that is Devī, the ruler of all, energised substance is the great Śiva. stances which possess energy are the manifestations of Śiva. The wise recognise the energies in substance to be Devī.\" Or Paramantra also is called Paraśakti.","dev":"पराशक्तिः","iast":"parāśaktiḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.222,"cpage":245,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":112},{"n":573,"gloss":"Supreme end","body":"Those sub-\nOr Paranishtha: a certain kind of knowledge. This alone is the goal of all desires and all worlds.\nThe Bh.\nGītā (4-33): O Pārtha, all kinds of actions end in knowledge.\" That kind of knowledge is described in the Su. Sam. (Sūta Gītā, 5-50 to 54): \"Convinced by instruction derived from the scriptures and from teachers, and by reasoning in conformity with the scriptures, that he is himself the witness of all, his mind becomes fixed, knowing the whole which appears different from Self as his own Self, again fully convinced by his own experience that he himself is the pure, nondual Brahman; merging that conviction in his own pure consciousness which is unchangeable and nondual; knowing that even that merging is of the nature of thought (cit rūpa), he should remain as the absolute. This ascetic is indeed the best of those who know Brahman; this is the highest end (the result) of scriptural teachings and of experience.\"","dev":"परानिष्ठा","iast":"parāniṣṭhā","pada":[{"dev":"परा","iast":"parā"},{"dev":"निष्ठा","iast":"niṣṭhā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":245,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":112},{"n":574,"gloss":"Concentrated knowledge","body":"Pra, superior, i.e., different from the mental modifications, jñāna, the eternal wisdom, ghana, concentrated, i.e., not contaminated by Avidyā. The Śruti (Br. Up., 4-5-13): Just as taste of a quantity of salt is neither inside nor outside but everywhere, so O wife, this Self is neither within nor without, but is full and concentrated knowledge.\"","dev":"प्रज्ञानघनरूपिणी","iast":"prajñānaghanarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"प्रज्ञानघन","iast":"prajñānaghana"},{"dev":"रूपिणी","iast":"rūpiṇī"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":246,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":112},{"n":575,"gloss":"Languid by drinking wine","body":"Languid by drinking wine.","dev":"माध्वीपानालसा","iast":"mādhvīpānālasā","pada":[{"dev":"माध्वी","iast":"mādhvī"},{"dev":"पान","iast":"pāna"},{"dev":"अलसा","iast":"alasā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.167,"cpage":246,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":112},{"n":576,"gloss":"Intoxicated. (Matta.)","body":"(Madhvīpānālasa.)\nOr Matta, state of egoism, i.e., the Parāhantā of Parā Śiva.","dev":"मत्ता","iast":"mattā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.25,"cpage":246,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":112},{"n":577,"gloss":"In the form of mātṛkā letters","body":"Or Mātṛkā letters from A to Kshu, varna, colours, white, etc., Rūpini, Devī is in the form of these. The Sanatkumāra Sam. says, Vowels beginning with A (16) are smokecoloured; Ka and others (to da, 13) are red ; Dha to pha (9) are yellowish; the five letters Ba, etc., are crimson ; Sa, etc., (5) are of the colour of gold; Ha and the last (Kṣa) are of the colour of lightning.\" Some Tantras liffer in this: The vowels (16), are the colour of crystal'\nKa to Ma (25) are the colour of coral, Ya and others (to ha. 9) are yellowish, and Kṣa is crimson.\" Some say, all the letters are white. The Mātṛkāviveka says, A is all divinity, red, controlling all things Thus to each letter a different colour is given.\nOr, the fiftyone letters (A to Kṣa) are her form1 Varna, the letters, p, indicates (her). The Su. Sam. (IV, 4, 21 and 22), says, \"Just as the Supreme Śiva is twofold as Śakti and Śiva, so this Mātṛkā Devī herself shines in two. The vowels indicate one form of Śakti, the others (consonants) indicate of Śiva. Thus the (Devī) of Vidyā under the form of letters indicate Śiva.\" The commentator on this explains 4, etc.. means Śrīkaṇṭha and other forins of Śiva and as well as Purodari and other Śaktis. Or, wearing the garland of letters (Akṣamālā, A to Kṣa). The Jñānārṇava says, A is the first, O Devī, and Kṣa is the last, thus she is called Akṣamālā (garland consisting of letters A, to Kṣa). She is the Brahman-Word itself, but in reality she transcends the Word.\"\nOr, Mātṛkāvarṇa, letters, rūpa, (she) creates. Refer to the Saubhāgya Sudhodaya wherein is described in order, the origin of the letters through the Icchās'akti belonging to Śiva in his Anuttara aspect.\nOr, Mātṛkāvarṇa, letters, which belong to the Śrīcakra: this is described in the Sixth chapter of the Mātṛkāviveka; hence the Sanandana Sam. says, \"Meditation on the letters and on the Śrīcakra as one is called Kailāsa Prastāra.\nAccording to some, this name is divided into two as Mātṛkā, (Mother) and rarnarpini (in the form of letters.)\"\n(116)\nThere are three Prastāras, ciz., Meru, Kailāsa, and Bhu Prastāras. The 1st is to identify the sixteen Nityā deities, the 2nd with the Mātṛkā (51 letters), and the 3rd is with the eight Vāsinīs.\n2 In the Varivasyārahasya the word mātṛkā is explained thus: ma means limits or measures, tri, crosses, ka, shines, i.e., one who knows Mātṛkā letters, knows Brahman (ma), crosses saṃsāra and shines.","dev":"मातृकावर्णरूपिणी","iast":"mātṛkāvarṇarūpiṇī","pada":[{"dev":"मातृका","iast":"mātṛkā"},{"dev":"वर्ण","iast":"varṇa"},{"dev":"रूपिणी","iast":"rūpiṇī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":246,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":112},{"n":578,"gloss":"Residing in the great Kailāsa","body":"Mahākailāsa is a place much beyond the present Kailāsa (mountain), known in the Śiva and other Prs. as the abiding place of Paramaśiva.\nOr, Mahākailāsa means as above, the Kailāsa Prastāra (of Śrīcakra). Or, kailāsu, the Sahasrāra which is in the Brahmarandhra. The Tripurasara says, This is called Kailāsa, the Kula,' and the seat of Akula, where the lord of the lords (Śiva) in the form of bindu resides.\" 25. Mahat (great) because Vāsinī, etc., the Nityā deities, which belong to the other Prastāras, have no place in this one, hence it is the great.","dev":"महाकैलासनिलया","iast":"mahākailāsanilayā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"कैलास","iast":"kailāsa"},{"dev":"निलया","iast":"nilayā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":248,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":113},{"n":579,"gloss":"Her creeperlike arms are soft as the lotus stem","body":"","dev":"मृणालमृदुदोर्लता","iast":"mṛṇālamṛdudorlatā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":248,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":113},{"n":580,"gloss":"Illustrious","body":"","dev":"महनीया","iast":"mahanīyā","pada":[],"pconf":0.7,"fit":1,"cpage":248,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":113},{"n":581,"gloss":"Personification of mercy","body":"","dev":"दयामूर्ति","iast":"dayāmūrti","pada":[{"dev":"दया","iast":"dayā"},{"dev":"मूर्ति","iast":"mūrti"}],"pconf":1,"fit":0.909,"cpage":248,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":113},{"n":582,"gloss":"Resplendent with wide dominion","body":"","dev":"महासाम्राज्यशालिनी","iast":"mahāsāmrājyaśālinī","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"साम्राज्य","iast":"sāmrājya"},{"dev":"शालिनी","iast":"śālinī"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":248,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":113},{"n":583,"gloss":"Spiritual knowledge","body":"(117)\nAtmavidya may mean eightsyllabled Mantra (Atmāshtakshara Mantra.)","dev":"आत्मविद्या","iast":"ātmavidyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":248,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":114},{"n":584,"gloss":"Exalted science","body":"Because it removes all sorrows.\nOr, Mahāvidya, Vanadurgā Mantra.","dev":"महाविद्या","iast":"mahāvidyā","pada":[{"dev":"महा","iast":"mahā"},{"dev":"विद्या","iast":"vidyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":248,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":114},{"n":585,"gloss":"Sacred science","body":"Or, the Panchandast Mantra. The Viṣṇu Pr. says,\nO fair one, the sacrificial science, exalted science, secret science, and spiritual science, O Devī, thou art all these, the bestower of salvation; also thou art Logic, Trayī, Vārtā, trade and justice.\" The commentators on the above say The meaning of the 1st four are-Science of action, devotion to deities, the science of Mantras, and the science of Brahman, l'ārtā, architecture, medicine, etc.","dev":"श्रीविद्या","iast":"śrīvidyā","pada":[{"dev":"श्री","iast":"śrī"},{"dev":"विद्या","iast":"vidyā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":248,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":114},{"n":586,"gloss":"Attended by Kāma","body":"The lower six cakras from Ajnā to Mūlādhåra are called Kula.\nKāma, Mahākāma.\nOr, Kāma, the bodiless one (god of love). The Arun. Up. (Tai. Ar., I-11), “The bodiless son of Lakṣmī, though with- He knew that jewel (Śrīvidyā). out mind, has animation. He, though without fingers, worshipped (folding his hands). Though without neck, he adorned himself (as with a necklace). Though without tongue, he tasted it. Without knowing that Ṛṣi (seer) one should enter the city. When one enters, he should enter after performing secret rites. is the vow of Manmatha.\" Without mind (Achetas), without eyes he saw the jewel. Since he saw it without eyes, he touched it without fingers, wore the necklace without a neck, tasted without tougue, he is the seer. The external cakra (worship) can be entered (performed) by knowledge of him alone; since no ceremonial worship is necessary (in order to enter the internal cakra), the Ṛṣi need not be known. Secret rites, i.e., the knowledge of the essential equality of Śiva with Devī. The meaning is that worship performed without the knowledge of the essential equality of Śiva with Devī cannot be fully effective.","dev":"कामसेविता","iast":"kāmasevitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":248,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":114},{"n":587,"gloss":"The science of sixteen syllabled (Mantra)","body":"เ\nThough the Gauḍapāda Sūtra (67) says, This Mantra consists of twentyeight syllables,\" yet there will be no contradiction, if we divide the fifteen syllabled Mantra into three divisions (and add some syllables more between the divisions). Or, it means a Vidyā where in the Sixteenth syllable (Sri) is used.","dev":"श्रीषोडशाक्षरीविद्या","iast":"śrīṣoḍaśākṣarīvidyā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":249,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":114},{"n":588,"gloss":"Three peaked","body":"Tri, may mean Brahmā, etc., the three states (waking, etc.), the three seats (Jālandhara, etc.), the three worlds, three qualities, etc.; or Trikāta means Vāgbhava and other Kūṭas.","dev":"त्रिकूटा","iast":"trikūṭā","pada":[],"pconf":0.7,"fit":1,"cpage":249,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":114},{"n":589,"gloss":"The spirit of Kāma","body":"Kūma, Paramaśiva, because the essential equality of\nŚiva with Devī is Brahman itself.\n(118)\n590. glances.","dev":"कामकोटिका","iast":"kāmakoṭikā","pada":[{"dev":"काम","iast":"kāma"},{"dev":"कोटिका","iast":"koṭikā"}],"pconf":1,"fit":1,"cpage":249,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":114},{"n":590,"gloss":"The spirit of Kāma. (Kamakotika.)","body":"Kūma, Paramaśiva, because the essential equality of\nŚiva with Devī is Brahman itself.\n(118)\n590. glances.","dev":"कटाक्षकिङ्करीभूतकमलाकोटिसेविता शिरः","iast":"kaṭākṣakiṅkarībhūtakamalākoṭisevitā śiraḥ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":249,"sure":0,"host":589,"v":115},{"n":591,"gloss":"Attended by millions of Lakṣmīs subdued by her (Katakshakinkarībhūta kamalāko/isevita.)","body":"Residing in the head. (Sirassthita.)\nSiras, Brahmarandhra, wherein she resides assuming the form of the Guru.","dev":"स्थिता","iast":"sthitā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.286,"cpage":250,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":115},{"n":592,"gloss":"Moonlike","body":"There is a moon in the lower part of the Brahmarandhra, that is the third division of the Vidyā (Pañcadaśī).","dev":"चन्द्रनिभा","iast":"candranibhā","pada":[{"dev":"चन्द्र","iast":"candra"},{"dev":"निभा","iast":"nibhā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":250,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":115},{"n":593,"gloss":"Residing in the forehead","body":"She resides in the forehead in the form of the Bindu of the syllable Hrīm.","dev":"भालस्थें","iast":"bhālastheṃ","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.769,"cpage":250,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":115},{"n":594,"gloss":"Rainbow hued","body":"The Nityāhṛdaya (Yoginīhṛdaya, I; 28) says, “The Ardhamātra (i.e.. Dot or Bindu of Om or Hrīm) is in the forehead in the form of the light; above that there is the Ardhachandra in the same form (i.e., of light) and it is a quarter of that \" (¿.e., Mātra consists of 256 lavas; Ardhamatra, 128 and Ardhachantra 64 lavas).”","dev":"न्द्रधनुः प्रभा","iast":"ndradhanuḥ prabhā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0.909,"cpage":250,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":115},{"n":595,"gloss":"Residing in the heart","body":"Because she is to be contemplated as in the heart.\n(119)\nOr, Parābīja is called Hṛdaya. The Kalpa Sūtra : “He who knows the heart of the Lord finds happiness at every step.\" Here heart means the Parabija.\nOr, Hṛdaya, a Upanished called Parameś'varahridaya. Or Hṛdaya, the seed of the universe, because it is the seed of all. The Anuttaratrims'ikasastra : Just as a great Baniyan tree is contained potentially in a tiny seed, so the animate and inanimate universe resides as a seed in the heart.\"\n23rd verse divides 35 names.","dev":"हृदयस्था","iast":"hṛdayasthā","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":1,"cpage":250,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":116},{"n":596,"gloss":"Resembling the sun","body":"In the heart there is a solar disc, that is the second\ndivision of the Mantra (of Panchadasî).","dev":"रविप्रख्या","iast":"raviprakhyā","pada":[{"dev":"रवि","iast":"ravi"},{"dev":"प्रख्या","iast":"prakhyā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":1,"cpage":250,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":116},{"n":597,"gloss":"The light within the triangle. dipika.)","body":"(Trikonantara-\n**The Supreme Energy is called Tripurā,\" ending with the verse Hence she is called Tripurā \" and we are going to explain in more details in its commentary.","dev":"त्रिकोणान्तरदीपिका","iast":"trikoṇāntaradīpikā","pada":[{"dev":"त्रिकोण","iast":"trikoṇa"},{"dev":"अन्तर","iast":"antara"},{"dev":"दीपिका","iast":"dīpikā"}],"pconf":0.78,"fit":0.5,"cpage":250,"sure":1,"host":0,"v":116},{"n":598,"gloss":"The light within the triangle. dipika.)","body":"(Trikonantara-\n**The Supreme Energy is called Tripurā,\" ending with the verse Hence she is called Tripurā \" and we are going to explain in more details in its commentary.","dev":"दाक्षायणी","iast":"dākṣāyaṇī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":250,"sure":0,"host":597,"v":116},{"n":599,"gloss":"The light within the triangle. dipika.)","body":"(Trikonantara-\n**The Supreme Energy is called Tripurā,\" ending with the verse Hence she is called Tripurā \" and we are going to explain in more details in its commentary.","dev":"दैत्यहन्त्री","iast":"daityahantrī","pada":[],"pconf":0,"fit":0,"cpage":250,"sure":0,"host":597,"v":116},{"n":600,"gloss":"The light within the triangle. dipika.)","body":"(Trikonantara-\n**The Supreme Energy is called Tripurā,\" ending with the verse Hence she is called Tripurā \" and we are going to explain in more details in its commentary.","dev":"दक्षयज्ञविनाशिनी","iast":"dakṣayajñavināśinī